export const LIBRARY_BODIES_HELP: Record<string, string> = {
  "browsing-the-shop": "\n# Browsing the Shop\n\nThis page covers how the shop is organized, how to move between departments, brands, and collections, and how to search for a product and narrow the results.\n\n## How the shop is organized\n\nEverything for sale is under `/shop`. The shop is organized by animal first, then by department. The shop landing page shows:\n\n- **Animal cards** for Cat and Dog, each with a current product count. Selecting a card opens that animal's departments.\n- **A category strip** covering every animal and department, for moving sideways between departments.\n- **Top sellers**, a row of products selected by the shop team.\n- **A department display** for dog and cat departments, with product counts and photos.\n\nA department address shows where you are, for example `/shop/dog/beds` or `/shop/cat/toys`. The breadcrumb on each page follows the same path. A bookmarked department address continues to work.\n\n## Department pages\n\nA department page shows the products in that department, with related departments listed for further browsing.\n\n- Product counts next to each department are current. A department with no products is not shown in navigation.\n- Each department page shows up to four bestsellers and four new arrivals from its own products, with a link to the full listing.\n\n## Brands and collections\n\n- **Brands** (`/shop/brands`) lists every brand with products in the shop. Each brand has its own page at `/shop/brands/[brand]`. Start there when you already know the brand you want.\n- **Collections** (`/shop/collections`) are groupings selected by the shop team, such as seasonal groupings and salon favorites. Start there when you are browsing for ideas rather than a specific item.\n\n## Searching the shop\n\nThe search box is in the shop header. Enter a term and submit to open a results page.\n\nResults show 24 products per page, with Previous and Next controls. Each result shows the product photo, name, price, a sale price where one applies, badges such as SALE, NEW, or BESTSELLER, and a star rating where reviews exist. Select a result to open the product page. See Reading a Product Page (§3.1.2).\n\n### Narrowing results\n\n1. Run a broad search first so you can see the range of matching products and prices.\n2. To filter by price, enter a minimum and a maximum in the sidebar and select Apply.\n3. To filter by brand, select a brand in the sidebar. Each brand shows the number of matching products. Select Clear brand to remove the brand filter without removing your other filters.\n4. To change the order, choose a sort: Best match, Price: low to high, or Price: high to low.\n\nThe heading states the number of matches. Your filter choices appear in the page address, so a filtered view can be shared or bookmarked.\n\n### Search tips\n\n- Fewer words match more products. \"Salmon treats\" returns more than a long description.\n- A brand name plus a product type, such as \"KONG bed,\" narrows results faster than a description.\n- Apply the price filter after the first search, once you can see the price range for the products that match.\n\n### When a search finds nothing\n\nThe results page states that there are no matches and suggests checking the spelling, trying a broader term, or browsing the departments. If filters were applied, select Clear filters to remove the brand, price, and page choices in one step while keeping your search term.\n\n## Browsing on a phone\n\nOn a small screen, the search filters appear above the results instead of beside them, and the department grid shows two columns instead of four. The bag, product pages, and checkout work the same way. If a filter is not visible beside the results, scroll up to find it above the product grid.\n\n## Related pages on the site\n\n- `/free-shipping` explains the Shipping Club. See Shipping, Taxes, and the Shipping Club (§3.1.5).\n- `/gift-cards` is where gift cards are bought. See Gift Cards (§3.2.11).\n- `/policies` lists the published policies with their full text.\n- `/faq` gives short answers to common questions.\n- `/docs` is the Help Center, with guides organized by audience.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about finding a product: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "reading-a-product-page": "\n# Reading a Product Page\n\nThis page covers what each part of a product page shows — price, options, stock, and reviews — how the Add to Bag and Buy Now buttons differ, and what to do when part of a page looks wrong.\n\n## Price and badges\n\nThe large number in the panel beside the photo gallery is the price you pay for the item. Below it you may see:\n\n- A struck-through price with a SALE badge and a saving percentage. The struck-through price appears only when the product is marked on sale and the comparison price is higher than the current price.\n- A NEW badge on recently added products, or a BESTSELLER badge on products that sell in volume. These badges describe the product; they do not change the price.\n\nPrices are governed by the Pricing, Availability and Errors Policy (§2.1.14). Product information is governed by the Product Information Disclaimer (§2.1.13).\n\n## Options and quantity\n\nMany products offer options such as size, flavor, or color. Options are grouped by type. Selecting an option can change the price and the photo. Each option you choose is treated as its own line in your bag, shown with a label such as \"Product Name — Large.\" A product with no options has no picker.\n\nThe quantity stepper on the product page sets how many you are buying, from 1 up to 10 of one item. Checkout permits up to 20 of one item.\n\n## Add to Bag and Buy Now\n\nThe two buttons do different things:\n\n- **Add to Bag** adds the item, with your chosen option and quantity, to your bag and keeps you on the page. Your bag count in the header updates. Use it when you are still shopping. See Your Bag (§3.1.3).\n- **Buy Now** takes you directly toward payment for that item. If you are not signed in, you are sent to sign in first and then returned to the product, with your selection kept.\n\nExpress wallet buttons, such as Apple Pay and Google Pay, may appear below the two buttons. They open the same checkout using your wallet's saved details. If a wallet payment fails, the page tells you so, and you can complete the purchase through Add to Bag instead.\n\n## One-time purchase and subscription purchase\n\nEligible consumable products — food, treats, supplements, and flea and tick products — may also offer a subscription purchase alongside the one-time purchase. Durable products, such as beds and toys, are sold one-time only. Enrollment, discounts, and how to pause, skip, or cancel are covered in Reship (§3.2.6).\n\nThis page summarizes the Reship Subscription Terms (§2.1.23), which governs.\n\n## Photos\n\nA product page shows up to eight photos, with the first as the main image. Selecting an option can change the gallery to show that option's image. A product with one photo shows one photo.\n\n## Details below the purchase panel\n\n- **Highlights** — up to four short points drawn from the product's own published description, specifications, and questions and answers.\n- **Detail tabs** — ingredients, directions, specifications, warranty, and the full description, depending on what the product carries. Pet food listings show ingredients and feeding directions. A product with no warranty text has no warranty tab.\n- **Frequently bought together** — companion products, each with its own add control.\n- **Reviews** — customer reviews for the product. The average rating and the number of reviews appear with the purchase panel. Reviews are governed by the User Content and Reviews Policy (§2.1.12).\n- **Customers Also Bought and You May Also Like** — further product rows based on the product and its department.\n- **Things for Your Pet** — department suggestions drawn from the product's own department path, limited to departments that have products.\n\n## Stock and availability\n\nThe purchase panel reflects the stock of the option you selected, not the product as a whole. One size can be available while another is sold out. When an item or option is out of stock, the purchase buttons are disabled and no backorder is offered. If an item in your bag sells out before you check out, checkout checks every line again and tells you which item is no longer available instead of charging you for it.\n\n## If a product page looks wrong\n\n- **The price differs from your last visit.** Prices can change, for example for a promotion. Checkout checks the current price for every line and shows the verified total at the review step before you pay.\n- **The page says the product is not available for online purchase yet.** The product is in the catalog but has no purchasable price attached. It cannot be bought until a price is attached.\n- **A photo does not load.** The purchase panel still works. Report the page address through the Help Center at `/docs`.\n- **The wrong option is in your bag.** The bag line shows the option label. Remove that line and add the item again with the correct option selected.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about a product page or a price: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "your-bag": "\n# Your Bag\n\nThis page covers where your bag is stored, what the bag page shows, how to change quantities and remove items, and why checkout asks you to sign in.\n\n## Where your bag is stored\n\nYour bag is stored in your browser, on your device. No account is needed to build a bag; you sign in only when you are ready to pay.\n\n- The bag survives refreshes, closed tabs, and navigation. Close the tab and return later, and the bag is as you left it.\n- The bag is specific to one browser on one device. A bag built on your phone does not appear on your laptop, and there is no transfer between devices.\n- Clearing your browser's site data clears the bag. A private or incognito window starts with an empty bag each session.\n\n## What the bag page shows\n\nThe bag is at `/shop/bag`. Each line shows the product photo, the name with its option label where one applies, the unit price, a quantity stepper, the line total, and a remove control. Below the lines:\n\n- **Subtotal** — the sum of every line, calculated from the stored prices.\n- **Standard shipping** — shown with no separate charge. Shipping is included in item prices. See Shipping, Taxes, and the Shipping Club (§3.1.5).\n- A note that taxes are calculated at checkout. The tax amount is shown at the review step, drawn out of your total by ZIP code. Your total does not change because of it.\n- A note that pickup in the salon is also available at checkout.\n\nAn empty bag shows a short message and a button back to `/shop`.\n\n## Changing quantities and removing items\n\n1. Use the plus and minus steppers to change a line's quantity, from 1 up to 10 per item. The minus control is disabled at 1; use remove to drop the line. The plus control is disabled at 10.\n2. Select remove to delete a line. Removal takes effect immediately and a short confirmation names the item removed.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Removing a line cannot be undone from the bag. To restore an item, add it again from its product page and select the option and quantity again.\n\nLine totals and the subtotal update as soon as you change a quantity.\n\n## Proceeding to checkout\n\nSelect Proceed to Checkout on the bag page.\n\n- If you are signed in, you enter checkout with your name and email already filled in.\n- If you are signed out, you are taken to sign in at `/access-customer` and returned to `/shop/bag` afterward. Your bag is unchanged.\n\nThere is no guest checkout. Sign-in is required to place an order, and the requirement is applied at checkout itself. If the sign-in check cannot be completed, for example because of a network interruption, the page leaves you on the bag rather than treating you as signed out. See Checkout Step by Step (§3.1.4).\n\n## What the bag does not do\n\n- **No promotion code field.** Promotions are applied through product prices, such as sale prices and subscription discounts, not through a code entered in the bag.\n- **No save-for-later list.** The bag is the list. To set an item aside, remove it and return to its product page later, or save it to your Wish List (§3.2.5).\n- **No price-change notice.** The bag shows the price stored when you added the item. If a price has changed since, checkout checks every line again and shows the current verified total at the review step, before you pay.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n- **The bag shows items you did not add.** The bag belongs to the browser, so anyone who used the same browser on a shared computer shares the bag. Remove the lines that are not yours.\n- **The bag is empty on a different device.** This is expected, because bags do not transfer between devices. Rebuild the bag on that device, or check out on the device where you built it.\n- **A quantity will not go above 10.** Ten per item is the limit on the product page and in the bag. Checkout permits up to 20 of one item, so you can raise the quantity at the bag step inside checkout.\n- **An item disappeared from the bag.** Items are removed only by you or by clearing site data. The site does not remove items from your bag.\n- **Proceed to Checkout does nothing.** A failed sign-in check leaves you on the bag page. Check your connection, refresh the page, and try again. You can also go directly to `/access-customer` to sign in.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about your bag or signing in to check out: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "checkout-step-by-step": "\n# Checkout Step by Step\n\nThis page covers each of the four checkout steps — bag, contact, delivery, and review — what you need at each step, how payment is taken, and what each checkout message means.\n\nCheckout opens on the `/shop` page. You stay on the site until the payment step, which hands you to Stripe's secure payment page and returns you to the site afterward.\n\nThis page summarizes the Order Formation and Acceptance Policy (§2.1.15), which governs, and the Payment Terms (§2.1.16), which governs.\n\n## Before you start\n\nThere is no guest checkout. Requirements before you begin:\n\n- A signed-in account. If you start checkout signed out, you are taken to `/access-customer` to sign in and returned to your bag afterward, with the bag unchanged.\n- Your contact email, a phone number, and, for delivery, a United States shipping address.\n\nIf you are signed in, the contact step arrives with your name and email already filled in.\n\n## Step 1 — Bag\n\nThe first step reviews your bag: every line with its photo, name, option, quantity, and line total, plus the subtotal. Quantity steppers work here as they do on the bag page. The step bar at the top shows all four steps. You can return to any step you have completed; you cannot move forward past a step you have not completed. Select Back, labeled Keep Shopping on this step, to leave checkout without losing your bag.\n\n## Step 2 — Contact\n\nEnter four required fields:\n\n1. First name and last name.\n2. Email. Your receipt and order updates are sent to this address. It is checked for a valid format, so check it carefully before you continue.\n3. Phone. This number is used for delivery problems, such as a driver who cannot find the address.\n\nIf you are signed in, a strip confirms the account you are signed in with. Details carried into this step can be changed for this order; changing them here does not change your account profile. Contact details on this step are used to fulfill this order.\n\nHow your personal information is handled is governed by the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2).\n\n## Step 3 — Delivery\n\nChoose one delivery method:\n\n- **Ship to me** — complimentary standard shipping, 5–7 business days. Enter the street address (required), apartment, suite, or unit (optional), city, state, and ZIP code (required). The address is checked for completeness, not for deliverability, so read it once before continuing. A mistyped street address will misroute the package.\n- **Pick up in salon** — your order is prepared at the Memphis salon and the team calls you when it is ready. The panel shows the salon address and hours: UnSaid Memories — Memphis Salon, 5515 Quince Rd, Memphis, TN 38119; Tuesday–Saturday 9am–6pm, Sunday 10am–4pm. No charge is made for pickup itself. Bring your order confirmation email when you collect the order.\n\nAn optional order notes field sits below the delivery choice. Use it for gate codes, delivery instructions, or a gift message. Notes travel with the order.\n\n## Step 4 — Review and pay\n\nThe review step shows your items on one side and your details on the other: name, email, phone, the delivery address or the pickup salon, and your order notes. Check the address character by character. An incorrect address is the most common cause of a failed delivery.\n\nThe totals block shows:\n\n- **Subtotal** — the sum of your lines.\n- **Standard shipping — FREE**, or Pickup in salon. Shipping is included in item prices rather than added here. See Shipping, Taxes, and the Shipping Club (§3.1.5).\n- **Sales tax** — where your ZIP code resolves, a line shows the tax portion of your total, labeled as included in prices. The total does not change because of this line. If the ZIP code cannot be resolved, the line does not appear. Sales tax collection is governed by the Marketplace Facilitator Tax Policy (§2.1.17).\n- **Total** — the amount on the pay button. It matches the subtotal, because shipping is included and tax is shown within the total.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Selecting the pay button creates your order and sends you to Stripe to pay the total shown. Check every line, the delivery address, and your email before you select it. Checkout checks every product and price against the current catalog when the order is created: if a price changed after you added an item, the current price applies and is the price in the payment session; if an item is no longer available, you are told so instead of being charged.\n\nOn Stripe's page you pay by card or wallet. Your card details are entered on Stripe's page and do not pass through this site. The address collected there is limited to United States addresses.\n\n## After payment\n\n- **Success** returns you to the shop with an Order Received confirmation. The page confirms the payment with Stripe, then confirms that payment was received and that a receipt email is on its way. Your bag is cleared when you continue shopping. See After You Order (§3.1.6).\n- **Cancel** — closing or backing out of Stripe's page returns you to the shop. No charge is made, your bag is unchanged, and you can restart checkout at any time.\n\n## Checkout messages and what to do\n\n- **Sign in required** — your session expired during checkout. Sign in again at `/access-customer`. Your bag is unchanged.\n- **Your bag is empty** — checkout received no items. Refresh the bag page; if lines are shown there, re-enter checkout.\n- **A valid email is required** — the email was rejected at order creation. Correct the format at the contact step and continue.\n- **One of the products in your bag is no longer available** — stock ran out or the listing was removed between bagging and paying. Remove that line and check out with the rest.\n- **Payment failed. Please try Add to Bag instead** — an express wallet payment failed. Pay by card on Stripe's page instead.\n- **Network error — please try again** — the request did not complete. Your bag is unchanged; retry, or complete checkout through the four steps.\n- **Payments not configured** — a configuration problem on the Company's side. No order was created. Wait and retry; if the message persists, contact support.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about a charge or a checkout problem: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "shipping-taxes-and-the-shipping-club": "\n# Shipping, Taxes, and the Shipping Club\n\nThis page covers how shipping is priced, how sales tax is shown, where orders can be delivered, and how the Shipping Club returns the shipping portion of a price on consumable products.\n\nThis page summarizes the Shipping and Delivery Policy (§2.1.18), which governs, and the Marketplace Facilitator Tax Policy (§2.1.17), which governs.\n\n## Shipping is included in the price\n\nEvery product price already includes its shipping cost. The shipping portion is set when the price is set and varies with the item's weight; a heavy bag of food carries a larger shipping portion than a collar.\n\nWhat this means for you:\n\n- The price on the product page is the whole price. No shipping charge is added at checkout, and the review step shows Standard shipping — FREE.\n- FREE in this context means the shipping cost is already included in the price you paid, not that shipping costs nothing.\n- Every buyer of the same item pays the same included shipping. There is no zone calculation and no carrier upgrade offered at checkout.\n\nStandard shipping takes 5–7 business days.\n\n## The Shipping Club\n\nThe Shipping Club, described at `/free-shipping`, is a membership for buyers who order consumables regularly. While your membership is active, the shipping portion included in each eligible item's price is credited back to you, per unit, at checkout.\n\n- **Consumables only.** The credit applies per unit to products classed as consumables: food, treats, supplements, flea and tick products, and litter. Beds, toys, and other durable products receive no credit, and their listed price applies to members and non-members alike.\n- **A minimum unit price applies.** The credit never reduces a unit price below $0.50.\n- **The credit is automatic.** No code and no setting is required. At checkout, your membership is checked against your account email and against the subscription record, and the credit is applied line by line.\n- **Bath Club members are already covered.** Membership in the PAWfection Bath Club includes the same shipping benefit, so there is no need to hold both memberships.\n- **Price.** $9.99 per month, or $99 per year. Joining, managing, and cancelling are covered in Shipping Club (§3.2.9).\n\n## Salon pickup\n\nPickup in the salon is the second delivery method at checkout. You pay the listed price — the included shipping portion stays in the price and no pickup discount applies — and you collect the order at UnSaid Memories — Memphis Salon, 5515 Quince Rd, Memphis, TN 38119, Tuesday–Saturday 9am–6pm, Sunday 10am–4pm. Orders are prepared within one business day and the team calls you when yours is ready. Bring your email receipt.\n\n## Sales tax\n\nPrices are shown tax-inclusive. At the review step, the tax portion of your total for your ZIP code is drawn out and shown as its own line, labeled \"Sales tax (by ZIP, included in prices).\" The line is informational: it shows the tax already inside your total and does not add to it. If the ZIP code cannot be resolved, the line does not appear.\n\nIn practice:\n\n- The total on the pay button is the total charged. There is no later \"plus tax\" step.\n- The same bag costs the same total in a different state. The tax amount shown can differ; the total does not.\n- Your receipt email states the tax portion separately for your records.\n\n## Where orders can be delivered\n\nCheckout delivers to United States addresses only. The address collection at payment is restricted to the United States, and no international, APO, FPO, or DPO delivery is offered. If your address is outside the United States, checkout cannot be completed for delivery.\n\n## Price accuracy at checkout\n\nTwo rules protect the total you pay:\n\n1. A struck-through comparison price appears only when the product is marked on sale and the comparison price is higher than the current price.\n2. The price stored in your bag is checked against the current price when your order is created. If the two differ, the current price applies, and you see it at the review step before you pay.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about shipping, tax on an order, or the Shipping Club: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "after-you-order": "\n# After You Order\n\nThis page covers what happens once payment is taken: the confirmation screen, your receipt, delivery and pickup timing, how to change or cancel, and what to do when an order goes wrong.\n\nReturns and refunds are governed by the Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.1.19). Protection for a paid order that does not arrive or does not match its listing is governed by the Buyer Protection Program (§2.1.43). This page summarizes those documents, which govern.\n\n## The Order Received screen\n\nA successful payment returns you to the shop. The page first confirms your payment with Stripe — you will briefly see \"Confirming your payment with Stripe…\" — and then shows the Order Received screen, confirming that payment was received, that your order is confirmed, and that a receipt email is on its way. Two buttons follow: Continue Shopping and Book a Groom.\n\nThree points about this screen:\n\n1. The success indicator is removed from the page address as soon as the page loads, so refreshing does not repeat the confirmation or create anything twice. If you refresh, you see the ordinary shop page.\n2. Your bag is cleared when you continue shopping, because the order has been placed.\n3. If payment confirmation is still settling, the screen states that your order has been received and payment is being confirmed. The order exists in either case, and the receipt email follows.\n\n## Your receipt email\n\nA receipt email is sent to the email address you entered at the contact step of checkout. It lists your items and quantities, the total charged, the tax portion stated separately, and your delivery selection — the delivery address or salon pickup.\n\nIf the email has not arrived within a few minutes:\n\n1. Check your spam folder.\n2. Check the address you entered at checkout. A mistyped address is the most common cause.\n3. Check your order in the customer portal. The order is recorded there whether or not the email arrived. See Order History (§3.2.2).\n\n## Delivery: 5–7 business days\n\nStandard shipping is 5–7 business days from fulfillment, to the address entered at the delivery step.\n\n- If you notice an address error, contact the salon before the order ships. Once an order is with the carrier, the address is much harder to change.\n- If tracking shows the order delivered but you do not have it, check with household members and with your leasing office or mailroom, and allow 24 hours. Then contact support with your order number.\n- Delivery is to United States addresses only.\n\nTracking is covered in Order Tracking (§3.2.3).\n\n## Pickup: prepared within one business day\n\nPickup orders are prepared at UnSaid Memories — Memphis Salon, 5515 Quince Rd, Memphis, TN 38119, Tuesday–Saturday 9am–6pm, Sunday 10am–4pm. The team prepares your order within one business day and calls the phone number from your contact step when it is ready. Bring your email receipt when you collect it.\n\n## Changing or cancelling an order\n\nBacking out of Stripe's payment page returns you to the shop with no charge made, your bag unchanged, and no order to cancel. You can restart checkout at any time.\n\nAfter a completed payment, there is no self-service order edit. Contact support as soon as possible and give your order number. An order that has not been fulfilled can usually be adjusted or cancelled. An order that has shipped is handled through the return process instead. Order notes cannot be added after checkout; send the note to support with your order number as soon as you can.\n\n## When something goes wrong\n\n- **You were charged but saw no confirmation screen.** Check your email for the receipt, then your card statement for the charge. If both are present, contact support with the charge amount and date. Your order is recorded when it is placed, so support can find it.\n- **You saw the confirmation but no receipt email arrived.** Check spam, then the email address on the order. The order stands as confirmed either way.\n- **Your statement shows two charges for one order.** Contact support promptly with the details of both charges. A duplicate charge for a single order is refunded.\n- **An item is missing from the shipment.** Report it with your order number. The order record shows exactly what was to be packed.\n- **The wrong item arrived.** Report it with your order number, what arrived, and what you expected. The Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.1.19) governs the remedy.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about an order, a charge, or a delivery: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "trust-safety-policies-and-help": "\n# Trust, Safety, Policies, and Help\n\nThis page covers where the rules that protect you are published, what cannot be sold in the shop, what a listing promises, and how to reach support when a guide does not resolve your question.\n\n## The Help Center\n\nThe Help Center at `/docs` holds guides organized by audience — shoppers, sellers, groomers, veterinarians, franchisees, and others. The articles in this section cover shopping. The Help Center covers the rest.\n\n## Policies\n\nEvery published policy is listed at `/policies`, with the full text of each policy on its own page. The index covers appointments, orders, cancellations, refunds, shipping, privacy, and terms.\n\n> [!NOTICE]\n> Where a summary in a help article and the full policy text differ, the policy text governs. Read the full policy before making a decision that depends on it, such as returning an item or disputing a charge.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n`/faq` gives short answers to common questions about shopping and the salon, together with policy summaries. It is the fastest place to check before contacting support.\n\n## What cannot be sold here\n\nThe Prohibited and Restricted Products Policy (§2.1.29) governs what may be offered. In summary:\n\n- No weapons, ammunition, or explosives. No tobacco. No alcohol.\n- Prescription pet medications are dispensed only by a licensed pharmacy on a valid prescription from a licensed veterinarian. See the Prescription Products Policy (§2.1.31). Flea, tick, and pesticide products must carry an EPA registration number to be listed.\n- Products under an active recall are not listed. See the Product Recall Policy (§2.1.32).\n- Counterfeit products are not permitted. Products subject to brand authorization require the seller to hold that authorization.\n- Live animals are not sold through the shop checkout. See the Live Animal Policy (§2.1.30). The shop sells pet products and pet services, such as grooming and veterinary consultations.\n- Hazardous materials are shipped only as the dangerous goods rules permit, or not at all.\n\n## What a listing promises\n\n- **Accurate descriptions.** A product must match its title, description, images, and identifiers. A product that materially differs from its listing is a policy violation with a defined remedy, not a risk the buyer carries.\n- **Complete pricing.** The displayed price includes shipping, and the tax portion is shown at checkout. No fee is first revealed at the last step. A struck-through price requires a genuine higher comparison price.\n- **Reviews from customers.** Ratings shown on product cards and product pages are the average of customer reviews for that product. See the User Content and Reviews Policy (§2.1.12).\n\n## Protection when an order goes wrong\n\nThe Buyer Protection Program (§2.1.43) states when the Company refunds a buyer for an order that does not arrive, materially differs from its listing, or was charged without authorization, and how the Company recovers that amount. Account security and reports of fraud are governed by the Fraud and Abuse Policy (§2.1.34) and the Account Registration and Security Policy (§2.1.27). Disputes that cannot be resolved with support are governed by the Dispute Resolution Policy (§2.1.36).\n\nTwo facts about payment limit the risk directly: your card details are entered on Stripe's hosted page and never reach this site, and every recurring charge on the site — a subscription or a club membership — is offered as a choice with its amount and how to cancel stated before you commit.\n\nThis page summarizes the Buyer Protection Program (§2.1.43), which governs.\n\n## Copyright notices\n\nThe page at `/dmca` sets out how to submit a copyright notice and a counter-notice to the Company's designated agent. Most shoppers never need it. Legal notices of this kind go to legal@unsaidmemories.com. See the DMCA and Copyright Policy (§2.1.7).\n\n## Gift cards\n\nGift cards are bought at `/gift-cards` in a preset or custom amount and delivered by email. A gift card can be redeemed for products and for grooming services. Because delivery is by email, there is no shipping wait. See Gift Cards (§3.2.11) and the Gift Card Terms (§2.1.20), which governs.\n\n## Contacting support\n\nWhen the guides do not resolve your question:\n\n1. Check the Help Center at `/docs` and the FAQ at `/faq` first.\n2. For an order problem, have your order number ready. It is in your receipt email, and the order record is the first thing support opens.\n3. For a product problem — wrong item, damage, or a product that differs from its listing — include the product page address from your address bar, so support sees the same page you saw.\n4. For a pickup order, contacting the salon directly is fastest. The salon is at 5515 Quince Rd, Memphis, TN 38119, open Tuesday–Saturday 9am–6pm and Sunday 10am–4pm, and the team can see your order and tell you whether it is ready.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about an order, a product, or a policy: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices, including copyright notices: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "contact-us": "\n# Contact Us\n\nThis page covers every way to reach the UnSaid Memories team: phone, email, and the message form, plus where to go when your question belongs to a specific team.\n\n## Phone\n\nCall the salon at **(901) 722-1114** during business hours. Phone is the fastest route for:\n\n- Appointment changes and cancellations\n- Same-week grooming availability\n- Questions about a service before you book\n\n## Email\n\nEmail **support@unsaidmemories.com** for anything that can wait a business day. Include your order or appointment number so the team can pull up the right record on the first reply.\n\n## Send a message\n\nThe message form on this page goes straight to the salon inbox. Use it for:\n\n- General questions about services, pricing, or products\n- Feedback about a visit\n- Requests the shop team can handle without a phone call\n\n> [!NOTICE]\n> For time-sensitive requests such as a same-day appointment change, call the salon. Message-form replies arrive within one business day.\n\n## Where specific questions go\n\n| Your question | Where it goes |\n| --- | --- |\n| An order, a delivery, or a return | Customer Portal help: After You Order |\n| A grooming appointment | The appointment guides under Customer Portal help |\n| Selling on the shop | Seller Center help |\n| A policy or legal question | Legal and Policies |\n| A website accessibility issue | Website Accessibility |\n\n## Response times\n\n- **Phone:** answered during salon business hours\n- **Email and message form:** within one business day\n- **Accessibility reports:** acknowledged within two business days\n",
  "website-accessibility": "\n# Website Accessibility\n\nThis page explains how UnSaid Memories keeps the website usable for every visitor, and how to tell us when something gets in your way.\n\n## What we aim for\n\nThe shop, the booking wizard, and the help center are built to work with:\n\n- Keyboard navigation alone, including forms and menus\n- Screen readers and other assistive technology\n- Text zoom and browser zoom up to 200 percent\n- Color combinations that meet contrast guidance\n- Plain page structure with clear headings and link text\n\n## What to do if something is hard to use\n\nIf any page blocks you, tell us and we will fix it or find a working alternative:\n\n- **Email:** support@unsaidmemories.com with \"Accessibility\" in the subject line\n- **Phone:** (901) 722-1114 during salon business hours\n- **Message form:** the Contact Us page, selected \"Website question\"\n\nDescribe the page, what you were trying to do, and what stopped you. A device or browser detail helps, but none is required.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Accessibility reports are answered within two business days. If you need an immediate answer during salon hours, call (901) 722-1114 and the front desk will route you to the right person.\n\n## Booking without the website\n\nEvery online action also has a phone path. Call (901) 722-1114 to book, change, or cancel an appointment, check on an order, or buy a gift card. The salon team completes the same steps for you on the spot.\n",
  "learning-center-overview": "\n# Learning Center Overview\n\nThis page covers the Learning Center at `/learn`: the public catalog of animal-care programs, how enrollment works through the waitlist, who the Learning Center serves, and where each related topic is covered in this section.\n\n## The catalog and the classroom\n\nThe Learning Center has two parts. The catalog is the public part: fifteen programs that you can search and filter by track (veterinary, practice management, behavior and training, grooming, and animal care), by level, and by duration. Each program has a detail page with its hours, terms, module highlights, and credential. See Browsing the Course Catalog (§3.11.2) and Program Detail Pages (§3.11.3).\n\nThe classroom is where enrolled learning takes place: structured learning days led by Professor, the teaching presence in the classroom, with lessons, checks, assignments, and support from human co-hosts. See The Classroom (§3.11.6).\n\n## Enrollment through the waitlist\n\nEnrollment begins with the waitlist:\n\n1. Open a program detail page or go to `/learn/enroll`.\n2. Enter your email address in the waitlist form.\n3. Submit the form. The success message on the page is your confirmation; no confirmation email is sent.\n4. Wait to be contacted when a cohort opens. Waitlist signups are worked first when enrollment opens.\n\nSubmitting the same email address twice does not create a second signup. Your customer account will also be your learner account, so you will not need a separate sign-in for the classroom. See Enrollment and Waitlist (§3.11.4) and Learner Account and Sign-In (§3.11.14).\n\n## Who the Learning Center serves\n\n- **Prospective learners** browse the catalog, read program pages, and join the waitlist. No account is needed for any of these steps.\n- **Enrolled learners** use the classroom: learning days, checks, assignments, the notebook, and progress records.\n- **Instructors and administrators** use the instructor surfaces and the admin console covered in Instructor Tools (§3.11.12) and Admin LMS Console (§3.11.13).\n\n## Page layout\n\nPublic learning pages — the catalog, program pages, the enrollment page, and the sign-in page — use the same header and footer as the rest of the site. The classroom, instructor, and admin surfaces run full-screen without that header and footer, so the working surface is not interrupted.\n\n## What each article in this section covers\n\n- Browsing the Course Catalog (§3.11.2) — the fifteen programs, the filters, and the programs and modules views.\n- Program Detail Pages (§3.11.3) — what is on a program page and how to read it.\n- Enrollment and Waitlist (§3.11.4) — joining the waitlist, step by step.\n- Leashed Diploma Pathways (§3.11.5) — the six diploma pathways in detail.\n- The Classroom (§3.11.6) — Professor, learning days, and how a school day works.\n- Assessments and Rubric (§3.11.7) — checks, assignments, quizzes, and safety gates.\n- Progress Tracking (§3.11.8) — the learning record and the practicum log.\n- Certifications and Credentials (§3.11.9) — what you earn and what each credential requires.\n- Continuing Education (§3.11.10) — staying current after graduation.\n- Raise Hand and Human Support (§3.11.11) — getting help from a person.\n- Instructor Tools (§3.11.12) — sessions, review, and the co-host queue.\n- Admin LMS Console (§3.11.13) — the operations console and its domains.\n- Learner Account and Sign-In (§3.11.14) — one account for shopping and learning.\n- Learning Center Roadmap (§3.11.15) — what is available and the sequence ahead.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about enrollment or the waitlist: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "continuing-education": "\n# Continuing Education\n\nThis page covers continuing education for academy graduates: why the curriculum requires a continuing-education plan at graduation, the five subjects a serious plan should cover, and how to build and keep your own plan.\n\n## Why a plan is required\n\nTechniques change, research advances, equipment evolves, and regulations shift. A credential records what you demonstrated when you graduated; it does not keep you current by itself.\n\nThe clearest statement is in the Professional Dog Trainer pathway: professional dog training requires lifelong learning — new methods, new research, new equipment — and your plan must show how you will stay current. The TRN-BIZ capstone requires a continuing-education plan for the next two years as a deliverable. It is not a suggestion: the panel reviews it, and you do not graduate as a trainer without one. See Leashed Diploma Pathways (§3.11.5).\n\nThe same expectation runs through the other pathways in different forms:\n\n- **IPDG** graduates work in a craft where breed standards, tools, and techniques evolve, and the business subjects in the pathway treat ongoing learning as an operating cost of the business.\n- **PPC** graduates treat staying current as a management duty: the owner who stops learning is outcompeted, and the 3-year projection in the PPC-BIZ capstone must account for it.\n- **The shared business subjects** — Life Skills & Health, Personal Readiness, Business Operations, Marketing, Technology, Finance, and Legal — are the subjects most likely to need refreshing as laws and platforms change.\n\n## The five subjects a plan should cover\n\n1. **Craft advancement** — new techniques, breed-standard updates, and equipment.\n2. **Regulatory currency** — the Legal subject: licensing changes, animal-welfare regulations, and business compliance. This is the subject where falling behind carries legal consequences.\n3. **Business operations** — pricing, marketing channels, and financial management.\n4. **Safety recertification** — CPR and first aid expire. Handling competencies covered by a safety gate should be re-verified periodically, not assumed to be permanent. See Assessments and Rubric (§3.11.7).\n5. **Teaching currency** — for graduates who go on to mentor or instruct, the methods for teaching the craft also change.\n\n## Building your plan\n\n1. List the five subjects above and note, for each one, what changed in your field in the last year.\n2. For each subject, name one concrete activity for the next twelve months: a course, a certification renewal, a conference, supervised practice, or a documented review of new regulations.\n3. Put dates on the activities, including expiry dates for CPR and first aid.\n4. Record what you complete, with dates and providers, alongside your practicum log and credential. See Progress Tracking (§3.11.8).\n5. Review the plan once a year and replace completed activities with new ones.\n\n> [!NOTICE]\n> An older credential carries more weight with an employer when it is accompanied by a current continuing-education record. Keep your record with your credential, and be ready to show both. If an employer asks what you have done to stay current and there is no record, the credential alone has to carry the answer.\n\n## Keeping safety current\n\nSafety-gate competencies are verified rigorously before live-animal work begins. That verification has no value as a lifetime guarantee: handling skills and first-aid certification decay if they are not practiced and renewed. Treat the expiry dates in your plan as hard dates, and do not take on live-animal work that depends on a competency you have allowed to lapse until it has been re-verified.\n",
  "raise-hand-and-human-support": "\n# Raise Hand and Human Support\n\nThis page covers how to get help from a person in the classroom: how to raise your hand, what the co-host receives, what happens while you wait and during the handoff, how a request is resolved, and how the meeting room works.\n\n## When to ask Professor and when to raise your hand\n\n- **Ask Professor** when you are stuck on the material — you need a different explanation, a smaller step, or a reframe. That is what Professor is for, and the Reframe control exists so that a teaching problem does not need a human escalation. See The Classroom (§3.11.6).\n- **Raise your hand** when you need something Professor cannot resolve: a technical problem with the room, a question about your program or schedule, a disagreement about an assessment, a personal situation affecting your learning, or anything that needs a person with authority.\n\n## Raising your hand\n\n1. Select the raise-hand button in the attention area of the classroom. It is always visible.\n2. The room sends your request with your context attached: your current course, your active work item, your attempts on it, and your conversation with Professor.\n3. Look for the confirmation: \"Hand raised. Your course, work, attempts and support request are in the co-host queue.\"\n\nThe context matters. The person who picks up your request does not start cold; they can see what you were working on, what you tried, and where it went wrong.\n\n## While you wait and during the handoff\n\nRaising your hand moves your learning day into the handoff state:\n\n- The lesson view shows the handoff screen: \"You don't have to start over.\" Your current work, attempts, and questions are saved in the co-host queue.\n- Professor steps back. New instruction questions are paused, with the message \"Resume with Professor after the handoff before asking for instruction,\" so you are not taught by two instructors at once.\n- The room is plain about the queue: until a person joins, the notice reads \"No human has joined yet.\" Help is not presented as instant when it is not.\n\n## How a request is resolved\n\n1. A co-host reviews your request and its context, then checks in. Your day moves into the handoff state with the co-host present.\n2. You and the co-host work through the problem, in the room or in a meeting (see below).\n3. The co-host records a resolution describing the support provided.\n4. The request is marked resolved with a time stamp, and your day returns to awaiting your next attempt, with Professor available again.\n\nThe day-event record keeps the whole sequence: hand raised, co-host joined, resolution, and return to Professor. See Progress Tracking (§3.11.8). Instructors also read raised hands in aggregate: one raised hand is a learner who needs help; several raised hands on the same check are a signal about the lesson.\n\n## The meeting room\n\nThe Meeting tool is where human support can move to a call. The classroom does not provide the call itself:\n\n1. Paste an `https://` meeting link of your own — for example, a Google Meet link — into the Meeting tool.\n2. The room saves the link in your browser and offers \"Open connected meeting.\"\n3. Open the link when your session is scheduled.\n\n> [!NOTICE]\n> No call is created by connecting a link, and the link is saved in your browser only. The camera preview in the room is labeled \"LOCAL PREVIEW · NOT TRANSMITTED\" — the room does not broadcast your camera. Where no meeting provider is connected, the meeting view says \"No provider connected\" instead of presenting a button that does nothing.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> If you need a person and the classroom queue is not the right route — for example, for an account, enrollment, or payment question — contact support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "instructor-tools": "\n# Instructor Tools\n\nThis page covers the instructor surfaces in the Learning Center: the live sessions board, the review surface for learner attempts, the co-host queue for raised hands, and the course builder. It is written for instructors and co-hosts.\n\n## Live sessions\n\nThe sessions surface at `/learn/instructor/sessions` is your live board. Each active or recent learning session appears as a card showing the course title, when the day was opened, and the day's current state — teaching, on break, closed, or handed off. When no session is active, the board states \"No active sessions.\"\n\nUse the board to answer three questions at a glance: who is learning now, which course each learner is in, and who may need attention because a day has stalled or been handed off.\n\n## Reviewing attempts\n\nThe review surface at `/learn/instructor/review` is where you review learner attempts: the learner's responses, the result, the misconception recorded where the reasoning went wrong, and Professor's feedback, with the work item and the companion material behind it.\n\nReview in this order:\n\n1. Read the learner's response in the learner's own words before you read the result.\n2. Read the recorded misconception and Professor's feedback. Note whether the feedback addressed the misconception the learner actually made.\n3. Look across learners. If several learners reach the same misconception on the same check, record it as a lesson problem and raise it through the escalation path below, rather than treating each case as an individual failure.\n4. Where a learner's record raises a concern about readiness for live-animal work, hold the relevant safety gate until you have reviewed the work yourself.\n\nSafety-gate sign-offs belong to instructors. A safety gate — for example, IPDG-103 before live-animal work, or PDT-201 in the trainer pathway — is signed off only when you are satisfied the learner is ready. The full gate list is in Assessments and Rubric (§3.11.7). Never sign off a gate on the basis of completion alone.\n\n## The co-host queue\n\nThe co-host review panel, opened with the Co-host button in the classroom, holds the intervention queue: every open raised hand, with the learner's course, the reason, the active work item, and the full attempts and context for that request.\n\nWork a request in this order:\n\n1. **Review.** Read the reason, the item, the attempts, and the conversation with Professor before you join. The context is attached so you do not walk in without it.\n2. **Check in.** Joining moves the learner's day into the handoff state and steps Professor back, so the learner is not taught by two instructors at once.\n3. **Support.** Work through the problem with the learner, in the room or in a meeting the learner connects. See Raise Hand and Human Support (§3.11.11).\n4. **Resolve.** Record a resolution that describes the support you provided. Resolving closes the request with a time stamp and returns the learner's day to awaiting the next attempt.\n\n> [!NOTICE]\n> The co-host panel identifies the records it shows. Always confirm whether you are looking at records for enrolled learners before you act on what you see, and do not treat demonstration records as learner records.\n\n## The course builder\n\nCourses in the classroom are companions generated from curriculum sources, not pages written one by one. The course builder takes the generation inputs — the area of study and its source material — together with the companion content, and saves the course so it appears in learners' course lists. The companion reader shows the source and review position for the content, so a learner or instructor can see where the material came from and whether it has been reviewed.\n\nBefore a generated course is used with learners:\n\n1. Read the companion content in full.\n2. Check worked examples, checks, and practice items against the source curriculum.\n3. Confirm the safety-related content matches the pathway's safety gates.\n4. Record the review in the course's source and review information.\n\n## Escalation\n\nEscalate to the admin team through the Admin LMS Console (§3.11.13) route when a problem is outside instructor authority: a disputed assessment that you cannot resolve, a pattern of misconceptions that points to a defect in a lesson, a learner record that appears incorrect, or any safety concern involving live-animal work.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Instructor questions about sessions, review, or the co-host queue: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "admin-lms-console": "\n# Admin LMS Console\n\nThis page covers the admin LMS console at `/admin/lms-dashboard`: its layout, its thirteen domains and what each one manages, the standalone pages that present the same surfaces, and how an administrator works a surface that returns no records.\n\n## Layout\n\nThe console presents thirteen domains in a left navigation, grouped by category, with the active tab's surface in the panel beside it. The console has 38 tabs in total. Each tab is a working surface that reads learner, course, and program records through the admin routes for the learning system.\n\nThe same surfaces are also presented as thirteen standalone pages under `/admin/lms-*` — for AI instruction, AI teaching, assessment, the platform bridge, communication, compliance, curriculum, the dashboard, enrollment, media, progress, skills, and support — so an administrator can send an instructor a direct address for a single surface, such as the grading queue, without sending the whole console.\n\n## The thirteen domains\n\n**Academy Overview.** The dashboard: enrollments, activity, and the key measures for the academy at a glance.\n\n**Curriculum.** Pathways, Courses, Lessons & Content Blocks, and Publishing & Versions. This is the content domain: define pathways, build courses from content blocks, and version and publish curriculum, so a curriculum change moves through preparation, review, and publication instead of being edited directly in a content file.\n\n**Media & Delivery.** Media Library, Delivery Modes, and Live & Community: the assets the academy uses and how programs are delivered, including the Hybrid Salon Lab format described in Program Detail Pages (§3.11.3).\n\n**Enrollment.** Cohorts, Enrollments, Pacing Schedules, and Learner Records. This domain turns the waitlist in Enrollment and Waitlist (§3.11.4) into classes: a cohort carries a start date, a seat count, and assigned instructors; enrollments place learners in cohorts; pacing schedules set the expected rate of progress; learner records hold the result.\n\n**AI & Progress.** AI Teaching Sessions, the AI Consent Gate, the Human Escalation Queue, AI Assessment & Remediation, Learner Progress, and the Clock-Hour Ledger, which is marked for audit. Two surfaces in this domain deserve particular care:\n\n- The **AI Consent Gate** records that a learner has consented expressly to instruction by Professor. Consent is a recorded step, not a line in general terms. See Learner Account and Sign-In (§3.11.14).\n- The **Clock-Hour Ledger** is the compliance-grade record of instructional hours — the record an accreditor asks for.\n\n**Assessment & Credentials.** Grading Queue, Quiz Bank, Quiz Reporting, Rubrics, Artifact Submissions, Skills Registry, Credentials & Diplomas, and Badges: the full path from assessment to credential, including the artifact submissions that carry capstone portfolios and the credential issuance described in Certifications and Credentials (§3.11.9).\n\n**Support & Comms.** Support Referrals, Safety Incidents, Workforce Outcomes, Learner Feedback, Announcements, and the Notification Log. Safety incidents have their own tab, consistent with the safety-gate approach that runs through the curriculum; workforce outcomes record where graduates go.\n\n**Compliance & Bridge.** The Audit Ledger, which is marked as a permanent record, Funder Reports, and the Platform Bridge, which connects learning records with the customer, commerce, and other portal records.\n\n**AI Instructor.** AI Instructor Config, AI Content Studio, the retrieval corpus Professor teaches from, and the Roleplay Studio: the configuration surfaces for Professor itself.\n\n## Working a surface\n\n1. Open the console and select the domain, then the tab for the record you need.\n2. If the surface returns no records, read the empty message on the surface before you conclude that a record is missing. The compliance surface, for example, states plainly when a module has no data to show.\n3. Record changes through the surface itself, so the change carries the publisher, the time, and any change note with it. Do not work around a surface by editing records elsewhere.\n4. For a learner-facing problem — access, a disputed result, a record that appears wrong — work from the learner record outward: enrollment, progress, attempts, then the support record.\n\n## Access and escalation\n\nConsole access is admin access; apply the sign-in and permission care described for admin surfaces generally, and never work under another administrator's sign-in, because actions in the console are attributed to the signed-in identity. Escalate a learner-data concern, a consent record that appears incorrect, or a clock-hour record that does not reconcile, to the Legal function at the address below; those records carry obligations beyond the console.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about the admin LMS console: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices and learner-data concerns: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "learner-account-and-sign-in": "\n# Learner Account and Sign-In\n\nThis page covers your learner account: why there is no separate learner sign-in, what one account across shopping and learning means in practice, what learning information your account carries, who can see it, and how to get help with access.\n\n## No separate learner sign-in\n\nYour customer account is your learner account. There is no second learner credential, no student number to memorize, and no separate password. The sign-in page at `/learn/sign-in` presents the same enrollment information as `/learn/enroll`, because browsing the catalog, reading program pages, and joining the waitlist need no account at all.\n\n1. Browse the catalog and read program pages without signing in.\n2. Join the waitlist with an email address; see Enrollment and Waitlist (§3.11.4).\n3. When you enroll, sign in with the customer account you already have. Your learner record is attached to that account.\n\n## What one account means\n\n- **One credential.** The sign-in you use for the shop is the sign-in for the classroom.\n- **One profile.** Your contact information lives in one place. Update it once and it is current everywhere.\n- **One history.** Your learning record attaches to the same identity as your orders, which is what makes receipts for tuition, credential verification, and support work from one record. \"I cannot reach my classroom\" and \"Where is my order\" go to the same support team.\n- **One classroom header.** In the classroom, the header shows your identity and your program standing, and sign-out ends your session and returns you to `/learn/sign-in`.\n\n## What your learning account carries\n\nYour learner record holds more than your shop record:\n\n- Your courses and your place in each one — current lesson, active work, and the state of your learning day.\n- Your attempts and evidence — the full assessment record described in Progress Tracking (§3.11.8).\n- Your notes and work in progress — saved for each course. Work saved in your browser is tied to that browser; clearing your browser data removes unsaved local work, so finish and submit work you cannot afford to lose.\n- Your schedule — school-day blocks and what is next.\n- Your support history — hands raised, resolutions, and messages.\n\nYour classroom search, inbox, and notebook are scoped to your identity: you see your courses, your notes, and your messages. Learners do not see one another's work, attempts, or records.\n\n## Your learning information and who sees it\n\nLearning information is more sensitive than shopping information, and it is handled on that basis:\n\n- **Assessment records** — your attempts, misconceptions, and results — are educational records. They are visible to you and to the instructors and administrators who teach and run your program, and to no other learner.\n- **Consent to instruction by Professor.** Learning with Professor means your questions, reasoning, and writing are part of the teaching exchange and part of your record. The admin console carries a dedicated consent surface for this; see Admin LMS Console (§3.11.13). Consent is asked for expressly, not buried in general terms.\n- **Meetings.** The meeting room is bring-your-own-link precisely so the Platform does not sit between you and your call, and your camera preview stays in your browser. See Raise Hand and Human Support (§3.11.11).\n- **Your waitlist email.** The address you give today is recorded for admissions follow-up. It is contact information you supplied for that purpose, and it is the address admissions will use when a cohort opens.\n\nHow the Platform collects, uses, and keeps personal information generally is governed by the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2), which controls over this summary.\n\n## Help with access\n\nThere is no separate learner account to be locked out of. If the waitlist form is not working, retry it, then contact support. When you are enrolled, password resets, access problems, and merging duplicate customer accounts are handled through the same support route as every other account question — one identity means one support queue.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about your learner account or sign-in: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "learning-center-roadmap": "\n# Learning Center Roadmap\n\nThis page covers the Learning Center as a whole for the people who operate it: what a learner can do today, the sequence in which the remaining parts are to be put into service, the questions that must be settled before enrollment opens, and the test for calling the Learning Center complete.\n\n## What a learner can do today\n\n- **Browse the catalog** at `/learn` — fifteen programs, with filters, search, and programs and modules views. See Browsing the Course Catalog (§3.11.2).\n- **Read a program detail page** for each of the fifteen programs, with terms, the weekly schedule, requirements, and outcomes. See Program Detail Pages (§3.11.3).\n- **Join the waitlist** at `/learn/enroll`. Signups are recorded for admissions follow-up, duplicate signups are removed, and the page's success message is the confirmation; no confirmation email is sent. See Enrollment and Waitlist (§3.11.4).\n- **Read the published curriculum** — the fifteen catalog programs and the six Leashed pathways, with their terms, safety gates, and capstones. See Leashed Diploma Pathways (§3.11.5).\n\nPublic pages carry the site header and footer; the classroom, instructor, and admin surfaces run full-screen.\n\n## The sequence ahead\n\nPut the remaining parts into service in this order, because each step depends on the one before it:\n\n1. **Classroom records first.** The records the classroom teaches from and writes to — courses, learning days, attempts, Professor messages, and the support queue. With these in place, the classroom described in The Classroom (§3.11.6) can open: Professor teaches, checks are recorded, and days are tracked.\n2. **The instructor view of live sessions.** The sessions surface in Instructor Tools (§3.11.12) needs a working route for its data. This is small work that gives instructors sight of who is learning and where each day stands.\n3. **Enrollment records.** Cohorts, enrollments, pacing schedules, and learner records. Cohorts are the step between a waitlist and a school: without a cohort there is nothing to enroll into.\n4. **Assessment and credential records.** Rubrics, submissions, the skills registry, and credentials — the issuance machinery behind Certifications and Credentials (§3.11.9).\n5. **Compliance and audit records.** The permanent audit record and funder reporting that accreditors ask for; see Admin LMS Console (§3.11.13).\n6. **Continuing education.** Courses, tracking, and renewal cycles for graduates; see Continuing Education (§3.11.10). Build this before the first credentials age, not after.\n\n## Questions to settle before enrollment opens\n\n- **The two Animal Care Assistant programs.** The catalog program (170 hours, 9 weeks) and the pathway program (988 hours, 35 weeks) share the code ACA. Settle on one definition, or rename one, before any learner enrolls in either.\n- **Curriculum review.** Companion content carries a notice that generated companion content needs curriculum review before classroom use. That review must be completed by a person before learners are taught from the material, and the review must be recorded on the course.\n- **The safety-gate sign-off.** The curriculum requires instructor sign-off before live-animal work; the precise act by which an instructor records that sign-off must be settled before live-animal blocks are scheduled. See Assessments and Rubric (§3.11.7).\n- **Learning information.** A written statement of what learning data is collected, who sees it, how long it is kept, and what happens to it when a learner leaves must be in place before the classroom opens. See Learner Account and Sign-In (§3.11.14).\n- **Tuition and payment.** No program price and no tuition payment step is published. The commercial terms for the academy — tuition, payment plans, and refunds — are business decisions that enrollment, the learner record, and accounting all depend on, and they must be made before cohorts are offered.\n- **One identity, in practice.** Classroom identity is keyed to a session record; the handoff between a customer account and a learner record must be built and tested, not assumed.\n\n## The test for complete\n\nThe Learning Center is complete when a learner can do all of the following in order: browse the catalog; enroll in a cohort rather than a waitlist; sign in with a customer account; open the classroom; learn with Professor through learning days; submit work; raise a hand and reach a person; follow a learning record; complete a practicum; defend a capstone; and receive a credential that an employer can verify — with each step recorded. Today the first step and the waitlist are in place. The sequence above is the distance between that position and complete.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about the Learning Center sequence or a pre-enrollment decision: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "browsing-the-course-catalog": "\n# Browsing the Course Catalog\n\nThis page covers the course catalog at `/learn`: the fifteen programs it lists, the filters and search you can use to narrow the list, the difference between the programs view and the modules view, and how to read a program card.\n\n## The fifteen programs\n\n| Code | Program | Hours | Length | Credential |\n|------|---------|-------|--------|------------|\n| VET | Veterinary Assistant | 395 | 26 wk | Veterinary Assistant |\n| VTE | Veterinary Technician | 1,010 | 104 wk | Veterinary Technician |\n| VST | Veterinary Surgical Technician | 445 | 52 wk | Veterinary Surgical Technician |\n| VTN | Veterinary Technology | 525 | 104 wk | Veterinary Technologist |\n| PVM | Pre-Veterinary Medicine | 770 | 104 wk | Pre-Veterinary Medicine Certificate |\n| VPT | Veterinary Pathology Technician | 510 | 104 wk | Veterinary Pathology Technician |\n| VPM | Veterinary Practice Management | 330 | 52 wk | Veterinary Practice Manager |\n| GRO | Pet Grooming | 345 | 26 wk | Professional Pet Groomer |\n| FEL | Felines & Health | 170 | 13 wk | Feline Care Specialist |\n| PRT | Professional Trainer | 345 | 26 wk | Professional Dog Trainer |\n| ABT | Animal Behavior Technician | 485 | 52 wk | Animal Behavior Technician |\n| ACA | Animal Care Assistant | 170 | 9 wk | Animal Care Assistant |\n| GSP | Grooming Salon Practice Management | 655 | 52 wk | Grooming Salon Practice Manager |\n| EQN | Equine Nursing Technicians | 440 | 52 wk | Equine Nursing Technician |\n| ZKA | Zookeeper Assistant | 440 | 52 wk | Zookeeper Assistant |\n\n> [!NOTICE]\n> The catalog's Animal Care Assistant program (ACA, 170 hours, 9 weeks) is different from the Animal Care Assistant pathway described in Leashed Diploma Pathways (§3.11.5), which is 988 hours over 35 weeks. The same code is used for both. Check which program you are looking at before you compare hours or plan a schedule.\n\n## Filtering and searching the catalog\n\nFive controls sit above the catalog, and they combine:\n\n1. **Pathway** narrows the catalog to one track: veterinary (VET, VTE, VST, VTN, PVM, VPT), practice management (VPM, GSP), behavior and training (PRT, ABT), grooming (GRO, FEL), or animal care (ACA, ZKA, EQN).\n2. **Level** filters modules by course level when you are in the modules view.\n3. **Duration** separates short programs (16 weeks or under) from longer programs.\n4. **Search** looks across program codes, titles, descriptions, track names, and module content. Searching \"safety\" or \"gate\" also surfaces modules marked as safety gates; searching \"practicum\" surfaces practicum modules.\n5. **Sort** changes the order in which programs are presented, beginning with featured programs.\n\n## Programs view and modules view\n\nUse the toggle above the catalog to switch views:\n\n- **Programs view** shows the fifteen programs as cards.\n- **Modules view** shows individual modules — the course units, with codes, titles, hours, and descriptions. When you search in modules view, matching modules are grouped under their parent program so you can see why a program matched.\n\n## Reading a program card\n\nEach card shows the program title, the credential, the number of weeks, the module count, and the total hours. Select a card to open the program detail page; see Program Detail Pages (§3.11.3). Tuition does not appear on the cards or in the catalog. For cost questions, join the waitlist as described in Enrollment and Waitlist (§3.11.4) and ask when you are contacted.\n\n## What the catalog does not do\n\nThe catalog is for browsing and reading. You cannot enroll from a card, save programs, compare programs side by side, or see cohort start dates in the catalog. Every enrollment path leads to the waitlist.\n\n## If program information looks out of date\n\nCatalog pages are generated from the published program content. If a program's hours or terms look wrong, or if they differ from what admissions told you, follow the information admissions gave you and report the difference so the page can be corrected.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about a program or its hours: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "program-detail-pages": "\n# Program Detail Pages\n\nThis page covers the program detail page at `/learn/courses/[slug]` — for example, `/learn/courses/veterinary-technician` — and explains the header, the five tabs, the sidebar, and what the delivery format means, so you can judge whether a program fits your schedule and goals before you join the waitlist.\n\n## The header\n\nThe top of the page gives you the essentials at a glance: the program title, a short description, the credential, the total weeks, the module count, and the clock hours. Both enrollment buttons on the page — one in the header and one in the sidebar — go to `/learn/enroll`, the waitlist page described in Enrollment and Waitlist (§3.11.4). Tuition does not appear anywhere on the page.\n\n## The five tabs\n\n### Program\n\nThe overview tab gives the program objective, the core competencies you will build, and the hour breakdown — how many hours are technical, how many are business and personal, and how many are applied. Read the breakdown before you decide: a program that is entirely technical hours is a different commitment from one that mixes in business modules.\n\n### Curriculum\n\nThe curriculum tab shows the term-by-term structure in three views:\n\n- **Terms** — each term's modules, with codes, titles, hours, and descriptions.\n- **Weekly** — the weekly schedule: Monday to Friday, 08:00–15:30, 30 hours a week, with technical lab, business module, quick check, practicum, and capstone or rubric review blocks.\n- **Catalog** — the module-level view.\n\nThe weekly view is the one to use when you are deciding whether a program fits your life. Thirty hours a week, 8:00 to 3:30, Monday through Friday, is the full-time expectation. The sidebar also lists a part-time duration; check both before you commit.\n\n### Outcomes\n\nThe outcomes tab states the completion requirements: the certificates earned along the way and what the final credential requires. Read this tab as the list of gates between you and the credential; see also Certifications and Credentials (§3.11.9).\n\n### Requirements\n\nTwo panels sit side by side:\n\n- **What You Need to Enroll** — the admission requirements. The standard requirement is age 18 or older; individual programs may add more.\n- **Attendance Policy** — including the Friday 08:00–12:00 open laboratory makeup block. If you miss time, Friday morning is when you make it up.\n\n### FAQ\n\nThe FAQ tab answers compliance and operations questions: attendance, safety, and credential-building. Read it before you contact admissions; most common questions are answered there.\n\n## The sidebar\n\nThe sidebar stays visible as you move through the tabs. It shows the official credential name, total hours, the full-time and part-time durations, the delivery format, the enrollment button, and a \"Build on Your Credentials\" card describing how the program builds into further credentials; see the stacking rules in Certifications and Credentials (§3.11.9).\n\n## What \"Hybrid Salon Lab\" means\n\nPrograms are not purely online. \"Hybrid Salon Lab\" means structured learning combined with hands-on lab work — practicum hours, live-animal work behind safety gates, and capstone panels. The Friday practicum block and the open-lab makeup window exist because the hands-on part is required, not optional. If you are considering a program from outside the area, ask admissions where the lab work takes place before you join the waitlist.\n\n## Points to check before you decide\n\n- **The Animal Care Assistant programs differ.** The detail page shows the catalog version of Animal Care Assistant (170 hours, 9 weeks). The pathway version described in Leashed Diploma Pathways (§3.11.5) is 988 hours over 35 weeks. Do not confuse the two when planning.\n- **No cohort dates appear on the page.** Detail pages describe the program, not the next start date. Start dates and seat availability are handled through the waitlist and admissions.\n- **If a page and admissions disagree,** follow what admissions told you and report the difference so the page can be corrected.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about a program's requirements or schedule: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "enrollment-and-waitlist": "\n# Enrollment and Waitlist\n\nThis page covers enrollment through the waitlist at `/learn/enroll`: what you see on the page, what happens when you submit the form, why enrollment starts with a waitlist, and what to do if the form fails or you hear nothing back.\n\n## What you see\n\nEvery enrollment button in the Learning Center leads to `/learn/enroll`. The page states that enrollment is opening soon, explains that your customer account will also be your learner account, and presents the waitlist form: one email field and one submit button. Links below the form return you to the course catalog at `/learn` or to the shop.\n\n## Joining the waitlist\n\n1. Go to `/learn/enroll`, or select an enrollment button on any program detail page.\n2. Enter an email address you read regularly. This address is how admissions will reach you.\n3. Submit the form.\n4. Look for the success message on the page: \"You're on the waitlist — we'll let you know when enrollment opens.\"\n\n> [!NOTICE]\n> No confirmation email is sent. The success message on the page is the confirmation. If you want a record, save a screenshot of the success message.\n\n## What happens when you submit\n\n1. **Your email address is checked.** An address in an invalid format is rejected, and the form shows an error so you can correct it and try again.\n2. **Duplicates are removed.** If the same email address is already on the waitlist, no second record is created, and you see the same success message.\n3. **Your signup is recorded for admissions follow-up.** A new signup is recorded as a waitlist lead, so follow-up runs through the same process admissions uses for other inquiries.\n4. **Admissions works the list when a cohort opens.** The waitlist is a notification list, not a numbered queue. There is no position number to check.\n\n## Why enrollment starts with a waitlist\n\nEnrollment into a cohort requires a start date, a seat count, assigned instructors, tuition handling, and classroom access. The waitlist collects the one thing needed first — the names of people who want to enroll — so admissions can open cohorts to those people first. When enrollment opens, your learner record, cohort assignment, and classroom access are created from your existing customer account; see Learner Account and Sign-In (§3.11.14).\n\n## If something goes wrong\n\n- **The form shows \"Something went wrong — please try again.\"** A network or server problem prevented the signup from being recorded. Your email address was not saved; submit the form again.\n- **You signed up twice.** No action is needed. Duplicate signups are removed automatically and do not affect your place.\n- **You signed up and have heard nothing.** The waitlist is worked when a cohort opens, not on a fixed schedule. If a long time has passed, contact admissions through the address below rather than signing up again.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about the waitlist or enrollment: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "leashed-diploma-pathways": "\n# Leashed Diploma Pathways\n\nThis page covers the six Leashed diploma pathways: the business and personal subjects every pathway shares, each pathway's terms, hours, safety gates, and capstone, and how the pathways build on one another.\n\n## The shared subjects\n\nEvery pathway runs the same Business & Personal Mastery subjects alongside its technical blocks: Life Skills & Health, Personal Readiness, Business Operations, Marketing, Technology, Finance, and Legal. The purpose is that you graduate with the foundation to run a compliant, profitable practice, not only the craft skill. The weekly schedule is Monday to Friday, 08:00–15:30: technical lab in the morning, business and personal modules at midday, review and practicum in the afternoon, with checkpoints on Wednesday and capstone and rubric work on Friday.\n\n## Professional Dog Groomer — IPDG (1,248 hours, 44 weeks)\n\nFour terms: Foundation — dog life stages, breeds, coat types, salon safety (311 hours); Core Skill — bathing, drying, and preparation technique (311 hours); Advanced Skill — breed-standard cuts and scissoring (346 hours); and Capstone — live salon floor and business launch (280 hours).\n\nTwo safety gates apply: IPDG-103 must be signed off before any live-animal work in Term 1, and IPDG-302 before advanced live-animal work in Term 3. Every live-client dog is assessed against the Competency Rubric, and safety-critical rows are pass or fail. See Assessments and Rubric (§3.11.7).\n\nThe capstone, GRM-BIZ, requires:\n\n1. A complete grooming business plan presented to an instructor panel — service menu, pricing, marketing, and a 12-month financial projection.\n2. A signed practicum log showing live-client hours and breed variety.\n3. A demonstration groom.\n\nThe curriculum's worked example uses 8 dogs a week at a $65 average ticket and about $650 a week in costs, and the panel tests whether your figures hold up.\n\n## Professional Dog Trainer — PDT (1,112 hours, 39 weeks)\n\nTerm 1 covers foundations: dog history, the ten key AKC breeds, equipment, vaccination protocols, and ADA service-animal guidelines (256 hours). Term 2 covers core craft: marker training, shaping, capturing, and reading body language (266 hours). Term 3 covers the distinction between training problems, solved with repetition, and behavior problems, solved with behavior-modification plans — including leash reactivity, resource guarding, and separation anxiety (336 hours). Term 4 is the TRN-BIZ capstone (254 hours): your training business plan, class structure, pricing, insurance and liability waivers, and a continuing-education plan for the next two years, which is a required deliverable. See Continuing Education (§3.11.10).\n\nThe safety gate is PDT-201, required before live-animal work. Corrections in this pathway are minimal and fair: a withheld reward or a reset, never fear or pain.\n\n## Animal Care Assistant — ACA (988 hours, 35 weeks)\n\nThis pathway covers puppies and developmental stages, breeds, workplace safety, handling, bathing, nail trims, ear cleaning, kennel operations, intake and checkout, vaccination record-keeping, medication administration, and customer service. Term 4 has no technical module; it is business readiness and the ACA-BIZ capstone: a workforce-readiness portfolio — resume, signed practicum log, customer-service philosophy, and career plan — presented to a panel.\n\nACA technical modules credit toward IPDG-101–204, and all business modules transfer, so this pathway is the first step on the bather-to-owner path: bather, then IPDG Term 3, then lead groomer, then your own salon.\n\n> [!NOTICE]\n> This 988-hour pathway is different from the catalog's Animal Care Assistant program (170 hours, 9 weeks) described in Browsing the Course Catalog (§3.11.2). The same code, ACA, is used for both.\n\n## Professional Pet Sitter — PPS (56 hours, 2 weeks)\n\nThis pathway covers client selection, multi-species care (dogs, cats, small mammals, birds, and reptiles), safe handling, sanitation, and CPR and first aid, plus scheduling, invoicing, insurance, bonding, and pricing models. A central lesson is the intake interview: the professional choice is sometimes to decline a job, or to offer a management plan or a referral instead — for example, for a dog whose separation anxiety has destroyed a door frame.\n\nSafety gates PPS-104 and PPS-105 are required before live-animal work. The capstone, PPS-BIZ, is a complete sitting-business launch in miniature: service menu, pricing, intake and contract templates, insurance plan, and marketing strategy. PPS builds into ACA, IPDG, PDT, or PPC.\n\n## Professional Cat Groomer — CAT (58 hours, 2 weeks)\n\nCats are not small dogs, and this pathway is built on that fact. It covers breed and coat-type recognition, temperament reading (relaxed, tense, overstimulated, fearful), and a \"less is more\" approach: a cat groom may be shorter than a dog groom, and pushing past a cat's tolerance is dangerous and unprofessional. Technique covers cat-safe bathing — warm water, a calm firm hold, no high-velocity dryer — short-coat and long-coat work, and the lion cut for matted longhairs. The curriculum prices cat grooming above dog grooming in its example ($85 against $65), because few groomers offer it, and teaches when to refer a cat to a veterinarian for sedation grooming instead.\n\nThe safety gate is CAT-102. The capstone, CAT-BIZ, requires a pricing model, a client-screening protocol, safety procedures, veterinarian-referral criteria, and a marketing plan. CAT builds into IPDG or PPC.\n\n## Professional Pet Care & Business Ownership — PPC (1,500 hours, 52 weeks)\n\nPPC is the final credential in this set. It covers all IPDG, PDT, PPS, and CAT technical objectives, a multi-service practicum across the salon floor, the training floor, and sitting visits, and the business subjects at the deepest level: funding (loans, investors, bootstrapping), staffing (hiring, compensation, retention), legal protection (LLC structure, employment law), and scaling (a second location, franchising). Its central lesson is that a multi-service owner cannot be the best groomer, trainer, and sitter at once; the owner builds systems, delegates, and holds quality standards.\n\nSafety gates apply at every level: PPC-105, 201, 306, 403, 405, and 410. The capstone, PPC-BIZ, requires a complete enterprise plan, a 3-year financial projection, a funding strategy, a staffing plan, standard operating procedures, a legal and insurance framework, and a multi-service practicum log, defended before a panel.\n\n## How the pathways build on one another\n\n- ACA builds into IPDG.\n- PPS builds into ACA, IPDG, PDT, or PPC.\n- CAT builds into IPDG or PPC.\n- PPC is the final credential and does not build into another pathway.\n\nYou may start with a two-week certificate and build toward the 52-week diploma, with credit transferring along the way. Each credential is complete on its own; see Certifications and Credentials (§3.11.9).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about a pathway, a safety gate, or a capstone: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "the-classroom": "\n# The Classroom\n\nThis page covers the classroom at `/learn/classroom`: the layout of the room, how to learn with Professor, the states a learning day moves through, the order of a school day, and the messages you may see when the room cannot proceed.\n\n## The room\n\nThe classroom has three columns and a tool rail:\n\n- **Left:** Professor, with a caption of what Professor is doing, Teach, Reframe, and transcript controls, and a box for your questions. Below that is your school-day agenda, with today's blocks, the current block marked, and a break button.\n- **Center:** the work surface — lesson tabs (Lesson, Quick check, Lesson overview), the check surface where you submit your reasoning, and a footer showing whether your work is saved.\n- **Right:** attention and inbox, recent submissions, quick notes, and what is next.\n- **Bottom rail:** Classroom, My courses, Companion, Assignments, Quizzes, Grades, Notebook, Resources, Calendar, Inbox, Files, and Meeting, plus a Co-host button for human support.\n\nThe header shows your identity, a course switcher, classroom search, a Professor voice toggle for read-aloud, and sign-out. Sign-out ends your session and returns you to `/learn/sign-in`.\n\n## Learning with Professor\n\nProfessor is the instructor in the room. You interact in four ways:\n\n- **Teach** — ask Professor to teach one small next step from the current companion material. Professor keeps the step short and does not answer the active check for you.\n- **Reframe** — ask for the same idea through a different example, when the first explanation did not land.\n- **Ask** — send a free-form question.\n- **Read aloud** — hear lessons and Professor's responses read aloud.\n\nYou can open the transcript at any time to review the full conversation. When you submit an attempt, Professor reviews your reasoning against the companion material, which is the source Professor teaches from. See Assessments and Rubric (§3.11.7).\n\n## Learning days and their states\n\nEach school day moves through these states:\n\n- **Checked in** — the day is open; you check in and begin.\n- **Teaching** — active instruction; you are working through lessons and checks.\n- **Awaiting attempt** — Professor is waiting for your response to a check or assignment.\n- **On break** — your place is saved and the room waits. A break applies across the whole school day; you cannot switch courses during a break.\n- **Human handoff** — you have raised your hand and a human co-host has your context. Professor steps back until you resume. See Raise Hand and Human Support (§3.11.11).\n- **Closed** — the day is done, with a recap of what you covered, homework for independent practice, and a note of what comes next.\n\n## A day in the room\n\n1. **Open the day.** Select a course, or follow the agenda's current block. The room opens the day and checks you in.\n2. **Learn.** The Lesson tab shows the companion material for the current lesson: the idea, a worked example headed \"In practice,\" and the source line for the material. The companion reader gives you the full text with previous and next navigation.\n3. **Check.** The quick check asks you to explain your thinking in writing. Submit it; Professor reviews your reasoning and responds. Attempts are recorded with a result and feedback; see Progress Tracking (§3.11.8).\n4. **Work.** Assignments and quizzes come from the companion's practice sections and checks. Starting one makes it your active work; submitting it records an attempt. Submitting work is not itself a grade.\n5. **Take a break.** Breaks last fifteen minutes, with a timer on screen and everything saved. Use the break button whenever you need one.\n6. **Close the day.** Closing writes the recap, the homework, and what comes next. The next day starts fresh, with the previous day's record intact.\n\n## Messages you may see\n\n- **\"The classroom service is starting up. Please wait a moment and try again.\"** The service returned a page instead of data. Wait briefly and retry.\n- **\"The classroom service returned an unreadable response. Please retry.\"** Retry the action.\n- **Break message.** If you try to change courses during a break, the room tells you the break applies across the school day and asks you to resume after the timer ends.\n- **Check message.** You cannot advance past a check you have not completed. Complete the active check first.\n- **Handoff message.** While a co-host has your case, Professor does not take new instruction questions. Resume with Professor after the handoff ends.\n\nYour writing in progress — an answer you are composing, a notebook note, a question — is saved automatically for each course. Close the tab mid-thought and your work is there when you return.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about the classroom or a learning day: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "assessments-and-rubric": "\n# Assessments and Rubric\n\nThis page covers assessment in the Learning Center: the three assessment types — quick checks, assignments, and quizzes — the Competency Rubric that sets the standard, and the safety gates that must be passed before live-animal work. This page summarizes how assessment works; the credential requirements themselves are stated in Certifications and Credentials (§3.11.9).\n\n## Quick checks: explain your thinking\n\nThe quick check is the main assessment type, and it is not multiple choice. After each lesson, the check surface asks you to explain your thinking in writing, in a box labeled \"Your reasoning.\"\n\nWhen you submit, Professor evaluates your response against the companion material, and the attempt is recorded with:\n\n- A score and a pass or needs-work result.\n- The stage the attempt sits at in your progression.\n- The specific misconception, if your reasoning went wrong, named in plain words.\n- Feedback stating what to do next.\n- A short summary of what the attempt showed.\n\nFeedback follows the diagnosis. Depending on the result, Professor will reframe the idea (\"Let's reframe this\"), point you back to the companion, reteach the idea by another approach, or set a parallel question.\n\nIf you are stuck, use \"I need a different explanation.\" That asks Professor for a reframe — one starting point, not the answer — and it does not use up your attempt. Help with understanding and evaluation of understanding are kept separate on purpose.\n\n## Assignments and quizzes\n\nAssignments come from the companion's practice exercises, quizzes from its section checks, and the applied project becomes the capstone project. Starting an item makes it the active work for your day; submitting it records an attempt in the same way as a quick check.\n\nTwo rules govern all submitted work:\n\n1. **Submission is not a grade.** Turning work in is an event. What the work shows is the assessment.\n2. **There is no grade point average.** The grades view tracks what you can do. It carries a notice that its scores are demonstration records and that Professor does not turn completion into a grade.\n\n## The Competency Rubric\n\nThe Competency Rubric is the standard across the Leashed pathways. Every row of the rubric must reach \"Competent,\" and safety-critical rows are pass or fail. There is no averaging: a strong theory row does not balance out a safety row.\n\nIn the classroom this is a weekly practice, not a document you read once. Live-client work — grooms and training sessions — is assessed against rubric rows, and the Friday schedule block is a rubric review with your instructor.\n\n## Safety gates\n\nA safety gate is a module that an instructor must sign off before you begin live-animal work at that level. It is a requirement, not a recommendation, and the classroom does not advance you into live-animal blocks without the sign-off recorded.\n\n| Pathway | Safety gates |\n|---------|--------------|\n| IPDG | IPDG-103 (before any live-animal work), IPDG-302 (before advanced live-animal work) |\n| PDT | PDT-201 |\n| ACA | ACA-201 |\n| PPS | PPS-104, PPS-105 |\n| CAT | CAT-102 |\n| PPC | PPC-105, 201, 306, 403, 405, 410 |\n\nThe pathways are described in Leashed Diploma Pathways (§3.11.5). If you are an employer evaluating a credential, the safety-gate sign-offs are the first thing to ask about: they are the record that no learner handled a live animal until an instructor confirmed the learner was ready.\n\n## What an accepted attempt means\n\n> [!NOTICE]\n> Reasoning accepted by Professor is formative evidence, not certified mastery. An accepted attempt shows that your thinking was sound in that attempt. Certification comes from the full record: attempts over time, practicum hours, instructor sign-offs, and the capstone panel. No single interaction, however strong, replaces that record.\n\n## For instructors\n\nThe attempts record shows scores and the misconceptions learners actually made, in their own words. If several learners reach the same misconception on the same check, treat that as a signal about the lesson, not as separate individual failures, and intervene where the pattern points. See Instructor Tools (§3.11.12).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about an assessment result or a safety-gate sign-off: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "progress-tracking": "\n# Progress Tracking\n\nThis page covers how your progress is recorded in the Learning Center: the evidence record of your attempts, the day-event record of each learning day, the practicum log required for a credential, and what you, your instructors, and administrators can each see.\n\n## The evidence record\n\nEvery attempt you submit — every quick check, assignment, and quiz — is recorded with the kind of work, the item, your response, Professor's feedback, and the pass or needs-work result, with the time it was submitted. Attempts accumulate; they are not edited or removed. An early attempt that missed the idea stays beside the later attempt where you reached it, because the record shows your path, not only the endpoint.\n\n> [!NOTICE]\n> Reasoning accepted by Professor is formative evidence, not certified mastery. Certification is the whole record, not any single entry. See Assessments and Rubric (§3.11.7).\n\n## The day-event record\n\nAlongside the evidence record, each learning day keeps an event record: the day opened and checked in, each change of state, breaks taken, hands raised and resolved, and the day closed. The classroom footer states the point briefly: \"Work is saved.\" The event record answers \"what happened on Tuesday\" — not only what you submitted, but how the day ran. Instructors use it, and it is the record an accreditation review would examine.\n\n## The practicum log\n\nThe practicum log is the most consequential record in the system, because a credential cannot be issued without it. What it must show depends on your pathway:\n\n- **IPDG** — live-client hours and breed variety, signed by your instructor. Required for the GRM-BIZ capstone and the diploma.\n- **PDT** — live-client training hours, signed. Required for TRN-BIZ.\n- **ACA** — live-animal hours and breed variety, signed. Part of the workforce-readiness portfolio.\n- **PPC** — multi-service hours across salon, training, and sitting work — the only pathway that requires demonstrated competency in all three service lines.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Practicum hours must be signed by an instructor. An unsigned hour does not count. Capstone panels review the log before the defense begins; if the hours or the variety are not there, the defense does not take place.\n\n## What you see\n\nIn the classroom, your progress appears as:\n\n- **The lesson overview** — the path through your current course, chapter by chapter, marked \"In progress,\" \"Previously visited,\" or \"Ahead.\"\n- **Your evidence and day records** — your attempts and the day's events, newest first.\n- **Recent submissions** — a short list beside the work surface and the full submissions view.\n- **The notebook** — your own notes, saved for each course.\n- **The calendar** — your school-day blocks, the current block marked, and what is next.\n\nThere is no percentage-complete bar and no level counter. The empty state for the evidence record states the reason: \"Evidence grows from your work — not completion counters.\" Moving through material is not the same as learning it, and the record is built to show learning.\n\n## What instructors and administrators see\n\nInstructors see the diagnostic view: your attempts, the misconceptions recorded against them, and your event history, including the context attached to a raised hand. See Instructor Tools (§3.11.12). The admin console's learner tracking surface covers per-learner progress, completion, attendance, and risk. See Admin LMS Console (§3.11.13).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about your learning record or practicum log: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "certifications-and-credentials": "\n# Certifications and Credentials\n\nThis page covers the credentials the academy issues: the fifteen catalog credentials, the six pathway diplomas and certificates, the three gates every credential requires, how credentials build on one another, and what a credential does and does not establish.\n\n## The fifteen catalog credentials\n\nEach catalog program carries one credential, earned by completing the program's terms, assessments, and any practicum requirements:\n\n- **Veterinary Assistant** (VET, 395 hrs)\n- **Veterinary Technician** (VTE, 1,010 hrs)\n- **Veterinary Surgical Technician** (VST, 445 hrs)\n- **Veterinary Technology** (VTN, 525 hrs)\n- **Pre-Veterinary Medicine Certificate** (PVM, 770 hrs)\n- **Veterinary Pathology Technician** (VPT, 510 hrs)\n- **Veterinary Practice Manager** (VPM, 330 hrs)\n- **Professional Pet Groomer** (GRO, 345 hrs)\n- **Feline Care Specialist** (FEL, 170 hrs)\n- **Professional Dog Trainer** (PRT, 345 hrs)\n- **Animal Behavior Technician** (ABT, 485 hrs)\n- **Animal Care Assistant** (ACA, 170 hrs)\n- **Grooming Salon Practice Manager** (GSP, 655 hrs)\n- **Equine Nursing Technician** (EQN, 440 hrs)\n- **Zookeeper Assistant** (ZKA, 440 hrs)\n\nSeveral programs also issue intermediate certificates along the way. Animal Behavior Technician, for example, includes a Certificate of Completion (Behavior Foundations) and a Certificate of Proficiency (Behavior Modification) before the full program credential. These are stackable proof of progress that you can show an employer before you finish the program.\n\n## The six pathway diplomas and certificates\n\n- **Professional Dog Groomer Diploma** (IPDG, 1,248 hrs) — GRM-BIZ capstone: business plan defense, demonstration groom, and signed practicum log.\n- **Professional Dog Trainer Diploma** (PDT, 1,112 hrs) — TRN-BIZ capstone: business plan, demonstration training session, and continuing-education plan.\n- **Dog Bather–Animal Care Assistant Diploma** (ACA, 988 hrs) — ACA-BIZ capstone: workforce-readiness portfolio and demonstration handling session.\n- **Pet Sitter Certificate** (PPS, 56 hrs) — PPS-BIZ: business launch portfolio.\n- **Professional Cat Groomer Certificate** (CAT, 58 hrs) — CAT-BIZ: service-line launch plan.\n- **Professional Pet Care & Business Ownership — Advanced Diploma** (PPC, 1,500 hrs) — PPC-BIZ: full enterprise plan defense. The final credential in the set.\n\nThe pathways are described in Leashed Diploma Pathways (§3.11.5).\n\n## What earning a credential requires\n\nThree gates, in order:\n\n1. **The work.** Every lesson, check, assignment, and quiz in the program, with attempts recorded. The Competency Rubric requires \"Competent\" on every row, with no averaging. See Assessments and Rubric (§3.11.7).\n2. **The practicum.** Signed live-animal or live-client hours for your pathway: breed variety for groomers, training hours for trainers, multi-service hours for PPC. Unsigned hours do not count. See Progress Tracking (§3.11.8).\n3. **The panel.** The capstone defense before instructors: present the business plan or portfolio, defend the figures, and demonstrate the craft live. Panels challenge assumptions, including pricing, staffing costs, and volume.\n\nIf any gate is not met, the credential is not issued.\n\n## How credentials build on one another\n\n- ACA builds into IPDG: technical modules credit toward IPDG-101–204, and all business modules transfer.\n- PPS builds into ACA, IPDG, PDT, or PPC.\n- CAT builds into IPDG or PPC.\n- PPC is the final credential; it does not build into another.\n\nThe \"Build on Your Credentials\" card on each program page shows the building path for that program. You do not have to choose the 1,248-hour diploma on your first day: you can begin with the 56-hour sitting certificate, work, return for the assistant diploma, and build into grooming. Each credential is complete on its own and counts toward the next.\n\n## What a credential establishes\n\n- **For employers,** the value of the credential is the gates behind it: safety-gate sign-offs, signed practicum hours, and a panel defense. When you evaluate a candidate, ask which pathway the credential belongs to, whether the practicum log is available, and what the capstone covered.\n- **For you,** the business subjects — Business Operations, Marketing, Finance, and Legal — carry value alongside the craft credential. The curriculum is built on the view that most pet-care businesses fail on business fundamentals, not on craft, so the owner-operator foundation is part of the credential on purpose.\n\n> [!NOTICE]\n> These are academy credentials, not state licenses. They do not substitute for a licensed veterinary credential where the law requires one. Programs that touch clinical veterinary work (VTE, VST, VTN, VPT) teach to the role; licensing requirements in your state are separate.\n\n## Verifying a credential\n\nTo verify a graduate's credential, contact the academy through the address below with the graduate's name and the credential name. State the pathway and the capstone as well if you have them; those details make the record easier to locate.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about a credential, or a request to verify one: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "entity-records-and-good-standing": "\n# Entity Records and Good Standing\n\nThis standard governs the corporate records of UnSaid Memories, LLC, a Tennessee limited liability company of Memphis, Tennessee, and the steps the Company takes to remain in good standing with the Tennessee Secretary of State. Every contract the Company signs, every tax filing it makes, and every license it holds rests on the entity. The Company's position on domicile and good standing is stated in Corporate Domicile & Good Standing (§2.9.9); this standard states how that position is maintained in practice.\n\n## Roles and duties\n\n- **The Managing Member** is the sole member of the Company and the only person who may bind the entity, unless the Managing Member delegates that authority in a signed writing kept in the corporate book.\n- **The Legal function** maintains the corporate book, calendars the annual report, verifies the registered agent's details each year, and obtains certificates of good standing when a counterparty requires one.\n- **The Registered Agent** accepts service of process and official mail for the Company at a physical Tennessee address, and forwards each item to the Legal function on the day it is received.\n\n## The corporate book\n\nThe corporate book is the Company's entity file, kept by the Legal function. It contains, at minimum:\n\n1. The Articles of Organization filed with the Tennessee Secretary of State, with the filed confirmation.\n2. The Operating Agreement for the single-member Company.\n3. The federal employer identification number confirmation letter.\n4. Each annual report as filed.\n5. The Registered Agent's written acceptance of appointment and a current record of the agent's name and address.\n6. Each certificate of foreign qualification, if the Company registers to do business in another state.\n7. Signed, dated membership resolutions for major actions, one page each: adopting the Terms of Use (§2.1.1), approving the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1), appointing the designated copyright agent, and opening bank and payment-processor accounts.\n8. Assumed-name filings and trademark applications and registrations, including \"Portal Shop.\"\n\n## Procedure: keeping good standing\n\nGood standing means the entity exists, its annual reports are current, its Registered Agent is on file and reachable, and it owes the State of Tennessee no penalties. It is proved by a certificate of good standing (a certificate of existence) ordered from the Secretary of State.\n\n1. Calendar the annual report in January of each year. The report is due **April 1** and is filed with the Secretary of State; see Compliance Calendar (§3.12.2).\n2. File the annual report before the due date and place the filed confirmation in the corporate book.\n3. In the same week, verify the Registered Agent's name, physical Tennessee address (not a post office box), and forwarding contact. Record the verification, with its date, in the corporate book.\n4. Keep a certificate of good standing less than 90 days old in the corporate book at all times, and order a new one whenever a payment processor, tax authority, landlord, or franchise regulator asks for proof.\n5. Keep Company funds and personal funds in separate accounts at all times. Paying personal expenses from a Company account, or Company expenses from a personal account, undermines the separation the Operating Agreement states.\n6. File each entity-level tax return copy in the corporate book when the return is filed; the tax procedure itself is governed by Tax Registrations and Reporting (§3.12.5).\n\n## When something has lapsed\n\n1. **Missed annual report.** File immediately and pay the penalties. Tennessee allows a cure period before administrative dissolution; a dissolved company that keeps operating exposes its member personally, because the liability protection the entity provides stops working.\n2. **Registered Agent failure.** If the agent has resigned, moved, or become unreachable, appoint a successor and update the Secretary of State filing at once, then check whether any service or official mail was directed to the former address.\n3. **Either event.** Record what happened, when it was discovered, and how it was cured, and place that record in the corporate book.\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nThis standard creates the corporate book itself, the dated Registered Agent verification, certificates of good standing, signed membership resolutions, and lapse-and-cure records. All are kept in the corporate book by the Legal function and retained under Record Retention (§3.12.6). Formation documents are kept permanently.\n\n## Escalation\n\nAny question about who may bind the Company, any notice of dissolution proceedings, and any service of process goes to the Legal function at legal@unsaidmemories.com on the day it is received. Operational questions about entity records go to support@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Legal function reviews this standard, the corporate book's completeness, and the Registered Agent's details once a year, in the same week the annual report is filed.\n",
  "compliance-calendar": "\n# Compliance Calendar\n\nThis standard establishes the compliance calendar: the single list of every recurring legal, tax, and regulatory deadline the Company owes, with its cadence, its due date, the role that owns it, and the consequence of missing it. A deadline that is not on the calendar is treated as a deadline that will be missed; every new registration, license, or filing obligation is added to the calendar in the week it is created.\n\n## Roles and duties\n\n- **The Legal function** owns the calendar: it maintains the list, runs the quarterly review, and confirms that evidence of each completed filing is attached to its entry.\n- **The Managing Member** owns the entity, licensing, domain, insurance, and franchise deadlines assigned to that role in the table below, and approves any change to who owns a deadline.\n- **The Tax Preparer** owns the tax filing deadlines assigned to that role and notifies the Legal function the same week any filing cadence changes.\n\n## The calendar\n\n| Obligation | Cadence | Typical due date | Owning role | If it slips |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Tennessee annual report | Annual | **April 1** | Managing Member | Penalties, then administrative dissolution; see Entity Records and Good Standing (§3.12.1) |\n| Tennessee sales and use tax filing | As assigned by the Tennessee Department of Revenue (monthly, quarterly, or annual) | Per notice | Tax Preparer | Penalty and interest |\n| Tennessee business tax return | Annual | Per notice | Tax Preparer | Penalty and interest |\n| Other-state marketplace facilitator filings | Per state (monthly, quarterly, or annual) | Per state | Tax Preparer | Penalty and interest, per state |\n| Form 1099 issuance for the Company's own reportable payments | Annual | **January 31** to recipients | Tax Preparer | Internal Revenue Service penalties per form |\n| Designated copyright agent registration | As required by the Copyright Office | Per registration | Legal function | Loss of eligibility for the DMCA safe harbor; see DMCA Agent Process (§3.12.4) |\n| Business license renewals (Memphis and Shelby County) | Annual | Per license | Managing Member | Fines and stop-work exposure |\n| Domain renewals, including unsaidmemories.com | Annual or longer | Staggered | Managing Member | Redemption fees, then loss of the domain |\n| Insurance renewals | Annual | Per policy | Managing Member | An uninsured gap |\n| Franchise Disclosure Document annual update | Annual, within 120 days of fiscal year end | Per the FTC Franchise Rule | Managing Member | Franchises may not lawfully be offered on an out-of-date disclosure document |\n\nThe tax obligations behind the tax rows are governed by Tax Registrations and Reporting (§3.12.5), and the filings that create new calendar rows are covered in Regulatory Filings and Registrations (§3.12.3).\n\n## Procedure: keeping the calendar\n\n1. Keep one list. Deadlines are not divided among separate lists kept by different people.\n2. Give every deadline a 30-day warning and a 7-day warning. Tax filings require the longer warning because the Tax Preparer requires lead time.\n3. When a deadline is completed, attach the evidence to its entry: the filed return, the receipt, or the renewed certificate. \"I think we filed\" is not a record.\n4. Add a row in the same week any new registration, license, or filing obligation is created.\n5. Open every notice from a revenue department on the day it arrives. A notice that changes a filing cadence — for example, from quarterly to monthly as volume grows — updates the calendar in the same week.\n\n## Procedure: when a deadline is missed\n\n1. File or renew immediately. Penalties usually stop growing on the day of filing.\n2. Pay the penalty and record the payment against the calendar entry.\n3. Establish why the calendar failed: the deadline was missing from the list, or a warning was ignored. Correct the process, not only the instance.\n4. Check for consequences that follow from the miss. A missed annual report can affect good standing, which can in turn affect a state registration that required good standing; one miss can be three problems.\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nThis standard creates the calendar itself, the evidence attached to each completed entry, records of penalties paid, and the notes of each quarterly review. The Legal function keeps them with the corporate records described in Entity Records and Good Standing (§3.12.1), retained under Record Retention (§3.12.6).\n\n## Escalation\n\nA missed deadline, a notice of penalty or dissolution, or a filing cadence change that no role can absorb is escalated to the Legal function at legal@unsaidmemories.com on the day it is identified.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Legal function reviews the whole calendar once a quarter: what falls due in the next 90 days, what is new, and what has changed. This standard is reviewed at the same quarterly review.\n",
  "regulatory-filings-and-registrations": "\n# Regulatory Filings and Registrations\n\nThis standard states how the Company registers and files in each regulatory area its sales touch: marketplace facilitator sales tax, Tennessee business tax, state feed registration for pet food, federal and state registration for pesticide products, and veterinary licensure and the veterinarian-client-patient relationship. The governing statements of the Company's position are cited in each part; this standard states the filing work and never restates a customer-facing obligation differently from the document that governs it.\n\n## Roles and duties\n\n- **The Tax Preparer** monitors sales tax nexus, completes registrations, and files on each assigned cadence.\n- **The Legal function** keeps each registration certificate and account number, adds every new obligation to the Compliance Calendar (§3.12.2) in the week it is created, and answers counterparty requests for proof of registration.\n- **Catalog Operations** applies the listing gates for pet food and pesticide products and escalates any product it cannot classify.\n- **Clinical Operations** verifies veterinary licenses at onboarding and maintains the record of which states permit a veterinarian-client-patient relationship to be established by telemedicine.\n\n## Sales tax: marketplace facilitator registrations\n\nThe Company's position is stated in Marketplace Facilitator Sales Tax (§2.9.6), and the Tennessee implementation in Tennessee Sales & Use Tax (§2.9.7). The customer-facing rule is stated in Marketplace Facilitator Tax Policy (§2.1.17), which governs.\n\n1. Monitor gross sales into each state on a rolling twelve-month basis. Tennessee's threshold is **$100,000** in gross sales to Tennessee buyers; other states set their own thresholds, commonly $100,000 or 200 transactions.\n2. Begin registration as soon as a state's threshold is crossed, or is about to be crossed.\n3. Register with the state's revenue department — in Tennessee, through TN TAP. A registration requires the Company's federal tax identification number, its formation documents, and identification of a responsible party.\n4. Record the account number and the assigned filing cadence, and add both to the Compliance Calendar (§3.12.2) in the same week.\n5. Collect and remit as the governing documents provide: tax collected at checkout is held in the tax liability account, kept separate from operating funds and seller funds, and reconciled to filings monthly. Sellers do not collect tax on marketplace transactions.\n6. File on the cadence the state assigns — monthly, quarterly, or annual. Open every revenue department notice on the day it arrives, because cadences change as volume grows.\n\n## Tennessee business tax\n\nThis is a separate tax on the Company's own revenue, governed by Tennessee Business Tax (§2.9.8), which states the posture at the **$10,000** platform-revenue threshold. It is filed annually and paid from operating funds. It is not tax collected from buyers, and its records are never combined with the sales tax records.\n\n## Pet food: state feed registration\n\nPet food sold in a state is generally registered in that state, per product and per state, with the state's feed control official. The registration duty sits primarily with the brand or manufacturer that owns the label. The Platform's part is verification at listing: a pet food listing without the registrations required for a state does not go live in that state. The seller-facing rule is stated in Pet Food and Treats Policy (§2.3.40), which governs.\n\n1. At listing, check the feed registration position for each state the product will be sold into as part of brand authorization.\n2. Hold the listing from any state where the required registration is not evidenced.\n3. Record the registration evidence with the listing record.\n\n## Pesticide products: FIFRA registration\n\nFlea, tick, and other pesticide products are regulated under the Federal Insecticide, Fungicide, and Rodenticide Act: federal registration plus state registration, with the registration number on the label. The seller-facing rule is stated in Flea, Tick and Pesticide Products Policy (§2.3.41), which governs: no registration number, no listing.\n\n1. Check the registration number on the product label at listing.\n2. Refuse the listing if no number is present.\n3. Escalate any product whose classification is unclear to the Legal function; do not decide borderline pesticide questions in Catalog Operations.\n\n## Veterinary services: licensure and the VCPR\n\nState law governs licensure and the veterinarian-client-patient relationship (VCPR). The Company's position is stated in VCPR Requirements (§2.9.16), which governs. Tennessee Code Annotated §63-12-103 does not allow a VCPR to be established by electronic means alone, and the Memphis launch therefore carries an in-person examination requirement.\n\n1. Verify each veterinarian's license at onboarding and record the verification.\n2. Maintain the state-by-state record of where a VCPR may be established by telemedicine, and apply it so that a virtual VCPR is not recorded where the patient's state does not permit it.\n3. Permit prescription authority only as the state record allows. Prescription dispensing requires a valid prescription from a licensed veterinarian; see Telemedicine Consent (§2.5.13) for the consent that accompanies a telemedicine encounter.\n\n## What a registration creates\n\nEach registration above creates three things, every time:\n\n1. A calendar row — renewals, filings, and cadence changes, under Compliance Calendar (§3.12.2).\n2. A record — the certificate, the account number, and the filed returns, kept under Record Retention (§3.12.6).\n3. A proof point — the corporate book or the tax file must answer a counterparty's request for proof in one lookup; see Entity Records and Good Standing (§3.12.1).\n\n## Escalation\n\nA threshold crossed without a registration, a registration refused or revoked, an ambiguous pesticide product, and any question about whether a state's law permits a service the Platform offers are escalated to the Legal function at legal@unsaidmemories.com on the day they are identified.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Legal function reviews this standard once a year, and the Tax Preparer reviews nexus figures and filing cadences quarterly, at the calendar review described in Compliance Calendar (§3.12.2).\n",
  "dmca-agent-process": "\n# DMCA Agent Process\n\nThis standard states how the Company receives, records, reviews, and acts on copyright notices under the Digital Millennium Copyright Act, 17 U.S.C. §512. The safe harbor in §512 protects a marketplace that hosts material uploaded by others, provided the Company maintains a designated agent, a public intake, and a working notice-and-takedown procedure. The customer-facing rule is stated in DMCA and Copyright Policy (§2.1.7), which governs; the seller-facing rule is stated in Copyright and DMCA Policy (§2.3.52). This standard states the internal procedure and does not restate either rule differently.\n\n## Roles and duties\n\n- **The Legal function** is responsible for the designated agent registration, for the completeness standard applied at intake, and for every decision to restore material after a counter-notice.\n- **Support Operations** operates the intake: it files each notice as a case, sends the acknowledgment, and keeps the correspondence on the case record.\n- **Trust & Safety** decides takedown on the substance of a complete notice, notifies the uploader, and records any repeat-infringer consequence against the seller record. Intake staff do not remove material.\n\n## The intake: `/dmca`\n\nThe public form at `/dmca` collects a notice as structured information:\n\n1. **The complainant** — full name, email address, mailing address, and telephone number, plus a statement of any copyright owner the complainant represents as an authorized agent.\n2. **The copyrighted work** — a description of the work, and optionally the address where the original may be seen.\n3. **The material complained of** — the addresses or listing and photo identifiers for the material on the Platform, one per line.\n4. **Statements and signature** — a good-faith statement, a statement under penalty of perjury, and a typed signature giving the full legal name.\n\n## Procedure: filing a notice\n\n1. **Check the email address.** A valid complainant email address is the one hard requirement, because the acknowledgment must be able to reach the complainant. A submission without one is refused.\n2. **Assess completeness.** Check the filing against the six elements §512(c)(3)(A) requires — signature, identification of the copyrighted work, identification of the material complained of, complainant name, address and telephone number, the good-faith statement, and the perjury statement — and record exactly which elements are present and which are missing. Completeness is assessed from the filing, never assumed.\n3. **Open the case.** Open a support case for the notice, marked as coming from an external rights holder (who usually has no Platform account), categorized as a DMCA notice, and given high priority. Attach the full notice text as the inbound message.\n4. **Create the notice record.** Record the complainant's identity, the signature text and signing time, the work description and its address, the addresses complained of, both statements, which elements were complete and which were missing, the case state, and the acknowledgment time.\n5. **Acknowledge.** Send the acknowledgment email and return the case reference to the complainant. Sending the acknowledgment is best effort: if it fails, the filing has still been made, the case exists, and the bounce is noted on the case.\n6. **If filing itself fails,** nothing is written and the notice does not exist as a case. The form tells the complainant the submission failed and asks for a retry. This is the only path on which a notice is not recorded.\n\n## Procedure: a notice that is missing elements\n\nA notice missing elements is filed, not refused and not discarded, and no material is removed on it.\n\n1. Record the filing as needing information, with the missing elements listed on the notice record.\n2. In the acknowledgment, name exactly what is missing and how to supply it: reply to the confirmation email quoting the case reference. The complainant does not need to file again.\n3. Act only when the missing elements are supplied and the notice is complete.\n\nUnder §512(c)(3) the Company has no duty to act on a notice that does not substantially comply, but nothing requires the notice to be discarded. Filing it creates the record that shows the notice was received and answered in good faith.\n\n## Procedure: review and takedown on a complete notice\n\n1. **Confirm the substance.** Trust & Safety confirms that the work description identifies an actual copyrighted work, that the addresses resolve to actual Platform content, and that the complainant, or the owner the complainant represents, is plausibly the rights holder.\n2. **Locate the material.** Match the addresses and identifiers to listings, images, or descriptions, and identify the seller who uploaded them.\n3. **Remove the material.** Remove or disable access to the identified material without delay. Record what was removed, when, and under which case.\n4. **Notify the uploader.** Tell the affected seller what was removed and why, and that a counter-notice may be filed. This notice is required by §512(g); it is not a courtesy.\n5. **Update the case.** Move the notice record through the review states and keep all correspondence on the case.\n\n## Procedure: counter-notice and restoration\n\n1. Receive the counter-notice on the same case, so the matter stays in one file. A counter-notice must carry the elements §512(g)(3) requires: signature, identification of the removed material, a good-faith statement that the material was removed by mistake or misidentification, consent to jurisdiction, and contact information.\n2. Forward the counter-notice to the original complainant.\n3. Wait **10 to 14 business days**. If the complainant files a court action seeking to restrain the material, the material stays down. If no such action is filed in that window, restore the material. The window is set by the statute; the Company does not shorten it or lengthen it.\n4. Record the forwarding date, the expiry of the window, and the restoration, or the court filing, on the case.\n\n## The designated agent\n\n1. The Legal function maintains the Company's designated agent registration with the U.S. Copyright Office and publishes the agent's contact information as the DMCA and Copyright Policy (§2.1.7) provides.\n2. Legal notices, including DMCA notices sent outside the intake form, are directed to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n3. The registration renewal is a row on the Compliance Calendar (§3.12.2). An expired designation puts the safe harbor at risk and is treated as an urgent lapse.\n\n## Repeat infringers\n\nThe safe harbor also requires a policy for terminating repeat infringers, applied in practice and not only stated. Trust & Safety records each sustained infringement finding against the seller record, and a takedown against a seller with earlier findings escalates faster. Record the count on the case itself — for example, \"this is the second sustained finding\" — so the count is never reconstructed from memory later. Seller consequences are governed by Copyright and DMCA Policy (§2.3.52), which controls.\n\n## Particular cases\n\n- **A notice aimed at a first-party listing.** Copyright takedown is not a brand-control tool. If a listing sold by Portal Shop uses a brand's product images lawfully — supplier-provided or licensed assets — the notice fails on substance. Do not use the DMCA queue to work around brand authorization, which does not reach first-party inventory.\n- **Addresses that do not resolve.** File the notice as needing information and ask for working identifiers. Identification of the material is the complainant's duty.\n- **A complainant who is the seller's competitor.** Motive does not invalidate a notice, and it does not lower the standard of review. Verify the substance in the usual way. Section 512(f) liability for knowing misrepresentation runs against a bad-faith complainant, and the Company's records are what prove what was known at the time.\n- **Bulk notices from enforcement vendors.** Assess each notice on its elements. A bulk filing with 200 addresses and one work description receives the same element check as a single notice, applied to each address.\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nThis standard creates the notice record, the support case and its messages, the acknowledgment record, takedown and restoration records, counter-notice records, and the repeat-infringer count on the seller record. Notice records and cases are kept in the support system, retained under Record Retention (§3.12.6), and preserved in full when a Legal Hold (§3.12.7) applies — a court action over a takedown is a common trigger.\n\n## Escalation\n\nA court filing, a counter-notice, a notice directed at first-party inventory, a claim of misrepresentation, and any request from a court or regulator are escalated to the Legal function at legal@unsaidmemories.com on the day they are received. Routine operational questions about a case in progress go to support@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Legal function reviews this standard, the designated agent registration, and a sample of closed cases once a year, and reviews the procedure against what actually happened after the first notice in each new category is handled.\n",
  "tax-registrations-and-reporting": "\n# Tax Registrations and Reporting\n\nThis standard covers the Company's tax registrations and reporting across four separate regimes, which must be kept separate: (1) sales tax the Company collects from buyers as marketplace facilitator, (2) business tax the Company pays on its own revenue to Tennessee, (3) information reporting on sellers' earnings, and (4) the Company's own income tax. The governing statements of position are cited in each part; this standard states the operating procedure.\n\n## Roles and duties\n\n- **The Tax Preparer** owns registrations, filings, nexus monitoring, and the Company's own information returns and income tax returns.\n- **The Legal function** keeps tax records with the corporate records, places every deadline on the Compliance Calendar (§3.12.2), and escalates any notice of deficiency or back-tax exposure.\n- **Seller Operations** confirms that each seller's payment-processor identity verification is complete, because an incomplete verification is also a reporting gap.\n- **The Managing Member** approves voluntary disclosure and back-registration decisions where a threshold was crossed without a registration.\n\n## Sales tax collection as marketplace facilitator\n\nThe Company's position is stated in Marketplace Facilitator Sales Tax (§2.9.6), the Tennessee implementation in Tennessee Sales & Use Tax (§2.9.7), and the customer-facing rule in Marketplace Facilitator Tax Policy (§2.1.17), which governs.\n\n1. Monitor nexus continuously. Tennessee's threshold is **$100,000** in gross sales to Tennessee buyers in twelve months; every other state sets its own. Liability runs from the date nexus attached, not the date it was noticed, so a threshold crossed unnoticed means tax owed on sales on which none was collected.\n2. Register per state. Tennessee registration runs through TN TAP. Each registration produces an account number and a filing cadence — monthly, quarterly, or annual, assigned by the state on volume — and both are placed on the Compliance Calendar (§3.12.2) in the week they are issued.\n3. Keep collected tax separate. Tax collected at checkout is held in the tax liability account — not operating funds and not seller funds — and reconciled to filings monthly.\n4. Do not let sellers collect. On marketplace transactions, sellers do not add tax at checkout. If a seller's exemption certificate is invalid or expired, tax is collected; that is a tax determination, not an enforcement decision open to appeal as one.\n5. Audit product taxability periodically. Taxability is an attribute of the listing — food against non-food, tangible against digital — read by the checkout against the ship-to jurisdiction. A seller who misclassifies a product to avoid collection breaches the listing accuracy rules, and the Company carries the exposure in the meantime.\n\n## Tennessee business tax\n\nTennessee Business Tax (§2.9.8) states the posture at **$10,000** in platform revenue. This is the Company's own tax on the privilege of doing business in Tennessee, filed annually and paid from operating funds. It has nothing to do with sales tax collected from buyers, and the two sets of records never touch.\n\n## Information reporting: Form 1099-K\n\nThe Company's position is stated in Tax Information Reporting (Form 1099-K) (§2.9.5), including the $600 threshold posture stated there.\n\n1. **Sellers' earnings are reported by the payment processor.** Under the processor's connected-account arrangement, the processor performs seller identity verification and issues Forms 1099-K to sellers who meet the threshold. The Company does not receive sellers' Social Security numbers or tax identification numbers in this flow, and staff shall not ask a seller to send them.\n2. **Confirm onboarding is complete.** A seller whose processor identity verification is unfinished cannot receive transfers, and that seller's tax reporting is incomplete by construction. Seller Operations checks completion before a seller goes live and again at year end.\n3. **The Company's own forms are the Company's.** Payments the Company makes to contractors, and any reportable payments it makes outside the processor flow, are the Company's own information-return obligations, due **January 31** to recipients. The Tax Preparer prepares them; the calendar reminds the Tax Preparer.\n\n## The Company's income tax\n\nThe limited liability company's federal and Tennessee franchise and excise obligations are the Tax Preparer's work. Operations has one duty in this area: keep clean books. The accounting records kept under Record Retention (§3.12.6) are what the return is built from, and books delivered in disorder in March produce extensions, penalties, and cost.\n\n## The annual tax cycle\n\n| When | What |\n|---|---|\n| January | Issue the Company's own information returns (January 31 deadline). Close the prior year's books. Confirm nexus thresholds per state for the new year. |\n| Quarterly | Make estimated payments as the Tax Preparer directs. Review nexus: start a registration for any state about to cross its threshold before it crosses. |\n| April 1 | Tennessee annual report — an entity filing, not a tax filing, but it shares the season; see Entity Records and Good Standing (§3.12.1). |\n| Ongoing | File sales tax on each state's cadence. Reconcile the tax liability account monthly. |\n| Year end | Planning session with the Tax Preparer: thresholds, rates, and new states expected to change in the coming year. |\n\n## Failure modes and the response to each\n\n1. **Nexus crossed without registration.** The most expensive common failure. Back-registration usually means back tax, penalty, and interest. On discovery, the Legal function and the Tax Preparer assess voluntary disclosure at once, and the Managing Member approves the approach.\n2. **A cadence change is missed.** The state moves the Company from quarterly to monthly filing and the notice goes unread. Open every revenue department notice on the day it arrives, and update the calendar in the same week.\n3. **The tax liability account is not reconciled.** Collected tax drifts from filed tax. Monthly reconciliation catches the drift at one month instead of twelve.\n4. **Seller verification is incomplete at year end.** A seller with volume and no completed processor verification is a reporting gap. Treat completion as a condition of going live and enforce it.\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nThis standard creates registration certificates and account numbers, filed returns and filing confirmations, monthly reconciliations of the tax liability account, and the Company's information returns. Tax filings and workpapers are kept in the Tax Preparer's file and in the corporate book, and tax records are retained for seven years under Record Retention (§3.12.6).\n\n## Escalation\n\nA notice of deficiency, an assessment of back tax, a threshold crossed without registration, or any inquiry from a revenue authority is escalated to the Legal function at legal@unsaidmemories.com on the day it is received. Operational tax questions go to support@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Tax Preparer reviews nexus and filing cadences quarterly, at the review described in Compliance Calendar (§3.12.2), and the Legal function reviews this standard once a year.\n",
  "record-retention": "\n# Record Retention\n\nThis standard states how the Company keeps and destroys records: where each class of record is kept, how long it is kept, how destruction is carried out and documented, and the two events that change the schedule — a legal hold and a personal-data deletion request. Keeping everything indefinitely increases breach exposure and conflicts with deletion duties; destroying too early breaks tax law and destroys evidence. How personal information is handled is governed by the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2) and the Company's position in Data Privacy Posture (§2.9.18), which control over this standard where the two meet.\n\n## Roles and duties\n\n- **The Legal function** owns the retention schedule: it assigns each new class of record a retention period when the record type is created, approves every destruction, and decides deletion requests that meet a legal retention duty.\n- **Each operating team** keeps the records its work creates in the place named in the table below, and carries out approved destruction completely, including exported copies.\n- **The Managing Member** approves destruction of any class of record not covered by a period already assigned in this standard.\n\n## Where records are kept\n\n| Record class | Where it is kept | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| DMCA notices and case files | The structured notice records and the support case system | Notice record plus correspondence; see DMCA Agent Process (§3.12.4) |\n| Published policies and their versions | The policy store and the policy version history in the admin portal | Every version, with change notes, publisher, and time; the enforcement record |\n| Support cases and messages | The support case system | Includes the DMCA queue |\n| Transaction and order records | The commerce order records | The commercial record; tax-relevant |\n| Seller records and verification material | Seller records and verification artifacts | Verification records carry their own retention logic under the INFORM Act; see INFORM Act Seller Verification (§2.9.1) |\n| Consent records | The cookie consent record and the server-side consent log | Proves what a user chose and when |\n| Financial and payment-processor records | The payment processor's records and the wallet and payout records | The money trail; the processor retains under its own schedule |\n| Tax filings and workpapers | The Tax Preparer's file and the corporate book | Seven-year retention |\n| Corporate records | The corporate book | Formation documents permanent; annual reports per schedule; see Entity Records and Good Standing (§3.12.1) |\n| AI disclosure records | The AI disclosure records | Who was told what, and when |\n| Enforcement records | Violation and appeal records | Enforcement decisions need their evidence preserved |\n\n## Procedure: retention and destruction\n\n1. **Classify at creation.** Every new record type receives a class and a retention period when it is designed, not years later. A DMCA notice record, for example, is litigation-relevant by nature and follows the legal proceedings class from the day it exists.\n2. **Retain by default.** Nothing is deleted until two questions are both answered yes: has the class's period expired, and does no legal hold apply? See Legal Holds (§3.12.7).\n3. **Destroy completely.** Destruction means the database records, copies in backups as the backup rotation cycles, and exported copies. A record that survives in exported spreadsheets held by individuals has not been destroyed.\n4. **Document the destruction.** Record what was destroyed, when, and under which period in this standard. Routine destruction under a stated schedule, documented in this way, is defensible; undocumented destruction is not.\n\n## The two events that change the schedule\n\n**A legal hold stops the schedule.** A legal hold suspends all routine destruction for the records within its scope, even if a period expired the day before. Release of the hold resumes the schedule; it does not retroactively authorize the destruction that was blocked. See Legal Holds (§3.12.7).\n\n**A deletion request bends the schedule.** A person may have a right to deletion of personal information under applicable privacy law. A deletion request does not override a legal retention duty — tax records are kept for seven years regardless of a request — but it does require the Company to delete what may lawfully be deleted and to record what was kept, and why. \"The order record was kept because tax law requires it\" is a complete answer; \"everything was kept because that was easier\" is not. Deletion rights and how they are exercised are governed by the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2).\n\n## Points that cause failures\n\n- **Backups.** A record deleted from the live records persists in backups until the rotation cycles. That is normal and defensible, provided the backup rotation itself has a defined period. Backups kept indefinitely become the retention policy by accident.\n- **The policy version history.** Every policy version is kept with its publisher and time because it is the proof of what rule was in force on a given date — the exact question an enforcement appeal or a legal proceeding asks. Policy versions are never removed to save space. See Policy Publication and Version Control (§3.12.8).\n- **Email.** Legally significant material that arrives or is sent by email — acknowledgment emails, DMCA confirmations, tax notices — is filed to the case or record it belongs to. An inbox is not an archive.\n- **AI-generated material.** Listings written by seller AI tools, triage transcripts, and agent action records are records with their own retention logic, including the 12-month period for agent action records. Classify them when they are created; do not let a new kind of record fall outside the schedule because it is new.\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nThis standard creates the retention schedule itself, destruction records, and the records made when a deletion request is partly refused on retention grounds. The Legal function keeps them with the corporate records, and destruction records are kept permanently.\n\n## Escalation\n\nAny destruction proposed outside an assigned period, any deletion request that meets a retention duty, any discovery that records within a class have been destroyed early, and any question about breach exposure from records the Company holds are escalated to the Legal function at legal@unsaidmemories.com. Breach duties themselves are governed by Data Breach Notification (§2.9.19).\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Legal function reviews the schedule, the backup rotation period, and all destruction records once a year.\n",
  "legal-holds": "\n# Legal Holds\n\nThis standard states how a legal hold is triggered, issued, carried out, and released. A legal hold is the instruction that stops routine record destruction when litigation is reasonably anticipated. It overrides Record Retention (§3.12.6) completely: every record within its scope is preserved, even if its retention period expired the day before. The duty to preserve arises when litigation is reasonably anticipated — not when a complaint is served.\n\n## Roles and duties\n\n- **The Managing Member** issues and releases every legal hold, in writing. Authority for both decisions sits with that role; see Authority and Governance (§3.12.9).\n- **The Legal function** prepares the hold notice, confirms receipt from every custodian, keeps the hold register, and advises on scope.\n- **Each custodian** — every member of staff, contractor, the Tax Preparer, and any vendor who holds Company data within the scope — preserves the records in their custody and complies with the duties below.\n\n## What triggers a hold\n\nAny of the following, and the list is not closed:\n\n- A lawsuit is filed against the Company, or against a seller where the Platform's records are at issue.\n- The Company receives a subpoena, a civil investigative demand, or a regulator's inquiry.\n- A credible threat of litigation arrives: a demand letter, a letter from a lawyer, or a notice asking the Company to preserve its records.\n- An incident occurs that foreseeably leads to litigation: a serious pet injury in connection with Platform-arranged services, a data breach with exposure, or major seller fraud.\n- The Managing Member decides a hold is prudent.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> The trigger is reasonable anticipation, which is deliberately broader than certainty. If custodians are debating whether anticipation is reasonable, it is reasonable. Issue the hold.\n\n## Procedure: issuing the hold\n\n1. The Managing Member issues the hold in writing. Email is acceptable; the notice must be express and must itself be preserved.\n2. State **what happened**: the matter — lawsuit caption, subpoena, or incident — in one paragraph.\n3. State **what is preserved**: the scope, specifically. \"All records relating to seller X, listings Y, and the DMCA notice of a stated date\" is a scope; \"all relevant documents\" is not.\n4. State **who is covered**: every person and vendor with custody of records within the scope.\n5. State **what to do**: preserve; do not delete; do not tidy up; do not auto-archive. Suspend any routine destruction that touches records within the scope.\n6. State **how long**: until the hold is released in writing. A hold does not expire by silence.\n7. Send the notice, confirm receipt from every custodian, and file the notice. The hold notice is itself a record, retained in the legal proceedings class under Record Retention (§3.12.6).\n8. Export or copy the records within the scope promptly. Live records change; a hold stops destruction, it does not freeze a database. A copy taken at issuance is the clean evidence of what existed.\n\n## Scope: what is preserved\n\nScope is set matter by matter. The classes usually included are:\n\n- **The case file** — DMCA notices and their cases (see DMCA Agent Process (§3.12.4)), enforcement records (violations and appeals), and the policy version in force at the time, from the version history described in Policy Publication and Version Control (§3.12.8). This is why policy versions are never removed.\n- **Communications** — support cases and messages with the parties involved, emails, and chat records.\n- **Transaction records** — orders, payments, payouts, refunds, and chargebacks touching the matter.\n- **System records** — relevant logs, the consent record if privacy is at issue, and agent action records if an AI tool is involved.\n- **The deployed code** — if the dispute turns on what the site did (the checkout flow, the DMCA intake, the consent banner), preserve the code version as deployed, by a version tag or deployment record. \"The code has changed since then\" is not an answer to \"what did it do on the date in question.\"\n\n## Custodian duties\n\nFor the whole life of the hold, each custodian shall:\n\n1. Delete nothing within the scope — including routine cleanup, inbox clearing, and reorganizing files.\n2. Discuss the hold with no one outside the group the notice covers. The existence of a hold is sensitive.\n3. Ask before acting on anything next to the scope. If a record might be within the scope, treat it as within the scope and ask the Legal function.\n\n## Procedure: releasing the hold\n\n1. The Managing Member releases the hold in writing when the matter is resolved, the subpoena has been complied with, or the limitations period has run.\n2. The release states its date and returns the retention schedule to normal operation for the records concerned.\n3. Release does not retroactively authorize the destruction that was blocked during the hold. Each record's retention period resumes, and destruction requires its own decision under Record Retention (§3.12.6).\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Destroying records under a hold, or losing them through carelessness, exposes the Company to sanctions for destruction of evidence: an instruction that the fact-finder may assume the destroyed records were unfavorable, monetary sanctions, and in extreme cases penalties that decide the matter. No convenience in record-keeping is worth that risk. When in doubt, preserve.\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nThis standard creates hold notices, custodian receipt confirmations, the hold register, copies of records taken at issuance, and release notices. The Legal function keeps them with the corporate records, in the legal proceedings class, for so long as the matter and any appeal period require.\n\n## Escalation\n\nAny trigger event, any custodian who cannot comply, any discovery that records within a hold's scope have been deleted, and any legal process of any kind are escalated to the Legal function at legal@unsaidmemories.com on the day they arise.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Legal function reviews every open hold quarterly, at the review described in Compliance Calendar (§3.12.2), confirms each hold is still required and correctly scoped, and reviews this standard once a year.\n",
  "policy-publication-and-version-control": "\n# Policy Publication and Version Control\n\nThis standard governs how the Company's policies are published and versioned. Policies are live records in the publishing system, not documents attached to messages: a policy is published from the admin portal, the site presents the current published version on every request, and every version is kept with its publisher, its time of publication, and its change notes. The version history is evidence, and this standard treats it that way.\n\n## Roles and duties\n\n- **The Managing Member** approves every material change to a policy before publication and decides whether a change requires notice to sellers or customers before it takes effect.\n- **The Legal function** reviews each change for consistency with the governing document above it — the Terms of Use (§2.1.1) for site policies, and the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1) for seller policies — and confirms the change notes describe the change accurately.\n- **The Policy Publisher** (the administrator who publishes) prepares the text away from the live record, publishes it, reads the rendered page immediately after publication, and records the publication in the publication log.\n\n## The publication route\n\nPolicies are edited at `/admin/policies/editor` and published from the admin portal. The public index at `/policies` lists every published policy as a card — title, an excerpt taken from the first paragraph of the body, in the order set at publication — linking to `/policies/[slug]`. The public pages read the current published record on every request: a newly published policy is visible immediately, with no rebuild and no deployment.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Because publication is immediate, the publish action is the release. There is no later step at which an error can be caught before readers see it. Prepare and review the text completely before publishing, and read the rendered page immediately after.\n\nPolicies are presented at `/policies`, their own home. They are not cataloged in the Help Center; the two systems link to each other and are not mixed.\n\n## The slug rule\n\nThe address of a policy is derived from its title: lower case, with runs of non-alphanumeric characters collapsed to hyphens — for example, \"CANCELLATIONS\" becomes `cancellations`. The same derivation is used by the index and by the detail page, so the two always agree. It follows that renaming a policy renames its address, and the former address no longer resolves.\n\n1. Before any rename, search for incoming references to the current address — in messages, in the Terms of Use (§2.1.1), in other policies, and in correspondence with regulators.\n2. Prefer editing a policy's body over retitling it.\n3. If a rename is unavoidable, record the former and new addresses in the publication log and update every internal reference in the same publication.\n\nKeep titles distinct enough that they produce distinct addresses. Two titles that reduce to the same address collide, and the detail lookup finds the first match.\n\n## Procedure: publishing a version\n\n1. Prepare the complete revised text away from the live record.\n2. Obtain the Managing Member's approval for any material change, and the Legal function's consistency check against the governing document.\n3. Write the change notes before publishing. Notes must state what changed in terms a later reader can act on — \"the refund window changed from 14 to 30 days,\" not a general remark. Empty change notes on a material change become an unanswerable question in a later appeal.\n4. Set the order deliberately. Group related policies together, and place the highest-traffic policies — cancellations, refunds, privacy, terms — first.\n5. Publish, then open the public page and read it as rendered. Correct any error by publishing a corrected version at once; do not leave a known error live.\n6. Give notice of material changes as the governing document requires. Publication without notice is for corrections that change no obligation.\n7. Record the publication — title, version, publisher, time, and change notes — in the publication log.\n\n## The version history\n\nThe version history at `/admin/policies/versions` keeps every published version of every policy: version number, change notes, publisher, and time, for site policies and marketplace policies alike. It exists to answer three questions:\n\n- **In an enforcement appeal:** what did the rule say on the date of the conduct in question?\n- **In a regulatory inquiry:** when did this policy take effect?\n- **In the Company's own record:** what changed, and when did the change publish — which is also the evidence behind any requirement that sellers re-accept changed terms.\n\nPolicy versions are never removed or pruned. They are kept in the permanent class under Record Retention (§3.12.6).\n\n## Standby text\n\nThe policy pages carry standby text so that a legal link never fails outright. If no Privacy Policy is published, the page presents general standby copy covering cookie categories, consent mechanics, data holdings, and the Company contact block, until a Privacy Policy is published from the admin portal, at which point the published policy takes precedence. A separate choices page takes its approved copy from the published copy set, and a legal document with no published version presents a notice directing readers to support@unsaidmemories.com.\n\nStandby text is a safety measure, not a substitute for a policy. It is general and does not describe the Platform's actual practices in full — marketplace sellers, telehealth, and franchises among them. The Legal function keeps a list of every place standby text is still being presented; each entry on that list is a policy the Company has not yet published in its own words. A notice that a document is being prepared is an honest statement for a short period; left in place indefinitely, it becomes a statement of a different kind.\n\n## Removing a policy\n\nRemoving a policy record removes its public page. Removal does not erase the version history, but the public link dies, and any document or enforcement action that referred to the policy loses its target. Supersede a policy with a new version in preference to removing it. Removal requires the Managing Member's approval and a note in the publication log stating what replaced the policy, or that nothing did.\n\n## The first paragraph\n\nThe index excerpt is the first paragraph of the body. Write the first paragraph of every policy as its summary; for most visitors it is the only part they read.\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nThis standard creates each published policy version, its change notes, the publication log, the list of places where standby text is presented, and rename records. Versions are kept in the policy store and its version history, permanently; the publication log and the standby-text list are kept by the Legal function with the corporate records under Record Retention (§3.12.6).\n\n## Escalation\n\nA conflict between a policy and the document that governs it, a change that a governing document relies on, a rename that would break an external reference, and any question about whether a change is material are escalated to the Legal function at legal@unsaidmemories.com before publication. Operational publication questions go to support@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Legal function reviews the published policy set, the standby-text list, and the publication log quarterly, and reviews this standard once a year.\n",
  "authority-and-governance": "\n# Authority and Governance\n\nThis standard records how authority is held and exercised in the Company: who decides, how a decision is delegated and recorded, the order in which the Company's documents govern when they appear to conflict, and how a document is made and changed. It creates no obligation toward a customer; where it touches one, the governing document cited controls.\n\n## The authority\n\n- **The Managing Member** of UnSaid Memories, LLC is the final authority on the Company's program rules, policies, and business decisions, and the only person who may bind the Company, except as the Managing Member delegates in a signed writing kept in the corporate book; see Entity Records and Good Standing (§3.12.1).\n- **The Legal function** states what the law requires, in the documents, as a matter of compliance fact: the applicable rule, the Company's duty under it, the control the Company maintains, and the record kept. Legal requirements are stated in that form; they are not presented as an approval step that a decision must pass.\n- **No approval by outside counsel is a condition of any Company action.** No workflow, policy, or document in this library requires a lawyer's sign-off before it takes effect.\n- **Delegation is express.** Staff, contractors, and agents act within the authority given to them in writing. No person binds the Company, changes a policy, or reverses an enforcement decision on that person's own authority.\n\n## The order of the documents\n\nWhen documents appear to conflict, the higher document controls and the lower document is corrected:\n\n1. Terms of Use (§2.1.1)\n2. Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1)\n3. The program rules for the audience concerned\n4. The policy for the subject concerned, in the relevant part of §2\n5. Help and guide content in §3, which explains the rules in practice and creates no obligation of its own\n\nEach part implements the part above it and does not contradict it. If a help article and a policy disagree, the policy controls and the article is corrected. If a subject policy and the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1) disagree, the Selling Terms control. The Terms of Use (§2.1.1) control over every other document.\n\nTwo working rules protect that order:\n\n- A defined term is defined once, in the Terms of Use (§2.1.1) or in the highest document that needs it, and is referred to thereafter; it is never redefined in a lower document.\n- A cross-reference points to the document that actually governs the point, by title and number, and states what that document governs.\n\n## Procedure: making and changing a governing document\n\n1. **Write the rule first, in full.** The document states who the parties are, what each must and must not do, who bears each risk, what happens on breach, how a decision may be appealed, and how the relationship ends. A rule that cannot be stated completely is not ready to be applied.\n2. **Check the order.** Confirm the new or changed document implements the document above it and contradicts nothing above it. Resolve any conflict by amending the lower document, or by a recorded decision of the Managing Member to amend the higher one.\n3. **Record the decision.** A decision adopting or materially changing a governing document is recorded in a signed, dated membership resolution in the corporate book; see Entity Records and Good Standing (§3.12.1).\n4. **Publish under version control.** Publication follows Policy Publication and Version Control (§3.12.8), with change notes that state what changed.\n5. **Build the operating record to match.** Where a rule requires a record, a check, or a step in the Company's systems, that mechanism is put in place as part of the same change. There is no obligation without a means of carrying it out, and no mechanism without a written rule behind it.\n\n## What this means for operators\n\n- The written rule is the rule. Do not work around a rule that appears wrong; escalate it.\n- Discretion exists only inside the range a document states. Where a document gives a range — for notice, for cure, for a consequence — act inside it and record the reason for the point chosen.\n- A question that no document answers is a question for the Managing Member, decided once and then written into the document concerned, so the next person in the same position finds an answer rather than a gap.\n- Formatting and presentation of a governing document may be corrected by the person publishing it. A change to substance is a change to the document and follows the procedure above, whoever first proposes it.\n\n## Records created and where they are kept\n\nThis standard creates signed delegations of authority, membership resolutions adopting or changing governing documents, and the record of each conflict resolved under the order above. All are kept in the corporate book by the Legal function; see Entity Records and Good Standing (§3.12.1) and Record Retention (§3.12.6).\n\n## Escalation\n\nA conflict between documents, a question no document answers, a request to act outside a stated range, and any question about who may bind the Company are escalated to the Legal function at legal@unsaidmemories.com, which puts the question to the Managing Member where a decision of that role is required. Operational questions go to support@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Review cadence\n\nThe Legal function reviews this standard and the delegations in force once a year, at the annual review described in Entity Records and Good Standing (§3.12.1), and whenever a governing document is adopted or materially changed.\n",
  "your-customer-portal": "\n# Your Customer Portal\n\nThis page covers how to sign in to your customer portal, what the dashboard shows, what the action-required panel means, and where each part of your account lives.\n\n## Signing in\n\nSign in at `/access-customer`. One sign-in opens the same account whether you came to shop, book a groom, or check a membership. Account registration and security are governed by the Account Registration and Security Policy (§2.1.27).\n\nEvery portal page requires a signed-in session. If you open a portal page signed out, or your session expires while you are away, the page shows a sign-in card with a button to `/access-customer`. Signing in returns you to the page you were trying to reach. Nothing is lost when a session expires; sign in again and your information is where you left it.\n\n> [!NOTICE]\n> Use the same email address everywhere. Your orders, appointments, pets, and memberships are all attached to your email address. If you check out with a different email from the one you sign in with, that order does not appear in your order history.\n\n## The dashboard\n\n`/customer/dashboard` is the page you land on after signing in. Four summary figures appear across the top:\n\n- **Total Spent** — the sum of your paid shop orders. Orders that are pending or cancelled do not count.\n- **Total Orders** — your lifetime order count.\n- **In Transit** — orders currently shipped or in transit.\n- **Latest Order** — the fulfillment status and date of your most recent order.\n\nBelow the figures are your three most recent orders, each with a tracking link where one exists, your upcoming grooming appointments, your pets, an account overview (name, email, phone, tax status, and credit limit), and your saved addresses. Each section links to its full page. For the complete record of any section, use the dedicated page rather than the dashboard summary.\n\n## The action-required panel\n\nIf you have an upcoming grooming appointment with something outstanding, the dashboard shows a panel above everything else with your dog's name, the service, and the date, followed by a numbered list of what remains: typically the pre-check-in questionnaire covering health, temperament, and vaccination status, any balance due, and the salon address for arrival. Each item has its own action button. The panel remains until everything is complete, then shows that you are all set. If you are unsure whether an appointment is fully prepared, check the dashboard first.\n\n## Where things live\n\n- **Orders** (`/customer/orders`) — order history, Buy Again, wish list, Bath Club subscriptions, Shipping Club membership, Perks, gift cards, and Reship.\n- **Pets** (`/customer/pets`) — your dogs' profiles and their records, including vaccinations and groom history, on one page.\n- **Profile** (`/customer/profile/...`) — address book, payment methods, and communication preferences.\n\nThe portal also has top-level pages for Dashboard, Appointments, Notifications, Receipts, Invoices, Support, Settings, Documents, Messages, and Learn. Appointments, documents, messages, health records, and learning are covered in their own guides. The articles in this section cover the commerce and account pages.\n\n## When something looks wrong\n\n- An empty message, such as \"No orders yet,\" \"No pets registered yet,\" or \"No saved cards yet,\" states the actual position. If you expected information — for example, an order placed last week — the usual cause is the email mismatch described above.\n- If a page cannot load your information because your session ended, you see the sign-in card rather than an indefinite loading state.\n- Support is available from every page at `/customer/support`. Support is a message inbox, not live chat. See Buyer Protection and Support (§3.2.16).\n\n## Related pages\n\n- Order History (§3.2.2), Order Tracking (§3.2.3), Buy Again (§3.2.4)\n- Joining the Bath Club (§3.2.7), Managing Your Bath Club Membership (§3.2.8), Shipping Club (§3.2.9), Perks (§3.2.10)\n- Your Pets (§3.2.12), Your Address Book (§3.2.13), Payment Methods (§3.2.14), Profile, Settings, and Preferences (§3.2.15)\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about your account or signing in: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "perks": "\n# Perks\n\nThis page covers Portal Perks: how points are earned, what they are worth, how they are spent, and how to check your balance and history at `/customer/orders/perks`.\n\nThis page summarizes the Loyalty Program Terms (§2.1.22), which governs.\n\n## How the program works\n\nYou earn 1 point for each $1 of qualifying spend, and 100 points are worth $1 off at checkout. There are no tiers and no status levels. The balance shown is calculated from your full points history rather than held as a separate figure, so the balance is always the sum of the entries beneath it.\n\nMembership is attached to a single account. Points are recorded on the member's account, have no cash value, and cannot be transferred between accounts, sold, or converted to cash. Points are redeemed at checkout; they cannot be redeemed for a gift card or applied to a purchase already made.\n\n## How you earn points\n\nPoints are posted on completed activity, not at the point of booking or ordering:\n\n- **Grooming visits** — points post when the visit is completed. Booking a visit earns nothing until the visit takes place.\n- **Shop orders** — points post when the order is paid and fulfilled.\n- **Bath Club payments** — each membership payment and renewal earns points, including the first payment, which counts once Stripe confirms it.\n\nPoints are earned on the merchandise subtotal, net of returns, refunds, and chargebacks. If a product on which points were earned is returned, the points for that product are removed or are not posted. A chargeback on a purchase reverses the points for that purchase. Tax, shipping, and the purchase of a gift card do not earn points.\n\n## Your history\n\nThe history lists every points entry, newest first, with its source: Shop order, Grooming visit, Bath Club, Redeemed, or Adjustment. Earned points show as positive entries and redemptions as negative entries. If a balance ever looks wrong, work from the history: find the entry that is missing or unexpected, rather than disputing the total. If support corrects a balance, the correction appears in the history labeled Adjustment, with a note. No correction is made silently.\n\n## How you spend points\n\nPoints are spent at checkout, at 100 points for $1. The perks page shows your balance and its dollar value — stated as what it is worth on your next booking — so you know what you hold before you reach checkout. Redemption happens at checkout, not in the portal.\n\n## Notes\n\n- **One balance per account.** Points sit on your sign-in email. Two email addresses mean two balances. See Order History (§3.2.2) for the same rule applied to orders.\n- **\"No points yet\" is a position, not an error.** Your first completed groom or fulfilled order starts your points history.\n- **Expiry.** Points expire as the Loyalty Program Terms (§2.1.22) and the program description on the perks page provide, and the page shows the expiry position for your points. If the program ever changes its expiry terms, the change is announced before it takes effect and is not applied to points already earned without that notice.\n- **Signed out,** the perks page shows a sign-in card instead of a balance. Points are private to the account.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about your points balance or an entry in your history: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "gift-cards": "\n# Gift Cards\n\nThis page covers the gift cards shown at `/customer/orders/gift-cards` — cards you have bought for others and cards you have received — how to read a card's balance, and what to know before you buy one.\n\nThis page summarizes the Gift Card Terms (§2.1.20), which governs.\n\n## How a card reaches your portal\n\nCards are matched to your account by email. A gift card appears in your portal when the recipient email on the card matches your sign-in email. Cards you have bought for other people also appear in your portal, labeled with the recipient's name, so you can check a remaining balance.\n\n## Reading a card\n\nEach card shows its current balance prominently, the card number beneath it, and then the details: the original value, the card's status, who the card is from or for, and any message the buyer wrote. The balance is the amount you can spend now. The original value is the amount the card started with. A card with a zero balance stays on record; it is not deleted, but it cannot buy anything. A part-used balance, such as $13.20 remaining on a $50 card, is normal.\n\n## If you received a card\n\n- In the salon, present the card number at checkout. The card itself carries this instruction: show the number at checkout in store.\n- A card is also redeemed by entering or presenting the card number at checkout online. The available balance is shown before the card is applied.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Treat a gift card number as cash. The value is held against the number, and a person who has the number can spend the value. A lost or stolen card is replaced only on proof of purchase, such as an order confirmation or receipt, and only the remaining balance is reissued on a new number. Without proof of purchase, no replacement is available.\n\nA gift card does not expire earlier than the law permits, and no dormancy or service fee reduces its balance except where the law permits the fee and the fee was disclosed when the card was issued. No cash redemption is available except where the law requires a remaining balance to be redeemed in cash; where it does, request it from support with the card number.\n\n## If you are buying a card\n\nBuy gift cards at `/gift-cards` on the site. Before you buy:\n\n1. Check the recipient's email address carefully. The card is issued to that address, and that is how it reaches the recipient's portal. A mistyped address leaves the card held under an address nobody signs in with.\n2. Write your message knowing it travels with the card and is shown in the recipient's portal.\n3. Keep your order confirmation. It is the proof of purchase needed if the card is ever lost.\n\n## Problems\n\n- **The recipient cannot see the card.** An email mismatch is almost always the cause. Compare the recipient email on the purchase with the email the recipient signs in with. Support can re-attach the card to the correct account.\n- **A card you paid for shows a non-active status.** A card that was never activated at purchase reads differently from a card that has been spent. This is a matter for support, not something that resolves by waiting.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about a gift card, a balance, or a replacement: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "your-pets": "\n# Your Pets\n\nThis page covers your pets page at `/customer/pets`: adding a pet, the records held on each pet's card, editing and removing a pet, and what to do when something goes wrong.\n\n## One list, used everywhere\n\nThe pet list on this page is the same list offered to you when you book an appointment, and the same list the salon sees before every visit. There are no separate copies. Add a dog here and it can be selected at booking straight away. Update a weight here and the salon sees the updated weight. Keep the information current, because it is working information, not a keepsake.\n\nHow your personal information is handled is governed by the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2).\n\n## Adding a pet\n\nAdd a pet with its name, breed, birthday, and weight. You do not choose a size tier: the tier is worked out from the weight. That tier is what the Bath Club (§3.2.7) and salon pricing use.\n\n> [!NOTICE]\n> Enter your dog's weight accurately. An understated weight puts your dog in the wrong tier and leads to a correction at check-in.\n\n## The record on each card\n\nEach pet's card combines the profile — name, breed, birthday, weight, and photo — with the record:\n\n- **Vaccination photos and notes.** Upload photographs of vaccination records, with notes. The salon requires proof of vaccination before grooming, and having it on file here is what makes check-in run smoothly. A clear photograph of a document your veterinarian emailed you is sufficient.\n- **Completed groom history.** Every completed groom appears on the pet's card with its date, the service, the groomer's notes, and the price. It serves as the pet's service record and your record of what you spent.\n\nNotes on a pet are for handling and care facts the groomer needs, such as nervousness around dryers or a sensitive paw. This page holds the working facts the salon needs — name, breed, age, weight, vaccination status, and groom history — and is not a clinical record.\n\n## Editing and removing a pet\n\nSelect the pencil control to edit a profile. Select the trash control to remove a pet. Removal asks you to confirm first.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Removing a pet removes its profile from your list. Grooms already completed remain in the salon's records; removing the pet does not remove that history.\n\n## Problems\n\n- **Your session expired while you were editing.** The page shows the sign-in pattern described in Your Customer Portal (§3.2.1), and unsaved edits are lost. On a long form, save early and often.\n- **A photo upload fails.** Check the file size and format; large phone photographs sometimes exceed the limits. The notes field saves independently of photographs, so record the facts first and retry the image afterward.\n- **The size tier looks wrong.** Correct the weight and the tier is recalculated. If the tier still looks wrong after the weight is corrected, message support.\n\nBooking preferences that sit alongside your pets — a preferred groomer or shampoo — are covered in Profile, Settings, and Preferences (§3.2.15).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about a pet record or a vaccination upload: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "your-address-book": "\n# Your Address Book\n\nThis page covers your address book at `/customer/profile/address-book`: adding an address, the three address types, setting your default address, and editing or deleting an address.\n\nYour address book is the same address book checkout reads. An address added here is available at checkout, and changing your default here changes the address checkout selects. There is one address book, not a separate copy for the portal and for checkout.\n\nHow your personal information is handled is governed by the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2).\n\n## Adding an address\n\nSelect Add address. Two fields are required: address line 1 and city. The form will not save without them. The remaining fields — line 2, company, state, ZIP code, and phone — are optional and worth completing: the phone number is what a carrier uses if a delivery fails, and the company line matters for deliveries to a workplace.\n\nChoose one of three address types:\n\n- **Shipping** — where orders are delivered.\n- **Billing** — the billing address for a card.\n- **Shipping and billing** — one address used for both. This is the usual choice for a home address.\n\nYour first address is marked as your default automatically. For a later address, check \"Use as my default address\" if you want it to become the default.\n\n## The default address\n\nExactly one address is the default, and checkout selects it in advance. Select the star control on any other address to make it the default immediately, with no form to complete. The default is also the address shown in the dashboard's address summary. Keep it current when you move: orders shipped to an out-of-date default address are a common cause of the problem covered in Order Tracking (§3.2.3).\n\n## Editing and deleting\n\nSelect the pencil control to open an address for editing, and the trash control to delete it. Two rules apply:\n\n- **You must keep at least one address.** Deleting your last address is refused. Checkout requires a delivery address, so add a replacement first and then delete the old address.\n- **Editing an address does not change past orders.** Orders already placed keep the address they were shipped to. The address book governs future orders.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Deleting an address cannot be undone from this page. Check the address type and the default marker before you delete, especially if the address is the billing address paired with a saved card. See Payment Methods (§3.2.14).\n\n## Problems\n\n- **\"Address line 1 and city are required.\"** Complete both fields and save again.\n- **A deletion is refused.** Either the address is your last one, so a replacement must be added first, or your session expired during the action, so sign in again.\n- **The address saved, but checkout shows the old one.** Checkout reads this same address book, so this is usually a page that has not been refreshed. Refresh checkout. If the old address still appears, check the type shown on the address card; the address may have been saved under a different type from the one you intended.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about your addresses or a delivery address on an order: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "payment-methods": "\n# Payment Methods\n\nThis page covers your saved cards at `/customer/profile/payment-methods`: how a card gets saved, how to set your default card, how to remove a card, and what each message on the page means.\n\nPayment is governed by the Payment Terms (§2.1.16). This page summarizes that document, which governs.\n\n## Where your card details are held\n\nYour full card number is held by Stripe, not by this site. The portal holds references only: the card brand, the last four digits, the expiry, and which card is your default. Listing your cards, setting a default, and removing a card are all carried out through Stripe. No one at the shop can see your full card number from this page, because the page never receives it.\n\n## How a card gets saved\n\nThere is no form on this page for adding a card. A card is saved at the point of payment — at salon checkout, shop checkout, or booking — when you choose to save it during that payment. If the page says \"No saved cards yet,\" you have not yet saved a card during a payment.\n\n## Setting a default and removing a card\n\n- **Set default.** The default card is selected in advance at checkout and booking. Any other card carries a Set default action; the default card carries a Default marker instead.\n- **Remove.** Select the trash control to remove a card. The reference is removed immediately, at Stripe as well as in the portal.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Removing a card takes effect immediately and cannot be undone. It affects future charges only: a payment already authorized is not affected. To use the card again, save it again at your next payment. Removing the reference does not affect the card account itself.\n\n## Reading the list\n\nEach row shows the brand — Visa, Mastercard, Amex, or Discover — the last four digits, and the expiry month and year. If the expiry reads \"on file with Stripe,\" Stripe holds that detail and the portal is showing what it was given; the card still works.\n\n## Problems\n\n- **\"We couldn't load your cards.\"** The connection to the payment provider failed. Retry. The page also has a set message for this position, \"Card management isn't available right now\": you can still pay at checkout as usual, and only managing saved cards is paused.\n- **You removed a card by mistake.** Save it again at your next payment. There is no restore, and no harm is done: you removed the saved reference, not the card.\n- **Your default card was declined at checkout.** The default is a convenience, not a guarantee; an expired or replaced card declines. Pay with the new card and choose to save it, then set it as default here.\n\n## Detaching everything\n\nSigning out of all devices ends your sessions, but it does not detach your saved cards at Stripe. To remove everything, sign out of all devices as Profile, Settings, and Preferences (§3.2.15) describes, and remove each card on this page.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about a saved card or a payment problem: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "profile-settings-and-preferences": "\n# Profile, Settings, and Preferences\n\nThis page covers three related surfaces: your profile and security settings at `/customer/settings`, and your communication and booking preferences at `/customer/profile/communication-preferences`. It covers what you can change yourself, what only support can change, and the four notification switches.\n\nHow your personal information is handled is governed by the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2). Consent to electronic and text communications is governed by the Electronic Communications Consent (§2.1.9) and the Text Message Program Terms (§2.1.10).\n\n## Your profile\n\nThe Profile section of Settings edits your name and phone number.\n\nYour email address is shown but cannot be edited. Your email is your sign-in identity: every order, pet, and membership is attached to it. See Your Customer Portal (§3.2.1). Changing an email address is not an edit but a transfer of your records, so it is done by support. Do not create a second account under a new email to work around this; doing so splits your history across two addresses.\n\n> [!NOTICE]\n> Contact support if you need your email address changed, and use the address you sign in with until the change is made.\n\nWhen you save your name, the full name you enter is divided into a first name and a last name. \"Mary Kay Smith\" is stored as first name \"Mary\" and last name \"Kay Smith.\" If your name does not divide that way, tell support rather than working against the form.\n\n\"Saved\" appears only after a change is confirmed. If a save fails, the error is shown in the form; the form does not report a success that did not happen.\n\n## Notification switches\n\nSettings and Communication Preferences show the same four switches, held on your customer record:\n\n- **Appointment reminders by email** — confirmations, reminders, and receipts for bookings.\n- **Appointment reminders by text** — the same reminders by text message.\n- **Promotions and offers by email** — sales, new products, and salon offers.\n- **Promotions by text** — occasional offers by text message.\n\nThe first two are about your own bookings and orders. Turning them off means no reminders, which can mean a missed appointment. The second two are marketing, and are optional. The starting position — appointment reminders by email on, the other three off — reflects that division.\n\nYour switch positions are the record of your consent for text messages. If you receive a promotional text you did not opt into, raise it with support; your switch history is the evidence.\n\n## Booking preferences\n\nThe second part of Communication Preferences holds the preferences used when you book and when the salon suggests times:\n\n- **Preferred groomer** — choose from the salon's staff list, or No preference.\n- **Preferred days** — select the days that work for you, Monday to Sunday.\n- **Preferred time window** — a start and an end time.\n- **Preferred shampoo** — free text, such as oatmeal or hypoallergenic.\n- **Preferred cut style** — free text, such as a teddy bear cut, or short in summer.\n\nThese are preferences, not guarantees. The salon works to them, but a fully booked day remains fully booked. The preferences are held on your customer record and are read by the booking process.\n\n## Security\n\nThe Security section of Settings is short by design:\n\n- **Password changes** are made through the account recovery process on the sign-in page, not through a form in the portal. Password reset belongs with the sign-in system.\n- **Sign out of all devices** ends every session you are signed into, on every device. Use it if you lose a device, use a shared computer, or want a clean start.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Signing out of all devices ends your sessions only. It does not remove your saved cards. To detach your cards as well, remove each one as Payment Methods (§3.2.14) describes.\n\n## Problems\n\n- **Preferences will not save.** The error is shown by the save control. The usual cause is a session that expired while you were editing: sign in again and retry. Your switch positions remain in the form, so nothing is lost.\n- **Texts are not arriving, or are still arriving.** Check the phone number on your profile first. Carriers filter text messages in both directions. If the number is correct, message support; the send record can be checked.\n- **A booking preference seems to be ignored.** Preferences are advisory. If one is never followed — for example, you are always assigned a different groomer — raise it when you book or through support. The preference recorded for you may not be what you believe it is.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about your profile, your email address, or your preferences: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "buyer-protection-and-support": "\n# Buyer Protection and Support\n\nThis page covers what to do when something goes wrong: the protection that applies to your orders, the exact steps for raising a problem order, and the portal pages that support that process — notifications, receipts, invoices, and the support inbox.\n\nThis page summarizes the Buyer Protection Program (§2.1.43), which governs, and the Buyer Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.2.4), which governs.\n\n## What is protected\n\n- You receive what you paid for, or your money back. Non-delivery after payment, a product that materially differs from its listing, and a charge made without your authorization are violations, not inconveniences.\n- The published returns policy is binding once stated. A failure to honor it is itself a violation of policy.\n- Recurring billing is disclosed before you commit. Every subscription surface states the amount, the billing interval, and how to cancel before you pay. Enrolling a person in recurring payments without clear disclosure of that kind is a prohibited practice. See the disclosures in Joining the Bath Club (§3.2.7) and Shipping Club (§3.2.9).\n\nIf practice ever departs from these points, that departure is the support case to raise.\n\n## Raising a problem order\n\nThere is no self-service returns button in the portal. Returns, refunds, and problem orders are handled by the shop team through your support thread, because the right remedy — a replacement shipment, a refund, or a partial credit — depends on the circumstances. Follow these steps:\n\n1. Check the order first at `/customer/orders`. See Order History (§3.2.2). Confirm the status, the delivery address, and the tracking number. Many problem orders resolve at this step: an order shipped to an old default address, covered in Your Address Book (§3.2.13), or a label created but not yet scanned, covered in Order Tracking (§3.2.3).\n2. Open `/customer/support` and write to the shop team. Keep one thread per matter; the team reads the same thread on their side. Include your order number — the eight-character code on the order — what happened, and what you want: a replacement, a refund, or an explanation.\n3. Wait for the reply in the same thread. Support is a message inbox, not live chat: there is no indicator that an agent is online and no response-time promise. The team reads every message, and a notification appears in your portal when they reply.\n\nRefunds and disputed charges are processed by the shop team against Stripe. Your thread is where the matter is opened and recorded.\n\n## The four common cases\n\n- **Non-delivery.** The order was paid for and never arrived, and tracking is absent or has stopped. Check the address and the carrier position first, as Order Tracking (§3.2.3) describes, then message support. The remedies are a replacement shipment or a refund; where both are possible, the choice is yours.\n- **Wrong or damaged item.** A photograph helps a great deal; attach one to the thread or have one ready. Do not dispose of the item until support confirms, because it may need to be returned.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Keep a wrong or damaged item, and its packaging, until support tells you it is no longer needed. Disposing of it first can remove the evidence a refund or replacement depends on. Where the error is the shop's, return shipping is the shop's cost, not yours.\n\n- **A charge you do not recognize.** Check first whether it is a Bath Club renewal, covered in Managing Your Bath Club Membership (§3.2.8), a Shipping Club renewal, covered in Shipping Club (§3.2.9), or a points-adjusted order total. Your order history and receipts show what each charge was for. If none of these explains it, message support promptly, and check your saved cards in Payment Methods (§3.2.14) for anything you do not recognize.\n- **A return because you changed your mind or ordered the wrong item.** Message support with the order number. Eligibility, time limits, and condition requirements are set by the Buyer Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.2.4); the portal applies that policy rather than restating it. Food and consumables are treated differently from durable goods, and the policy is the authority. Disputes that cannot be resolved with support are governed by the Buyer Dispute and Escalation Policy (§2.2.5) and the Dispute Resolution Policy (§2.1.36).\n\n## Notifications\n\n`/customer/notifications` holds the in-portal copy of every customer-facing email the shop sends: appointment confirmations and reminders, invoice copies, payment receipts, shop order updates, subscription events, account notices, and staff replies to your support thread. If an email did not reach your inbox, its copy is here. Notifications are grouped by type — Appointments, Invoices, Orders, Subscriptions, Messages — with a count for each, and grouped by Today, Yesterday, and Earlier. Unread items are marked New. Open an item to mark it read, or use Mark all read. The bell at the top of the portal shows your unread count.\n\n## Receipts and deposits\n\n`/customer/receipts` covers what you paid at the salon and at the register. Shop orders are in Order History (§3.2.2), not here.\n\n- **Receipts** — salon and register receipts, with receipt number, date, amount, tip, payment method, and status.\n- **Deposits** — appointment deposits, with their position stated in plain words: Held, meaning the deposit is held for your appointment and comes off your total; Applied; Released; Refunded; and Forfeited. Forfeited is the position to ask about, because it means the deposit did not come back to you.\n\n## Invoices\n\n`/customer/invoices` shows your invoices: number, status, total, balance due, amount paid, and due date. Statuses are Paid, Partially paid, and Unpaid, with a total balance across all open invoices. The page is read-only: there is no online invoice payment route, and no payment button is shown for a route that does not exist. The page states where payment is made, and paid invoices lead to Receipts. To settle an open balance, message support or pay at the salon.\n\n## Help Center and contacting the shop\n\nThe support page also links to the Help Center, where guides and answers are published, and sets out how to contact the shop directly by phone, email, or in person at the salon.\n\n## When to wait and when to write again\n\n- **Wait** where a tracking label has been created but not yet scanned (allow 12–24 hours), where a subscription payment is still being confirmed after Stripe checkout, and during the first business day after you open a support thread.\n- **Write again in the same thread** where no reply has arrived in 2 business days; where a refund was agreed but has not appeared in 5–10 business days, because a refund being issued and a refund appearing on a statement are different events and card refunds take that long through the banking system; or where a deposit is in the wrong position.\n- **Contact the shop directly** for anything time-sensitive: an appointment today, a charge you do not recognize, or a delivery that has arrived damaged and needs to be recorded at once.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about an order, a return, a refund, or a charge: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices and formal disputes: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "order-history": "\n# Order History\n\nThis page covers what your order history at `/customer/orders` shows, how to read the summary figures and the two status badges on each order, and what to do if an order you placed does not appear.\n\n## The summary figures\n\nFour figures sit above the order list, calculated from your order history:\n\n- **Total Orders** — every order on your account, whatever its status.\n- **Items Purchased** — the total quantity of items across all orders.\n- **Total Spent** — the sum of subtotals on paid orders only. Pending, cancelled, and refunded orders are excluded, so this figure is money that was actually paid.\n- **Open Orders** — orders that are neither cancelled nor paid. If this number surprises you, check the payment badge on your orders; it is usually an order awaiting payment.\n\n## Reading an order\n\nEach order shows an order number — the first eight characters of the order ID, in capitals, such as Order #A3F9C2E1 — the date placed, and whether the order is for shipping or store pickup. Two badges appear on the right:\n\n- **Order status** — the fulfillment state, such as awaiting payment, shipped, in transit, delivered, cancelled, or refunded.\n- **Payment status** — such as paid or unpaid. This is shown separately from fulfillment on purpose: an order can be paid and still awaiting shipment, or shipped while a refund is being processed.\n\nThe body of each order shows the items, quantities, the price per item, and the total, together with the delivery method and the shipping address. For a shipped order, the footer carries the tracking number and the carrier name. See Order Tracking (§3.2.3).\n\nThe prices shown are the prices you paid, held as they were at purchase. If a catalog price has changed since, your history is not rewritten. Buy Again (§3.2.4), by contrast, prices items from the current catalog.\n\n## If an order does not appear\n\nThe page reads your orders each time you visit, so there is nothing to refresh manually. If you have ordered and the page shows no orders, the cause is one of the following:\n\n1. **Email mismatch.** Orders attach to the email address used at checkout. If you checked out with a different address from your portal sign-in — for example a variation of the same address — the system treats them as different customers. Sign in with the checkout email, or contact support to have the records joined.\n2. **Checkout under a different email.** The same root cause: the order exists, but it is not attached to the sign-in you used. The remedy is the same.\n\n## What order history does not show\n\nGrooming appointments are not orders. Salon visits appear under Appointments and Pets, and what you paid at the salon appears under Receipts. See Buyer Protection and Support (§3.2.16).\n\nReturns and refunds are governed by the Buyer Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.2.4). This page summarizes that document, which governs. How to raise a problem order is covered in Buyer Protection and Support (§3.2.16).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about an order or a missing order: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "order-tracking": "\n# Order Tracking\n\nThis page covers where to find your tracking number, which carriers link directly to a tracking page, how to read your order's fulfillment status, and what to do when tracking looks wrong.\n\nDelivery itself is governed by the Shipping and Delivery Policy (§2.1.18). This page summarizes that document, which governs.\n\n## Finding your tracking number\n\nWhen your order ships, the tracking number appears in two places: in the footer of the order on `/customer/orders`, and in the Recent Orders section of your dashboard. On the dashboard, where the carrier is a known carrier, the tracking number is a link to that carrier's tracking page.\n\nThe portal links out for four carriers:\n\n- **USPS** — the USPS tracking page, with your label number already entered.\n- **UPS** — the UPS tracking page.\n- **FedEx** — the FedEx tracking page.\n- **DHL** — the DHL tracking page.\n\nFor any other carrier, including a regional carrier or a supplier's own delivery service, the tracking number is shown as plain text rather than a link. Copy the number and enter it on the carrier's own site.\n\n## Reading fulfillment status\n\nThe status on each order states where the order is:\n\n- **Pending or processing** — the order is confirmed but has not left yet.\n- **Shipped or in transit** — the order is moving. This is when the tracking number becomes useful.\n- **Delivered** — the carrier reports delivery.\n- **Cancelled or returned** — the order is not coming.\n\nEach order carries two statuses, one for fulfillment and one for payment. See Order History (§3.2.2). A delivered fulfillment status alongside an unusual payment status usually means a refund or adjustment is in progress, not that the package is lost.\n\n## When tracking looks wrong\n\n- **No tracking number yet on a recent order.** The number appears when the shipment is created, not at checkout. Items fulfilled by a supplier can take longer, because the supplier creates the label and the tracking is passed back when they do.\n- **The carrier's site says the number is not found.** Labels are created before the carrier's first scan. Allow 12–24 hours. Until the first scan, the carrier has no record of the label.\n- **The status has not moved for days.** The portal reports what the carrier reports; it does not create its own scans. If the carrier's own site shows the same position, message support from `/customer/support` and include your order number.\n- **Delivered, but you do not have the package.** Check the shipping address on the order first; a package is sometimes delivered to an old default address. Then check with household members and look for the carrier's delivery photo or proof of delivery. Then contact support.\n\n## Pickup orders\n\nPickup orders have no tracking number, because there is nothing to track. The order shows Store pickup as the delivery method and moves through its own statuses. To check whether a pickup order is ready, use the status on the order and your notifications. See Buyer Protection and Support (§3.2.16).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about a shipment or a missing package: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "buy-again": "\n# Buy Again\n\nThis page covers how Buy Again at `/customer/orders/buy-again` builds your reorder list, why the prices shown are today's prices, and the three states a product on the list can be in.\n\n## How the list is built\n\nBuy Again reads your full order history and collects every distinct product you have bought, most recent first, with repeats shown once. Each product appears as a card with an Add to Bag button. Adding from here puts the item in the same bag the shop uses, so you can add items here and continue shopping in the shop with everything in one bag at checkout. The button shows a brief Added confirmation so you can see that the tap registered.\n\n## Prices are today's prices\n\nEvery product is priced from the current catalog when the page loads, not from the price recorded on your old order. If the price has risen since you last bought the item, you see the new price before you add it to your bag.\n\nYour order history keeps showing what you actually paid. Buy Again shows what you would pay now. See Order History (§3.2.2).\n\n## The three states of a product\n\nEach card is checked against the current catalog:\n\n1. **Available and in stock.** A normal card: current price, product image, an Add to Bag button, and a link to the product page. Where a genuine comparison price exists — a real earlier price or an approved promotion — the struck-through price appears beside the current price.\n2. **Available but out of stock.** The card shows the product marked out of stock, and Add to Bag is disabled. The card remains visible so you can decide whether to wait or choose a substitute.\n3. **No longer in the shop.** The card shows the product name from your order history with a note that it is no longer available in the shop. The product was discontinued or removed from sale. It is shown rather than silently dropped, so you can see what happened to it.\n\n## Notes\n\n- If you have never bought anything, the page shows an empty message and points you to the shop. It is not an error.\n- A product bought several times appears once. Its position is set by your most recent purchase of it.\n- For products you have saved but not yet bought, use your Wish List (§3.2.5). For scheduled repeat shipments of eligible consumables, see Reship (§3.2.6).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about reordering or a product that is no longer available: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "wish-list": "\n# Wish List\n\nThis page covers how your wish list works, where it is stored, how prices on it are set, and how it differs from Buy Again.\n\n## Saving products\n\nSelect the heart on any product in the shop to save it to your wish list. Your list is at `/customer/orders/wish-list`. Each saved product shows its current price and image, with an Add to Bag button, in the same card form as Buy Again (§3.2.4).\n\n> [!NOTICE]\n> Your wish list is saved per device, in the same place the shop's heart buttons save to. Products you save on your phone do not appear on your laptop. This is how the list is stored, not a failure to update: if your list looks empty on a new device, check the device you saved the products on.\n\n## Prices are current prices\n\nEach product on the list is checked against the current catalog when the page loads. Prices are current, not the price from when you saved the item. Each product is in one of three states:\n\n- **In stock** — price, image, Add to Bag, and a link to the product page.\n- **Out of stock** — marked out of stock, with Add to Bag disabled.\n- **No longer in the shop** — shown with a note that the product is no longer available in the shop, together with a remove control. Unavailable products are not silently removed from your list.\n\n## Removing a product\n\nSelect the X in the corner of a card to remove that product from your wish list. Removal takes effect immediately, with no confirmation step.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Removal cannot be undone from the list. To restore a product, find it in the shop and select the heart again.\n\n## Wish list and Buy Again\n\nThe two pages look alike and answer different questions:\n\n- **Wish List** — products you have saved and may never have bought. You build the list yourself, and it is held per device.\n- **Buy Again** — products you have actually purchased, drawn from your order history and attached to your account.\n\nUse the wish list for products you want to consider later, and Buy Again for restocking products you already buy.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about your wish list: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "reship": "\n# Reship\n\nThis page covers the Reship subscription program: which products can be placed on a recurring shipment, what you are told before the first charge, the discounts that apply, and how to pause, skip, or cancel.\n\nThis page summarizes the Reship Subscription Terms (§2.1.23), which governs.\n\n## What Reship is\n\nReship is a subscription arrangement for the periodic shipment of an eligible product at a quantity and frequency you select. Each billing creates the renewal order for that cycle, and shipments continue at that frequency until you pause or cancel. Your subscriptions are managed from `/customer/orders/reship` and from your account's subscription controls.\n\n## Which products are eligible\n\nEligibility is decided by category. Eligible products are consumables, including food, treats, supplements, litter, and grooming consumables. Durable products, including beds, crates, toys, and equipment, are not eligible and are not offered for subscription. The product page states eligibility before enrollment. A product with no subscription option on its page is not eligible, and support cannot make an ineligible product eligible.\n\n## Before the first charge\n\nEnrollment is not complete until the full disclosure has been presented and you have confirmed enrollment. Before the first charge, the enrollment confirmation states:\n\n- The product and the quantity.\n- The frequency of shipment.\n- The price of the first order, including any introductory discount.\n- The recurring price that applies after any introductory period.\n- The date of the first charge and the frequency of later billing.\n- That charges recur at the chosen frequency until you pause or cancel.\n- How to pause, skip, hold, and cancel.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> A Reship subscription charges your stored payment method at each interval until you pause or cancel it. Charges are recurring. Cancelling stops future charges and shipments; it does not refund an order that has already shipped. A shipped order is a completed purchase, and returning it is a separate action under the Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.1.19).\n\n## Discounts\n\nThe rates in effect are 35% off the first Reship order and 5% off each recurring Reship order. If a price, or a discount, is to increase for a renewal, you are notified before that renewal, with the current price, the new price, and the renewal date from which the new price applies, in time for you to cancel before that renewal is charged. No increase applies to an order already charged.\n\nBefore each renewal, a renewal notice states the renewal date, the product and quantity, the exact amount to be charged, and the controls to skip, hold, pause, or cancel before that date.\n\n## Pause, skip, hold, and cancel\n\nYou may pause, skip, hold, or cancel at any time before the next billing date, from your account, without contacting support.\n\n1. **Skip** omits one cycle only. The subscription stays active, the next cycle is billed at the following scheduled date, and subscription benefits, including free shipping where it applies, are not lost because of a skip.\n2. **Pause** suspends shipments and charges for the duration of the pause, and suspends subscription benefits for that duration. You may resume a paused subscription from your account at any time; the resume date and next billing date are stated before charges resume.\n3. **Cancel** stops all future charges and shipments from the time the cancellation is confirmed, where cancellation occurs before the next billing date. There are no cancellation fees, no minimum commitment period, and no final-shipment charge.\n\n## Product changes and problems\n\nIf a subscribed product is discontinued, materially reformulated, or becomes ineligible, you are notified before the next renewal and given the options available: a substitute product, which is supplied only if you approve it, or cancellation.\n\nIf a charge appears that you do not recognize, check your Bath Club membership in Managing Your Bath Club Membership (§3.2.8) and your Shipping Club membership in Shipping Club (§3.2.9), which are also recurring, before raising the charge. How to raise a charge is covered in Buyer Protection and Support (§3.2.16). For one-off restocking without a subscription, use Buy Again (§3.2.4).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about a Reship subscription or a recurring charge: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "joining-the-bath-club": "\n# Joining the Bath Club\n\nThis page covers joining the PAWfection Bath Club: choosing a size tier, monthly and annual billing, the one-dog-per-membership rule and the multi-pet discount, and the full billing terms stated before you pay.\n\nThe Bath Club is a grooming membership. It is a different program from the Shipping Club (§3.2.9), with different billing and different benefits. The one overlap is that Bath Club membership includes free shipping on shop orders, so there is no need to hold both memberships. Subscriptions in general are governed by the Reship Subscription Terms (§2.1.23) where they apply; the membership terms presented at signup govern your Bath Club membership.\n\n## What membership covers\n\nMembership covers up to 4 baths a month for one dog. You still book each visit; membership covers the baths, it does not book them. Unused visits in a month do not carry over to the next month.\n\n## Before you join\n\n- Add your dog in Your Pets (§3.2.12) first. Signup asks you to choose which dog the membership is for, from your registered pets. Without a registered pet, there is nothing to choose.\n- Know your dog's weight. Tiers are priced by dog size, and the size tier is derived from the weight on your dog's record.\n\n## Choosing a tier\n\nTiers are Small, Medium, Large, and X-Large, each with a weight range shown on the tier card. Choose the tier that matches your dog. The weight on your dog's record is the figure the salon works from, so keep it accurate.\n\nThe X-Large tier has no listed price. It is quoted individually, because very large dogs vary enough that a single price does not fit. To join on the X-Large tier, contact the salon through support.\n\n## Monthly and annual billing\n\n- **Monthly** — you pay each month, and you may pause or cancel as Managing Your Bath Club Membership (§3.2.8) describes.\n- **Annual prepay** — 12 months for the price of 10, charged in full at signup. The annual total is calculated from the tier's monthly price and is shown before you commit.\n\n## One dog per membership\n\nEach membership covers one dog. For two dogs, you need two memberships. The second membership, and each additional membership in the same household, receives a multi-pet discount of 10%, shown at signup.\n\n## Promotion codes\n\nThe signup form includes a promotion code field. A code is checked before you pay: if a code does not apply to the Bath Club, you are told before payment, not after. An applied discount is reflected at Stripe checkout, so the amount shown is the amount charged. Promotions are governed by the Promotional Offers Terms (§2.1.21).\n\n## The billing terms\n\nRead these terms before you select Start:\n\n- **This is a recurring charge.** Monthly membership is charged every month; annual membership is charged once a year. Both renew automatically until you cancel. The amount is the tier price you selected, with any promotion discount applied. The exact figure is shown on the button before you select it.\n- **Payment is taken by Stripe on the salon's behalf.** The charge on your statement refers to UnSaid Memories. Your card details are held by Stripe; the portal does not receive them.\n- **Cancelling stops future charges, not past ones.** Cancellation takes effect at the end of your current billing period. You keep your baths until then. There are no refunds for a part-used month or year.\n- **Pausing is not cancelling.** Pausing suspends the membership — no charges and no baths — and resuming restarts it. Cancellation at period end ends it. The two actions are separate. See Managing Your Bath Club Membership (§3.2.8).\n- **A change of plan is billed from the next period.** If you change tier, the new price starts at the next billing period. The current period is unchanged, and no part-period adjustment is made.\n- **A failed payment puts the membership past due.** If your card is declined at renewal, the membership goes past due and baths stop until billing is current. Update the card and the membership recovers. No late fee is charged by the portal.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Joining starts a recurring charge that renews automatically until you pause or cancel it. The tier price shown at signup, with any promotion discount applied, is charged each period. Cancellation takes effect at the end of the period you have paid for, and there are no refunds for a part-used period.\n\nTwo further points stated at signup: standard baths are covered, and heavy matting or work well beyond a standard bath may carry an additional charge by the salon; and an appointment is required for each visit.\n\n## How signup and payment work\n\n1. On `/customer/orders/subscriptions`, choose the tier, the billing interval, the dog, and any promotion code.\n2. Select Start. The portal creates a Stripe subscription checkout and sends you to Stripe's payment page.\n3. Pay on Stripe's hosted page.\n4. Stripe returns you to the portal's success page.\n5. The portal checks with Stripe whether the payment went through. Only then is the membership activated and your signup perk points added. See Perks (§3.2.10).\n\nIf you close the page after paying, or the return step fails, the membership is not lost. Each time you open the subscriptions page, a pending membership is checked against Stripe and activated once payment is confirmed. If payment is still settling, the page says so. If you back out of Stripe checkout before paying, nothing is charged and the page tells you so.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about joining, a tier, or a membership charge: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "managing-your-bath-club-membership": "\n# Managing Your Bath Club Membership\n\nThis page covers managing a PAWfection Bath Club membership from `/customer/orders/subscriptions`: reading the membership summary, pausing and resuming, cancelling, changing plan, and what happens when a payment fails. Joining, including the billing terms you accepted at signup, is covered in Joining the Bath Club (§3.2.7).\n\n## Your membership summary\n\nThe page shows your plan name, size tier, the dog covered, your price per month or per year, the baths used against the baths included in the current period (for example 2 of 4), your renewal date, and a status: Active, Paused, or Payment pending, for a membership whose payment has not yet been confirmed. If you have scheduled a cancellation, the summary shows that the membership ends at period end.\n\n## Pause and resume\n\n1. To pause, select Pause on the membership. Pausing takes effect immediately: no charges are made while the membership is paused, and no baths are available.\n2. To resume, select Resume on the paused membership. The membership becomes active again.\n\nPausing suits a break — travel, or a dog recovering from an operation — because it holds the membership without ending it.\n\n## Cancel\n\nThe control is labeled Cancel at period end, and that wording is exact.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Cancellation takes effect when your current billing period ends, not when you select it. You keep every remaining bath through the renewal date. There are no refunds for a part-used month or year: a period you have paid for remains yours to use.\n\nIf you change your mind before the period ends, select Keep it going. That control withdraws the cancellation. A scheduled cancellation can be withdrawn at any time before the period ends.\n\n## Change plan\n\nSelect Change plan to open the tier choices and the monthly and annual billing choices. Three rules apply to every change:\n\n1. The new price is taken from the plan itself. The price shown in the preview is the price you will pay.\n2. The new price starts at the next billing period. The period you are in is finished at the old price, and no part-period adjustment is made in either direction.\n3. Your membership record is updated only after the change is confirmed with Stripe. If the update fails, nothing changes and nothing is charged, and you see an error message.\n\nThe multi-pet discount from signup does not carry into a change of plan; it is a discount on additional memberships taken out at signup. Check the preview figures before you confirm, because the preview is the final word on the new price.\n\nThe X-Large tier cannot be selected through a change of plan, because it is quoted individually. To move to the X-Large tier, contact the salon through support.\n\nOnly an active or paused membership can change plan. A membership with a cancellation scheduled must first be restored with Keep it going. Changing to the plan you are already on is not permitted, and the confirm control is disabled in that case.\n\n## Failed payments\n\nIf your card is declined at renewal, the membership goes past due and baths stop until billing is current. Update the card on file and the membership reactivates, with no need to join again and no loss of history. No late fee is charged by the portal, and there are no baths during a grace period, because there is no grace period for bathing while payment is outstanding.\n\n## Errors you may see\n\nYou can manage only your own memberships; access is checked against your sign-in email. A \"Membership not found\" message on a membership you expect to see usually means the sign-in email and the membership email differ. See Your Customer Portal (§3.2.1).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about a membership, a renewal, or a billing problem: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "shipping-club": "\n# Shipping Club\n\nThis page covers the Free Shipping Club: what the membership does, which products it applies to, what it costs, and how to join and cancel. The pricing model it works within — shipping included in every product price — is explained in Shipping, Taxes, and the Shipping Club (§3.1.5).\n\nThe Shipping Club is a separate program from the PAWfection Bath Club (§3.2.7), which is a grooming membership with different billing and different benefits.\n\n## How the benefit works\n\nShipping is included in every shop price, so there is no shipping charge to remove. Instead, while your membership is active, the shipping portion included in each eligible item's price is credited back at checkout. The credit is applied automatically: there is no code to enter and no setting to switch on. If the membership is active, the credit applies; if it has ended, it does not.\n\n## What it covers\n\nThe credit applies to consumables: food, treats, supplements, flea and tick products, and litter. It does not apply to beds, toys, and other durable products, which are bought at the listed price by members and non-members alike. For a buyer who orders consumables regularly, one membership is covered by roughly two orders. If you mostly buy beds and toys, this membership gives you nothing.\n\n## Price and joining\n\nThe membership costs $9.99 per month or $99 per year. Both prices are shown on the `/free-shipping` page. Join from that page:\n\n1. Sign in. An account is required; the membership cannot be taken out without one.\n2. Choose monthly or annual billing and continue to Stripe checkout.\n3. Pay on Stripe's hosted page. On success you are returned to the `/free-shipping` page.\n\nIf you are already a member, you are told so instead of being charged for a second membership. One membership per customer is permitted.\n\n## The billing terms\n\n- **This is a recurring charge** that renews automatically, monthly or annually according to your choice, and is charged to your card through Stripe until you cancel.\n- **Cancel at any time from your customer portal.** The benefit continues through the end of the period you have paid for: cancel mid-month and the credit still applies until the renewal date.\n- **No refunds for a part-used period.**\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Joining starts a recurring charge that renews automatically until you cancel it. Cancellation takes effect at the end of the paid period, and there are no refunds for a part-used period.\n\n## Do not hold both memberships\n\nPAWfection Bath Club members already receive free shipping as part of that membership. Joining the Shipping Club as well buys nothing further. If you have joined both by mistake, cancel the Shipping Club from your portal and message support about the overlap. See Buyer Protection and Support (§3.2.16).\n\n## Problems\n\n- **Membership active, but no credit at checkout.** Check that you are signed in with the same email address that holds the membership. The credit is attached to your account email. See Your Customer Portal (§3.2.1).\n- **Cancelled, but the credit still applies.** The benefit runs to the end of the paid period by design. It stops at the renewal date.\n- **Monthly or annual.** The annual price is roughly two months less than paying monthly for a year. Choose according to how steadily you order consumables.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about the Shipping Club or a membership charge: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "seller-center-overview": "\n# Seller Center Overview\n\nThis page explains how you enter Seller Center, how the portal identifies your seller account, and where each area of the portal is found. Seller Center is where you manage your listings, orders, shipping, returns, payments, and account settings for selling on UnSaid Memories.\n\n## Entering Seller Center\n\nSeller Center is not open to the public, and there is no self-service sign-up on the storefront. A platform administrator creates your seller account first; you cannot create one yourself.\n\n1. Go to the seller sign-in page at `/access-seller`.\n2. Enter the email address and password for your seller account. The sign-in form offers the email and password path only.\n3. If the email address has no seller account, the page tells you that no seller account was found for that address and that seller accounts are created by an administrator.\n\nYour seller sign-in is separate from your storefront customer sign-in. Every seller page checks your seller session, and a customer session does not carry over into Seller Center.\n\n## How the portal identifies you\n\nAfter you sign in, the portal matches the email address you signed in with against your seller record. That match controls everything you see:\n\n- If the two email addresses match, the portal shows your listings, orders, and account data.\n- If they do not match, pages come back empty, with no error message. An empty dashboard with no error is most often an email mismatch. Contact your administrator with both email addresses so the record can be corrected.\n\nYour seller record holds your legal name, display name, public page address, email, phone, website, seller type, commission rate, and approval status. Your display name and page address are the identity buyers see on the storefront, where your products are shown as \"Sold by {your seller name}.\"\n\n## What is inside\n\nAll seller areas sit under `/seller/`:\n\n| Area | Route | What it is for |\n|---|---|---|\n| Dashboard | `/seller/dashboard` | Your daily landing page; it leads to the `/seller` overview |\n| Onboarding | `/seller/onboarding/*` | The eight-step application, covered in Seller Onboarding in Eight Steps (§3.3.2) |\n| Catalog | `/seller/catalog/*` | Listings, variants, media, quality, suppression, imports, bundles, and categories |\n| Products | `/seller/products` | An alternate product management view |\n| Inventory | `/seller/inventory/*` | Stock, movements, replenishment, low stock, and Fulfillment by Portal Shop inbound shipments |\n| Orders | `/seller/orders/*` | Order inbox, unshipped, late, and cancelled orders, bulk acknowledge and ship, and pause operations |\n| Payments | `/seller/payments/*` | Payment overview, wallet, payouts, methods, fees, statements, and tax documents |\n| Pricing | `/seller/pricing/*` | Bulk price editor, AutoPrice, buy box, discounts, and promotions |\n| Shipping | `/seller/shipping/*` | Shipping templates, labels, carriers, delivery promises, return address, and exceptions |\n| Returns | `/seller/returns/*` | Returns overview, requests, rules, refunds, disputes, and insights |\n| Performance | `/seller/performance/*` | Performance overview, late shipment, item-not-received rate, negative feedback, return rate, carrier, and regional views |\n| Communication | `/seller/communication/*` | Inbox, notifications, forum, and support |\n| Advertising | `/seller/advertising` | Paid placement tools |\n| Analytics | `/seller/analytics/*` | Sales, traffic, conversion, search insights, reports, and the success hub |\n| Governance | `/seller/governance/*` | Policies, violations, appeals, and the audit log |\n| Account | `/seller/account/*` | Profile, company information, contact, users, security, notifications, policy acceptances, and health and compliance |\n| Settings | `/seller/settings` | Shop-level settings |\n| Docs | `/seller/docs` | Documentation inside the portal |\n\n## The agreement behind the portal\n\nEverything in Seller Center operates under the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1), including the term under which the Company collects buyer payments as your limited payment collection agent. The performance standards measured in the Performance area are stated in the Service Level Requirements (§2.3.2), and enforcement for sellers is stated in the Seller Enforcement Policy (§2.3.3). This page summarizes the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1), which governs.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about access to Seller Center or your seller account: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "shipping-templates-labels-and-delivery-promises": "\n# Shipping Templates, Labels and Delivery Promises\n\nThis page explains how shipping works in Seller Center: reusable templates, buying and printing labels, delivery promises, carriers, your return address, and delivery exceptions. This page summarizes the Shipping Policy (§2.3.54), which governs.\n\n## Templates are the system\n\nShipping (`/seller/shipping`) opens on Shipping templates (`/seller/shipping/templates`), because shipping on this platform is template-first. A shipping template is a reusable preset of rates and services that you attach to listings: create it once, configure it, and apply it across your catalog. Setting shipping per listing by hand lets a catalog drift out of sync; a small set of templates keeps it consistent.\n\nTo build your template set:\n\n1. Identify your real shipping profiles — for example small parcel ground, small parcel expedited, and oversized.\n2. Create one template per profile, with the rates and services that profile actually uses.\n3. Name each template so its purpose is plain (\"Ground under 5 lbs\" is clearer than \"Template 1\").\n4. Attach the correct template to each listing, and audit the attachments quarterly.\n\n## Labels\n\nShipping → Labels (`/seller/shipping/labels`) is where you buy and print shipping labels. The label is the moment an order becomes a parcel, and three mistakes cost real money:\n\n1. **Wrong weight.** An under-declared weight is caught by the carrier and billed back as an adjustment, usually at a worse rate than declaring correctly. Weigh the packed parcel, not the product.\n2. **Wrong service.** The service on the label must match the delivery promise the buyer saw. Downgrading the service after purchase to save money breaks that promise.\n3. **Label created, parcel never handed over.** A label is not a shipment. Until the carrier scans the parcel, the order is unshipped and the late-shipment clock is running (see Orders, Acknowledgment and Shipping (§3.3.9)).\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Buying a label charges postage for the service and weight you select. Confirm the address, service, and packed weight before you buy; a wrong label must be voided and replaced, and an under-declared weight is billed back by the carrier as an adjustment.\n\n## Delivery promises\n\nShipping → Delivery promises (`/seller/shipping/delivery-promises`) shows what the buyer is told at checkout. The promise is computed from your handling time plus the transit time of the service. Set your handling time honestly and the promises follow; set it aspirationally and the metrics expose the gap (see Performance and Account Health (§3.3.14)).\n\n## Carriers, return address, and exceptions\n\n- **Carriers** (`/seller/shipping/carriers`) — your carrier accounts and service selections.\n- **Return address** (`/seller/shipping/return-address`) — where returns and undeliverable parcels go. Set it deliberately and update it the week you move; a stale return address sends returned parcels somewhere you cannot retrieve them.\n- **Exceptions** (`/seller/shipping/exceptions`) — delivery exceptions on your shipments: address problems, failed delivery attempts, parcels held in transit. Check this view daily. An exception caught on day one is a reshipment; an exception caught on day seven is a chargeback.\n- **Performance** (`/seller/shipping/performance`) — your shipping metrics: on-time rate, valid tracking rate, and exception rate. Review it weekly.\n\n## Shipping under Fulfillment by Portal Shop\n\nIf you use Fulfillment by Portal Shop, the Company handles the parcel, but you remain responsible for getting accurate, well-packed inventory to the warehouse (see Inventory Management (§3.3.8)) and for the delivery promise the buyer sees. The program changes who executes the shipment, not who is accountable for the promise. The program is governed by the Fulfillment by Portal Shop — Program Terms (§2.3.47).\n\n## If something goes wrong\n\n- **Template attached to the wrong listings:** audit which listings use which template, correct the attachments, and re-check after any bulk catalog change.\n- **Delivery promise missed:** determine whether the cause was handling time (your operations — fix the input) or transit time (the carrier — consider a different service).\n- **Return address out of date:** update it immediately and check Exceptions for parcels already routed to the old address.\n- **Exception ignored:** the buyer notices before you do. Work the Exceptions view daily.\n\nRelated documents: the Tracking Policy (§2.3.55), the Packaging Policy (§2.3.56), and the fulfillment and shipping obligations in the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about templates, labels, or a delivery exception: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "returns-refunds-and-disputes": "\n# Returns, Refunds and Disputes\n\nThis page explains the Returns area of Seller Center — requests, rules, refunds, disputes, and insights — how refunds are accounted for, and how chargebacks reach your balance. This page summarizes the Return and Refund Policy — Sellers (§2.3.6), which governs.\n\n## The returns hub\n\nReturns → Overview (`/seller/returns/overview`) shows four numbers: open requests, refund requests, open disputes, and active rules. A rising count in any of the first three is an early warning; act on it before it becomes a performance problem (see Performance and Account Health (§3.3.14)).\n\nThe sub-pages:\n\n- **Requests** (`/seller/returns/requests`) — incoming return requests, for you to review and act on. Work them daily, oldest first: a return request left unactioned becomes negative feedback, then a metric, then an enforcement matter.\n- **Rules** (`/seller/returns/rules`) — your return rules: Keep It rules, replacement rules, and exemption rules. A Keep It rule (the buyer keeps the item and you refund, for low-value items where return shipping exceeds the item's value) saves money and handling for both sides. Exemption rules define what cannot come back. Set rules deliberately, then honor them as published — a published rule you do not follow destroys buyer trust.\n- **Refunds** (`/seller/returns/refunds`) — every refund issued. This is your audit trail; reconcile it against your payouts monthly (see Payments, Wallet and Payouts (§3.3.12)).\n- **Disputes** (`/seller/returns/disputes`) — chargebacks and payment disputes, covered below.\n- **Insights** (`/seller/returns/insights`) — why buyers return your products. Return reasons cluster, and a cluster is a product problem you can fix: sizing, description accuracy, packaging, or quality.\n\n## How returns work for a seller who wants to keep selling\n\nThe return terms you accepted at onboarding are both a compliance obligation and a business practice. Fast, straightforward returns cost money per transaction and retain the customer over time. Know the edges of the policy as well: exemption rules exist for a reason (final-sale items, and opened consumables where safety requires it), and abuse exists too. A buyer returning a fifth \"defective\" item in a month is a pattern — document it and raise it with support rather than confronting the buyer.\n\n## Disputes and chargebacks\n\nA dispute means the buyer went to their bank instead of to you. Under the limited payment collection agent term in the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1), the Company may recover the disputed amount from you — by delaying or withholding payouts, by reversing a transfer against your payment balance, and by enforcing a reserve. That is the Chargeback Policy (Sellers) (§2.3.21) operating as written.\n\nYour defense is documentation, provided on time, every time:\n\n1. Ship on time.\n2. Provide valid, carrier-scanned tracking.\n3. Describe products accurately.\n4. Handle return requests promptly — a slow refund is one of the most common triggers of a chargeback.\n\nAn \"item not received\" claim supported by valid tracking and delivery confirmation is defensible at the bank; without tracking, it usually is not. An unanswered dispute is a conceded dispute.\n\n## Refund mechanics\n\nRefunds come out of your money, and the wallet and payout system accounts for them (see Payments, Wallet and Payouts (§3.3.12)). Issue a refund promptly when one is owed.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> A refund, once issued, is paid to the buyer and deducted from your balance; it cannot be recalled because you later change your mind. Confirm the order, the amount, and the return's status before you issue it. A fast, correct refund costs the refund; a slow one can cost the refund, a chargeback fee, and your dispute rate.\n\n## If something goes wrong\n\n- **Return requests piling up unactioned:** triage daily, oldest first.\n- **A dispute you did not see coming:** check whether a return request or a buyer message preceded it; there is almost always a warning that was missed.\n- **Refund issued but the buyer says it has not arrived:** refunds take days to post to the buyer's statement. Give the buyer the date the refund was issued and the typical posting period before treating it as a problem.\n- **A return pattern that looks like fraud:** document every instance with dates and details, then raise it with support. Do not confront the buyer. Fraud matters are governed by the Fraud and Abuse — Zero Tolerance (§2.3.22) and the Payment Fraud Prevention (§2.3.23).\n\nThe platform-wide rule for buyers is stated in the Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.1.19).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about a return, a refund, or a dispute: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Chargeback and other legal notices, and appeals: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "payments-wallet-and-payouts": "\n# Payments, Wallet and Payouts\n\nThis page explains how money moves for your sales, the Payments area of Seller Center — overview, wallet, payouts, and methods — the hold that applies to new sellers, and how the Company recovers disputed amounts. This page summarizes the Payment Holds and Reserves (§2.3.18), which governs, together with the payment terms of the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1).\n\n## How money moves\n\nThe platform collects payment through its payment processor using destination charges. The buyer pays the platform; the platform's fee is taken at charge time; your share is routed to your connected payment account. Funds flow from the buyer, through the processor, to you. The processor performs seller identity verification for payment purposes and issues your Form 1099-K (see Business Verification and Tax Identity (§3.3.3)).\n\nThe legal basis is the limited payment collection agent term you accepted in the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1):\n\n1. You appoint the Company as your limited payment collection agent.\n2. The buyer's payment to the Company satisfies the buyer's obligation to you.\n3. The Company may delay or withhold payouts, reverse transfers to recover amounts owed, and enforce reserves.\n4. You bear losses from disputes, chargebacks, or fraud that you originated, as that document provides.\n\n## The Payments area\n\n**Payments → Overview** (`/seller/payments/overview`) shows three figures:\n\n- **Available balance** — ready to pay out to your payout method.\n- **Pending balance** — yours, but still within its processing and settlement period.\n- **Lifetime earnings** — total since you started selling.\n\n**Wallet** (`/seller/payments/wallet`) is the transaction ledger: every sale, fee, refund, and adjustment in one place. When a number does not look right, the wallet is where you find out why. Reconcile it monthly against your own books; the wallet is the Company's record and your books are yours, and the two should agree.\n\n**Payouts** (`/seller/payments/payouts`) lists money sent from your wallet to your payout method, with date, amount, status, and destination method. Payouts are issued when your wallet reaches the payout threshold, on the scheduled cycle, and settle on a rolling delay of 7–14 days. The delay is the dispute window made mechanical: buyers have time to report problems before money leaves the processor's system, which is why a recovery can usually be taken from a balance that still holds the funds.\n\n**Payment methods** (`/seller/payments/methods`) is how you receive payouts. At least one payout method is required. The page offers Credit Card, ACH / Bank Account, and PayPal. ACH is the standard method; funds typically arrive 1–3 business days after a payout is issued. Keep a backup method on file — a payout sent to a closed bank account is a delay you do not need.\n\n## New sellers: the hold\n\nDuring your first 90 days of active selling, amounts collected for your orders are held for 21 days after delivery confirmation, and a rolling reserve of 10% of each disbursement is retained against future disputes and refunds. The hold is risk management for an account with no track record, not a penalty. On graduation to the standard schedule, disbursement is at delivery plus 7 days, with any reserve set under the Payment Holds and Reserves (§2.3.18). The full terms are stated in the New Seller Payment Hold (§2.3.16). The fastest way through the introductory period is consistent performance: on-time shipping, low dispute rates, and completed orders.\n\n## When the Company takes money back\n\nIf a buyer wins a dispute or files a chargeback, the Company recovers the amount from your payment balance by transfer reversal — and, if the balance does not cover it, from your linked bank account. If dispute or fraud activity spikes, or disputes go unanswered, the Company may restrict your payout capability while keeping funds available for reversals and refunds. The recovery and hold terms are stated in the Final Settlement and Payout Policy (§2.3.17) and the Chargeback Policy (Sellers) (§2.3.21).\n\n## Statements and tax documents\n\n**Statements** (`/seller/payments/statements`) is where you download monthly statements, and **Tax docs** (`/seller/payments/tax-docs`) holds your Form 1099-K and related documents. The processor issues the Form 1099-K using the tax identity you provided at onboarding; if that identity is wrong, correct it before year-end, not after the form is issued. Statements, fees, and reconciliation are covered in Fees and Statements (§3.3.13).\n\n## If something goes wrong\n\n- **Payout delayed:** check whether the introductory hold applies, whether a reserve is being retained, and whether any dispute is holding funds; then check that your payout method is valid.\n- **Payout failed:** usually a closed or changed bank account. Update the method; the payout is retried on the cycle.\n- **Wallet does not match your books:** reconcile line by line. Fees, refunds, adjustments, and the rolling payout delay all create legitimate timing differences. If a line is genuinely wrong, raise it with support with the specific transaction identified.\n- **Negative balance:** disputes and refunds exceeded incoming funds. The Company recovers the balance from future payouts; you do not need to send money, but you do need to stop the cause.\n\nFees are governed by the Fee Terms (§2.3.13), the Seller Fee Schedule (§2.3.14), and the Referral Fee Schedule (§2.3.15); adjustments by the Fee Reversals and Adjustments (§2.3.19); and statements by the Statement and Reconciliation (§2.3.20).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about a payout, a hold, or a wallet entry: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "fees-and-statements": "\n# Fees and Statements\n\nThis page explains the fee categories that can apply to your account, the rule that a fee exists only if the fee schedule states it, and how to use your monthly statements and tax documents. This page summarizes the Seller Fee Schedule (§2.3.14), which governs. A fee is payable only at the amount or rate stated in that schedule.\n\n## The fee categories\n\nPayments → Fees (`/seller/payments/fees`) lists the five fee types that can apply to your account:\n\n1. **Referral fees** — a percentage of the item price, varying by product category, charged on each sale at settlement. Category rates are stated in the Referral Fee Schedule (§2.3.15).\n2. **Closing fees** — a fixed fee per item sold, in addition to the referral fee for most categories.\n3. **Fulfillment fees** — per-unit packing and shipping fees when you use Fulfillment by Portal Shop.\n4. **Storage fees** — monthly fees for inventory held in Portal Shop fulfillment centers.\n5. **Refund and return fees** — fees for processing customer refunds and returns.\n\nThe amount or rate of each fee is the amount or rate stated in the Seller Fee Schedule (§2.3.14). Do not model your margins on an assumed rate; read the rate for your category in the schedule.\n\n## The exclusivity rule — your protection\n\nThe Fee Terms (§2.3.13) and the Seller Fee Schedule (§2.3.14) state the exclusivity rule: all fees are set exclusively in the fee schedule; no fee exists until it is stated there; and where the schedule names a fee but states no amount or rate for it, the amount of that fee is zero and it is not chargeable until an amount or rate is stated in the schedule. If a charge appears that is not in the schedule, you do not owe it — raise it with support and cite the schedule. Fee changes follow the amendment and re-acceptance terms of the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1).\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> A price change you make, and a category you place a listing in, can change the fees a sale carries, because referral fees vary by category. Check the schedule for the category before you reprice or recategorize a listing; the fee that applies is the scheduled fee for the sale as made.\n\n## Statements\n\nPayments → Statements (`/seller/payments/statements`) is where you download your monthly statements. Each statement is the full accounting for the period: gross sales, referral and closing fees, fulfillment and storage fees where applicable, refunds and their fees, adjustments, reserves held and released, and net payout.\n\nDownload and keep every statement. Statements are your records for taxes, for any disagreement with the Company about a period, and for understanding your actual margin. Reconciliation discipline is simple: your statements, your wallet (see Payments, Wallet and Payouts (§3.3.12)), and your own books should agree. When they do not, work from the statement — it is the Company's record of what happened — and reconcile line by line. Statement and reconciliation terms are stated in the Statement and Reconciliation (§2.3.20), and corrections to fees are governed by the Fee Reversals and Adjustments (§2.3.19).\n\n## Tax documents\n\nPayments → Tax docs (`/seller/payments/tax-docs`) holds your Form 1099-K and related forms. The payment processor issues the Form 1099-K as the payment processor; the Company does not prepare tax forms itself. The identity on the form comes from your onboarding tax identity (see Business Verification and Tax Identity (§3.3.3)), which is governed by the Tax Identity and 1099 Reporting Policy (§2.3.10). Download your forms when they are issued and give them to your accountant. Do not reconstruct your Form 1099-K income from bank deposits: timing and amounts differ, by design of the payout delay.\n\n## If something goes wrong\n\n- **A fee on a statement you do not recognize:** check the fee schedule first, then the wallet transaction detail. If the fee is not in the schedule, raise it with support and cite the exclusivity rule.\n- **Statement does not match your books:** look for timing differences first — the rolling payout delay, refunds posted in a different period, and reserves held versus released — then reconcile line by line before treating a line as an error.\n- **A tax document is missing:** check that your tax identity is complete and current. A form cannot be issued against an incomplete identity.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about a fee, a statement, or a tax document: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices and fee disputes under the Dispute Standards (§2.3.4): legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "performance-and-account-health": "\n# Performance and Account Health\n\nThis page explains the performance metrics Seller Center reports, where each metric is found, the standards the metrics are measured against, and how performance connects to enforcement. This page summarizes the Performance and Account Health Policy (§2.3.62), which governs.\n\n## What performance measures\n\nPerformance → Overview (`/seller/performance/overview`) reports your account health and the metrics below, each with its own detail page. The overview also shows your account standing, including whether any violation is active on your account.\n\n- **Account health** — your overall standing: the summary of everything below.\n- **Return rate** (`/seller/performance/return-rate`) — the share of your orders returned by buyers. A high return rate points to a product problem: description accuracy, sizing, quality, or packaging.\n- **Item-not-received rate** (`/seller/performance/inr-rate`) — item-not-received claims against shipped orders. In practice this is a shipping and tracking metric: valid, carrier-scanned tracking is the defense.\n- **Negative feedback** (`/seller/performance/negative-feedback`) — 1–2 star ratings in the last 90 days.\n- **Late shipment rate** (`/seller/performance/late-shipment`) — orders shipped after the promised date. This is the metric most directly under your control, and the one that feeds the others.\n\nCarrier (`/seller/performance/carrier`) and regional (`/seller/performance/regional`) views let you divide the same data by carrier and by region.\n\n## The standards\n\nThe Service Level Requirements (§2.3.2) set the numeric bars the metrics are measured against, including late shipment rate, valid tracking rate, order defect rate, cancellation rate, and response time. Those figures are stated in that document; know the numbers that apply to you. They are thresholds, not aspirations: sustained performance beyond a threshold leads to the measures described in the Seller Enforcement Policy (§2.3.3).\n\nThe highest-leverage practice for your performance is also the simplest: ship on time, with valid tracking, for a product described accurately. A large share of performance problems reduces to those three.\n\n## Reading your metrics like an operator\n\nRead your metrics regularly, comparatively, and with attention to what changed:\n\n- **Late shipment climbing:** decide whether it is a handling time problem (your operations) or a carrier problem (your carrier choice). The fixes are different.\n- **Return rate climbing on one product:** a product problem. Read the return reasons in Returns → Insights (see Returns, Refunds and Disputes (§3.3.11)) and fix the listing or the product.\n- **Item-not-received rate climbing:** a tracking problem. Confirm that every shipment carries carrier-scanned tracking, as the Tracking Policy (§2.3.55) requires.\n- **Negative feedback clustering:** read the feedback itself. It usually names something specific — slow shipping, product not as described, poor packaging, or unanswered messages.\n\n## The enforcement connection\n\nPerformance is not advisory. Under the Seller Enforcement Policy (§2.3.3), enforcement proceeds in ordinary steps — investigation, notice, an opportunity to cure where the violation can be cured, then measures proportionate to the violation, which may include listing suppression or restriction, a payout hold, suspension, or termination. Severe violations, such as counterfeit goods, fraud, or a safety risk, may proceed directly to a serious measure. You are told where you stand at each step, and a measure may be appealed under the Appeals Policy (§2.3.5) (see Policies, Violations and Appeals (§3.3.17)). \"I did not know the standard\" is not a ground available to you: the standards are published, and you accepted them at onboarding.\n\n## If something goes wrong\n\n- **Account standing shows good standing but a listing is suppressed:** suppression is a listing-level measure and standing is account-level. Check `/seller/catalog/suppression` for the listing and `/seller/governance/violations` for the account.\n- **A metric spikes:** find the cause before the cause reaches you as a notice. A spike has a reason, and a reason usually has a fix.\n- **A metric looks wrong:** compare it against your orders, tracking records, and return records for the same period, and raise a specific discrepancy — period, orders, and records — with support.\n\nPerformance terms are also stated in the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about a metric or your account standing: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Appeals and legal notices: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "brand-tools-and-advertising": "\n# Brand Tools and Advertising\n\nThis page explains the two growth surfaces available to you in Seller Center — Advertising, which is paid placement, and the Success Hub, which provides recommended actions for your shop — and the analytics that should inform both. Advertising spend is money out of your account, so read the cost warnings before you start a campaign.\n\n## Advertising\n\nAdvertising (`/seller/advertising`) is paid product placement: you pay for visibility, and the return is measured in incremental sales at your margin after advertising cost. Work in this order:\n\n1. **Advertise products that convert.** Check your conversion data first (`/seller/analytics/conversion`). Paying to show a listing that rarely converts buys more non-buyers. Fix the listing first (see Listing Content Standards (§3.3.5)), then advertise it.\n2. **Know your unit economics.** Work out your margin after fees (see Fees and Statements (§3.3.13)) and your cost per sale before you set a budget. If the cost of acquiring a sale exceeds the margin on that sale, the campaign loses money no matter how well it is targeted.\n3. **Start narrow, then expand.** A few proven products, measured carefully, outperform a catalog-wide campaign whose results you cannot attribute.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Advertising charges accrue as your placement runs. Set a budget and a review date before you launch, and check spend against sales while the campaign is live — not after it ends.\n\n## The Success Hub\n\nAnalytics → Success Hub (`/seller/analytics/success-hub`) is the platform's recommended-action surface for your shop. The tools there are:\n\n- **AutoPrice** (`success-hub/AutoPrice`) — automated repricing recommendations.\n- **Match buy box** (`success-hub/match-buy-box`) — what it would take to win the buy box on your contested listings.\n- **Match price** (`success-hub/match-price`) — price competitiveness recommendations.\n- **Flash deals** (`success-hub/flash-deals`) — participation in deal events.\n- **Fulfill with Portal** (`success-hub/fulfill-with-portal`) — enrollment recommendations for Fulfillment by Portal Shop.\n- **AI edit** (`success-hub/genai-edit`) — AI-assisted improvement of listing content.\n- **Customer favorites** (`success-hub/customer-favorites`) — featured placement.\n- **Restock** (`success-hub/restock-seller-fulfilled`, `success-hub/restock-wfs`) — replenishment prompts for seller-fulfilled and Fulfillment by Portal Shop inventory.\n- **Review incentives** (`success-hub/review-incentives`) — post-purchase review requests.\n- **SEM** (`success-hub/sem`) — search engine marketing guidance.\n\nTreat every recommendation as a lead, not an instruction. The hub knows patterns across the platform; it does not know your margins, your supplier constraints, or your strategy. Check each recommendation against your own numbers before you act on it.\n\nTwo cautions apply to specific tools:\n\n- **AI edit** produces suggested content, not a finished listing. Review every word before it goes live: generated copy can be fluent and still wrong about your product's specifics, and you are accountable for every claim on your listing (see Listing Content Standards (§3.3.5)). Disclosure duties for automated content are stated in the Seller Automation Disclosure Policy (§2.3.66).\n- **Review incentives** must stay within the Product Review Policy for Sellers (§2.3.35): requests must be neutral, sent to all buyers, never conditioned on a positive review, and never paid.\n\n## Analytics that feed decisions\n\nThe wider Analytics area (`/seller/analytics/*`) — sales, traffic, conversion, search insights, inventory insights, pricing insights, and reports — is your market intelligence. Review it monthly at a minimum, and weekly if advertising or pricing decisions depend on it.\n\n- **Search insights** (`/seller/analytics/search-insights`) — what buyers search for. A gap between searches and your assortment is a product opportunity.\n- **Conversion** (`/seller/analytics/conversion`) — where your funnel loses buyers. Traffic without conversion is a listing problem.\n- **Reports** (`/seller/analytics/reports`) — exportable data for your own analysis.\n\n## If something goes wrong\n\n- **Advertising spend with no return:** the listing did not convert before you paid to promote it. Pause the spend, fix conversion, then restart narrowly.\n- **A Success Hub recommendation that would hurt your margin:** the hub optimizes for platform patterns, not your profit. Verify every recommendation against your own figures before acting.\n- **AI-assisted content containing a false claim:** the violation is yours, not the tool's. Review all generated content before publication.\n\nRelated documents: the Seller Pricing Policy (§2.3.36), the Promotions and Coupons Policy (§2.3.37), and the Fulfillment by Portal Shop — Program Terms (§2.3.47).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about advertising charges or a Success Hub tool: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "seller-center-troubleshooting": "\n# Seller Center Troubleshooting\n\nThis page works through the problems sellers report most often in Seller Center — an empty portal after sign-in, a listing that is not on the storefront, a missing order, a late payout, a bulk action that completed in part, a policy warning, and a portal fault — in the order to check each one.\n\n## \"I signed in but everything is empty\"\n\nNo error and no data — a blank dashboard, no orders, no products — is almost always the seller identity match. The portal matches your sign-in email against your seller record; if they do not match, every page comes back empty. Check:\n\n1. Are you signed in with the same email address that is on your seller record?\n2. Did your administrator create the account under a different email address — for example, a personal address instead of your work address?\n\nCorrecting the record is an administrator fix: contact your administrator with both email addresses. Less commonly, your session has expired; sign out and back in at `/access-seller`. See Seller Center Overview (§3.3.1).\n\n## \"My listing is not on the storefront\"\n\nWork this checklist in order:\n\n1. **Is it still in pending review?** Check Catalog → Listings and read the status badge. New listings go into the review queue (see Creating Listings (§3.3.4)); submitted does not mean live.\n2. **Was it suppressed?** Check Catalog → Suppression for the reason. Common causes are a missing or lapsed brand authorization (see Brand Gating and Authorizations (§3.3.6)) and a content quality return (see Listing Content Standards (§3.3.5)).\n3. **Is it a gated brand?** If so, confirm you hold an approved authorization whose validity window includes today.\n4. **Did you finish and save it?** In particular, check express listings whose details were left to be completed later.\n\nDo not relist a suppressed listing without fixing the cause; relisting without fixing it is a separate violation under the Seller Enforcement Policy (§2.3.3).\n\n## \"An order is missing from my inbox\"\n\nThe inbox shows 100 orders per view, newest first. At volume, older orders sit behind that limit — use the status filter to narrow the view. Also confirm the order belongs to you: orders are matched to your seller account, and in a multi-seller order each seller sees only their own lines. See Orders, Acknowledgment and Shipping (§3.3.9).\n\n## \"My payout is late or missing\"\n\nCheck in this order:\n\n1. Does the new seller hold apply to the funds? (See Payments, Wallet and Payouts (§3.3.12) and the New Seller Payment Hold (§2.3.16).)\n2. Is a rolling reserve retaining a percentage of the disbursement?\n3. Is a dispute holding funds?\n4. Is your payout method valid? Bank accounts close and cards expire.\n5. Did your wallet reach the payout threshold?\n\nThe Payouts page shows the status of each payout; \"scheduled\" is not \"sent.\"\n\n## \"A bulk action half-worked\"\n\nBulk acknowledge and bulk ship can complete for some orders and fail for others. Before you retry, check which orders actually changed state — the order detail pages are the record. Retrying without checking can double-acknowledge an order or double-ship it, and a double shipment is a real second parcel with real postage.\n\n## \"I received a policy warning, a suppression, or a restriction\"\n\nDo not ignore it, and do not relist around it. Follow these steps:\n\n1. Read the notice. It cites the policy and the specific issue.\n2. Fix the underlying cause. The notice is about a condition, not only an incident.\n3. Check where the measure sits under the Seller Enforcement Policy (§2.3.3), so you know what follows if the condition continues.\n4. Appeal if you have grounds, through Governance → Appeals, with evidence (see Policies, Violations and Appeals (§3.3.17)). \"I disagree\" is not a ground; \"the finding is factually wrong, and here is the proof\" is.\n\n## \"Something in the portal is broken\"\n\nIf a page errors or a control does nothing:\n\n1. Note the exact page, what you selected, and what happened. Take a screenshot if you can.\n2. Report it through Communication → Support (`/seller/communication/support`) with those specifics.\n\nA specific report is resolved faster than a general one: \"the payouts page loads indefinitely after I select a payout, starting Tuesday\" can be investigated; \"the payouts page is broken\" cannot, without further exchanges.\n\nFurther help: Seller Center Overview (§3.3.1) for sign-in and session behavior, Policies, Violations and Appeals (§3.3.17) for appeals, and Account Settings and Security (§3.3.18) for support channels. Appeals procedure is governed by the Appeals Policy (§2.3.5).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Report a portal fault or ask about any problem above: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Appeals and legal notices: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "policies-violations-and-appeals": "\n# Policies, Violations and Appeals\n\nThis page explains the Governance area of Seller Center — where your policies are listed, where violations are recorded, how to file an appeal and within what period, and where the audit log is kept. This page summarizes the Appeals Policy (§2.3.5), which governs appeal procedure, and the Seller Enforcement Policy (§2.3.3), which governs the measures appealed.\n\n## Policies\n\nGovernance → Policies (`/seller/governance/policies`) lists the published marketplace policies that apply to your account, with an acceptance action on each policy that requires your acceptance. The policies that govern you include the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1), the Service Level Requirements (§2.3.2), the Seller Enforcement Policy (§2.3.3), the Dispute Standards (§2.3.4), the Appeals Policy (§2.3.5), the Return and Refund Policy — Sellers (§2.3.6), and the Seller Program Terms (§2.3.64).\n\nYour record of acceptance — including the six policies you accepted at onboarding — is held under Account → Policy acceptances (`/seller/account/policy-acceptances`). That record is what is relied on if a question arises about whether you agreed to a rule. Read the policies that touch your daily operations — fulfillment, listings, and returns — before you need them in a dispute.\n\n## Violations\n\nGovernance → Violations (`/seller/governance/violations`) lists the violations recorded against your account, each with its severity and status, and shows your account standing. When no violation is active, the page records that your account is in good standing.\n\nEnforcement under the Seller Enforcement Policy (§2.3.3) proceeds in ordinary steps: investigation, notice, an opportunity to cure where the violation is capable of cure, and then a measure proportionate to the violation — which may include listing suppression or restriction, a payout hold, suspension, or termination. A severe violation, such as counterfeit goods, fraud, or a safety risk, may proceed directly to a serious measure. Each violation is recorded with the measure applied, any cure period, and the appeal path, so you can see where you stand at every step.\n\n## Appeals\n\nGovernance → Appeals (`/seller/governance/appeals`) is where you file an appeal. The form asks for the violation being appealed and the reason for the appeal, and the appeal is reviewed by a person who did not make the original decision.\n\nFile your appeal as follows:\n\n1. Identify the measure you are appealing — the finding, suppression, hold, suspension, or other measure recorded on your account.\n2. State your grounds: a material error of fact, a material error in how the policy was applied, remediation you have completed, evidence that was incomplete or incorrect, a procedural irregularity, or a measure that exceeds what the finding permits.\n3. Attach the evidence specified for the violation class. For authenticity and sourcing violations, that means supplier invoices meeting the requirements of the Invoice Evidence for Appeals (§2.3.63) — itemized, dated, identifying the supplier and the purchaser, sufficient in quantity, and unaltered — together with proof of payment, and brand authorization where the brand is gated.\n4. Submit the appeal, and keep your own copy of the appeal and the evidence, with dates.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> You must file an appeal within 30 calendar days after the measure. A late appeal may be denied as untimely, and completing remediation does not extend the period — file the appeal and remediate in parallel. Filing an appeal does not pause the measure: a suppression, a payout hold, and a suspension remain in effect during review unless the Company directs otherwise in writing.\n\nThe Company acknowledges an appeal within 2 business days, stating who will review it, the decision period, and whether the measure is stayed. A decision is issued within 14 calendar days after filing, or within 21 calendar days where the appeal is supported by invoice evidence, to allow time to verify supplier documents. The Company's decision on an appeal is final within the marketplace. Dispute resolution after the appeal process is governed by the Dispute Resolution Policy (§2.1.36), and enforcement appeals at platform level are addressed in the Enforcement and Appeals Policy (§2.1.37).\n\nKeep your invoices in the shape the Invoice Evidence for Appeals (§2.3.63) requires from the day you start selling. An appeal that depends on sourcing evidence cannot be repaired at appeal time if the underlying documents were never kept.\n\n## Audit log\n\nGovernance → Audit log (`/seller/governance/audit-log`) is the record of actions on your account — enforcement measures, appeals, and policy acceptances among them. Review it periodically. If you ever need to reconstruct what happened and when, this log is your evidence.\n\n## The posture to hold\n\nThe rules summarized here bind you because they are published and you accepted them, and your operations should meet the standard before any measure is taken. The sellers who are comfortable under this system are the ones whose records, invoices, and operations already meet it.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about a policy or a violation notice: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Appeals and legal notices: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "account-settings-and-security": "\n# Account Settings and Security\n\nThis page explains the Account area of Seller Center — profile, company information, contact details, users, notifications, policy acceptances, and health and compliance — how password and account security work, and how to get help. Account security is governed by the Seller Account Security (§2.3.24).\n\n## Your account pages\n\n- **Account → Profile** (`/seller/account/profile`) — your public-facing seller identity: display name and page address, the details buyers see. Keep it professional and current; your storefront identity is governed by the Storefront and Identity Display Policy (§2.3.12).\n- **Account → Company info** (`/seller/account/company-info`) and **Account → Contact** (`/seller/account/contact`) — your legal and contact details. Update these the week something changes. Stale company information is how verification mismatches happen (see Business Verification and Tax Identity (§3.3.3)), and stale contact information is how a notice is missed.\n- **Account → Users** (`/seller/account/users`) — who else can access your seller account. Give each person the access their job needs and no more, and remove a person's access the day they leave. A former team member with active portal access is a risk you have chosen to keep.\n- **Account → Notifications** (`/seller/account/notifications`) — what the Company tells you and how. Keep every operational notice on: orders, returns, disputes, policy notices, and payout notifications. You may reduce promotional notices; do not turn operational notices off — a missed dispute notice can start a response clock you do not see running.\n- **Account → Policy acceptances** (`/seller/account/policy-acceptances`) — your record of the policies you have accepted. This is the page that matters if a question arises about whether you agreed to a rule.\n- **Account → Health & compliance** (`/seller/account/health-compliance`) — your compliance standing: verification status, tax identity status, and policy acceptance status. Check it quarterly; it is the early warning for administrative problems that become selling problems.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Removing a user under Account → Users ends that person's access to your listings, orders, payouts, and refunds. Remove access on the day a team member leaves, and review the user list whenever a role changes.\n\n## Security\n\nAccount → Security (`/seller/account/security`) is where you change your password: enter your current password, your new password, and confirm the new password.\n\nBecause Seller Center controls listings, orders, payouts, and refunds, treat its credentials as money-adjacent credentials:\n\n1. Set a strong password that is unique to this portal. Do not reuse the password from your email, your bank, or any other service.\n2. Store it in a password manager.\n3. Change it on your normal rotation schedule, and immediately if you suspect it has been shared or compromised.\n4. Never share your sign-in. Each person who needs access gets their own access under Account → Users.\n\n## Getting help\n\n**Communication → Support** (`/seller/communication/support`) is your channel to the platform team. Use it for billing and payout questions with specifics (statement period, transaction, amount), technical problems with the portal (see Troubleshooting (§3.3.16) for what to include), appeals questions (see Policies, Violations and Appeals (§3.3.17)), and anything the documentation does not answer.\n\nA support request is answered fastest when it states the specific page, the specific order, listing, or transaction reference, what you expected, what happened instead, and when. A request without a period, an amount, or a reference cannot be investigated without further exchanges.\n\n**Communication → Inbox** (`/seller/communication/inbox`) and **Messages** (`/seller/messages`) carry buyer–seller messaging. Write professionally, promptly, and factually; every message may become evidence in a dispute. Response-time expectations are stated in the Customer Service Standards Policy (§2.3.61), and messaging conduct is governed by the Buyer-Seller Communication Policy (§2.3.60).\n\n**Communication → Forum** (`/seller/communication/forum`) is seller community discussion — suitable for asking whether other sellers see the same behavior, and not a substitute for support when you need an answer about your own account.\n\n**Docs** (`/seller/docs`) holds documentation inside the portal, including this help library.\n\n## Settings\n\nSettings (`/seller/settings`) holds shop-level settings. Review them after onboarding and revisit them quarterly; the configuration that was right when you launched may not be right after a year of growth.\n\n## If something goes wrong\n\n- **Locked out:** contact your administrator. Accounts are administrator-created (see Seller Center Overview (§3.3.1)), so recovery runs through them.\n- **Password change not accepted:** re-enter the current password exactly, and confirm the new password matches in both fields. If it still fails, report it through Communication → Support.\n- **A critical notice missed:** check your notification settings, check your spam folder, and allowlist the Company's sender addresses. Then check the audit log under Governance → Audit log (see Policies, Violations and Appeals (§3.3.17)).\n- **A former team member still has access:** remove it now, then review every remaining user.\n\nRelated documents: the Seller Onboarding Policy (§2.3.7), the Business Verification Policy (§2.3.8), and the account terms of the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1). Your personal data is handled under the Privacy Policy (§2.1.2) and the Data Protection Policy for Sellers (§2.3.67).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about account settings, users, or security: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices and appeals: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "seller-onboarding-in-eight-steps": "\n# Seller Onboarding in Eight Steps\n\nThis page walks through the eight steps of the seller application in Seller Center, what each step asks for, and what happens after you submit. Each step is its own page under `/seller/onboarding/`, each step saves as you go, and you can leave mid-application and return — your progress is kept. The overview page at `/seller/onboarding/overview` shows a progress bar (\"X of 8 complete\") and a card for each step. This page summarizes the Seller Onboarding Policy (§2.3.7), which governs.\n\n## Before you start\n\nHave these ready before Step 2, because the same legal identity must agree across Steps 2, 3, and 4:\n\n- Your legal business name, exactly as it appears on your tax documents.\n- Your business address and business type, and documents that prove the business exists and that you are authorized to act for it.\n- Your tax ID (EIN or SSN) and business type for payouts and Form 1099-K reporting.\n- The bank account for payouts, in a name that matches your legal business name.\n\nName mismatches across these three steps are the most common reason an application is returned for corrections.\n\n## The eight steps\n\n### Step 1 — Overview\n\nOrientation only: what to expect, the step list, and the progress bar. Nothing to fill in.\n\n### Step 2 — Business Verification (`/seller/onboarding/business-verification`)\n\nEnter your legal business name, business address, and business type, and upload supporting documents. Verification of high-volume sellers under the INFORM Act starts here; see Business Verification and Tax Identity (§3.3.3) and the INFORM Act Compliance Policy (§2.3.11).\n\n### Step 3 — Tax Identity (`/seller/onboarding/tax-identity`)\n\nSelect your business type — Individual / sole proprietor, LLC, Corporation (C-corp / S-corp), Partnership, or Nonprofit — and your Tax ID type, EIN or SSN. Your tax ID is encrypted and is never displayed publicly. A sole proprietor may use an SSN; an EIN, available from the IRS at no charge, keeps an SSN out of a third-party system.\n\n### Step 4 — Bank Info (`/seller/onboarding/bank-info`)\n\nEnter the payout account where the Company sends your money. This is the initial account of record collected during the application; you manage payout methods later under Payments (see Payments, Wallet and Payouts (§3.3.12)). If the bank account name does not match the legal name from Step 2 — for example, a DBA account or a personal account for a business entity — expect a verification question.\n\n### Step 5 — Policy Acceptance (`/seller/onboarding/policy-acceptance`)\n\nRead and accept all six policies, each with its own checkbox. All six must be checked to continue; there is no partial acceptance:\n\n1. Seller Agreement — terms of selling, the fee schedule, and account responsibilities.\n2. Listing Policy — accurate titles, images, and descriptions; prohibited items and claims, including veterinary claims.\n3. Fulfillment & Shipping SLA — ship within your stated handling time, provide tracking, and meet delivery promises.\n4. Returns & Refunds Policy — accept returns under the platform policy, issue refunds promptly, and handle damaged-item claims.\n5. Intellectual Property Policy — list only items you have the rights to sell, and respond to intellectual property complaints within 48 hours.\n6. Seller Code of Conduct — fair pricing, no review manipulation, and professional customer communication.\n\nAcceptance is recorded against your account and can be reviewed later under Account → Policy acceptances. An appeal does not succeed on the ground that you did not know a rule you accepted.\n\n### Step 6 — Global Markets (`/seller/onboarding/global-markets`)\n\nSelect the regions you sell in. Your shipping templates, delivery promises, and tax obligations key off the markets you select, so select only regions you can ship to reliably.\n\n### Step 7 — Customer Favorites (`/seller/onboarding/customer-favorites`)\n\nSet your preferences for how your products may appear in featured placements. This step sets preferences; it does not guarantee placement.\n\n### Step 8 — First Listing (`/seller/onboarding/first-listing`)\n\nCreate your first product using the same listing flow as the catalog (see Creating Listings (§3.3.4)), so your shop is not empty when review completes.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> A new listing enters the review queue before it appears on the storefront. Submitting a listing in Step 8 does not make it live, and nothing sells until your application is approved.\n\n## After you submit\n\nThe platform team reviews completed applications within 24–72 hours. You can keep working in the portal while you wait — more listings, shipping templates, inventory — but nothing sells until you are approved. Approval marks your account approved, and that is the moment your listings can go live.\n\nIf your application is returned for corrections:\n\n1. Fix exactly what is flagged.\n2. Check the common causes first: name mismatches across Steps 2–4, blurry or expired verification documents, and a bank account in a different name than the business.\n3. Resubmit the application.\n\nEligibility and business verification requirements are stated in the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1), the Seller Onboarding Policy (§2.3.7), and the Business Verification Policy (§2.3.8). The tax and identity steps are explained by the Tax Identity and 1099 Reporting Policy (§2.3.10) and the INFORM Act Compliance Policy (§2.3.11).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about your application or a correction request: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "business-verification-and-tax-identity": "\n# Business Verification and Tax Identity\n\nThis page explains what the Company verifies about your business and your tax identity, what you must provide, how to avoid the common reasons verification is returned, and your duty to keep the information current. This page summarizes the Business Verification Policy (§2.3.8), which governs.\n\n## Why verification is required\n\nThe INFORM Act requires marketplaces to verify the identity of high-volume sellers and to keep that verification current. The Company verifies every seller at onboarding, under one standard, rather than waiting until a seller crosses the high-volume threshold. The legal basis is stated in the INFORM Act Compliance Policy (§2.3.11).\n\nYour verification lives on your seller record: your legal name, the last four digits of your tax ID, and your approval status. The full tax ID is encrypted and is never displayed anywhere in the portal.\n\n## Business verification: what to provide\n\nProvide your legal business name, business address, and business type, together with supporting documents — typically your business registration and a government ID for the person authorized to act for the business. The Company checks that the business exists, that the documents are current and legible, and that the person applying is authorized to act.\n\nTo avoid a return for corrections:\n\n- Check expiry dates before you upload. Expired documents are the most common reason for rejection.\n- Show the authority of the person applying. If the registered agent is your accountant but you operate the account, provide a document that connects you to the business, such as an operating agreement or a corporate resolution.\n- Keep addresses consistent. Your business address here, the address on your tax documents, and your return address for shipping do not have to be identical — a warehouse differs from a registered office — but unexplained contradictions are flagged.\n\n## Tax identity: what to provide\n\nThe tax identity form collects your business type (Individual / sole proprietor, LLC, C-corp / S-corp, Partnership, or Nonprofit) and your Tax ID type (EIN or SSN). It feeds two things:\n\n1. **Payouts.** The Company must know who it is paying.\n2. **Form 1099-K reporting.** The payment processor issues Form 1099-K, and the tax identity on file must match IRS records or the form is issued incorrectly. Reporting is governed by the Tax Identity and 1099 Reporting Policy (§2.3.10).\n\nIf your business type changes — sole proprietor to LLC is the common case — update your tax identity promptly. A Form 1099-K issued to the wrong entity type takes weeks to correct.\n\n## Keeping verification current\n\nVerification is not a one-time event. When any of the following changes, update it under Account → Company info (`/seller/account/company-info`) and Account → Contact (`/seller/account/contact`), and expect the Company to re-verify:\n\n- Legal name\n- Business type\n- Tax ID\n- Authorized contact\n\nSelling under a different legal identity than the one on file is a compliance violation, not a clerical error, because INFORM Act verification must stay current.\n\n## If verification fails or lapses\n\n- **Application returned for corrections:** fix what is flagged and resubmit. Do not re-upload the same blurry document.\n- **Name mismatch across steps:** business verification, tax identity, and bank information must agree on who you are. If your bank account is in a personal name and your business is an LLC, use a business account or be ready to document why.\n- **Verification expired:** business registrations lapse. If yours does, your selling privileges may be paused until you re-verify. Track the expiry date yourself.\n\nEligibility terms are stated in the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about verification or your tax identity: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "creating-listings": "\n# Creating Listings\n\nThis page explains where your listings live in Seller Center, the three listing statuses, the five ways to create a listing, what the review queue checks, and how to edit listings, variants, media, bundles, and categories. This page summarizes the Listing Accuracy Policy (§2.3.25), which governs, and listing obligations are stated in the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1).\n\n## Where listings live\n\nManage listings under Catalog → Listings (`/seller/catalog/listings`). Your listings are catalog items tagged to your seller account, shown up to 200 per view, each with its name, public address, current price, primary image, SKU, status, and stock. The index page provides a search box and a status filter: All statuses, Active, Pending review, and Suppressed.\n\nLearn the three statuses, because they are the life cycle of every listing:\n\n- **Pending review** — where every new listing starts. It is in the review queue and is not visible on the storefront.\n- **Active** — the listing passed review and is visible and sellable.\n- **Suppressed** — the listing has been pulled from the storefront by the Company, for example for a policy violation, a brand gating issue, or a safety flag. It remains yours and remains in your catalog, but it is not selling. Suppression is an enforcement action; see Policies, Violations and Appeals (§3.3.17).\n\n## The five ways to create a listing\n\nGo to Catalog → Listings → Add a listing (`/seller/catalog/listings/new`) and choose the method that matches your situation:\n\n1. **Single listing** (`/seller/catalog/listings/new/single`) — the full form: name, description, price, brand, SKU, and everything else. Slowest per item and highest quality per item. Use it for your first listings while you learn what review checks (see Listing Content Standards (§3.3.5)).\n2. **Express** (`/seller/catalog/listings/new/express`) — name and price only, with details completed after. Finish every express listing the same day; an unfinished listing sits in pending review or goes live thin and is suppressed for content quality.\n3. **Match catalog** (`/seller/catalog/listings/new/match-catalog`) — search the platform catalog and attach your offer to an existing product. Use this when the product already exists on the platform, so one product page carries multiple sellers. Creating a parallel listing for a product that already exists is a catalog quality violation.\n4. **Spreadsheet upload** (`/seller/catalog/listings/new/spreadsheet`) — CSV upload from the provided template for bulk creation. The template fixes the columns, formats, and required fields. Before you upload, check your file against the template; after you upload, check the row count. Renamed columns, wrong date formats, and HTML in plain-text fields are the common causes of a partial import.\n5. **Supplier import** (`/seller/catalog/listings/new/supplier-import`) — import products from a connected supplier's catalog. Every imported listing still goes through review, and supplier-provided titles and descriptions are a common source of claims violations, so treat imports as starting points and review the content before you rely on it.\n\n## The review queue\n\nNew listings go into the review queue before they appear on the storefront. Review checks content quality, policy compliance, brand gating (see Brand Gating and Authorizations (§3.3.6)), and restricted and prohibited categories. Submit early if you are timing a listing to a promotion or a season.\n\nThe items most often returned are missing or low-quality images, keyword-stuffed titles, descriptions copied from another site, missing identifiers (UPC or GTIN where required), and veterinary or health claims on pet products.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Publishing a listing puts it into review for the storefront, and an active listing is an offer buyers can buy. Check the product, price, images, and shipping template before you submit, and do not relist a suppressed listing without first fixing the cause recorded for the suppression — relisting without fixing the cause is itself a violation.\n\n## Editing and related catalog tools\n\n- **Listing detail** (`/seller/catalog/listings/[id]`) — edit an individual listing.\n- **Variants** (`/seller/catalog/variants`) — manage variant structure: one product family, with variant SKUs underneath it.\n- **Media** (`/seller/catalog/media`) — manage product images across listings.\n- **Bundles** (`/seller/catalog/bundles`) — manage bundles.\n- **Categories** (`/seller/catalog/categories`) — set category placement for your listings.\n- **Imports** (`/seller/catalog/imports`) — history and status of your bulk imports.\n- **Suppression** (`/seller/catalog/suppression`) — your suppressed listings and the reason for each.\n\n## If something goes wrong\n\n- **Listing held in pending review:** check it against the content standards in Listing Content Standards (§3.3.5) first, then check brand gating if it is a gated brand.\n- **Listing suppressed after going live:** check `/seller/catalog/suppression` for the reason — a complaint, a policy flag, or a brand authorization that lapsed — then follow the appeals path in Policies, Violations and Appeals (§3.3.17).\n- **Spreadsheet import shows fewer rows than you uploaded:** download the template fresh, compare your columns to it, and re-upload.\n- **Duplicate listing flagged:** use match catalog for that product, and request a merge rather than contesting the flag.\n\nThe listing policy family governs the details: the Listing Accuracy Policy (§2.3.25), the Product Detail Page Policy (§2.3.26), the Product Image Policy (§2.3.27), the Product Title Policy (§2.3.28), the Product Description Policy (§2.3.29), the Product Identifiers Policy (§2.3.30), the Catalog and Variant Policy (§2.3.31), the Category Placement Policy (§2.3.32), the Product Claims Policy (§2.3.33), the Search Manipulation Policy (§2.3.34), the Product Review Policy for Sellers (§2.3.35), the Seller Pricing Policy (§2.3.36), and the Promotions and Coupons Policy (§2.3.37). Product compliance is governed by the Restricted Products Policy (§2.3.38), the Prohibited Products Policy (§2.3.39), the Pet Food and Treats Policy (§2.3.40), the Flea, Tick and Pesticide Products Policy (§2.3.41), the Prescription Products Policy (Sellers) (§2.3.42), the Live Animals Policy (§2.3.43), the Dangerous Goods Policy (§2.3.44), the Product Recall Policy (Sellers) (§2.3.45), and the Product Warranty Policy (§2.3.46).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about a listing or a review outcome: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices and appeals: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "listing-content-standards": "\n# Listing Content Standards\n\nThis page explains the content standards your titles, descriptions, images, identifiers, category placement, prices, and reviews must meet, and the 48-hour rule for responding to an intellectual property complaint. This page summarizes the Listing Accuracy Policy (§2.3.25), which governs, together with the product content policies cited in each section below.\n\n## The standard in one paragraph\n\nEvery word, image, and data point on your listing must be accurate, yours to use, and free of claims you cannot substantiate. The Listing Policy you accepted at onboarding requires accurate titles, images, and descriptions, and prohibits prohibited items and claims, including veterinary claims. The Intellectual Property Policy requires that you list only items you have the rights to sell and that you respond to intellectual property complaints within 48 hours.\n\n## Titles\n\nWrite the title as brand, product type, and key differentiator, in that order. A title is returned or a listing is suppressed for:\n\n- **Keyword repetition** — repeating product words to influence search. That conduct is governed by the Search Manipulation Policy (§2.3.34).\n- **Claims in the title** — \"Vet-Recommended,\" \"All-Natural,\" and \"Best\" must be provable if used, and a superlative such as \"best\" cannot be proved.\n- **All capitals, excessive punctuation, and emoji** — presentation that reads as spam fails review.\n\nTitle requirements are stated in the Product Title Policy (§2.3.28).\n\n## Descriptions\n\nDescribe the actual product: what it is, what it is made of, dimensions, care instructions, and what is in the box, as required by the Product Description Policy (§2.3.29).\n\nTake particular care with claims:\n\n- Veterinary and health claims on pet food, supplements, and topicals — for example \"supports joint health,\" \"reduces anxiety,\" \"vet-recommended,\" \"cures,\" or \"treats\" — are regulated claims. Pet food labeling is governed by the Pet Food and Treats Policy (§2.3.40); pesticide claims for flea and tick products are governed by the Flea, Tick and Pesticide Products Policy (§2.3.41); prescription products are governed by the Prescription Products Policy (Sellers) (§2.3.42). If your product makes a health claim, you need the substantiation and the regulatory basis for it, or you must remove the claim. Claims are governed by the Product Claims Policy (§2.3.33).\n- Supplier-provided descriptions are the most common source of claims violations, because supplier copy is written to sell the product to you. Rewrite it in your own words.\n- Do not copy descriptions from other sites. Apart from the rights problem, duplicate content degrades search across the platform and flags the listing in quality review.\n\n## Images\n\nImages must show the actual product you are selling, as required by the Product Image Policy (§2.3.27). A stock photo of a different variant, a rendering that does not match the physical item, or an image with a text overlay making a claim fails review or leads to suppression. Manage images under Catalog → Media (`/seller/catalog/media`), and set the primary image deliberately: the storefront uses the primary image you designate.\n\n## Identifiers and categorization\n\nProvide real product identifiers (UPC, GTIN, MPN) where they exist, as required by the Product Identifiers Policy (§2.3.30). Identifiers feed catalog matching (see Creating Listings (§3.3.4)), brand gating checks, and recall targeting. Place each product in the correct category under Catalog → Categories, as required by the Category Placement Policy (§2.3.32); a miscategorized listing surfaces in the wrong searches and can be treated as search manipulation.\n\n## Pricing content\n\nYour price must be real, as required by the Seller Pricing Policy (§2.3.36). Deceptive pricing is prohibited, including a compare-at price that was never a genuine price and a price set only to make a discount look larger. See Pricing, Promotions and the Buy Box (§3.3.7).\n\n## Reviews\n\nYou must not write your own reviews, pay for reviews, review your own products from another account, or offer an incentive conditioned on a positive review, as stated in the Product Review Policy for Sellers (§2.3.35). The review request tools in the success hub are for neutral post-purchase requests sent to all buyers. Review manipulation is treated as a severe violation under the Seller Enforcement Policy (§2.3.3).\n\n## The 48-hour intellectual property rule\n\nIf you receive an intellectual property complaint, you have 48 hours to respond. The period is 48 hours, not 48 business hours. If the complaint has merit — you are selling something you do not have the rights to sell — take the listing down yourself before the Company does; a voluntary takedown and an enforced suppression are recorded differently when later decisions are made about your account. Intellectual property matters are governed by the Intellectual Property Policy (Sellers) (§2.3.50), the Trademark Policy (§2.3.51), the Copyright and DMCA Policy (§2.3.52), and the Counterfeit Policy for Sellers (§2.3.70).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about listing content or a complaint notice: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Copyright and other legal notices, and appeals: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "brand-gating-and-authorizations": "\n# Brand Gating and Authorizations\n\nThis page explains brand gating: which brands require authorization, the two ways to prove you may sell a gated brand, how approval and expiry work, and the mistakes that lead to suppression. This page summarizes the Brand Gating Policy (Sellers) (§2.3.48), which governs.\n\n## What brand gating is\n\nSome brands may be listed only by sellers who have proved they are authorized to sell them. There are two proof paths:\n\n1. **Purchase order** — a purchase order or invoice from the brand owner, the manufacturer, or a distributor the brand owner recognizes, showing that you legitimately sourced the goods.\n2. **Manufacturer authorization** — a written authorization from the brand owner or manufacturer naming you as an authorized reseller.\n\nYou upload the evidence with your authorization request, and the Company reviews it.\n\nBrand gating applies to marketplace sellers only. It does not apply to products sold by the Company itself, which are shown as \"Sold by Portal Shop.\" If you are a marketplace seller, the gate applies to you, with no exceptions.\n\n## How gating works\n\n1. You prepare to list a product under a gated brand, or a brand you already list becomes gated.\n2. The listing cannot go active without a current, approved authorization for that brand — approved, with today's date inside its validity window.\n3. Submit your evidence — a purchase order or an authorization letter — through the brand authorization flow.\n4. The Company reviews the request. Approval opens the gate for that brand. A denial states what is missing.\n\nAuthorizations expire. When the expiry date passes, your listings for that brand are suppressed. Track your renewal dates yourself; a lapsed authorization can suppress your best-selling brand's listings until it is renewed.\n\n## The purchase order path, done right\n\nThe purchase order must evidence legitimate sourcing: from the brand owner, the manufacturer, or a distributor the brand owner recognizes — not a wholesaler several steps removed from the brand. Quantities on the document should be consistent with what you list; a purchase order for 12 units does not support a listing showing 500 units in stock. Keep your purchase orders organized and current, because re-verification happens, and a statement that your supplier holds the evidence is not evidence.\n\n## The authorization path, done right\n\nA manufacturer authorization must name you, specifically, as authorized to resell the brand. It must carry your business name and the brand, and a signature from a person at the brand owner or manufacturer with authority to grant it. A general letter about the brand's wholesale program, addressed to no one in particular, does not meet the standard. Check the expiry date on the authorization itself — some are annual — and make sure the expiry recorded with the Company matches it.\n\n## What gets sellers in trouble\n\n- **Listing first, authorizing later.** The listing is suppressed, not activated, and the suppression is recorded on your account. Submit the authorization first.\n- **Sourcing that does not trace back to the brand.** If your purchase order chain does not reach the brand owner or an authorized distributor, the authorization is denied. If the goods are counterfeit, the Counterfeit Policy for Sellers (§2.3.70) applies, which is at the severe end of enforcement under the Seller Enforcement Policy (§2.3.3).\n- **Letting authorizations lapse.** Set a renewal reminder for every authorization.\n- **Assuming the gate does not apply to you.** It applies to every marketplace seller.\n\nRelated documents: the Brand Registry Policy (Sellers) (§2.3.49) governs the registry of brands, the Trademark Policy (§2.3.51) governs use of brand marks, and listing obligations generally are stated in the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about an authorization request or a denial: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Trademark matters and appeals: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "pricing-promotions-and-the-buy-box": "\n# Pricing, Promotions and the Buy Box\n\nThis page explains the pricing tools in Seller Center — the bulk editor, AutoPrice, discounts, and promotions — how the buy box is decided, and where pricing insights are found. This page summarizes the Seller Pricing Policy (§2.3.36), which governs, and the Promotions and Coupons Policy (§2.3.37).\n\n## Your price, your responsibility\n\nYour marketplace listings carry the prices you set, and the price you set is the price you are accountable for. A separate pricing rule governs products sold by the Company itself. On your pricing, the Company enforces two things: no deceptive pricing (a compare-at anchor that was never a genuine price, or a price set only to manufacture a discount), and enough price stability that the price means something. Pricing that games search ranking is governed by the Search Manipulation Policy (§2.3.34).\n\n## The pricing tools\n\n**Pricing → Overview** (`/seller/pricing/overview`) is the command page: listings priced, active AutoPrice rules, buy box win rate, and active promotions, each linking into its tool.\n\n**Bulk editor** (`/seller/pricing/bulk-editor`) updates many prices at once and saves them in one action. Use it for cost changes, category-wide adjustments, and seasonal repricing, on a deliberate schedule with a reason for each change.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Prices saved in the bulk editor take effect as the prices buyers see and pay. Preview the changes and check a sample of listings before you save: one wrong value can reprice hundreds of listings at once.\n\n**AutoPrice** (`/seller/pricing/AutoPrice`) automates repricing with conditions and actions — for example, a rule that responds when a competitor's price falls, adjusts your price, and stops at a floor you set. Follow these rules:\n\n1. Set a floor on every rule. A rule without a floor can sell at a loss for weeks before you notice.\n2. Review rule activity weekly.\n3. Turn off rules that are not performing.\n\n**Discounts** (`/seller/pricing/discounts`) and **Promotions** (`/seller/pricing/promotions`) cover coupons, sales events, and promotional pricing. Use promotions for launches, seasons, and clearing slow inventory, and give every promotion an end date. A product that is always on sale teaches buyers that the sale price is the real price.\n\n## The buy box\n\n**Pricing → Buy box** (`/seller/pricing/buy-box`) shows which of your listings can win the buy box — the default seller shown on a product page when several sellers offer the same product. Eligibility is not price alone. It takes into account your performance (including late shipment rate and feedback), your fulfillment reliability, and your price competitiveness together. The cheapest seller with a poor late shipment rate does not win over a slightly higher-priced seller who ships on time. If you are losing the buy box, check Performance and Account Health (§3.3.14) before cutting price.\n\nThe success hub tools at `/seller/analytics/success-hub/match-buy-box` and `/seller/analytics/success-hub/match-price` give targeted recommendations. Treat a recommendation as a lead, not an instruction: check it against your own margins before you act.\n\n## Pricing insights\n\n**Pricing → Pricing insights** (`/seller/pricing/pricing-insights`) and the analytics view at `/seller/analytics/pricing-insights` show where you stand: the listings where you are priced out of contention, and the listings where you are priced below what the market supports. Both directions matter.\n\n## If something goes wrong\n\n- **Two sellers lowering prices against each other automatically:** set floors on your AutoPrice rules and know your walk-away price before you automate.\n- **A promotion that never ends:** set an end date; permanent sale pricing destroys the anchor price.\n- **Buy box lost after a price cut:** price was not the problem. Check your late shipment rate and feedback first.\n- **A bulk edit repriced the wrong listings:** correct the affected prices in the bulk editor and check a sample of listings to confirm the correction.\n\nListing obligations, including pricing accuracy, are stated in the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about pricing tools, promotions, or the buy box: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "inventory-management": "\n# Inventory Management\n\nThis page explains the Inventory area of Seller Center — stock, movements, replenishment, and low stock — and how inbound shipments work when you use Fulfillment by Portal Shop. This page summarizes the Inventory Accuracy Policy (§2.3.57), which governs.\n\n## What the Inventory area covers\n\n**Inventory → Overview** (`/seller/inventory/overview`) is the landing page. Under it:\n\n- **Stock** (`/seller/inventory/stock`) — per-SKU quantity management. Each row shows SKU, product, and quantity. Click a quantity to edit it inline, or select multiple rows and set a bulk value.\n- **Movements** (`/seller/inventory/movements`) — the record of stock changes.\n- **Replenishment** (`/seller/inventory/replenishment`) — reorder planning.\n- **Low stock** (`/seller/inventory/low-stock`) — what is running out.\n- **Fulfillment inbound** (`/seller/inventory/wfs-inbound`) — shipments of your goods into Portal Shop fulfillment centers.\n\nQuantities are whole numbers, zero or above, keyed by SKU, saved per row or in bulk.\n\n## Keeping quantities accurate\n\nInventory accuracy is a performance obligation, not bookkeeping. Overselling — selling units you do not have — produces cancellations, and cancellations feed directly into your performance metrics (see Performance and Account Health (§3.3.14)) and into enforcement under the Seller Enforcement Policy (§2.3.3). The consequences of cancelling an order you cannot fill are stated in the Order Cancellation Policy (§2.3.58).\n\nDo the following as routine practice:\n\n1. Keep an accurate quantity for every SKU.\n2. Know your lead times from your suppliers, and set reorder points that reflect them.\n3. Work the Low stock view so a stock-out is a decision, not a surprise.\n4. Do not list inventory you cannot ship.\n\n## Inbound shipments to Fulfillment by Portal Shop\n\nIf you use Fulfillment by Portal Shop, plan and track shipments of your goods into the fulfillment centers under Inventory → Fulfillment inbound. The program is governed by the Fulfillment by Portal Shop — Program Terms (§2.3.47).\n\nInbound accuracy matters as much as listing accuracy: the units the warehouse receives are the units recorded as yours. To protect yourself in a discrepancy:\n\n1. Count the units before you ship.\n2. Document what you sent, and keep your inbound shipment records.\n3. For large shipments, photograph the packed contents and keep the bill of lading.\n\nA discrepancy between what you recorded as sent and what arrived is yours to resolve, and a dispute without documentation is difficult to sustain.\n\n## If something goes wrong\n\n- **Stock page shows no rows:** check that you are signed in with the email address on your seller record (see Seller Center Overview (§3.3.1)); an empty page under the wrong sign-in is an identity mismatch, not lost data.\n- **A quantity edit does not match what you ship:** correct the SKU quantity in Stock, then review Movements to confirm the change was recorded.\n- **Overselling:** pause the affected listing, work the resulting orders under Orders, Acknowledgment and Shipping (§3.3.9), and correct the quantity before the listing sells again.\n- **Inbound mismatch:** compare your pack-out count, photographs, and bill of lading against the receiving record, and raise the discrepancy with those documents attached.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about inventory records or an inbound shipment: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "orders-acknowledgment-and-shipping": "\n# Orders, Acknowledgment and Shipping\n\nThis page explains how your orders reach you in Seller Center, the order views, how to acknowledge and ship — including in bulk — how to pause operations, and the fulfillment standard you are held to. This page summarizes the Fulfillment Policy (§2.3.53), which governs.\n\n## How your orders reach you\n\nYour orders are platform order records tagged to your seller account. The portal matches your sign-in email to your seller record and shows your orders newest-first, 100 per view, with an optional status filter. Each order shows its total, status, payment status, fulfillment status, date, and the customer's email.\n\nTwo consequences follow. The 100-order limit means the inbox is a working view, not an archive — use the status filter to find older orders. And if your sign-in email stops matching your seller record, your orders do not appear in the portal (see Seller Center Overview (§3.3.1)).\n\n## The order views\n\n- **Orders → Inbox** (`/seller/orders/inbox`) — every order, newest first. Your daily starting point.\n- **Orders → Unshipped** (`/seller/orders/unshipped`) — the orders that need you now. If you open one orders page per day, make it this one.\n- **Orders → Late** (`/seller/orders/late`) — orders past their promised ship date. Every order here is damaging your late shipment rate (see Performance and Account Health (§3.3.14)). Work this view oldest first.\n- **Orders → Cancelled** (`/seller/orders/cancelled`) — your cancellation history. Review it weekly; repeated cancellations of the same product, or cancellations clustering after weekends, point to a listing or operations problem.\n- **Orders → [id]** (`/seller/orders/[id]`) — the order detail page: line items, customer information, payment status, fulfillment status, and shipping address. Open this page before you reply to a buyer about an order.\n\n## Acknowledge and ship\n\nAcknowledging an order confirms you have seen it and will fulfill it.\n\n1. Open Orders → Unshipped.\n2. Select the orders you are actually shipping today.\n3. Acknowledge them — individually, or in one action under Orders → Bulk acknowledge (`/seller/orders/bulk-acknowledge`).\n4. As parcels go out, mark them shipped with tracking — individually, or under Orders → Bulk ship (`/seller/orders/bulk-ship`).\n\nDo not acknowledge orders you cannot ship. The buyer's clock runs from your acknowledgment, so an acknowledged order that does not ship is worse than one not yet acknowledged.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Marking an order shipped and buying a label are not the shipment itself. An order counts as shipped when the carrier scans the parcel; a label that is created but never handed to the carrier leaves the order unshipped and the late-shipment clock running.\n\n## Pause operations\n\nOrders → Pause operations (`/seller/orders/pause-operations`) temporarily stops new orders when you cannot fulfill — a vacation, a warehouse move, a supplier disruption, or illness in a one-person shop. Pausing is always better than accepting orders you cannot ship: orders accepted into a known fulfillment gap become late shipments and cancellations, and both outlive the gap in your metrics. Pause, fix the problem, then resume.\n\n## The fulfillment standard you are held to\n\nThe fulfillment and shipping commitment you accepted at onboarding is the operating contract: ship within your stated handling time, provide tracking, and meet the delivery promise shown to the buyer. Set your handling time to what you can do on your worst day, not your best — the metric that matters is the promise kept, not the promise made. The numeric standards are stated in the Service Level Requirements (§2.3.2).\n\n## If something goes wrong\n\n- **Inbox is empty but you know you have orders:** check for a sign-in email mismatch first, then use the status filter — the 100-order view may be hiding older orders behind newer ones.\n- **A bulk action completes for only some orders:** check which orders actually changed state on their detail pages before you retry. Retrying without checking can double-acknowledge or double-ship an order.\n- **Tracking never shows a carrier scan:** the tracking number was entered incorrectly, or the parcel was never handed to the carrier. Carrier-scanned tracking is what counts under the Tracking Policy (§2.3.55).\n- **You cannot fulfill and have not paused:** pause now. Every additional order accepted is another late shipment.\n\nRelated documents: the Shipping Policy (§2.3.54), the Tracking Policy (§2.3.55), the Packaging Policy (§2.3.56), the Order Cancellation Policy (§2.3.58), and the fulfillment obligations in the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about an order or a fulfillment problem: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "brand-registry": "\n# Brand Registry\n\nThis page explains how the Brand Registry works: what a registry record holds, how a brand enters the registry, how brand status changes, and how to fix common registry problems. This page summarizes the Brand Registry Policy (§2.4.1), which governs.\n\n## What the registry is\n\nEvery brand sold on the Platform is held in one registry, with one record per brand. That record is the source the rest of the Platform reads to answer who owns a brand, whether the brand is gated, and who is allowed to sell it.\n\nA registry record holds the brand name, public address, description, logo, website, pricing tier, owner of record, status, and publication date. The owner of record and the status matter most in daily use: the owner of record is the seller account that owns the brand, and the status controls whether the brand is visible and sellable on the Platform.\n\nTwo other brand lists exist, and they do different work:\n\n- The admin brand list is a small presentation list managed by the Company (logo, sort order, active flag). It is not the registry.\n- The public A–Z brand directory is built from the brand names on sellable catalog products. It is not built from the registry or the admin list.\n\nIf a brand appears in the public directory but has no registry record, the brand is selling without a governing record. Register the brand or confirm it is a first-party-only brand the Company sources directly.\n\n## How a brand enters the registry\n\nA brand enters the registry in one of two ways:\n\n1. Submit the brand partner application on the seller page under \"Own a pet brand? Claim your brand portal.\" The application goes to the brand team's review queue.\n2. The Company creates the record directly during onboarding or catalog work.\n\nIn either case, the record starts in pending status and is not visible to shoppers. Visibility is held until ownership is verified and the brand team approves the record.\n\n### Requirements before you apply\n\nPrepare the following before you submit:\n\n- Brand name, website, and logo.\n- Proof that you own the brand or are authorized to represent it, such as a trademark registration, a letter from the manufacturer, or a distributor agreement naming you.\n\nScreenshots of a storefront on another marketplace do not meet the evidence standard. The evidence standard is stated in the Brand Registry Policy (§2.4.1) and the Brand Ownership Verification Policy (§2.4.2), which govern.\n\n## Brand status\n\nA brand record moves through four statuses:\n\n- **Pending** — application received and not yet reviewed. The brand is not visible to shoppers and cannot be attached to listings as a governed brand.\n- **Active** — verified and published. The brand appears in governed contexts and its gating rules, if any, are enforced.\n- **Suspended** — removed from visibility while the Company investigates, for example after counterfeit reports, a disputed Minimum Advertised Price matter, or an ownership challenge. Existing listings are not deleted, but the brand no longer resolves as active.\n- **Removed** — the brand is out of the registry. The effect of removal on listings, authorizations, and historical records is governed by the Brand Registry Removal & Termination Policy (§2.4.20).\n\nOnly the Company changes a brand's status, and every change is recorded. A brand owner does not change the status of their own brand.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Removal of a brand record affects listings and authorizations tied to that brand. Confirm the effect under the Brand Registry Removal & Termination Policy (§2.4.20) before you request removal.\n\n## Owner of record\n\nThe owner of record is exactly one seller account. One brand has one owner of record; shared ownership is not recorded.\n\nIf a brand changes hands through an acquisition, a rebrand, or a distributor taking over, ownership is verified again:\n\n1. Notify the Company that ownership has changed.\n2. The new owner provides ownership evidence that meets the same standard as the first verification.\n3. The Company updates the owner of record only after verification is complete.\n\nIf ownership is disputed, the Company holds the record at its current status until the dispute is resolved with documents. The Company does not decide trademark disputes; it holds the record still until a party produces conclusive paperwork.\n\n## Fixing common registry problems\n\n### Two records for the same brand\n\nThis happens when a brand applies twice or a record is created for a brand that already applied. The Company merges the records: one record survives with the public address and history, references such as ownership links, authorizations, and catalog brand names are moved to the surviving record, and the duplicate is then removed.\n\n### Public address conflicts\n\nThe public address is the URL-safe identifier for the brand. If two brands would produce the same address, the second brand receives a distinguishing addition. The public address of an active brand does not change, because changing it breaks links and embeds that refer to it.\n\n### Brand selling with no registry record\n\nBecause the public directory is built from catalog brand names, a brand can appear publicly without a registry record. When you find one:\n\n1. Register the brand through the application process above, or\n2. Confirm with the brand team that it is a first-party-only brand the Company sources directly.\n\nDo not leave the brand unrecorded.\n\n### Broken logo link\n\nIf a logo was uploaded and the storage path later changed, the record may still point to the old link. Re-upload the logo through the brand admin tools so the correct public link is written. Do not edit the link by hand.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about a registry record, ownership, or brand status: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices about ownership or removal: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "private-label-and-white-label": "\n# Private Label and White Label\n\nThis page explains private label and white label arrangements on the Platform: who is the brand of record, what the Platform requires, who carries regulatory responsibility, and what happens in common failure cases. This page summarizes the Private Label & White Label Policy (§2.4.27), which governs.\n\n## Private label in brief\n\nPrivate label means products manufactured by one company and sold under another company's brand. The product is made by a contract manufacturer; the bag, box, or label carries your brand. The arrangement is a legitimate and common way to build a product line, and it raises one question the Platform must answer clearly: whose brand is it, legally?\n\n## The brand of record rule\n\nIn a private label arrangement, the **label owner is the brand of record** — not the contract manufacturer and not the formulator. The company whose name is on the package:\n\n- Claims the brand. See Claiming Your Brand (§3.4.2).\n- Holds the owner of record entry.\n- Sets Minimum Advertised Price for the brand. See Minimum Advertised Price (§3.4.6).\n- Answers for the product on the Platform.\n\nPrivate label concentrates responsibility in the label owner while knowledge is divided between the label owner and the manufacturer. The label owner knows the market; the manufacturer knows the formula. When something goes wrong — a mislabeled ingredient, contamination, or a recall — the Platform works with one responsible party, and the policy selects the label owner. Choose your contract manufacturer as if the liability is yours, because on the Platform it is.\n\n## What the Platform requires\n\nPrivate label and white label arrangements operate through the standard Brand Registry and manufacturer application processes. See Brand Registry (§3.4.1) and Manufacturer Program (§3.4.9). The requirements are:\n\n### The brand must be established\n\nPrivate-label brands go through the same registry and verification as any other brand. A new brand still requires the application and the evidence. See the Brand Ownership Verification Policy (§2.4.2), which governs ownership verification.\n\n### The manufacturing relationship must be documented\n\nProvide the following:\n\n- The manufacturer's identity.\n- The facility where the product is made.\n- The agreement between you and the manufacturer.\n\nThis information supports the recall procedure. When a lot is defective, the Platform traces the lot through you to the facility promptly. A manufacturer who will not identify its facilities may not supply ingestible products for sale on this Platform.\n\n### Regulatory responsibility follows the label\n\nAAFCO labeling, state feed registrations, and EPA numbers for pesticide products are the label owner's obligations, governed by the Private Label & White Label Policy (§2.4.27), which governs. Product data must meet the Manufacturer Product Data Standards (§2.4.23), which governs, and the label owner is responsible for that data.\n\nYour contract manufacturer may prepare paperwork as your agent, but registrations are held in your brand's name and the liability for a missing registration is yours.\n\n### No pass-through of responsibility to the Platform\n\nYour contract with your manufacturer may provide that the manufacturer indemnifies you. That agreement is between you and the manufacturer. It does not reduce what you owe the Platform under the brand and seller terms, and the Platform's remedies run against the brand of record. Resolve any recovery from your manufacturer separately.\n\n## The white-label distinction\n\nWhite label means generic products sold under many different brands. Each brand selling a white-label product is independently responsible for:\n\n- Its own listing accuracy.\n- Its own Minimum Advertised Price compliance.\n- Its own customer obligations.\n\nThe fact that many brands sell the same white-label product does not reduce any one brand's responsibility for what its listing states. The catalog rule still applies: one product family has one product record, and brand-specific listings attach as offers, not as separate products.\n\n## Common failure cases\n\n### The contract manufacturer changes the formula without telling you\n\nPrevent this in your contract by requiring change notification, and check supply on receipt. If the shipped product no longer matches the listing, the listing-accuracy violation is yours as label owner. The fact that the manufacturer changed the formula without notice may affect how intent is assessed; it does not erase the violation.\n\n### The manufacturing relationship ends\n\nThe brand stays yours, because you are the brand of record. You need a new supply source before you continue selling.\n\n1. Pause listings for product you cannot supply.\n2. Secure the new supply source.\n3. Resume the listings only when supply is in place.\n\nContinuing to list product you cannot supply creates a fulfillment violation.\n\n### The manufacturer sells your formula to someone else\n\nThat is a contract dispute between you and the manufacturer, not a Platform matter, unless the other party lists the product under your brand. In that case the listing is treated as counterfeit and handled through Complaints, Takedowns and Anti-Counterfeit (§3.4.7). Recall duties for the label owner are governed by the Manufacturer Recall Cooperation Policy (§2.4.25), which governs.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> As label owner, you remain responsible for listing accuracy, regulatory registrations, and recall notification for private-label products even when a contract manufacturer made the product or changed it without notice.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about private label or white label arrangements: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices about a manufacturing or labeling dispute: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "franchise-program-overview": "\n# Franchise Program Overview\n\nThis page gives an overview of the UnSaid Memories franchise program: the franchise model, what a franchise includes, how territory works, and how program economics are set. This page summarizes the Franchise Program Policy (§2.4.29), which governs.\n\n> [!NOTICE]\n> The Franchise Disclosure Document controls the offer and sale of franchises. No statement in this page or in any portal content makes an earnings representation. Have your own counsel review the Franchise Disclosure Document before you sign. Where the franchise law of the state in which the franchised business is located gives the franchisee greater rights, that law prevails to the extent of any conflict.\n\n## The franchise model\n\nA franchise is a business unit on the Platform, parented to the UnSaid Memories master business. Your business runs on the same Platform and the same commerce systems as Company locations, with your data scoped to your franchise.\n\nIn practice:\n\n- **Your storefront and portals are yours.** Your customers see your business name, your location, and your branding within the Platform's design system. Your web address is chosen at signup and must be unique across the Platform; a conflicting address is rejected at creation.\n- **Your data is yours.** Staff, customers, appointments, and sales are scoped to your franchise. Another franchisee cannot see them, and marketplace sellers cannot see them.\n- **Oversight runs through the parent relationship.** The master business sees what it needs for royalties, compliance, and brand standards through that relationship.\n- **The Platform's rules still apply.** The franchise agreement adds to the Platform's policies; it does not replace them. Consumer protection, data handling, and payment rules bind you as they bind every participant.\n\nFranchise sales are regulated, including disclosure requirements under the FTC Franchise Rule and state franchise registration where applicable. That framework is stated in the Franchise Program Policy (§2.4.29), which governs.\n\n## What the franchise includes\n\n- **The operating platform.** Booking, point of sale, customer records, inventory, and reporting — the same systems Company locations run, provisioned for your franchise.\n- **The brand.** UnSaid Memories branding, signage standards, and marketing assets, governed by the Franchise Brand Standards Policy (§2.4.36).\n- **Training.** Initial training for you and your team, plus ongoing education through the learning portal, governed by the Franchise Training Policy (§2.4.37).\n- **Supply and catalog access.** Access to the enterprise catalog and supplier supply, so your location can stock products from opening.\n- **Territory.** A defined territory, as described below.\n\nThe agreement itself is governed by the Franchise Agreement Policy (§2.4.30), which governs.\n\n## Territory\n\nYour franchise agreement defines your territory — the geographic area where you are the UnSaid Memories presence. Territory is governed by the Franchise Territory Policy (§2.4.33), which governs, covering:\n\n- What is protected, including that no other franchise is granted in your territory.\n- What is reserved, including online sales and national accounts, which typically remain Platform-level.\n- Your right of first refusal on additional units nearby, where the agreement provides one.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> The map attached to your signed agreement is your territory. Do not rely on a map in marketing material, which may differ. Read the territory exhibit in the agreement carefully before you sign.\n\n## Program economics\n\nThe franchise program's economics — the initial fee, the royalty rate and how gross sales is defined, the marketing fund contribution, and transfer and renewal fees — are stated in your franchise agreement and governed by the Franchise Fees & Royalties Policy (§2.4.34) and the Franchise Marketing Fund Policy (§2.4.35), which govern.\n\nThe mechanics of how royalties are calculated and reported, how the fund is held separately and reported, and what happens on non-payment are stated in those documents and in Franchise Operations, Reporting and Royalties (§3.4.13). The figures themselves are commercial terms of the individual agreement.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Do not rely on any figure quoted outside your signed agreement. If a figure is not in the agreement, it is not part of your arrangement.\n\nEligibility and approval are governed by the Franchise Eligibility & Approval Policy (§2.4.31). Onboarding steps are covered in Franchise Onboarding (§3.4.12).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about the franchise program or an application: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices about a franchise offer or agreement: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "franchise-onboarding": "\n# Franchise Onboarding\n\nThis page explains franchise onboarding: what must be in place before signup, what information the signup form collects, the provisioning steps that create the franchise, and the opening checklist that follows. This page summarizes the Franchise Onboarding Policy (§2.4.32), which governs.\n\n## Before signup: eligibility and the agreement\n\nOnboarding starts after approval, not before.\n\nRequirements that must be met before provisioning:\n\n1. Eligibility and approval are complete, covering financial qualification, background, and operational capability, governed by the Franchise Eligibility & Approval Policy (§2.4.31), which governs.\n2. The franchise agreement is ready to be created and signed. The agreement is governed by the Franchise Agreement Policy (§2.4.30), which governs. The agreement record is created in pending status during signup and moves to active when signing is complete.\n\nA franchise is not provisioned before the agreement process has begun. The Platform provisions businesses, not prospects.\n\n## The signup form\n\nFranchise signup is run by an admin at the franchise signup page in the admin portal. There is no self-service franchise signup. The franchisee provides the information and the Platform team runs provisioning, because creation of a franchise unit is a consequential action.\n\nThe form collects:\n\n- **Business identity.** Business name (required) and web address (derived from the name and editable; it must be unique across the Platform, and a conflict is rejected with an error naming the address already in use).\n- **Owner.** Owner name and owner email (both required) and owner phone. The owner email is where the portal invite is sent, so confirm it is correct.\n- **Location.** Street address, city, state, postal code, phone, and business email. This becomes the primary physical location record.\n- **WordPress URL** (optional). For a franchisee moving an existing site, the Platform records the address for the transition.\n- **Royalty rate** (optional at signup). Entered as a number and stored on the agreement. The rate itself is a commercial term of the agreement, governed by the Franchise Fees & Royalties Policy (§2.4.34), which governs.\n- **Send invite** (on by default). Turn it off only if you are provisioning ahead of the owner's start date and will send the invite later.\n\n## Provisioning steps\n\nSubmitting the form runs provisioning as one operation, in this order:\n\n1. Create the franchise unit — the franchise's identity on the Platform, to which everything else is attached.\n2. Create the franchise location record — business name, web address, address, contact details, WordPress URL, and active status.\n3. Create the franchise agreement record — pending status, carrying the royalty rate and fee schedule references, pending until the agreement is signed.\n4. Create the physical location record — the primary physical location, so location-aware features such as booking, local inventory, and tax resolve correctly from the first day.\n5. Create the portal user for the owner — the owner as a portal user with the franchise owner role. This is the login that runs the franchise.\n6. Write the audit record for the provisioning.\n7. Send the invite email to the owner email, unless the invite was turned off.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Provisioning succeeds or fails as a whole. If any step fails, no partial franchise is left behind. If the form reports an error, correct the input and resubmit. Do not create any of the seven items by hand: records created by hand bypass the audit record and the invite process and corrupt the franchise's record.\n\n## After provisioning: the opening checklist\n\nProvisioning is the beginning of onboarding, not the end. Complete the opening checklist before you open:\n\n1. **Team onboarding.** Create staff accounts under the franchise, assign roles, and enroll staff in training. Staff sign in to the franchise's portals.\n2. **Location setup.** Configure services, pricing, hours, and appointment settings for the physical location.\n3. **Supply and catalog.** Select the opening assortment from the enterprise catalog and confirm supply.\n4. **Payments.** Set up the franchise's payment account for its own transactions. Payout arrangements must be live before opening.\n5. **Brand standards review.** Confirm signage, interior, uniforms, and customer experience against the Franchise Brand Standards Policy (§2.4.36), which governs. The pre-opening review checks compliance.\n\n## Problems and how to handle them\n\n### Web address already in use\n\nThe address must be unique across the Platform. If it is taken, the form returns an error naming the conflict. Choose a different address.\n\n### Invite email does not arrive\n\nThe franchise records are created even if the email fails, and the response notes that the email was not sent. Re-send the invite from portal user management. Do not run the whole signup again.\n\n### Agreement not signed\n\nThe franchise and location may be configured while the agreement sits at pending status.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Do not open a franchise without a signed agreement in active status. Operating without a signed agreement is a liability event, not an administrative delay.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about onboarding or provisioning: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices about the franchise agreement: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "franchise-operations-reporting-and-royalties": "\n# Franchise Operations, Reporting and Royalties\n\nThis page explains how an operating franchise reports sales, pays royalties and fees, contributes to the marketing fund, and meets audit and brand standards, and how renewal, transfer, and termination work. This page summarizes the Franchise Fees & Royalties Policy (§2.4.34), which governs.\n\n> [!NOTICE]\n> The Franchise Disclosure Document controls the offer and sale of franchises, and your signed franchise agreement states your commercial terms. No statement in this page makes an earnings representation. Where the franchise law of the state in which your franchised business is located gives you greater rights, that law prevails to the extent of any conflict.\n\n## Reporting: your information duty\n\nYou report gross sales for each reporting period — weekly or monthly, as your agreement states — through the Platform's reporting. The Platform verifies reported figures against payment-processing records; reporting is not self-certification on trust alone.\n\n- **Gross sales is the royalty base.** Its definition — including whether gift-card sales, refunds, and taxes are included or excluded — is set in your agreement. Confirm the definition in writing before you sign; do not assume your understanding matches the Platform's.\n- **The report is due on schedule whether sales were strong or not.** A missing report is itself a compliance matter, separate from what the numbers would have shown. Late reporting follows the same notice process as late payment.\n- **Records support the report.** Keep the underlying records — point-of-sale exports, bank deposits, and tax filings — for the retention period stated in your agreement.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> An audit that finds no supporting records treats the gap as underreporting. Keep complete records for every reporting period.\n\n## Royalties and fees: how payment works\n\nThe fee structure is governed by the Franchise Fees & Royalties Policy (§2.4.34), which governs:\n\n- **Initial franchise fee** — one-time, due on signing, and non-refundable except as the agreement provides.\n- **Royalty** — the ongoing fee for the license and the system, calculated on gross sales at the royalty rate in your agreement.\n- **Marketing fund contribution** — collected alongside royalties, accounted for separately, and governed by the Franchise Marketing Fund Policy (§2.4.35).\n- **Other fees** — transfer fee, renewal fee, audit-cost reimbursement, training fees, technology fees, and late charges, each as the fee schedule in your agreement states. No fee is charged that is not in the agreement or the fee schedule.\n\nThe amount or rate for each item above is a commercial term stated in your individual agreement.\n\n### Collection\n\nCollection runs by ACH debit or by deduction against your connected payouts, on the schedule your agreement states. Requirements:\n\n1. Maintain a valid payment method.\n2. Maintain sufficient collected funds for each scheduled collection.\n\nIf a collection fails, the sequence is:\n\n1. Notice is given at 3 business days.\n2. A second collection attempt is made at 7 business days.\n3. Late charges accrue from the original due date, as the agreement provides.\n\nRoyalties and marketing fund contributions are accounted for separately. The Company does not offset one against the other.\n\n### Fee disputes and non-payment\n\n1. Raise a fee dispute in writing within 30 days of the statement.\n2. Pay undisputed amounts on schedule while the dispute is open.\n\nNon-payment beyond 30 days past due is a material breach. Non-payment beyond 60 days past due is grounds for termination proceedings, subject to notice and an opportunity to cure. Termination for non-payment follows the Franchise Termination Policy (§2.4.41), which governs; it does not happen by informal notice.\n\n## The marketing fund\n\nThe marketing fund is the franchisees' collective advertising money, governed by the Franchise Marketing Fund Policy (§2.4.35), which governs:\n\n- **Separate accounting.** Fund money is not the Company's operating revenue. It is held separately and spent on marketing.\n- **Annual reporting.** Franchisees receive an accounting of how the fund's money was spent. If you contribute to the fund, you are entitled to see the spending.\n- **No cross-subsidy.** Your contribution funds system marketing. It does not fund another franchisee's local shortfall or the Company's general expenses.\n\n## Audits and brand standards\n\nThe Company audits. Audit and inspection rights, including inspection of financial records and the physical location, are governed by the Franchise Audit & Inspection Policy (§2.4.38), which governs. Underreporting found on audit is assessed with interest, and audit costs are allocated to the franchisee above the threshold stated in the policy. Keep accurate books and an audit is a routine review.\n\nBrand standards cover the customer-facing consistency the franchise system offers: signage, interior, uniforms, service protocols, and customer experience, governed by the Franchise Brand Standards Policy (§2.4.36), which governs. Inspections check compliance.\n\nTraining is ongoing, not only initial. New programs, new products, and refresher requirements are delivered through the learning portal and completion is tracked, governed by the Franchise Training Policy (§2.4.37), which governs.\n\n## Renewal, transfer, and termination\n\n- **Renewal.** Your agreement has a term, and the end of the term is a process. Renewal conditions, any renewal fee, and the timeline are stated in your agreement and governed by the Franchise Renewal Policy (§2.4.39), which governs.\n- **Transfer.** Selling your franchise to another person requires Company approval, with a right of first refusal and an approval schedule, governed by the Franchise Transfer Policy (§2.4.40), which governs. The buyer must qualify in the same way you did.\n- **Termination.** Termination follows the Franchise Termination Policy (§2.4.41), which governs: cure periods for breaches that can be cured, and immediate termination only for the causes listed there.\n\nDisputes between the Company and a franchisee follow the path in the Franchise Dispute Resolution Policy (§2.4.42), which governs: negotiation, then mediation, then arbitration. The marketplace's general dispute process does not apply to franchise disputes, because the franchise agreement is its own legal relationship.\n\n## Diligence for prospective franchisees\n\nBefore you commit:\n\n1. Read the agreement offered to you in full.\n2. Confirm every figure is in the agreement itself, not only in conversation.\n3. Have your counsel review the Franchise Disclosure Document.\n4. Ask the Company directly about the timeline to first opening.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about reporting, royalty statements, or the marketing fund: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Fee disputes and other legal notices: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "claiming-your-brand": "\n# Claiming Your Brand\n\nThis page explains how to claim a brand you own so the brand is governed on the Platform, what evidence proves ownership, what claiming gives you, and what it does not give you. This page summarizes the Brand Ownership Verification Policy (§2.4.2), which governs.\n\n## Why claiming matters\n\nClaiming connects your real-world ownership of a brand to the owner of record entry on the registry record. Until that connection exists, the Platform treats your brand as an unclaimed name in the catalog, and an unclaimed brand receives no brand protection, no gating control, and no complaint rights.\n\n## Requirements before you apply\n\nGather the following before you start:\n\n- Your business name, contact name, email, phone, and website.\n- The brand names you own or represent.\n- Ownership evidence (see below).\n\nEvidence that meets the standard:\n\n- A trademark registration in your name or your company's name.\n- A letter from the manufacturer naming you as the brand owner or authorized brand manager.\n- A distributor or licensing agreement that names the brand and names you.\n\nEvidence that does not meet the standard includes screenshots of a storefront on another marketplace, a domain registration alone, or a statement that the brand is widely known to be yours. Read the Brand Ownership Verification Policy (§2.4.2), which governs, before you apply. A weak first submission adds a round trip.\n\nIf you are a distributor or agency rather than the owner, say so in the application. An authorized representative may claim management rights without owning the brand. The Company verifies the authorization with the actual owner, and the owner of record remains the owner.\n\n## How to claim your brand\n\n1. Go to the seller page.\n2. Select the brand partner application: \"Brand partner — Own a pet brand? Claim your brand portal.\"\n3. Complete the form with your business name, contact name, email, phone, website, and the brand names you own or represent.\n4. Submit the application. The application enters the brand team's review queue.\n5. Wait for the brand team to contact you by email. There is no instant approval; review is manual.\n6. Provide your ownership evidence when the brand team requests it.\n7. When verification is complete, the registry record names your seller account as owner of record and the brand status becomes active.\n\n## What claiming gives you\n\nOnce your brand is verified and active, you receive the brand owner tools:\n\n- **Gating control.** You decide whether your brand is gated, so sellers need your authorization or a qualifying purchase order to list it. See Brand Gating and Authorized Sellers (§3.4.3). Gating is governed by the Brand Gating Policy (§2.4.3).\n- **Listing control.** You set which listing fields are brand-controlled and which remain seller-editable. See Listing Content Standards and Listing Control (§3.4.4). Listing control is governed by the Brand Listing Control Policy (§2.4.14).\n- **Complaint and takedown.** You may file infringement complaints against listings, with a counter-notice process for the seller. See Complaints, Takedowns and Anti-Counterfeit (§3.4.7). Complaints are governed by the Brand Complaint & Takedown Policy (§2.4.12).\n- **Brand analytics.** You receive aggregated marketplace data for your brand, such as search performance, conversion, and review sentiment, governed by the Brand Data & Analytics Policy (§2.4.17). Individual seller data is not disclosed to you.\n\nUse of the brand portal is governed by the Brand Portal Terms (§2.4.5).\n\n## What claiming does not give you\n\n- You cannot suppress the Company's own first-party listings of your brand. Products sold by the Company are \"Sold by Portal Shop.\" Brand gating is a seller-side control and does not apply to first-party inventory. Using complaints or portal tools against first-party listings is abuse of the brand program under the Unauthorized Seller Enforcement Policy (§2.4.11).\n- You cannot see other sellers' private data. Your reports show aggregate performance for your brand only.\n- You cannot rewrite the Platform's policies for your brand. You choose from the instruments the policies provide; you do not create new ones.\n\n## Special cases\n\n### Two companies claim the same brand\n\nBoth applications are held. The brand team asks both parties for ownership evidence, and the brand stays at its current status until one party produces conclusive evidence. If neither party does, the brand stays unclaimed and neither party receives owner tools. The Company does not decide trademark disputes.\n\n### You sold the brand\n\nTell the Company. Ownership transfer requires verification of the buyer, and ownership moves only after the new owner proves it.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> If you sell a brand and do not notify the Company, the buyer cannot receive owner tools and you remain responsible for the obligations attached to the brand record.\n\n### Your brand is already sold through first-party supply\n\nClaiming still works, and you still receive the full toolkit. You do not receive a veto over the first-party assortment. Products sourced through legitimate wholesale supply are sold by the Company on its own right.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about claiming a brand or ownership evidence: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices about ownership disputes: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "brand-gating-and-authorized-sellers": "\n# Brand Gating and Authorized Sellers\n\nThis page explains brand gating: how a brand becomes gated, how a seller proves authorization to list a gated brand, how long an authorization lasts, and what happens when a seller lists a gated brand without authorization. This page summarizes the Brand Gating Policy (§2.4.3), which governs.\n\n## What gating means\n\nA gated brand is a brand where the registry record requires proof before selling. A seller may list a gated brand only while holding a current, approved authorization. Without an authorization, the seller may not list the brand.\n\n> [!DEFINITION]\n> **Authorization** — a current, approved permission recorded for a named seller to list a named gated brand, supported by purchase-order evidence or a written authorization from the brand owner or manufacturer.\n\n## How a brand becomes gated\n\nGating is designated; it is not automatic.\n\n1. The brand owner requests gating after claiming the brand. See Claiming Your Brand (§3.4.2).\n2. Or the Company designates a brand for gating because it falls in a high-risk category, such as ingestibles or topical pesticides, where a counterfeit or mishandled product can harm an animal.\n3. The Company flags the registry record. From that point, the gate applies to every seller listing attempt for the brand.\n\nGating is a seller-side control only. It does not apply to first-party inventory — products sold by the Company as \"Sold by Portal Shop.\" Brand owners do not have a veto over the Company's own sourcing. This rule is stated in the Brand Gating Policy (§2.4.3), which governs.\n\n## How a seller proves authorization\n\nA seller proves authorization by one of two paths. Requirements must be met before the listing goes live.\n\n### Path 1: Purchase order\n\nProvide a purchase order that meets all of the following:\n\n- Dated within the last 180 days.\n- Itemized by product.\n- Shows quantities consistent with the assortment the seller is listing.\n- Issued by the brand owner, the manufacturer, or a distributor the brand owner has named as authorized.\n\nA purchase order for a small quantity does not support a listing for a much larger assortment.\n\n### Path 2: Written authorization\n\nProvide a written authorization that meets all of the following:\n\n- Names you as the seller.\n- Covers the brand.\n- Is signed by a person with authority to sign it.\n- Is current — unexpired and not revoked.\n\nAn authorization with no renewal that has expired does not count.\n\n### Review and expiry\n\n1. Upload your evidence with your authorization request.\n2. Catalog operations review the evidence. The review decision is given within 5 business days.\n3. If approved, the authorization is recorded with an expiry date. An authorization lasts no more than 12 months from approval.\n4. Renew before the authorization expires. There is no grace period after expiry.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Listings for a gated brand are suppressed automatically when the authorization lapses. Renew before expiry to avoid suppression.\n\nThe authorization lifecycle is governed by the Brand Gating Policy (§2.4.3) and the Authorized Seller Policy (§2.4.4), which govern.\n\n## Listing a gated brand today\n\nGet authorization first, even before you create the listing. Enforcement is carried out through listing review. A seller who lists first and is later found in review without authorization faces suppression, and the fact that a form accepted the listing is not a defense.\n\n## Listing without authorization: what happens\n\nListing a gated brand without a current authorization results in suppression of the listing. Where the unauthorized listing involves counterfeit or infringing goods, the matter is treated as a counterfeit or intellectual property matter, with more serious consequences up to removal from the Platform.\n\nIf your listings were suppressed only because an authorization lapsed, and you then obtain a renewal approval, your listings are restored within 24 hours of the renewal approval. No separate appeal is needed for a lapse cured by renewal.\n\nBrand owners also have duties. Using gating complaints to harass legitimate authorized sellers, filing complaints in bad faith, or delaying authorization renewals to pressure sellers is abuse of the brand program under the Unauthorized Seller Enforcement Policy (§2.4.11), which governs, and may result in enforcement against the brand owner.\n\n## Appeals\n\nYou may appeal a denial or revocation of an authorization. An authenticity or sourcing appeal must include supplier invoices that meet the invoice evidence standard and, where required, brand authorization.\n\n1. Prepare your invoices and authorization evidence.\n2. Submit your appeal with the evidence attached, stating the decision you are appealing and the grounds.\n3. The appeal is decided under the enforcement and appeals process for the Platform.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about gating or an authorization request: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Appeals and legal notices about a denial or revocation: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "listing-content-standards-and-listing-control": "\n# Listing Content Standards and Listing Control\n\nThis page explains which listing fields the brand owner controls, which fields belong to the seller making the offer, the content standards every listing for your brand must meet, and what to do when a listing is wrong. This page summarizes the Brand Listing Content Standards (§2.4.6), which governs.\n\n## Brand-controlled fields and seller offer fields\n\nWhen you claim your brand, control is divided by the kind of field.\n\n**Brand-controlled fields** are set by the brand owner and apply to every listing of the brand's products:\n\n- Product titles in the brand's standard form.\n- Product description.\n- Image set.\n- Enhanced content.\n- Brand name itself.\n\nA seller may not rewrite your description to add keywords or replace your product photography with a different image. If a seller's listing shows the wrong title or images for your product, that is a content violation.\n\n**Seller offer fields** belong to the seller making the offer:\n\n- Price, subject to Minimum Advertised Price where one applies. See Minimum Advertised Price (§3.4.6).\n- Quantity available.\n- Handling and shipping time.\n- Condition notes for the specific unit.\n- Seller SKU.\n\nThe brand owner does not set offer fields and cannot override them through content tools. Pricing discipline is handled through Minimum Advertised Price, not through the description editor.\n\nThis division gives shoppers a consistent presentation of the same product no matter which seller fulfills it, while preserving each seller's control over their own offer. Listing control is governed by the Brand Listing Control Policy (§2.4.14), which governs.\n\n## Content standards for every listing\n\nEvery listing carrying your brand must meet the following standards. As brand owner, you are the first line of review for your own products.\n\n- **Titles** follow the standard form: brand, product line, item, and key attribute such as size, flavor, or count. No keyword stuffing, no text in all capitals, and no promotional text in the title.\n- **Images** must show the actual product. The primary image shows the product on a clean background; lifestyle and explanatory images follow. No watermarks, no competitor logos, and no promotional badges placed inside the image.\n- **Descriptions** describe the product as the manufacturer specifies it: ingredients, guaranteed analysis for foods, feeding directions, net weight, and warnings. Marketing claims must be substantiated. A claim such as \"vet recommended\" requires the recommendation to support it, and a claim such as \"all-natural\" must meet the applicable definition.\n- **Identifiers** — GTIN or UPC where the product has one — must be accurate. An incorrect identifier breaks catalog matching and is treated as a listing-accuracy violation.\n\n## One product, one record\n\nWhere the catalog already holds a product record, seller listings attach to that record rather than creating duplicates. The same product from two sellers is one product with two offers, not two products. A seller who creates a duplicate product to avoid brand-controlled fields violates the catalog rules, and duplicates are merged.\n\n## What to do when a listing is wrong\n\nBrand owners do not edit a seller's listing directly and do not have write access to a seller's offer fields. Use this process instead:\n\n1. Flag the listing through the brand complaint channel. See Complaints, Takedowns and Anti-Counterfeit (§3.4.7). Identify the listing and the specific field that violates the standard.\n2. Catalog operations reviews the complaint. Content violations such as wrong images or stuffed titles are corrected or suppressed.\n3. If the same seller repeats the violation, the matter is handled as a conduct matter, not only as a content correction.\n\nFor counterfeit or infringing listings — not merely wrong content, but a fake or infringing product — use the takedown path in Complaints, Takedowns and Anti-Counterfeit (§3.4.7), which runs on a faster schedule and carries repeat-infringer consequences. Takedowns are governed by the Brand Complaint & Takedown Policy (§2.4.12), which governs.\n\n## How control is enforced\n\nBrand-controlled fields are enforced by review and complaint. Monitor your listings, flag violations promptly with the specific field identified, and do not assume an edit you have not flagged has been prevented.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about listing content or a listing complaint: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices about infringing listings: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "brand-assets-and-logos": "\n# Brand Assets and Logos\n\nThis page explains how brand assets — logos, product photography, and lifestyle imagery — enter the Platform, where they appear, the standards they must meet, and what sellers may and may not do with them. This page summarizes the Brand Asset Standards (§2.4.7), which governs.\n\n## Getting your logo into the Platform\n\nRequirements before you send a logo:\n\n- The brand name.\n- A clean logo file: a transparent PNG or SVG at a reasonable resolution, named sensibly before you send it. The file system removes unusual characters and shortens long names, so a file with a long working name may be stored under a shorter name.\n\nBrand logos are uploaded by the brand team through the admin tools. Brand owners do not upload directly; the brand team handles uploads as part of onboarding and asset updates.\n\n1. Send the brand team the brand name and the logo file.\n2. The brand team uploads the file to the Platform's media storage under the brands folder and stores the resulting public link on the brand's admin record.\n3. Verify the result. Open the brand record and confirm the logo link loads.\n\n> [!NOTICE]\n> The brand name is required for an upload; other fields are optional. If you omit the public address, it is derived from the name. Check the derived address before you publish anything against it. If a storage upload fails, the process may continue with no logo link rather than stopping with an error, so always verify that the record holds a working logo link after upload.\n\nThe admin brand record holds name, public address, logo link, description, active flag, and sort order. This is the admin-managed presentation list. It is not the marketplace registry, which governs ownership and gating. See Brand Registry (§3.4.1).\n\n## Where logos appear\n\nBrand logos appear wherever the Platform presents brands: the A–Z directory, brand filter options, brand cards, and brand landing pages. A missing or broken logo link affects all of these at once. The brand still sells, but it presents as a text link where other brands present a visual identity.\n\nIf your logo is not rendering:\n\n1. Check the brand record's logo link and confirm it loads.\n2. Confirm the file still exists in media storage.\n3. Ask the brand team to re-upload through the admin tools if either check fails.\n\n## Asset standards\n\nBrand imagery must meet the following standards, stated in the Brand Asset Standards (§2.4.7), which governs:\n\n- **Logos** must be the current, official mark — not an old version and not a low-quality copy taken from another document.\n- **Product images** must depict the actual product as currently packaged. If you reformulate or repackage, update the images with the change. Outdated packaging imagery on a new formula is a listing-accuracy problem.\n- **No third-party marks** in your assets: no retailer logos, no certification badges you do not hold, and no endorsement claims placed inside the image file.\n\n## What sellers may do with your assets\n\nWhen your assets are on the Platform, sellers listing your products may use them only to present those products accurately. The license is governed by the Brand Asset License Policy (§2.4.8), which governs.\n\nPermitted:\n\n- Displaying your logo and product images on listings of your genuine products, at the resolution the Platform serves.\n\nNot permitted:\n\n- Altering the logo, including recoloring, stretching, or adding text.\n- Using your assets to sell another brand's products.\n- Using your assets in off-platform advertising without a separate agreement.\n- Implying an endorsement relationship that does not exist.\n\nIf you are a seller: presence of an image online is not a license. If the brand's assets are in the Platform's media system, use those. If they are not, do not copy the brand's website or a search result — request the assets through the brand team.\n\n## Misuse of assets\n\nIf your assets are misused — for example, an altered logo on a listing or your product photography used to sell a substitute product — treat the matter as an intellectual property complaint, not a content suggestion. Use the takedown path in Complaints, Takedowns and Anti-Counterfeit (§3.4.7). Asset misuse tied to counterfeit goods is handled under the Brand Anti-Counterfeit Program Policy (§2.4.13), which governs.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about logo uploads or brand assets: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices about misuse of brand assets: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "minimum-advertised-price": "\n# Minimum Advertised Price\n\nThis page explains Minimum Advertised Price (MAP): what MAP is, what it is not, how to put MAP in force for your brand on the Platform, what counts as a violation, and how violations are handled. This page summarizes the Minimum Advertised Price (MAP) Policy (§2.4.9), which governs.\n\n> [!NOTICE]\n> MAP is legally sensitive. Have your MAP policy reviewed by your own counsel before you publish it on this Platform. The Platform provides the process for recording and acting on a MAP schedule; the legal validity of your MAP policy is your responsibility.\n\n## What MAP is\n\nA Minimum Advertised Price policy is a unilateral announcement by the brand owner. The brand states that it will not supply resellers who advertise its products below a stated price. The brand sets the price, publishes the policy, and enforces it by choosing who it does business with.\n\n## What MAP is not\n\nMAP is not an agreement with sellers about the price they will charge. MAP must not become a negotiated arrangement — for example, an exchange of better terms for a promise to advertise at a stated price, or sellers agreeing with each other to hold a price. Conduct of that kind is assessed differently under antitrust law. The compliance facts are stated in the Minimum Advertised Price (MAP) Policy (§2.4.9), which governs. In short:\n\n- You announce MAP. You do not negotiate MAP terms with individual sellers.\n- You enforce MAP through your supply relationships, by deciding whether to continue supplying a violator. You do not fine a seller, recover the amount of a discount, or coordinate with other sellers against a violator.\n- MAP covers advertised price, not selling price, unless your written policy states otherwise with precision. The Platform does not decide that distinction for you beyond what your policy states.\n\nPricing conduct is also governed by the Brand Pricing Integrity Policy (§2.4.10), which governs.\n\n## How to put MAP in force\n\nRequirements: your MAP terms must be written before any enforcement, and sellers must receive them before enforcement begins. Retroactive MAP enforcement does not exist.\n\n1. Publish the policy. State which products are covered, the MAP price for each, what counts as advertising, and the effective date.\n2. Register the MAP schedule against your brand. Ask the brand team to record the schedule against your brand record so enforcement has a schedule to check against.\n3. Allow a transition period. Sellers already advertising below MAP receive notice and an opportunity to correct the price before enforcement begins.\n\n## What counts as a violation\n\nA MAP violation is an advertised price below the MAP floor, in a channel your policy covers, after the effective date, by a seller subject to the policy.\n\nCommon cases:\n\n- **Strikethrough pricing.** Advertising \"was $50, now $40\" when MAP is $45 is a violation. The advertised price is $40, whatever the earlier price says. The dollar figures in this example are illustrative only.\n- **Coupons and sitewide promotions.** Whether a seller's coupon applied to your product violates MAP depends on your policy's terms and on who funds the coupon. Promotions funded by the Platform are the Platform's responsibility to reconcile with MAP schedules, not the seller's.\n- **Cart price and advertised price.** If your policy defines the violation at the advertised price, a lower price shown only in the cart is not a violation. If your policy is written to cover the in-cart price, it is. The policy text controls, which is why the text must be precise.\n- **First-party listings.** The Company's own first-party inventory, sold as \"Sold by Portal Shop,\" is not subject to your MAP policy. MAP is a seller-side instrument. A first-party listing below your MAP floor is not a violation to report.\n\n## How violations are handled\n\nMAP enforcement is review-driven. To report a violation:\n\n1. Identify the listing, the advertised price, the MAP floor in your published schedule, and the effective date.\n2. File the violation through the brand complaint channel. See Complaints, Takedowns and Anti-Counterfeit (§3.4.7).\n\nHandling is graduated: notice and an opportunity to correct first, then suppression of the violating offers, then review of the seller's authorization for repeat violations. A MAP violation does not move directly to termination; the process is built around correction because most first violations are mistakes.\n\nIf you are a seller and you receive a MAP notice, check three things before you respond:\n\n1. Is the product actually covered by a published MAP schedule?\n2. Is your advertised price actually below the floor?\n3. Was the policy in effect before your listing went live?\n\nIf any answer is no, say so in your response with the evidence. If all three answers are yes, correct the price and confirm the correction.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about recording a MAP schedule or reporting a violation: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices about a MAP dispute: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "complaints-takedowns-and-anti-counterfeit": "\n# Complaints, Takedowns and Anti-Counterfeit\n\nThis page explains the three paths for problems with listings of your brand — content complaint, intellectual property takedown, and the anti-counterfeit program — when to use each path, how to file, and the rights of the seller on the other side. This page summarizes the Brand Complaint & Takedown Policy (§2.4.12), which governs.\n\n## Choose the right path\n\nUsing the wrong path slows the result. Using the right path badly slows it more.\n\n- **Content complaint** — the listing is wrong but not fake: wrong images, a stuffed title, an inaccurate description, or wrong brand attribution. This is a quality problem. It goes through catalog review and is corrected or suppressed. See Listing Content Standards and Listing Control (§3.4.4).\n- **Intellectual property takedown** — the listing infringes your trademark or copyright, or the product is counterfeit. This is a legal matter and runs on the faster takedown schedule, with counter-notice rights for the seller.\n- **Anti-counterfeit program** — you have evidence of systematic counterfeiting: multiple listings, a network of sellers, or test purchases confirming fakes. This path triggers an investigation, not only listing action.\n\nDo not file a content problem as a takedown. A wrong image is not trademark infringement, and filing it as infringement harms your credibility for genuine cases.\n\n## How to file a takedown\n\nRequirements: a takedown notice must contain all four items below. A notice missing any item is returned for completion instead of entering the queue.\n\n1. Identification of the right you own — the trademark registration number or the copyrighted work. \"My brand\" alone is not an identification.\n2. Identification of the infringing listing — the URL or listing ID, and what specifically infringes: the mark as used, the copied image, or the counterfeit product.\n3. A good-faith statement that you believe the use is unauthorized. This is a legal statement, not a formality.\n4. Your contact information and signature. An electronic signature is accepted.\n\nFile the complete notice through the brand complaint channel to the brand team. Intake is handled by the brand team as a process, so the completeness of your notice directly determines its speed: a complete notice moves; an incomplete notice is returned.\n\n## Counter-notice and seller rights\n\nTakedown is not one-sided.\n\n1. The seller receives notice of what was removed and why.\n2. The seller may file a counter-notice identifying the removed listing, stating in good faith that the removal was a mistake or a misidentification, and providing contact information.\n3. On a valid counter-notice, the listing is restored unless you seek court or administrative relief within the window stated in the policy.\n\nThis process exists because takedown abuse is real. Filing takedowns you know are without basis is abuse of the brand program under the Unauthorized Seller Enforcement Policy (§2.4.11), which governs, and may result in enforcement against you.\n\n## Repeat infringers\n\nThe Platform applies a repeat-infringer policy: sellers who accumulate valid infringement findings face escalating consequences up to permanent removal. The structure is stated in the Brand Complaint & Takedown Policy (§2.4.12), which governs.\n\nStrikes are counted on valid findings, not on filings. A takedown that was successfully counter-noticed is not a strike against the seller. Complaint accuracy is tracked: brand owners whose complaints consistently fail review lose expedited handling.\n\n## The anti-counterfeit program\n\nWhen the problem is systematic, the anti-counterfeit program provides heavier measures, governed by the Brand Anti-Counterfeit Program Policy (§2.4.13), which governs:\n\n- **Test purchases.** The Platform purchases suspected counterfeit products and documents what arrives, keeping a clean record of the order, the unboxing, and the comparison against the genuine product.\n- **Authorization freeze.** During an investigation, brand authorizations for the affected brand may be suspended Platform-wide. No new authorized sellers are added until the investigation closes.\n- **Network action.** Where evidence shows coordinated sellers, enforcement runs against the network, not one listing at a time.\n- **Evidence preservation.** Listings, seller records, and communications relevant to the investigation are preserved. Destruction of evidence by a seller under investigation is itself a serious violation.\n\nIf you see your product counterfeited as a pattern, do not start with many individual takedowns:\n\n1. Gather the pattern: the listings, the sellers, and what you have verified.\n2. Contact the brand team with the pattern.\n3. Let the investigation run in a coordinated way.\n\nIndividual takedowns filed in sequence can alert the network; a coordinated action does not.\n\n## If your listing is taken down\n\nIf you are a seller whose listing was taken down:\n\n1. Read the notice carefully and check whether the claim is valid. Wrong brand attribution does occur, and sometimes the complaint is correct.\n2. Either correct the listing and re-list in compliance, or file a counter-notice if the claim is wrong.\n3. Keep your authorization evidence current. In a counterfeit investigation, sellers who produce purchase orders and authorizations promptly are cleared promptly.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Do not re-list identical taken-down content under a new listing ID to avoid a takedown. Evasion is a separate and more serious violation than the original complaint.\n\nThe effect of brand removal on listings and complaints is governed by the Brand Registry Removal & Termination Policy (§2.4.20).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about filing a complaint or the status of a takedown: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Takedown notices, counter-notices, and other legal notices: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "brand-stores-and-advertising": "\n# Brand Stores and Advertising\n\nThis page explains brand stores and brand advertising: what a brand store is, who is eligible, the rules for advertising, how promotions interact with Minimum Advertised Price, what data you receive, and how fees work. This page summarizes the Brand Store Policy (§2.4.15), which governs.\n\n## Brand stores\n\nA brand store is a curated storefront for a single brand on the Platform: the brand's products, story, and imagery in one place shoppers can browse. It is governed by the Brand Store Policy (§2.4.15), which governs.\n\nWhat you control:\n\n- Which of your products are featured and how they are organized.\n- The brand narrative and imagery, within the asset standards in Brand Assets and Logos (§3.4.5).\n- Promotional placements for your own products.\n\nWhat you do not control:\n\n- Checkout. Checkout is the Platform's.\n- The product data model. Prices still follow the pricing rules and inventory still belongs to the seller making the offer.\n- Other brands' presence on the Platform.\n\nA brand store is not a separate website. It lives inside the Platform, uses the Platform's cart and checkout, and is subject to the Platform's policies. The store promotes your brand; it does not exempt your brand from any policy.\n\n### Eligibility\n\nRequirements before you request a brand store:\n\n1. You hold an active, verified brand record.\n2. You are the owner of record for that brand. See Claiming Your Brand (§3.4.2).\n\nA brand under an ownership dispute or in suspended status does not receive a store until the underlying issue is resolved.\n\n## Advertising\n\nBrand owners may run campaigns promoting their products, including sponsored placement in search and category results, brand-featured placements, and promotional pricing support. Advertising is governed by the Brand Advertising Policy (§2.4.16), which governs. The rules that keep advertising accurate are:\n\n- **Ads are labeled.** Sponsored placements are identified as sponsored. No placement may be presented in a way a reasonable shopper would mistake for an organic result.\n- **Ads make no claim the product page cannot support.** If an ad states a claim, the product page must carry the substantiation. Advertising review checks the claim against the listing.\n- **No disparagement of competitors.** Your ad promotes your product; it does not attack a competitor's product. A comparative claim requires substantiation the Platform can verify.\n- **Budget and billing are explicit.** Ad spend is tracked, invoiced, and reported against the budget you set. No additional spend is charged beyond that budget.\n\n## Promotions and the MAP boundary\n\nPromotions interact with Minimum Advertised Price, and the funding source decides which process applies. See Minimum Advertised Price (§3.4.6).\n\n- A **brand-funded promotion**, where you fund the discount, is set by you on your terms and executed by the Platform.\n- A **seller-funded promotion** priced below your MAP floor is a MAP matter, not a promotion matter. It is handled through the MAP process.\n- A **Platform-funded promotion** is the Platform's responsibility to reconcile against MAP schedules.\n\nBefore you plan any promotion on your brand's products:\n\n1. Check the MAP schedule for the products involved.\n2. Coordinate the promotion with the brand team.\n\nThe most common brand program dispute after takedowns is a promotion that triggered a MAP issue no one checked in advance.\n\n## Data you receive\n\nBrand stores and advertising generate performance data. The line is stated in the Brand Data & Analytics Policy (§2.4.17), which governs:\n\n- You receive aggregated performance for your brand: impressions, clicks, conversion, spend, and return on ad spend.\n- You do not receive individual seller data, individual shopper data, or anything that would let you reconstruct a competitor's business.\n\n## Fees\n\nBrand stores and advertising placements carry program fees. A fee is payable only if the fee is stated in the Brand Program Fees Policy (§2.4.19), which governs. No fee is charged that is not in the published schedule. When you set an advertising budget, confirm the current schedule before you commit spend.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Advertising spend follows the budget you set and is invoiced as tracked. Review the fee schedule and your campaign budget before launch; spend once incurred is payable under the Brand Program Fees Policy (§2.4.19), which governs.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about brand stores, advertising, or program fees: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices about advertising claims or fees: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "manufacturer-program": "\n# Manufacturer Program\n\nThis page explains the Manufacturer Program: how to apply as a manufacturer, the rules of selling direct as the manufacturer of record, the product data standard you must meet, and your warranty, recall, and regulatory duties. This page summarizes the Manufacturer Direct Program Policy (§2.4.21), which governs.\n\n## Manufacturers on the Platform\n\nAs a manufacturer, you make the products. That gives you a direct relationship with the Platform that resellers do not have, and a distinct set of obligations, because when something is wrong with the product itself — formulation, labeling, or safety — responsibility leads to you.\n\n## How to apply as a manufacturer\n\nRequirements and information to prepare:\n\n- Business name, contact name, email, and website.\n- Product categories you manufacture.\n- Production capacity. This field is specific to manufacturers. The merchandising team uses it to assess whether you can supply what you propose to list.\n\nTo apply:\n\n1. Go to the seller page.\n2. Select the manufacturer application: \"Manufacturer — Make pet products and want them on our shelves? Apply as a manufacturer.\"\n3. Complete the form with the information above.\n4. Submit the application. The application enters the merchandising team's review queue.\n5. Respond to the team's follow-up by email and provide any further information requested.\n\nReview for manufacturers goes deeper than review for resellers. The team assesses whether you actually manufacture or are a distributor presenting as a manufacturer, your regulatory posture, and your product data readiness.\n\nAuthorization issuance to sellers is governed by the Manufacturer Authorization Issuance Policy (§2.4.22).\n\n## Selling direct as a manufacturer\n\nThe Manufacturer Direct Program Policy (§2.4.21), which governs, lets you sell on the Platform as the manufacturer of record: your products, your brand, and your content, sold by you. Two rules define the program:\n\n- **No dual-standing advantage.** As a manufacturer-seller you receive no ranking benefit, no data advantage, and no enforcement preference over resellers selling the same products. Your listings compete on the same terms. Manufacturing status does not change marketplace placement.\n- **Brand tools stay within their purpose.** If you also own the brand, your brand owner tools may not be used against lawful authorized resellers of your products. Gating, complaints, and takedowns address counterfeits and unauthorized sellers, not resellers you wish to remove as competitors. Using brand tools as a competitive weapon against legitimate distribution is abuse of the program.\n\n## Product data standards\n\nManufacturers are held to the highest product-data standard on the Platform, because your data is the source data. The standard is stated in the Manufacturer Product Data Standards (§2.4.23), which governs. Your product data must include:\n\n- **Complete specifications.** Ingredients; guaranteed analysis for foods and treats, covering the eight required labeling elements — product name, species, quantity statement, guaranteed analysis, ingredient statement, nutritional adequacy, feeding directions, and manufacturer name and address; net quantity; country of origin; and lot or batch coding.\n- **Accurate imagery.** Current packaging and current formulation. When either changes, the data updates with the change.\n- **Identifiers.** GTINs for every product that has one. The catalog matches on identifiers; an incorrect GTIN creates duplicate products.\n- **Regulatory flags.** If a product is a pesticide, an animal drug, or a feed requiring state registration, declare that fact at listing. Do not wait for a compliance review to surface it. An EPA registration number is required at listing time for a pesticide product covered by federal registration: no number, no listing.\n\n## Warranty and recalls\n\nWarranty obligations sit with you as the manufacturer, governed by the Manufacturer Warranty Policy (§2.4.24), which governs. If your product fails and the failure is a manufacturing defect, you provide the remedy — replacement, refund, or repair. That duty survives your exit from the program: termination does not end warranty duties for products already sold. Program exit is governed by the Manufacturer Program Termination Policy (§2.4.28).\n\nRecalls are governed by the Manufacturer Recall Cooperation Policy (§2.4.25), which governs. The procedure is:\n\n1. Notify the Platform within 24 hours of deciding to recall a product, or of being ordered to recall it.\n2. Provide the affected lot and batch scope.\n3. Bear the cost of the remedy.\n\nThe Platform carries out the marketplace side — buyer notification, suppression of listings for affected lots, and return handling — but the recall is yours.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> If you learn of a safety issue, notify the Platform promptly under the recall procedure above. Delay creates product-liability exposure, and the Platform preserves records of when notice was given.\n\n## Regulatory compliance\n\nPet products are subject to several regulatory regimes. The Platform states them as compliance facts, not as legal advice. Compliance is governed by the Manufacturer Regulatory Compliance Policy (§2.4.26), which governs:\n\n- **AAFCO** standards for pet food labeling, including the eight required elements above.\n- **EPA registration under FIFRA** for pesticide products — federal registration plus state registrations where required.\n- **FDA Center for Veterinary Medicine** requirements for animal drugs. Animal drugs may be sold only through licensed pharmacy channels with a valid veterinarian-client-patient relationship, never as ordinary marketplace listings.\n- **State feed registration** for pet foods sold across states.\n\nThe Platform's listing checks verify the registrations it can verify. The underlying compliance is yours. Acceptance of a listing by a form is not a defense to a regulatory requirement.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about applying or product data: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Recall notices and other legal notices: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "how-bookings-reach-the-salon": "\n# How Bookings Reach the Salon\n\nThis page explains how an online booking reaches your schedule: the five steps the customer completes, what checkout verifies before anything is saved, and what information the finished booking carries. This page summarizes the Appointment Operations Policy (§2.6.15), which governs.\n\n## The five steps the customer sees\n\nEvery appointment on your schedule arrives through the same online booking flow.\n\n1. **The pet.** The customer enters the pet's name, breed from the breed list, birth date, and weight in pounds. Weight is the pricing key. Weight converts to a size tier — Small, Medium, Large, or X-Large — and every package price is stored per tier, with a separate member price for Bath Club members. The same package at two different tiers is two different prices by design.\n2. **The service menu.** The menu loads in a single view: breeds, packages with all tier prices, premium treatments (one per appointment at most), flat-priced add-ons, the salon's tax rate, the location card, and the location's deposit rule. The customer sees rendered prices, and the server verifies every number again at checkout. If a customer says the site showed one price and checkout shows another, the checkout repricing is the authority; the menu prices were display values.\n3. **The slot.** The customer picks a date and time from the availability check. See Schedule, Slots and Availability (§3.6.2).\n4. **Extras.** The customer may add a promo code or Perks points. Both are validated on the server.\n5. **Payment choice.** The customer pays in full, or pays the deposit now and the balance at the salon. The deposit is never a fixed amount for all locations. It is the location's rule from Booking Rules settings: a toggle, a flat amount, or a percentage of the total. If no rule is set, no deposit is charged and the flow does not offer one. Deposits are governed by the Deposit & Prepayment Policy (§2.6.5), which governs.\n\n## What checkout verifies\n\nWhen the customer confirms, checkout completes the following before the booking is treated as confirmed:\n\n1. **Sign-in check.** No one books anonymously. If the session has expired, the flow pauses with a prompt to sign back in. The booking is not lost.\n2. **Repricing.** The server reprices the entire booking from the current service catalog at the customer's size tier, and at the member price if the customer is an active Bath Club member. Prices sent from the customer's browser are ignored for payment.\n3. **Promo and points check.** The promo code is validated again against the actual booking. Perks points are checked against the customer's balance; if the customer does not hold the points claimed, checkout rejects the claim and shows the actual balance.\n4. **Slot recheck.** Availability is checked again at the moment the booking is written. If two customers seek the same slot, the first completed booking takes it and the second customer is told the slot was just taken and asked to pick another time.\n5. **Records.** The customer record is found or created by email and the account link is refreshed so the customer portal finds the customer. The dog is created or updated against that customer. The booking is created with status Pending Payment, carrying the full priced details: items, subtotal, tax, total, deposit amount, balance due, and payment mode.\n6. **Customer-record update.** Customer records update automatically in the background. An update delay never blocks a booking.\n7. **Payment.** A payment session is issued for the full total or for the deposit. Completed payment changes the booking to Confirmed. Payment confirmation is processed once, so a repeated confirmation does not create a second charge.\n\nTwo cases to know:\n\n- If payment is not configured, the booking still exists and the portal shows its pending state honestly. The salon confirms payment in person.\n- If the location charges no deposit, there is no payment session at booking. The booking stands and the balance is settled at the salon.\n\n## What lands on your schedule\n\nThe booking you see carries: owner name, dog name, breed, weight, size tier, service package name, date, time, notes, phone, email, service price, deposit amount, balance due, payment status such as Unpaid or Deposit Paid, and the assigned groomer.\n\n> [!NOTICE]\n> An unpriced row may display a fallback price with a fallback deposit. Treat those displayed fallbacks as a \"price at intake\" flag, not as the customer's quoted price. Confirm the correct catalog price at intake. See Pricing, Surcharges, Deposits and Discounts (§3.6.5).\n\n## Common questions at the counter\n\n- **Balance due higher than expected after a deposit was paid.** A promo or points adjustment is usually reconciled after redemption, and the booking record is rewritten to the actual redemption. See Pricing, Surcharges, Deposits and Discounts (§3.6.5).\n- **Booking stuck in Pending Payment with no payment session.** The payment step never completed. The slot is held but the money has not been received. Do not treat the booking as confirmed until its status changes.\n\nBooking integrity and record requirements are governed by the Appointment Operations Policy (§2.6.15), which governs.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about a booking, a deposit, or a payment status: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "schedule-slots-and-availability": "\n# Schedule, Slots and Availability\n\nThis page explains how grooming slots are generated, what removes a slot from availability, how double-booking is prevented, and how to read the schedule view. This page summarizes the Scheduling & Availability Policy (§2.6.16), which governs.\n\n## Salon hours and slot structure\n\nThe salon runs on 30-minute slots from 7:00 AM to 6:00 PM, and it is closed on Mondays. These rules are applied by the booking system and decide what every customer sees.\n\nFor any date, the day's slots are generated from 7:00 AM through 5:30 PM, every 30 minutes — 22 slots — and then slots that do not fit are removed.\n\n## What makes a slot unavailable\n\n- **Mondays.** The system reports the salon as closed with the reason \"Closed Mondays.\" No slots are offered and no exception is made through the booking flow.\n- **Existing bookings.** Each booking blocks its start time plus its duration. Where a booking carries no duration, 120 minutes applies. For example, a 10:00 AM groom with a 2-hour duration occupies 10:00 AM to 12:00 PM. A 90-minute service starting at 11:00 AM would run to 12:30 PM and overlaps, so it is not offered.\n- **Blocked times.** A blocked-time entry removes a range of time or a whole day. A whole-day block with a reason such as staff training or deep cleaning closes the date in the same way as a Monday.\n- **Closing time.** A slot remains available only if the whole service finishes before 6:00 PM. A 2-hour service cannot start at 5:00 PM; its last bookable start is 4:00 PM.\n\nA cancelled booking blocks nothing. Any booking with a cancelled status is excluded, so a cancelled 10:00 AM appointment frees the slot immediately. Cancellation handling is covered in Cancellations, No-Shows and Fees (§3.6.6) and governed by the Booking, Cancellation & No-Show Policy (§2.6.6), which governs.\n\n## How double-booking is prevented\n\nAvailability is checked twice:\n\n1. When the customer picks the slot.\n2. Again at the moment the booking is written during checkout. This second check decides.\n\nIf two customers seek the same slot, the first completed booking takes it. The second customer is told the slot was just taken, with a reason they can read, and picks another time. No partial booking is created.\n\nManual bookings created through the portal use the same availability check. You cannot double-book a customer by creating an appointment in the portal while that customer is completing checkout for the same slot.\n\n## Reading the schedule view\n\nThe employee schedule view shows the day's appointments with the customer, dog, and groomer together: name, breed, service, time, duration, price, deposit state, and notes. You can narrow the view by date, by status, and by groomer.\n\nStatuses you will see include Scheduled, Confirmed, Pending Payment, Deposit Paid, Unpaid, Canceled, No Show, and Completed.\n\n### Pending Payment needs judgment\n\nPending Payment means the customer started checkout but the payment did not complete. The slot is held but the appointment is not yet confirmed.\n\n1. Do not turn away a walk-in solely for a Pending Payment slot if the customer never completes payment.\n2. Do not delete the booking yourself, because the customer may still be completing payment.\n3. When in doubt, call the phone number on the booking.\n\n## Booking horizon and availability problems\n\nDo not promise customers a booking cutoff that the system does not apply. If you are unsure how far ahead bookings are accepted at your location, check with your manager before stating a limit to a customer.\n\nTwo failure cases to recognize:\n\n- **No slots load at all.** If the availability check fails, the booking flow cannot show slots. That is a system problem, not a full book. Tell the customer so, rather than saying the salon is fully booked.\n- **Slot shows as available, then fails at checkout.** The slot was taken by another booking seconds earlier. Apologize, offer the nearest alternatives, and help the customer rebook.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about scheduling, blocked times, or a booking conflict: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "intake-and-handling-profiles": "\n# Intake and Handling Profiles\n\nThis page explains the handling profile: what it holds, what intake captures at booking, and the discipline for reading and updating the profile before and after every groom. This page summarizes the Pet Safety and Handling (§2.6.7), which governs.\n\n## Why the profile matters\n\nThe handling profile is the written record of how a specific animal behaves during handling. Reading it before you touch the dog is required. It is the difference between a routine groom and an incident report.\n\n## What the profile holds\n\nEvery dog with a grooming history has a handling profile tied to that dog. It contains:\n\n- **Temperament** — the headline summary. Read this first.\n- **Handling fields by area:** nails, face, feet, ears, dryer, and clippers. Each field answers how the dog reacts in that part of the groom. A dog may be easy for nails and difficult for the dryer. That is why the fields are separate.\n- **Handling notes** — free-text warnings, including bite history, seizure disorders, and handling approaches that make the groom worse for that dog. Red-flag designations are recorded here.\n- **Grooming notes** — cut notes and style history, including what was done last time and what the owner preferred.\n- **Owner notes** — what the owner told the salon, in the owner's words.\n- **Coat fields** — type, texture, length, condition, shedding level, current haircut style, and current body length.\n\n> [!NOTICE]\n> A profile with blank handling fields is not a clean record. It means nothing has been recorded yet. Treat an empty profile as a new dog: work cautiously on the first groom, observe everything, and record it all afterward.\n\n## What intake captures\n\nBooking intake is brief by design. The customer provides name, breed, weight, birth date, and a free-text notes field for temperament and sensitivities at booking. Everything the customer enters appears on the booking's notes, and the salon's notification carries the full intake, including the dog's details, so whoever reads the booking before the appointment sees it.\n\nThe full handling profile is built over time, not at booking. Detailed handling notes on the pet record are written through the customer records system, and the per-area handling fields accumulate as groomers record what they observe. Every groom either confirms the profile or corrects it. Record-keeping is governed by Grooming Records and Continuity of Care (§2.6.17), which governs. The health part of intake is governed by the Vaccination Requirements Policy (§2.6.8), which governs.\n\n## Intake discipline\n\nComplete these steps for every appointment:\n\n1. **Read the profile before the dog is on the table**, not after the first adverse reaction.\n2. **Check red flags first.** Handling notes showing bite history, severe anxiety, seizure disorder, breathing risk for a short-nosed breed, or a senior or fragile designation change the assignment. Under the Pet Safety and Handling (§2.6.7), which governs, a red-flagged pet is assigned only to a groomer whose specialties include special handling.\n3. **Trust the area fields, not only the headline.** A calm temperament with a blank dryer field still requires a careful first introduction to the dryer.\n4. **Write it down the same day.** Anything the dog taught you — a flinch at the clippers near the ears, standing calmly for nails — goes into the profile before you finish your shift. The next groomer depends on today's notes.\n5. **Correct a profile that contradicts observed behavior the same day.** An outdated profile is how incidents happen.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Never let an owner's assurance that a dog \"is fine\" override a red flag in the handling notes. Owners are often inaccurate about their own dogs' behavior under handling. The profile governs the assignment.\n\n## When the profile cannot be found\n\nA customer who books under a different email may create a second customer record, and the dog lookup then misses the existing profile. The profile exists, but the booking does not link to it.\n\n1. If the booking notes describe behavior the linked profile does not show, trust the notes for this appointment.\n2. Handle the dog according to the more cautious information.\n3. Reconcile the records after the appointment so the next booking links correctly.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about a handling profile, a red-flag assignment, or a pet record: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "running-the-appointment": "\n# Running the Appointment\n\nThis page explains how to run a grooming appointment in the employee portal: the step sequence you record, how to message the customer during the groom, and why the groomer notes step must never be skipped. This page summarizes the Appointment Operations Policy (§2.6.15), which governs.\n\n## The step sequence\n\nOnce the dog is in the building, record the appointment through this sequence in order. The steps are the record of what happened, and they drive customer updates. Skipping steps makes the record inaccurate, and the record is what protects you if anything goes wrong later.\n\n1. **Precheck Completed** — intake verification. Confirm the dog matches the booking, read the profile, acknowledge red flags, and confirm vaccination status. This is where Intake and Handling Profiles (§3.6.3) is applied.\n2. **Wash Complete** — bath finished.\n3. **Trimming In Progress** — the cut is underway.\n4. **Nails Manicure Completed** — nails finished.\n5. **Groomer Notes Completed** — the post-groom record: what was done, what was observed, and profile updates.\n6. **Completed** — the appointment is finished and the dog is ready for pickup.\n\nThe middle steps record progress within the groom. They are progress markers on the way to Completed. The appointment's standing is separate — Scheduled, Completed, Cancelled, or No Show. Do not confuse a progress marker such as Trimming In Progress with the appointment's standing, which remains Scheduled until the appointment is Completed.\n\nQuality of the groom itself is governed by the Service Standards Policy (§2.6.3), which governs.\n\n## Messaging the customer during the groom\n\nThe portal provides customer messaging with prepared texts. Use them at the right moment:\n\n- **Mid-groom update.** Send when the bath starts. The prepared text tells the customer the pet is in the bath with the named groomer and doing well, and that a further message will follow when drying and scissor work are complete. Send it when the bath actually starts.\n- **Pickup message.** Send the moment the dog is actually ready. The prepared text tells the customer the pet is completely finished and ready for pickup at the salon. \"Ready for pickup\" means the dog can leave with the owner now — not when notes are started and not when you expect to finish shortly.\n\nThese messages go to the customer's portal Messages page. They are not text messages. If the customer does not check the portal, they will not see the message.\n\nFor anything urgent — the dog is distressed, or pickup is overdue by an hour — call the phone number on the booking. Do not rely on a portal message.\n\n## Groomer notes are part of the job\n\nThe Groomer Notes Completed step is the step most often skipped under time pressure, and it is the most important step after the groom itself. This is where the profile receives its updates: behavior observed, cut details, and anything the next groomer needs. A completed appointment with no notes breaks continuity — the next appointment starts without the benefit of this one. The notes duty is governed by Grooming Records and Continuity of Care (§2.6.17), which governs.\n\nComplete the notes before you mark the appointment finished:\n\n1. Record what was done, including cut details.\n2. Record behavior observed, by handling area where relevant.\n3. Update the handling profile with anything new.\n\n## Mistakes to avoid\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Do not mark an appointment Completed before the customer has actually collected the dog if your location's practice records completion at pickup. A record that states the dog has left the building when it has not creates exposure if anything happens in the gap. Follow your location's completion practice exactly and keep the record accurate.\n\n- **Pickup message never seen.** A customer who arrives two hours late may not have ignored the message; they may never have seen it. Check that the message was actually sent before treating the delay as the customer's fault.\n- **Portal unavailable mid-appointment.** Use the booking's phone number and your own contemporaneous notes. The system is the record, but the dog in front of you is the priority. Enter the steps and notes as soon as the portal is available again.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about appointment steps, customer messaging, or groomer notes: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "pricing-surcharges-deposits-and-discounts": "\n# Pricing, Surcharges, Deposits and Discounts\n\nThis page explains how salon pricing works: the catalog price, size-tier pricing, surcharges, deposits, and the three discount paths, and how to handle a dispute about the total at pickup. This page summarizes the Service Pricing & Surcharge Policy (§2.6.4), which governs.\n\n## The governing principle\n\nThe price the customer was quoted is the price the customer pays, and every number on the ticket traces back to the catalog — not to memory and not to a note at the register.\n\n## The catalog price\n\nThe service catalog's set price is the price authority. It is the only number you may quote unless an authorized pricing element — a surcharge, a discount, or a member price — changes it.\n\nIf a customer says the website shows one price and the catalog shows another, the catalog price is used for the ticket. Report the website discrepancy to be fixed. Do not honor a different website price on the spot without manager approval.\n\nIn the booking flow, prices are set by size tier. Every package carries a price per size tier — Small, Medium, Large, and X-Large — plus a separate member price for active Bath Club members. The tier comes from the dog's weight. For X-Large, if no X-Large price is set in the catalog, the service requires a custom quote. The booking flow states that honestly rather than presenting an invented number.\n\n## Surcharges\n\nA surcharge applies only as recorded in the surcharge list for your location. Pricing and surcharges are governed by the Service Pricing & Surcharge Policy (§2.6.4), which governs, including the requirement that only one active surcharge of a given kind may apply per location.\n\nBefore you quote a price that includes a surcharge:\n\n1. Confirm which surcharge record is the active one for your location.\n2. Confirm whether it applies automatically or must be added.\n3. If two active records appear to cover the same charge, ask your manager which record is correct before you quote.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Do not choose between two conflicting surcharge amounts on your own authority. Once you state a price to the customer, an error in that quote is attributed to you. Escalate the conflict to your manager first.\n\n## Deposits\n\nThe deposit is the location's booking rule, never a fixed amount for all locations. It is set in the location's booking settings as a toggle, a flat dollar amount, or a percentage of the total. Where both a flat amount and a percentage are set, the flat amount applies. The deposit never exceeds the total. If no rule, no toggle, and no amount are set, no deposit is charged. There is no built-in default deposit.\n\nDeposits are governed by the Deposit & Prepayment Policy (§2.6.5), which governs. What the customer paid is recorded as a deposit with status \"held.\" It is not revenue yet, and it is not applied except as the deposit rules provide. See Cancellations, No-Shows and Fees (§3.6.6) for how deposits interact with cancellations and fees.\n\nAt booking, the customer chooses to pay in full or to pay the deposit. The balance due is the total minus what was paid, and the booking carries all three numbers — service price, deposit amount, and balance due — so the front desk does not need to recompute them.\n\n## Discounts, promo codes, and points\n\nThree discount paths exist, and they behave differently:\n\n- **Promo codes** are validated again on the server at checkout against the actual booking. The preview the customer saw is not final; the checkout result controls. If a code was rejected, the rejection reason is the explanation. Do not override it at the desk.\n- **Perks points** are checked against the customer's balance before checkout, and the redemption is recorded after the booking exists, because the booking is the record for the redemption. The recorded redemption reflects the actual redemption, which may limit points to the subtotal. If the ticket shows fewer points applied than the customer expected, that limit is usually the reason.\n- **Manual discounts** are recorded on the service line, with the reason in the notes. Authority for manual discounts is a management matter under the Service Pricing & Surcharge Policy (§2.6.4), which governs.\n\nTax is calculated on the discounted subtotal. That calculation is applied in the checkout figures and is not adjusted by hand.\n\n## Handling a dispute about the total at pickup\n\nWork through the likely causes in this order:\n\n1. **Size tier.** Re-weigh the dog and confirm the tier.\n2. **Promo that did not apply.** Check the rejection reason recorded on the booking.\n3. **Surcharge.** Confirm the active surcharge record and amount with your manager.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Never change a service line's unit price to make the ticket match what the customer expected. Changing the unit price corrupts the record. Issue a proper discount line with a reason instead, with manager approval where required.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about a price, surcharge, deposit, or discount: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "cancellations-no-shows-and-fees": "\n# Cancellations, No-Shows and Fees\n\nThis page explains how to cancel an appointment, how deposit refund requests work, and how late-cancellation and no-show fees become invoices. This page summarizes the Booking, Cancellation & No-Show Policy (§2.6.6), which governs.\n\n## Cancelling an appointment\n\nRequirements before you confirm a cancellation: select the true reason. The reason list is fixed:\n\n- Customer Request / Change of Plans\n- Pet Illness / Veterinary Quarantine\n- Illness\n- Inclement Weather / Emergency\n- Salon Staffing / Rescheduled by Salon\n- Other\n\nTo cancel:\n\n1. Open the appointment and start the cancellation.\n2. Select the true reason from the list. The reason is stored on the appointment and added to its notes in the form \"Canceled: [reason],\" and it becomes part of the permanent record.\n3. Decide whether to request a deposit refund (see below).\n4. Save the cancellation.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Select the true reason. Recording \"Customer Request\" for an appointment cancelled for a safety reason creates a false record that will mislead anyone who reads it later.\n\nWhen the cancellation is saved, the customer receives a message in their portal Messages page notifying them of the cancellation, with the service, the dog's name, and the date and time. If a customer says no one told them the appointment was cancelled, check whether the notification was sent before you respond.\n\n## Deposit refund requests\n\nThe cancellation screen offers a deposit refund option. Checking it records \"Deposit refund requested\" in the cancellation reason. It does **not** issue a refund.\n\nThe refund itself is issued from Payments, by a person, as a separate action. The confirmation states this plainly: a deposit refund was requested and must be issued from Payments.\n\n1. Record the refund request during cancellation.\n2. Issue the refund from Payments the same day.\n3. Confirm the refund was issued; do not leave the request open for the customer to discover days later.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Do not promise a customer an instant refund at the counter. The request is recorded at cancellation; the money is returned only when the refund is issued from Payments. Promising an immediate refund the process does not perform is how a chargeback is created. Deposit handling is governed by the Deposit & Prepayment Policy (§2.6.5), which governs.\n\n## Late-cancellation and no-show fees\n\nWhen an appointment is marked as a no-show, or cancelled inside the location's cutoff window, a fee may become a real open invoice — numbered in the form INV-####, owed by the customer and visible on their customer record. The amounts and windows come only from your location's booking settings:\n\n- **Late-cancel fee** — the amount charged for a late cancellation.\n- **Cancellation cutoff** — the window, in hours before the start time, inside which a cancellation counts as late.\n- **No-show fee** — the amount charged for a no-show.\n\nThe fee amounts and cutoff hours are commercial terms configured for your location.\n\n### Rules that always apply\n\n1. **No configured rule means no fee.** An unset or zero rule means no fee is charged. The system never substitutes a general default and never invents a fee. If your location has not configured these settings, do not tell customers a fee applies.\n2. **A fee is an invoice, never a silent card charge.** The customer sees the invoice, may dispute it, and pays it through normal channels.\n3. **A held deposit applies first.** If the salon holds a deposit for the appointment, the deposit changes to \"applied\" against the fee invoice. A deposit larger than the fee is not split; it remains held, and the invoice notes state this so staff can apply the balance manually.\n4. **Marking twice charges once.** The fee invoice carries a marker tying it to the appointment. Marking the same appointment as a no-show twice finds the existing invoice and charges nothing further.\n\nTwo timing points:\n\n- A cancellation recorded after the appointment's start time is treated as a no-show, with the no-show fee, not as a late cancellation.\n- If the location configured a fee but no cutoff window, the late-cancel fee applies to any cancellation before the start time.\n\n## Mistakes to avoid\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Verify the appointment before you mark a no-show. Marking the wrong appointment creates a real invoice against the wrong customer, and reversing a posted invoice is accounting work, not a single correction.\n\n- **Customer disputes the cutoff.** A customer disputing a late-cancel fee usually disputes timing — for example, stating they cancelled the day before. The invoice notes and the appointment's status history are the evidence. Check them before you concede or refuse.\n- **Refund requested but never issued.** If the deposit refund option was selected at cancellation but no one issued the refund from Payments, close the loop the same day.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about a cancellation, a fee invoice, or a deposit refund: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices about a disputed fee: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "incident-reports-safety-and-commissions": "\n# Incident Reports, Safety and Commissions\n\nThis page explains how to file an incident report, what a report triggers, the safety rules that carry the most serious consequences, and how commission terms are set. This page summarizes the Incident & Injury Reporting Policy (§2.6.9), which governs.\n\n## When to file an incident report\n\nFile a report the same day when something goes wrong: a bite, a clipper injury, a dog that collapses during handling, or a confrontation with a customer that crossed a line. File promptly. Memory fades quickly and detail is the purpose of the report. A report filed days later reads as an after-the-fact account even when it is honest.\n\nIf you are unsure whether an event counts as an incident, file it at Low severity. Reporting an event that turns out to be minor costs nothing. Failing to report a real incident costs a great deal: an unreported bite makes the next bite look like a first occurrence, and the salon's record is weakened.\n\n## Requirements for the report\n\nFile through the Add Incident Report action in the employee portal. The report requires:\n\n- **Severity**, from a fixed list only: Low, Medium, High, or Critical. Choose honestly. As a guide: a minor clipper injury that drew a small amount of blood and was cleaned is Low; a bite that broke skin is at least Medium; breathing difficulty, collapse, or uncontrolled bleeding is High or Critical.\n- **Description**, required, up to 4,000 characters. Write what happened, in order, with times where you know them. State what was happening, what the dog did, what you did, and what the outcome was. A single sentence such as \"dog snapped during nail trim\" is not a sufficient report.\n- **Action taken**, up to 2,000 characters. Record the response: work stopped, manager notified, customer called, veterinarian contacted, first aid applied.\n- **Appointment link**, where applicable. If you name an appointment, it must be an appointment assigned to you; a link to another groomer's appointment is refused. The pet defaults to the appointment's pet, or you may name a pet directly.\n\nTo file:\n\n1. Open Add Incident Report in the employee portal.\n2. Select the severity.\n3. Write the full description in order.\n4. Record the action taken.\n5. Link the appointment or name the pet, where applicable.\n6. Submit the report the same day.\n\n## What the report triggers\n\nIncident reports form part of the salon's safety record, governed by the Incident & Injury Reporting Policy (§2.6.9), which governs. Patterns across reports — the same groomer, the same breed, the same service — are how recurring problems are found.\n\nSafety rules are governed by the Pet Safety and Handling (§2.6.7), which governs. The most serious violations include striking a pet, leaving a pet unattended on a table or in a heated dryer, working while impaired, and concealing an injury. Concealing an injury is itself in that category. Filing an honest report about your own mistake is how you stay on the right side of that line: the report protects the person who reports honestly.\n\nA medical-distress event also updates the dog's handling profile and its handling notes, so the next groomer knows what happened before handling the dog again. See Intake and Handling Profiles (§3.6.3).\n\nConduct consequences are governed by the Groomer Conduct & Enforcement Policy (§2.6.19), which governs.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Never edit another person's incident report and never ask another person to soften theirs. The report is a record, not a negotiation. Altering a safety record exposes you and the salon.\n\n## Commissions\n\nCommission terms — rates, any division between services and retail, tip handling, and any minimum guarantees — are commercial terms stated in the commission arrangement that applies to you. Commission terms are governed by the Groomer Compensation & Commission Terms (§2.6.11), which governs.\n\nUntil a commission arrangement is recorded for your role:\n\n1. Do not quote commission terms to new hires.\n2. Do not promise a split percentage to any person.\n3. Do not assume a default rate exists, because none applies unless it is recorded.\n\nWhen an arrangement is recorded, payroll calculations follow it. Keep any pay arrangement agreed at hiring in writing.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about an incident report, a safety matter, or commission terms: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal notices about an injury or a safety investigation: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "signing-in-to-the-employee-portal": "\n# Signing In to the Employee Portal\n\nThis page covers how to sign in to the Employee Portal at /employee, how the portal routes each role after sign-in, what to do when a session stops working, and how to sign out safely on a shared computer.\n\n## Signing in\n\nThe Employee Portal has one entry point: /employee. An older door page permanently redirects to /employee, so an old bookmark lands in the right place. If you are signed out, /employee shows the sign-in door in place; there is no separate login page.\n\n1. Go to /employee.\n2. Enter your credentials at the sign-in door.\n3. The portal hands you to the dashboard at /employee/dashboard.\n\nIf you arrive from a Google sign-in that failed, the door stays up and shows the error message rather than redirecting you elsewhere.\n\n## How role routing works\n\nSigning in does not guarantee you land in the Employee Portal. Each time the portal loads, it checks your role and routes you to the portal that matches it:\n\n- **Groomer** — you stay in the Employee Portal. The sidebar renders in its groomer variant: station dashboard, your appointments, shifts, handling notes, and style records. Business navigation (CRM, orders, accounting) does not appear.\n- **Admin** — you are redirected to /admin/dashboard. Admin-scope work happens in the admin portal, not here.\n- **Customer** — you are redirected to /customer/dashboard.\n\nThis explains the most common sign-in complaint: \"I logged in but I don't see my appointments.\" The usual cause is signing in under the wrong role — often an admin account the person also uses. Check which account you are in before filing a ticket. Credential handling and session expectations are governed by the Authentication Controls (§2.8.5).\n\n## If you are bounced back to the sign-in door\n\nYour browser can hold a session cookie that looks valid but is not — it expired on the server, or your staff record was deactivated. When the cookie verifies but the portal finds no valid user behind it, you get the sign-in door instead of a broken, half-loaded portal. If you see the login screen right after signing in, do not keep retrying the same credentials. Clear the site's cookies, or sign in fresh through the door, and the problem resolves.\n\n## Signing out\n\n1. Sign out from the header.\n2. Wait for the sign-out to complete before leaving the page. The portal completes the sign-out before navigating, because /employee is session-aware: if it navigates while the session is still alive, it sends you straight back into the portal and it looks as though sign-out failed.\n\nIf you tap sign out and land right back inside, the cookie did not clear. Close the tab, clear cookies for the site, and sign in fresh.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> On a shared register or back-office computer, always sign out at the end of your shift. The next person who opens the browser inherits whatever session is still alive. Shared-device hygiene is part of the Employee Code of Conduct (§2.8.1).\n\n## When sign-in genuinely fails\n\n- **Wrong credentials** — the door tells you. Reset through the recovery flow your manager gave you; the door itself has no self-service password reset.\n- **\"Your sign-in isn't linked to a staff profile\"** — your login works, but no staff record is tied to your user account, so the portal has nothing to show you. This is a manager action: your manager links your profile on the admin side, and then the portal opens. You cannot fix this yourself.\n- **Everything loads but it is the wrong portal** — see the role routing above. You are in the wrong account.\n\n## Related pages\n\n- Your Dashboard (§3.7.2)\n- Support Tickets and Internal Communication (§3.7.8)\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Sign-in and account problems: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "your-dashboard": "\n# Your Dashboard\n\nThis page covers the employee dashboard at /employee/dashboard: the day-at-a-glance counts, the Next Up card, the time clock widget, and the My Modules list that shows what you are permitted to touch in the business systems.\n\n## The day at a glance\n\nThe dashboard answers one question: what is on your plate right now? Everything on it is driven by your appointments feed, filtered to your user ID. Ownership is enforced on the server, so you only ever see appointments assigned to you. If an appointment is not yours, it is not on your dashboard.\n\nThe top of the page counts your day in the location's timezone (America/Chicago unless the location sets its own): how many appointments you have today, how many are currently in service, how many you have completed, and how many are coming up. Below that sits the **Next Up** card — the nearest appointment in an actionable status (scheduled, confirmed, checked in, in service, hold, precheck, or rescheduled). That card is your cue for what to walk toward next.\n\nEach appointment row shows the pet names, the service names, the customer name, and the start time, with a status pill:\n\n- **Checked In** — the pet is here; work has not started.\n- **In Service** — you are actively working the appointment.\n- **On Hold** — paused mid-service, for example waiting on the owner or letting a matted coat soak. Hold is an honest state, not a failure state.\n- **Completed** — done and closed out.\n\nTap through to an appointment to work it. The check-in, start, and complete flow lives on the schedule page; see Working Your Appointments (§3.7.3).\n\n## The time clock widget\n\nThe dashboard hosts the time clock widget — your clock-in and clock-out control. It shows today's entries and whether you currently have an open entry. Clock in when your shift starts and clock out when it ends. The full rules, including the one-open-entry rule and what to do when you forget, are in Shifts and the Time Clock (§3.7.4).\n\n## My Modules\n\nFurther down, the dashboard lists your modules: the twelve business areas (Customers, Appointments, Payments, Invoices, Deposits, Refunds & Disputes, Gift Cards & Credits, Inventory, Products & Services, Purchasing, Communications, Reports) and the access level you hold in each — Read, Write, or Full access. These come from your permission grants, which the server reads on every load.\n\nThis list is the truth about your permissions. If a module is not listed, you do not have it; the model is additive, and there is no \"none\" row. The modules themselves open in the admin portal; the dashboard is your view of what you are granted. If you need access you do not have — Refunds & Disputes at Write level, for example — that is a manager conversation, not a setting you can change. The full explanation is in Module Permissions: What You Can and Can't Touch (§3.7.9). Access is granted on a least-privilege basis under the Data Access & Least Privilege policy (§2.8.4), which is also why you see only your own assignments.\n\n## When the dashboard looks wrong\n\n- **Empty appointment list on a day you know you are booked** — first check that you are signed in as yourself, not a coworker. Then check with your manager that the appointments are actually assigned to your staff record. The feed filters on assignment, and an appointment assigned to the wrong groomer is invisible to you by design.\n- **Counts that do not match the schedule page** — both pages read the same data, so a mismatch means one of them loaded stale data. Refresh. If it persists, file a ticket; see Support Tickets and Internal Communication (§3.7.8).\n- **The page never loads past the loading screen** — the layout shows a loading screen until your session resolves. If it hangs there, your session is not resolving; sign out and back in. See Signing In to the Employee Portal (§3.7.1).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Dashboard and assignment problems: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "working-your-appointments": "\n# Working Your Appointments\n\nThis page covers the schedule page at /employee/schedule: how to find an appointment, move it through check-in, start, and complete, record sub-steps, release a pet to the front desk, and add notes, photos, and product recommendations. Every action on this page writes to the real records — there is no undo for a careless tap.\n\n## Finding the appointment\n\nFive views read your appointments feed, filtered to your assignments: **Today**, **Tomorrow**, **This Week**, **This Month**, and **By Hour**. Today is the default. By Hour is the useful view when the day is stacked and you need to see where the gaps are. Everything displays in the location's timezone. If a customer says \"I booked for 2\" and your schedule says 2, you are looking at the same clock; if they do not match, confirm the timezone before assuming an error.\n\n## The lifecycle: check-in, start, complete\n\nEach appointment moves through statuses, and the server writes status history, so the record of when things happened exists whether you remember it or not.\n\n1. **Check-in** — the pet has arrived. This stamps the arrival. Do not check in an appointment for a pet that is not in the building yet, because everything downstream — wait times, pickup notifications — keys off these timestamps.\n2. **Start** — you begin work. The appointment becomes \"in service\" on your dashboard and your manager's view.\n3. **Complete** — the groom is done. Complete only when it actually is; once completed, the appointment leaves your active queue.\n\n**Hold and resume** sit alongside these steps. Hold is for legitimate pauses: the owner needs to approve a dematting charge, the pet needs a break, you are waiting on a dryer. Use hold instead of leaving an appointment sitting \"in service\" while nothing happens. Status history is how the business knows what a groom actually costs in time, and inaccurate timestamps corrupt that record.\n\n## Sub-steps: Wash, Trimming, Nails, Notes\n\nInside an in-service appointment there is a checklist — Wash, Trimming, Nails, Notes — recorded as sub-steps in the status history. This is the handoff surface: if you are pulled off a groom mid-way and someone else finishes it, the sub-steps tell them exactly where you stopped. Check them as you go, not from memory at the end. Notes is the step people skip, and it is the one that matters most weeks later when the same dog returns and nobody remembers the ear problem from last time.\n\n## Release to Front Desk\n\nWhen the groom is complete and the pet is ready for pickup, **Release to Front Desk** stamps the pickup notification time. That stamp is the trigger the front desk works from.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Do not release before the pet is actually ready to walk out the door, or the customer arrives to a pet that is not ready. If you released early by mistake, tell the front desk directly — there is no un-release action, but a conversation fixes it immediately.\n\n## Groomer notes\n\nThe notes action writes a groomer note against an appointment. The ownership rule is strict: the appointment must be assigned to you, or the write is refused. You cannot annotate someone else's appointment; if something needs recording on a coworker's groom, tell them or their manager.\n\nWhat belongs in a note: behavior observations (\"nervous with the dryer, did better with a happy hoodie\"), skin and coat findings in plain observational language (\"raised pink lump, about 1 cm, left flank — told owner at pickup\"), and handling quirks for next time. What does not belong: diagnoses — the Pet Safety & Handling policy (§2.6.7) covers the boundary between observation and diagnosis — complaints about the customer, or anything you would not want read back in a dispute. Notes are business records, and customer and pet information in notes and photos is handled under the Confidentiality & Non-Disclosure policy (§2.8.3). Record-keeping duties are set by the Employee Code of Conduct (§2.8.1).\n\n## Photos\n\nThe photo upload sends before-and-after photos to the salon's photo storage and records them against the appointment. Before-and-after photos are the simplest dispute protection in the business: a matted shave-down documented with a photo ends the \"you ruined my dog\" conversation before it starts. Upload photos at the time; a photo taken but never uploaded might as well not exist.\n\n## Product recommendations\n\nThe recommendation action logs a product recommendation against the appointment, with catalog search behind it. When you tell an owner \"you should be using this shampoo between grooms,\" log the recommendation. It ties your advice to the actual catalog item, which is what makes follow-up marketing honest — the business can see what was genuinely recommended rather than guess.\n\n## Problems worth knowing\n\n- **An action fails with \"not found\"** — the appointment is not assigned to you. Do not retry it repeatedly; check the assignment.\n- **The page shows yesterday's data** — the page always fetches fresh data, so stale data means the request failed and you are looking at the last successful load. The error banner tells you. Do not work from it; refresh until it clears.\n- **You completed the wrong appointment** — tell your manager immediately. Status history is append-only; it can be corrected, but only by someone with the access to do it, and the sooner the better.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Appointment and schedule problems: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "shifts-and-the-time-clock": "\n# Shifts and the Time Clock\n\nThis page covers the difference between your shifts and your time clock entries, the rules the clock enforces, how breaks work, and what to do when you forget to clock in or out. Your shifts are when you are scheduled to work, set by your manager. Your time clock is when you actually worked, recorded by you. The business needs both, because the gap between them is where payroll questions live.\n\n## My Shifts: read-only by design\n\nThe schedule page has a **My Shifts** roster showing your scheduled shifts — the same shifts your manager writes in Admin → Staff scheduling. It shows the recent past week plus everything upcoming, oldest first, each with the location name, role, break minutes, and any notes your manager attached.\n\nYou cannot edit your shifts here. Shift changes are a manager action: if you are sick, swapping with a coworker, or need a time changed, that is a conversation with your manager, not a field you update. The roster is read-only by design — the schedule is the business's commitment to staffing the floor.\n\nUse the roster to check next week's hours without asking, confirm which location you are at on a given day, and see break expectations. If a shift is missing or wrong, tell your manager; they own the data. No shifts showing at all is an honest empty list, not an error: it means nothing is scheduled for you in the window.\n\n## The time clock rules\n\nThe time clock, on the dashboard and the schedule page, records clock-in and clock-out. Your identity resolves from your session first, then from your staff profile — either way, the entries are yours and nobody else's. Timekeeping honesty is required by the Employee Code of Conduct (§2.8.1), and time entries are kept as records under the Record Keeping & Audit policy (§2.8.17).\n\n- **One open entry per employee.** Clocking in when you already have an open entry is refused, and clocking out with nothing open is refused too. The clock never invents a shift to make the math work.\n- **Clock in when you start, clock out when you end.** Not when you remember at lunch, and not the next morning. Every entry records how it was created, and reconstructed entries look different from real ones. If there is ever a payroll dispute, the entry made at the time carries the weight.\n- **Entries are recorded in the location's timezone**, which comes from the location settings (falling back to America/Chicago). If you work across locations in different timezones, your entries follow the location's timezone, not your phone's.\n\n## If you forgot to clock out\n\nYou cannot fix it yourself. Because of the one-open-entry rule, clocking out the next morning would create an entry spanning midnight that misstates your hours.\n\n1. Tell your manager what time you actually left.\n2. Your manager corrects the entry on the admin side, and the correction is visible as a correction.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Do not try to repair the record yourself by clocking in and out rapidly. That creates a cluster of entries that is harder for your manager to untangle than the original mistake, and reconstructed entries are visible for what they are.\n\nThe same rule applies in reverse: if you forgot to clock in, do not simply clock in at noon and let the morning vanish. Tell your manager.\n\n## Breaks\n\nYour shift carries your break length in minutes. The time clock does not deduct breaks automatically — clock out for an unpaid break and back in after, or follow your location's convention. If you are not sure what the convention is, ask your manager on day one, not after your first paycheck looks wrong.\n\n## What your manager sees\n\nYour manager sees your shifts, your clock entries, and the gaps between them. The HR page rolls your clocked hours into a monthly performance view; see HR & Your Profile (§3.7.6). These records exist so the business can pay you correctly and staff the floor; keep your entries accurate and there is never a problem.\n\n## Related pages\n\n- My Pay (§3.7.5)\n- Your Dashboard (§3.7.2)\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Time clock and shift problems: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "my-pay": "\n# My Pay\n\nThis page covers the pay page at /employee/pay: your pay profile, today's time clock entries, and this month's commission ledger. The page is a window, not a control panel — everything about what you are paid is set by your manager on your staff record and is read-only here, because pay changes go through a process with a record.\n\n## Your pay profile\n\nThe profile block reads directly from your staff record:\n\n- **Pay type** — one of Hourly, Salary, Commission, Salary + Commission, or Hourly + Commission.\n- **Hourly rate, or salary amount and period** — whichever applies to your pay type.\n- **Commission rate** — your percent of the service price, if you are on commission.\n\nIf a field shows \"not set,\" that is the truth; the page never invents a number. A blank commission rate does not mean zero — it means nobody entered one, and those are very different things at payroll time. If your profile looks wrong (wrong rate, wrong pay type), tell your manager. This is exactly the kind of error to catch before payroll runs, not after.\n\n## Today's clock entries\n\nBelow the profile, the page shows today's time clock entries — the same data as the time clock widget, in the location's timezone. Use it to check your day against your pay at a glance: did this morning's clock-in actually register? If the morning entry is missing, see Shifts and the Time Clock (§3.7.4) — do not assume payroll will figure it out.\n\n## The commission ledger\n\nThe ledger shows this calendar month's commissions in two views.\n\n**The period summary** shows four numbers — pending, approved, paid, and total — plus the entry count:\n\n- **Pending** — earned but not yet approved. It is money you have earned, but it has not cleared the approval step.\n- **Approved** — cleared and queued.\n- **Paid** — paid out.\n\nIf your pending number looks healthy but your paycheck did not, the gap is almost always in the approved-to-paid transition, which is your manager's or payroll's step, not yours.\n\n**The line items** show every commission line: the date earned, the appointment number, the appointment's start time, the service name, the basis amount (what the percent was calculated on), your rate, the commission amount, and the line's status. This is your receipt. If a groom you did is not here:\n\n1. Check that the appointment was actually completed, not left in an earlier status.\n2. Check the date. The ledger is monthly, and a groom completed on the 31st belongs to a different month than one completed on the 1st, regardless of what you think of as \"this pay period.\"\n\n## What this page cannot tell you\n\nIt cannot tell you when pending becomes approved or approved becomes paid — those are business processes outside the portal. It cannot show you anyone else's numbers. And it cannot fix anything: if a line is missing or a rate is wrong, the fix is a conversation with your manager, who works the admin side. Come with specifics — appointment number, date, what you expected — because \"my commission looks off\" gives them nothing to investigate.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Your commission rate and pay details are your business and the Company's. Do not share them with coworkers and do not photograph the screen. Pay confidentiality is required by the Employee Code of Conduct (§2.8.1), and compensation information is handled under the Confidentiality & Non-Disclosure policy (§2.8.3).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Pay and commission questions: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "hr-your-profile": "\n# HR & Your Profile\n\nThis page covers the HR page at /employee/hr: the two profile fields you can edit, your documents, your training records, and your performance numbers. Think of it as your employee file — the parts you are allowed to see, and the one part you are allowed to edit.\n\n## What you can edit: phone and bio\n\nThe portal accepts exactly two fields from you: **phone** and **bio**. Everything else — pay fields, role, identity, hire date — is manager-owned, and the portal will not take changes to it from you.\n\nKeep your phone current. It is how the business reaches you for shift changes and emergencies, and an out-of-date number has real consequences when someone is trying to fill a sick call at 6am. The bio is what shows where staff bios surface, such as meet-the-team pages; keep it professional and current.\n\nIf your name is misspelled, your role is wrong, or your hire date is off, that is a manager fix. Do not sit on it: employment records feed calculations such as tenure, and they should be right.\n\n## Documents\n\nThe documents section shows your file: offer letter, handbook acknowledgments, certifications, and whatever your location requires. Each row shows the name, type, status, upload date, and expiry where applicable.\n\n1. **Check expiries.** If a certification or document has an expiry date, it is your job to notice it coming up, not your manager's job to chase you. An expired certification can take you off the schedule.\n2. **Do not assume a document exists because you handed it in.** Paper handed to a manager and a document in your file are different things. If you gave someone your updated certification weeks ago and it is not here, follow up. The portal is the record.\n\nOne limitation to know: document links resolve only when the stored path is already a full URL. Some documents show a name, type, and status with no link. If you see a document with no link, ask your manager for the file directly.\n\n## Training records\n\nYour training history lives here: what you took, its status, when you completed it, when it expires, and the certificate where one exists. Apply the same discipline as documents — watch the expiries, and do not assume a class you attended made it into the record. If you completed required training and it is not here, close the gap the same week: \"I took the class\" without a record is a hard position to defend if compliance ever asks.\n\nWhat training is required, and how often refreshers happen, is governed by the Training & Certification policy (§2.8.19). The portal shows your record against those requirements; it does not set them.\n\n## Performance\n\nThe performance block shows this month's numbers from the real records: appointments completed, total appointments, what is in service right now, hours clocked, commission pending, and commission paid. It is the same data your manager sees, which is the point — no surprises in either direction. If your completed-appointment count looks low, check whether appointments were actually moved to completed status rather than left in an earlier state; see Working Your Appointments (§3.7.3). The numbers only know what the statuses say.\n\n## What is not here\n\nWrite-ups, disciplinary records, and manager notes about you do not surface in the employee portal, by design. This page is the shared surface of your employment record. Everything else lives on the manager side, under the internal policies. Your own and others' HR information is handled under the Confidentiality & Non-Disclosure policy (§2.8.3). What happens to access and records when employment ends is governed by the Termination & Offboarding policy (§2.8.20).\n\n## Related pages\n\n- Filing Incident Reports (§3.7.7)\n- My Pay (§3.7.5)\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> HR record and document problems: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "filing-incident-reports": "\n# Filing Incident Reports\n\nThis page covers the incident report form on your HR page (/employee/hr): when to file, how to choose a severity, what to write, and what happens after you submit. Incident reporting is an employment duty under the Employee Code of Conduct (§2.8.1), not an optional extra, which is why the form sits next to your own record.\n\n## When to file\n\nFile when something happened that the business needs a record of:\n\n- A pet injured in your care, even minor — a nick from clippers counts.\n- A bite or scratch to you or a coworker.\n- A customer confrontation.\n- Property damage.\n- A near-miss that could have hurt someone or something.\n- Anything involving a pet's medical distress.\n\nThe rule of thumb: if you would tell your manager about it in person, file the report too. The conversation handles the immediate moment; the report creates the record that protects everyone later. Do not file incident reports for routine difficulties — a customer who was merely difficult is not an incident. But when in doubt, file. An unnecessary report costs five minutes; a missing report costs credibility when the facts are disputed weeks later. The Company's response once your report lands is governed by the Incident Response policy (§2.8.12).\n\n## Choose the severity honestly\n\nSeverity is the form's fixed vocabulary — four values, no custom entries:\n\n- **Low** — minor, handled on the spot, no ongoing risk. A small clipper nick with no bleeding; a knocked-over product display.\n- **Medium** — needed real attention but resolved. A bite that broke skin but did not need medical care; a pet that panicked and had to be calmed over twenty minutes.\n- **High** — significant harm or risk. A pet injured badly enough to need a veterinarian; a staff injury needing treatment; a serious customer altercation.\n- **Critical** — the worst category. A pet in medical distress; a severe bite; anything involving emergency services.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Pick the severity that matches what happened, not what you wish had happened. Severity drives who gets notified and how fast. Downgrading a high to a medium because the paperwork feels heavy is how small problems become big ones.\n\n## What to write\n\nThe form takes three fields — severity, description, and action taken (optional) — plus an optional link to an appointment and pet.\n\n- **Description** — what happened, in order, in plain language. Times help. \"At about 2:15pm during the nail trim, the dog jerked as the trimmer caught the quick on the left front paw. Bleeding controlled with styptic powder by 2:20. Owner informed at pickup at 3:05.\" That is a good description. \"Dog got hurt during nails\" is not.\n- **Action taken** — what you did about it: first aid administered, owner notified, veterinarian called, area sanitized, manager informed. If you have not taken action yet because you are filing mid-situation, say so — \"filing now, owner notification pending\" is honest and useful.\n- **Appointment link** — if the incident ties to an appointment, name it. The appointment must be assigned to you, or the write fails. When you name an appointment, the pet defaults to that appointment's first pet. This keeps reports attached to the real record instead of floating as free text.\n\nWrite the report as a factual record, not an argument. State what happened and what you did. If a coworker's actions contributed, state the facts without editorializing. The report may be read in a dispute, a workers' compensation claim, or an insurance review — write it as though it will be.\n\n## What happens after you file\n\nYour report is recorded with your staff ID, timestamp, and everything you wrote, and your manager sees it on the admin side. The incident list shows you the reports you filed, newest first, with the appointment number and pet name attached — that is your copy of the record.\n\n- **Filing does not replace telling your manager.** The report is the record; the conversation is the response. For anything medium and up, do both, and have the conversation first if it is urgent.\n- **You cannot edit someone else's report, and you cannot file on someone else's appointment.** The ownership checks are enforced on the server. If you witnessed something on a coworker's appointment, file what you observed under your own name, or tell your manager and let them handle the assignment.\n- **If the incident involves misconduct**, the protected reporting channel in the Whistleblower & Internal Reporting policy (§2.8.18) applies, not the ordinary report flow.\n\n## The serious end\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> For anything involving pet medical distress, stop the groom first and handle the animal. The report comes after the pet is stable. The response itself is governed by the Pet Safety & Handling policy (§2.6.7); this page governs the paperwork. Both matter, in that order.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Incident reporting questions: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "support-tickets-and-internal-communication": "\n# Support Tickets and Internal Communication\n\nThis page covers the messages page at /employee/messages: what the support ticket system is for, how to file a ticket the desk can act on, how the thread and statuses work, and what response to expect. Your tickets file into the same Support Desk queue the admin side works — your ticket lands next to customer and seller tickets, tagged as an employee ticket and attached to your staff profile, and the desk's replies come back to this same page.\n\n## What tickets are for\n\nUse a ticket for questions about your schedule, pay, the register, or anything else the support team can work asynchronously: a commission line missing an appointment, the schedule page failing to load, your shift roster showing the wrong location, a card reader down with nobody answering the phone, or a request for access to a module you do not have.\n\nDo not use a ticket for:\n\n- **Emergencies** — call your manager.\n- **Incident reports** — those go in the incident system; see Filing Incident Reports (§3.7.7).\n- **Anything time-critical on the floor right now** — a ticket is asynchronous by nature. If the shop is on fire, literally or figuratively, use the phone.\n- **Misconduct** — use the protected channel in the Whistleblower & Internal Reporting policy (§2.8.18), not a support ticket.\n\n## Filing a ticket\n\n1. Pick a category and a priority.\n2. Write a subject.\n3. Describe the problem with specifics.\n\nThe categories are the desk's shared vocabulary:\n\n- **general** — the default; when nothing else fits, start here.\n- **technical** — the portal, the register, or the app misbehaving.\n- **billing** — pay questions that are not for your manager first. Most pay questions are for your manager; see My Pay (§3.7.5).\n- **payments** — payment processing issues.\n- **seller_support** and **dmca** — you will rarely use these; they exist because the queue is shared.\n\nPriorities are low, normal, high, and urgent. Use them the way the words mean. If everything is marked urgent, nothing is treated as urgent; marking a routine schedule question urgent spends credibility you will need when something actually is. Urgent means someone is blocked right now and losing money or customers.\n\nWrite the ticket as though the person reading it has never met you and cannot see your screen. \"Schedule page broken\" is a bad ticket. \"Schedule page shows a loading error on the Today view since about 9am, tried refresh twice, Chrome on the front-desk tablet\" is a good ticket — it gives the desk something to reproduce. Include the appointment number, the date, and the error text wherever you have them. Proper use of shared systems is covered by the Acceptable Use of Admin Tools policy (§2.8.7).\n\n## The thread\n\nYour ticket is a thread: the desk replies in it, you reply back, all on this page. Statuses move through open → pending → resolved → closed. If a ticket goes quiet, reply in the thread rather than filing a duplicate — duplicates split the conversation across two tickets and slow everything down.\n\nInternal staff notes on your ticket never appear here. That boundary is deliberate: the desk needs room to coordinate without every half-formed internal message landing in front of you. You see the replies addressed to you. If a ticket seems stalled, the right move is a polite nudge in the thread, not a demand to see the internal notes.\n\n## If you see the unlinked-profile message\n\nIf the page says \"Your sign-in isn't linked to a staff profile yet, so support can't attach tickets to you,\" your login works but there is no staff record behind it, so the desk has nothing to attach the ticket to. The page stays unusable until a manager links your profile — the same root cause as an empty dashboard; see Signing In to the Employee Portal (§3.7.1). Do not try to file anyway; the ticket would go nowhere.\n\n## Response expectations\n\nThe desk works tickets in priority order during business hours. A normal-priority ticket filed Tuesday morning should get movement the same day; low-priority items can take longer. If something is genuinely blocking the floor — register down, nobody can check in — say so in the ticket and call. The ticket creates the record; the call creates the urgency. Both is fine.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Support Desk: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "module-permissions-what-you-can-and-can-t-touch": "\n# Module Permissions: What You Can and Can't Touch\n\nThis page explains the My Modules list on your dashboard: the twelve modules, the three access levels, where grants come from, and what to do when your access is wrong in either direction. This page summarizes the Data Access & Least Privilege policy (§2.8.4), which governs.\n\n## The twelve modules\n\nThe module list is fixed:\n\n1. **Customers** — customer records and profiles\n2. **Appointments** — the booking book\n3. **Payments** — taking and viewing payments\n4. **Invoices** — invoicing\n5. **Deposits** — deposits held against bookings\n6. **Refunds & Disputes** — issuing refunds, working disputes\n7. **Gift Cards & Credits** — gift card and store credit handling\n8. **Inventory** — stock levels and adjustments\n9. **Products & Services** — the catalog and service menu\n10. **Purchasing** — ordering from suppliers\n11. **Communications** — messaging customers\n12. **Reports** — business reporting\n\nEach module you hold carries an access level: **Read** (see it), **Write** (see it and change it), or **Full access**. The levels mean what they say: Read on Payments lets you look up what a customer paid; it does not let you take a payment.\n\n## Where grants come from\n\nGrants are permission records assigned per staff member in **Admin → Staff** by someone with the authority to grant them. The server checks the grant on every request; this is not a display setting that a clever URL can bypass. Your grants are tied to your identity and end with your session, under the Authentication Controls (§2.8.5).\n\nThe model is **additive**: you have what you are granted, and what you are not granted simply is not there. There are no \"denied\" rows and no \"none\" placeholders. An ungranted module is absent from your list. This answers the most common confusion: \"I used to see Invoices and now it is gone\" means the grant was removed, not that the page is broken.\n\n## Where modules open\n\nYour dashboard describes your grants, but the modules themselves open in the **admin portal**. The employee portal is your station surface — appointments, shifts, HR, support; the business systems live next door. If you hold a grant and cannot find where to use it, you are probably looking in the wrong portal. Your manager can point you to the right place. Rules for working inside granted modules are in the Acceptable Use of Admin Tools policy (§2.8.7).\n\n## When your access is wrong\n\n**You need access you do not have.** A groomer who needs Write on Gift Cards & Credits to sell them at the front desk, or a shift lead who needs Read on Reports, makes a manager request. State which module, which level, and why the job needs it. \"I need access to everything\" will correctly get you nothing: least privilege is the policy, and specific, justified requests are approved faster than broad ones.\n\n**You have access you should not.** Perhaps you changed roles and kept the old grants, or you were covering a shift and temporary access was never pulled back. Say something.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Holding Full access to Payments when your job no longer touches money is not a perk — it is a liability for you if anything goes wrong in that module. Grants should match the job you actually do today, not the job you did six months ago.\n\n## What managers do on their side\n\nGrants are managed in Admin → Staff, per staff member, per module, per level. Granting is itself a privileged action — not everyone in the admin portal can hand out Full access to Payments. If your manager says \"I can't change that,\" they may genuinely lack the authority, and the request goes up a level. That is the system working, not bureaucracy.\n\n## The boundary that matters most\n\nModule permissions govern business systems. They do not control your station work: your appointments, your shifts, your time clock, and your HR page are yours by virtue of being you, not by grant. If you can sign in, you can work your day. The modules govern everything beyond that — the money, the customers, the inventory, the reports. That split is intentional: your day-to-day never depends on a grant, and the business's sensitive operations always do.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Permission and access problems: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "getting-into-your-franchise-portal": "\n# Getting Into Your Franchise Portal\n\nThis page covers how you sign in to your franchise portal, how the shop switcher scopes everything you see, what your franchise-owner role can and cannot reach, and what to do if you are locked out. The franchise relationship itself is governed by the Franchise Program Policy (§2.4.29) and your signed agreement under the Franchise Agreement Policy (§2.4.30); this page covers the mechanics of getting in.\n\n## Your portal is a vanity URL\n\nWhen your franchise is granted, you receive a portal of your own: a vanity URL is created for your shop from your franchise code, and that address is delivered to you during onboarding. It is your front door — where you sign in from, and what your bookmark should point at. No UnSaid Memories page ever prints the address of the platform's own login door: a published login address invites attacks, which is why your entry is a provisioned vanity URL rather than a public one.\n\nBehind your vanity URL you are using the same admin portal the corporate shops use, with your view fenced to your business. The fencing is the workspace system: your franchise is its own workspace, linked under the UnSaid Memories master, and everything you see is filtered to your shop. \"The franchise portal\" means the admin portal with your shop active.\n\n## Your login\n\nYour login is created during onboarding. When the corporate team runs franchise signup, the system creates your owner account: franchise-owner role, marked as the owner, on the admin portal, with your name, email, and phone. If the \"send invite\" option was selected — it is by default — you receive an email with the subject line \"Welcome to UnSaid Memories — [your business name] franchise onboarding\" and a button that takes you to your franchise portal.\n\nThe invite email is sent once. If it never arrives — spam filter, a typo in the address, a mail provider problem — your login still exists. Do not wait for a second invite. Contact franchise support, ask them to confirm the email address on your login, and have the invite resent. The single most common onboarding snag is a transposed letter in the owner email; it takes moments to fix once someone looks.\n\nYour login record is the authoritative source for who you are in the system. If your role ever looks wrong — you can see things you should not, or cannot see things you should — the fix is in that record: your role, your shop, and whether the record is active. A deactivated login means no access at all, which is also how access is revoked when someone leaves.\n\n## The shop switcher: check it every time\n\nIf you own one shop, you will barely notice the switcher. If you own several, it is the most important control in the portal.\n\nThe switcher lists every shop your login may touch. As a franchise owner, you see every shop you own; pick one and the portal remembers it, and from that moment every screen, every report, and every list is scoped to that shop.\n\nBuild the habit: glance at the switcher before you do anything that matters — payouts, reports, staff changes. Doing payroll or pulling sales figures while switched to the wrong shop is the franchise equivalent of mailing the wrong envelope. The portal shows the active shop name; if you cannot see it, you are not looking.\n\nOne edge case: a stale switcher setting does not produce an error — it silently falls back to the default shop. If the numbers on a screen look wrong for your shop, first check whether the switcher actually has your shop active, not whether the data is broken.\n\n## What you can and cannot reach\n\nYour franchise-owner role makes you the owner of your shop. Inside your shop you run the business: CRM, appointments, catalog, orders, staff, locations, settings. You cannot cross the fence:\n\n- You cannot see the master shop or any other franchisee's data. The switcher lists only shops you own.\n- You cannot change platform-level configuration; that belongs to the master shop.\n- Your staff hold narrower roles, which you manage yourself under Settings → Users in the admin portal. Give people the least access that lets them do their job; the owner role is yours, not a convenience login for the front desk. See Your Staff: Users, Roles, and Access (§3.8.10).\n\nEvery permission change on your shop is written to the permission audit log — who changed what, and when. If something about your access ever looks off, that log is where the answer is.\n\n## If you are locked out\n\nWork the chain in order:\n\n1. Confirm you are on your franchise's vanity URL (the address created from your franchise code), not a bookmarked oddity.\n2. Check whether the invite email reached the address on file.\n3. Have franchise support verify that your login is active and carries the franchise-owner role on your shop.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Do not create a second login to work around a lockout. Duplicate identities across shops are how permission problems start.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Franchise portal access and sign-in problems: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "your-staff-users-roles-and-access": "\n# Your Staff: Users, Roles, and Access\n\nThis page covers how you manage your staff's access to your shop: the roles available, how to add and remove people, and why shared logins are prohibited. The tools are Settings → Users in the admin portal; the discipline is yours. Your staffing obligations under the franchise agreement are in the Franchise Agreement Policy (§2.4.30), and the access discipline here follows the same least-privilege principle as the Data Access & Least Privilege policy (§2.8.4).\n\n## The roles\n\nUser accounts are the authoritative record of who holds which role. The roles that matter to you:\n\n- **Franchise owner** — you. Marked as the owner, with full control of your shop. This role is not shared, not lent, and not used as the front-desk login because it is convenient.\n- **Franchise employee** — your staff. Scoped to your shop, with permissions set by you. The front desk gets booking and point of sale; they do not get financial settings or user management.\n- **Narrower functional roles** exist for specific functions (grooming partners, pharmacy staff, and similar). Assign the narrowest role that lets the person do their job.\n\nLeast privilege is not paranoia; it is blast-radius control. The employee who can only see the appointment book cannot accidentally — or deliberately — touch payouts, refund records, or your Gross Sales reports. Every permission you grant beyond necessity is risk you have chosen to carry.\n\n## Adding someone\n\n1. Invite the person through Settings → Users. They receive a login tied to your shop with the role you assign.\n2. Verify they can get in — have them log in once while you are available, not the morning of their first solo shift.\n3. Verify they see what they should: no more, no less.\n\nThe most common misconfiguration is the new hire with owner-level access \"just until we get set up.\" Temporary permissions have a way of becoming permanent.\n\n## Removing someone, promptly\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> When someone leaves, deactivate their login the same day — not at the end of the week. A departed employee with active access is a liability with a login: they can see customer data, issue refunds, and export records. Deactivation is one action. If the departure is contentious, check the permission audit log for what they touched in their final days; the log exists for exactly this.\n\nRole changes for continuing employees — a promotion, new responsibilities — get the same-day treatment in the other direction: update the role when the job changes, not when someone remembers.\n\n## The shared-login problem\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Do not share logins. One login per person, always. Shared logins destroy accountability — when three people use \"frontdesk,\" nobody did anything and everybody did. They also break the audit log's usefulness: the log records which login acted, and a shared login makes that record meaningless. If someone needs access, they get their own invite. It takes two minutes.\n\n## Multi-shop staff\n\nIf you own multiple franchises and someone works across them, their access is per shop — logins are shop-scoped. A manager covering two shops needs the right role in both shops, and the shop switcher governs which shop they are acting in at any moment; see Getting Into Your Franchise Portal (§3.8.1). Make sure cross-shop staff understand the switcher. Acting in the wrong shop is the multi-shop version of the shared-login problem.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Staff access and role problems: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "renewal": "\n# Renewal\n\nThis page covers how franchise renewal works in practice: when to start, what good standing requires, how the renewal agreement differs from the original, and what happens if the franchise is not renewed. This page summarizes the Franchise Renewal Policy (§2.4.39), which governs. Renewal is a process you start — not a button you click, and not something that happens automatically because you have been a good operator.\n\n## Start eighteen months out\n\nOperators who treat renewal as an event scramble in the final quarter. Treat it as a project:\n\n1. **18 months out:** Read your agreement's renewal section fresh. Know the notice requirements, the conditions, and the fees. This is also when you make an honest self-assessment: are you in good standing? Any uncured defaults, unpaid fees, open audit findings? Every one of those is a renewal obstacle, and every one is fixable with time.\n2. **12 months out:** Cure everything. Pay what is owed, close what is open, complete what is incomplete. A renewal application filed while you are in breach is an application filed with a handicap.\n3. **Within the agreement's notice window:** File the renewal notice in writing. Verbal assurances, handshake conversations, \"we talked about it at the conference\" — none of these is a renewal notice. Paper.\n4. **Before expiry:** Execute the renewal agreement. The new term's start date, expiry date, rate, and fee schedule are recorded just like the original; see Your Franchise Agreement (§3.8.3). Confirm the record reflects what you signed.\n\n## The renewal agreement is not the old agreement\n\nExpect the new agreement to differ from the one you signed years ago. Royalty rates change, fee schedules are restructured, standards are updated, territory language is refined. The renewal fee, if any, and the other renewal terms are stated in the renewal agreement and in the fee provisions of the Franchise Fees & Royalties Policy (§2.4.34). Read the renewal agreement with the same care as the original — \"it is just a renewal\" is how people sign things they have not read. The term you are agreeing to is the next years of your business life; it deserves a full read.\n\n## Good standing is the price of admission\n\nRenewal is generally conditioned on being in good standing: fees current, reports filed, no uncured defaults, standards compliance reasonably maintained. This is the quiet payoff of the discipline in Reporting Your Gross Sales (§3.8.5) through Audits and Inspections (§3.8.9) — operators who reconciled monthly, cured notices promptly, and kept training current do not have a renewal project; they have paperwork. The renewal process does not create good standing. It reveals whether you had it.\n\n## If renewal is not offered, or you decline\n\nNot every relationship renews. If the franchisor declines renewal under the agreement's terms, or you decide not to renew, the post-term obligations activate:\n\n- **De-identification** — the brand comes off: signage, materials, digital presence.\n- **Final accounting** — all fees settled, and a final Gross Sales report filed.\n- **Post-term restrictions** — any that your agreement imposes.\n\nStart the wind-down early. De-identification done calmly over months is a project; done frantically after expiry is a breach waiting to happen. The Franchise Termination Policy (§2.4.41) governs termination-related obligations, and the renewal-decline path follows the same obligations.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Renewal questions: support@unsaidmemories.com. Renewal notices and other legal notices: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "selling-your-franchise-transfer": "\n# Selling Your Franchise (Transfer)\n\nThis page covers how a franchise transfer works in practice: the franchisor's right of first refusal, the buyer approval process, the transfer fee and financial close-out, what changes in the system at closing, and what happens if the buyer is not approved. This page summarizes the Franchise Transfer Policy (§2.4.40), which governs. Your franchise is a business, and businesses get sold — but the franchise agreement runs between you and the franchisor, and the franchisor has a say in who the next operator is.\n\n## Check the right of first refusal first\n\nBefore you market the business to anyone, check the agreement's right-of-first-refusal clause. The franchisor typically holds a right of first refusal on the sale: you present the bona fide offer, or the proposed terms, and the franchisor gets a window to match it. This is not a formality; skipping it can void the transfer. Know the window, know what triggers it, and start the clock with written notice, not a phone call.\n\n## The approval process\n\nThe buyer is not buying your shop the way they would buy an independent business. They are applying to become a franchisee, and the franchisor approves or denies them under the same eligibility standards you met — the Franchise Eligibility & Approval Policy (§2.4.31). The policy sets a 45-day approval clock once the application is complete. Your job in this phase:\n\n- **Give the buyer everything early.** Financials, the agreement, the fee schedule, the standards. A buyer who discovers the royalty structure during due diligence instead of from you is a buyer getting nervous.\n- **Keep operating normally.** The shop does not get to coast while it is for sale. Standards compliance, reporting, and fee currency all continue. A shop in visible decline during the sale process invites a lower offer and a harder approval.\n- **Do not promise what is not yours.** You can sell your business; you cannot sell the franchise rights — those are the franchisor's to grant. Any purchase agreement should be contingent on franchisor approval. A buyer who \"didn't know\" approval was required is a deal that dies expensively.\n\n## The transfer fee and the money\n\nThe agreement's fee schedule includes a transfer fee, in the amount stated there, typically paid at closing. Beyond the fee, the financial close-out has to be exact:\n\n- All royalties and fund contributions current through the transfer date.\n- A final Gross Sales report filed for the stub period — the discipline in Reporting Your Gross Sales (§3.8.5) applies to partial periods too.\n- Any audit findings resolved.\n\nThe seller's books close clean; the buyer's start clean. A stub-period reporting dispute discovered six months later, with the seller gone, is everyone's problem and nobody's responsibility — which is why it gets settled at closing.\n\n## What changes in the system\n\nOn transfer, the records move:\n\n1. The franchise record points to the new owner's login.\n2. Your owner login is deactivated — the same-day rule in Your Staff: Users, Roles, and Access (§3.8.10). A former owner with active access is the worst version of the departed-employee problem.\n3. The buyer completes their own Stripe onboarding for the shop. Bank links do not transfer; see Connecting Your Bank (Stripe Onboarding) (§3.8.4).\n\nThe shop itself continues; the business continues; the person changes. The agreement record may be replaced or assigned depending on the structure — confirm with the franchisor which it is, because it determines whose signature is on what going forward.\n\n## If the buyer is not approved\n\nIt happens. The buyer does not meet the financial requirements, does not clear the eligibility review, or the franchisor exercises its right of first refusal and buys the business itself. Have the contingency in your sale agreement before you need it: what happens to deposits, who bears the transfer-application costs, and how long the window stays open for a second buyer. The 45-day clock runs per application — a second buyer means a second clock.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Transfer questions: support@unsaidmemories.com. Transfer and right-of-first-refusal notices: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "getting-help-and-resolving-disputes": "\n# Getting Help and Resolving Disputes\n\nThis page is the map for when something is wrong: where to go for what, in what order, and what happens when the normal channels do not resolve it. The dispute path described here is set by the Franchise Dispute Resolution Policy (§2.4.42), which governs.\n\n## The support order: use it in sequence\n\n1. **The portal first.** Most operational questions answer themselves in the screens: a report that looks wrong is usually a filter; a payout question is usually the Stripe connections page; a permission problem is usually the users screen. Check before you ask — not because asking is discouraged, but because the answer is faster.\n2. **Franchise support.** Your day-to-day channel for things the portal cannot answer: onboarding issues, standards questions, fee questions, system problems. Use the support ticket system rather than direct messages and side channels. Tickets create a record, and the record is what turns \"I told someone about this weeks ago\" into something verifiable.\n3. **Formal notice.** When it matters legally — fee disputes, cure notices, renewal, transfer, right of first refusal — it goes in writing through the formal channel the agreement specifies. A support ticket is operational; a formal notice is legal. Do not confuse them. The 30-day fee dispute window under the Franchise Fees & Royalties Policy (§2.4.34) runs from the statement whether your ticket got answered or not.\n\nKeep your own log of significant issues: date, what happened, who you spoke to, what was promised. Nobody maintains this log until the first time they need it, and by then it is too late to reconstruct.\n\n## The dispute resolution path\n\nThe Franchise Dispute Resolution Policy (§2.4.42) sets the escalation: **negotiation → mediation → arbitration**. Each stage has a purpose:\n\n- **Negotiation** is the direct conversation — you and the franchisor working it out. Most disputes end here, and that is the system working. Come with records, not grievances.\n- **Mediation** brings in a neutral third party to facilitate. The mediator does not decide; they help you both find the resolution. It is cheaper and faster than arbitration, and it preserves the working relationship, which matters because you are still in business together afterward.\n- **Arbitration** is binding. The arbitrator decides, and that decision stands. The administrator and seat for arbitration are as stated in the Franchise Dispute Resolution Policy (§2.4.42) and your agreement.\n\nMarketplace disputes under the Dispute Resolution Policy (§2.1.36) are a different track for a different relationship. Your franchise disputes run through the Franchise Dispute Resolution Policy (§2.4.42), not the marketplace process.\n\n## What not to do in a dispute\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Do not stop performing. Withholding royalties, skipping reports, or letting standards slide \"until this is resolved\" converts your dispute into your breach. The agreement's obligations do not pause because you are unhappy, and the Franchise Termination Policy (§2.4.41) is unambiguous about what sustained non-performance leads to.\n\n- **Do not litigate in public.** Social media, review sites, franchisee group chats — nothing said there helps your case, and plenty hurts it. Disputes are resolved in the process, not in the comments.\n- **Do not sign under pressure.** If a resolution is proposed, read it the way you read the original agreement (see Your Franchise Agreement (§3.8.3)). A settlement signed in frustration binds just as firmly as the agreement did.\n\n## The relationship is the asset\n\nIt sounds soft for the last article of a help portal, but it is the truest thing in it: the franchise relationship works when both sides act like long-term partners, because the economics require it. The franchisor needs successful operators; you need a franchisor worth paying. Disputes handled professionally — documented, escalated properly, resolved and moved past — are part of a healthy long-term relationship. The ones that fester are not.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Franchise support: support@unsaidmemories.com. Formal notices and dispute correspondence under the agreement: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "how-your-franchise-gets-created": "\n# How Your Franchise Gets Created\n\nThis page explains what happens when the corporate team creates your franchise on the platform: the six records the signup action builds, why your URL slug deserves care, and what your agreement's pending status means. Eligibility and approval before any of this happens are governed by the Franchise Eligibility & Approval Policy (§2.4.31), and the onboarding obligations on both sides by the Franchise Onboarding Policy (§2.4.32). The Franchise Disclosure Document controls the offer and sale of franchises; this page describes platform mechanics only and makes no representation about earnings or outcomes.\n\n## Signup starts on the corporate side\n\nNobody self-signs-up for a franchise. Onboarding starts when someone with admin access opens the franchise signup screen, enters your business name, your name and email, the shop address, and a royalty rate, and selects \"Create Franchise.\" That one action builds your entire existence on the platform — six records, in order.\n\n## The six records\n\n1. **Your workspace.** Your franchise's fenced-off area on the platform, linked under the UnSaid Memories master. Everything the platform knows about your shop hangs off this.\n2. **Your franchise record.** Business name, URL slug, address, phone, email, and your storefront URL if you have one. This is the business's identity on the platform — the name customers see, the slug in URLs.\n3. **Your agreement.** Created in pending status — created but not signed. The royalty rate entered at signup lands here; if the field was left blank, it stays empty until it is set before signing.\n4. **Your primary location.** Named \"[Business Name] — Main,\" marked primary and active, with its timezone set to America/Chicago. Your shop's physical footprint starts here; additional locations are added later.\n5. **Your login.** The owner login described in Getting Into Your Franchise Portal (§3.8.1) — franchise-owner role, marked as the owner. The franchise record is then linked to it, so the system knows which person owns which business.\n6. **The audit trail.** A permission-audit entry recording the signup. Then, unless the option was deselected, the invite email goes out.\n\nThe whole thing is one action. If any required piece is missing — no business name, no owner name, no owner email — signup is rejected and creates nothing. It is all-or-nothing: there is no such thing as a half-created franchise in the system.\n\n## The slug: choose it carefully\n\nYour URL slug is derived from your business name (\"Portal Grooming — Austin\" becomes \"portal-grooming-austin\") unless a custom one is entered. It must be unique across the platform; if it is taken, signup fails with \"Slug already taken\" and nothing is created. The corporate team chooses another.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Treat the slug as permanent. It appears in URLs, in records, and in the audit log. Changing it later is possible the way repainting a storefront is possible — technically doable, practically a mess. Get it right at signup: short, spelled the way customers spell your name, with no clever punctuation. The system strips anything that is not a lowercase letter, number, or hyphen.\n\n## What \"pending\" means for you\n\nYour agreement starts pending. The franchise record is active from the first moment — the business exists — but the agreement is not signed yet. Until it is, you are operating on the onboarding handshake, not the contract. Get the signature done promptly; the Franchise Agreement Policy (§2.4.30) governs what the agreement contains, and the longer it sits pending, the longer the ambiguity about which terms bind you. If the royalty rate field was left blank at signup, the agreement has no rate yet — that is resolved before signing, not after. The rate itself is set in the agreement; see Your Franchise Agreement (§3.8.3).\n\n## When signup fails\n\nThe failure modes are all visible. \"Slug already taken\" means choose another slug. A mid-transaction failure rolls back rather than leaving you half-built. If the corporate team is not sure whether signup went through, they check for your franchise record by slug before retrying — retrying blind is how duplicates happen.\n\nThe invite email failing is the one silent failure, and it is handled: signup succeeds regardless, marked as email-not-sent. Your records exist; only the email did not go out. The fix is resending, not re-running signup.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Franchise signup and onboarding problems: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "your-franchise-agreement": "\n# Your Franchise Agreement\n\nThis page covers your franchise agreement as it lives on the platform: what the record contains, what to read before you sign, what happens at signing, and how amendments work. This page summarizes the Franchise Agreement Policy (§2.4.30), which governs. The Franchise Disclosure Document controls the offer and sale of franchises, and no page in this portal makes any representation about earnings.\n\n## What the agreement is in the system\n\nOne agreement record holds: your franchise, the franchisor, the agreement type, its status, the royalty rate, the fee schedule (every other fee, itemized), the signed date, start date, and expiry date, and a link to the executed copy. When the corporate team creates your franchise, this record is born pending, carrying the royalty rate from the signup form — or empty if that field was left blank. See How Your Franchise Gets Created (§3.8.2).\n\nPending is not signed. It means the paperwork exists and is waiting. Do not let it wait long.\n\n## Read these parts twice\n\nRead the whole agreement, but read these parts twice:\n\n- **The fee exhibit.** The fee schedule is where every amount you owe lives: the initial fee, royalty, marketing fund contribution, transfer fee, renewal fee, audit-cost reimbursement, training fees, technology fees, and late charges. The rule is simple: no fee is charged that is not in the agreement or the fee schedule. If you are invoiced for something you cannot find in that schedule, that is a dispute, not a bill. How each fee is defined, calculated, collected, and disputed is set out in the Franchise Fees & Royalties Policy (§2.4.34).\n- **The term.** Start date to expiry date is how long you are in. Know both dates the way you know your lease dates. Renewal is not automatic — it is a process under the Franchise Renewal Policy (§2.4.39), and it starts well before expiry. See Renewal (§3.8.11).\n- **The territory clause.** What geography is yours, and what is reserved — online sales, national accounts. This is the clause that prevents the awkward conversation about a second shop opening nearby. The Franchise Territory Policy (§2.4.33) is the policy behind it.\n- **The post-term obligations.** What survives when the agreement ends: de-identification, any non-compete, and the final accounting. Read it now, while signing is voluntary, not later, when it is urgent.\n\n## The signature moment\n\nWhen you sign, the status moves from pending to executed, the signed date is stamped, and the record points to the executed copy. Keep your own copy as well — downloaded and saved somewhere that is not this portal. The platform is reliable; your records should not depend on any single system.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> If the royalty rate was empty at creation, it must be filled in before you sign. An agreement signed with no rate is a blank left to be filled later, to someone's detriment. Do not sign blanks.\n\n## Amendments\n\nAgreements get amended: rates change, territories adjust, terms get clarified. An amendment is a new documented state, not a handshake. If it is not in the agreement record, it did not happen — that is operational reality, because every downstream system (billing, reporting, audits) reads the record, not your memory of a phone call. When an amendment is executed, confirm that the fee schedule and royalty rate in the record reflect it before you go back to work.\n\n## When you will open it day to day\n\nYou will open the agreement when a fee looks wrong (check the exhibit), when you are thinking about selling (the transfer terms; see Selling Your Franchise (Transfer) (§3.8.12) and the Franchise Transfer Policy (§2.4.40)), when the term approaches expiry (renewal), or when there is a dispute (start with the agreement, then the Franchise Dispute Resolution Policy (§2.4.42)). The agreement is the reference, not the daily driver — but when you need it, you need the executed copy with the right dates and the right numbers, which is why the signature moment matters.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Questions about your agreement: support@unsaidmemories.com. Legal notices under the agreement: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "connecting-your-bank-stripe-onboarding": "\n# Connecting Your Bank (Stripe Onboarding)\n\nThis page covers how your franchise connects its bank through Stripe onboarding: how to start, what happens on Stripe's side, the multi-shop trap, what \"connected\" guarantees, and how to change banks later. The money movement described here operates under the Limited Payment Collection Agency policy (§2.9.3) and the Funds Flow & Custody Controls (§2.9.4); the legal basis for the payment flow is the payment-collection term of the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1). The franchise fee mechanics that ride on these rails are in the Franchise Fees & Royalties Policy (§2.4.34).\n\n## How the money moves\n\nYour franchise is paid through its own Stripe Express account — not through the corporate bank account, and not through anyone else's. Your shop's bank link records the connection between your shop and your Stripe account. Money flows buyer → Stripe → your account → your bank. The platform never holds your money in its own bank account, which is the point of the arrangement — and the reason onboarding has to be done right.\n\n## Before you begin\n\n- Check the shop switcher first. The Express account is created for the **active shop**. Onboarding while switched to the wrong shop puts the bank link on the wrong business. See Getting Into Your Franchise Portal (§3.8.1).\n- Have the business's legal details, the representative's identity information, and the bank account details ready. Bank details are entered on Stripe's hosted pages only; the platform never sees or stores your account and routing numbers.\n\n## Starting onboarding\n\n1. In the admin portal, go to the Stripe connections area and start onboarding.\n2. The system resolves your shop from the switcher and checks whether your shop already has a bank link. If one exists, it reuses it and gives you a fresh onboarding link. If not, it creates a new Stripe Express account (US, with card payments and transfers capabilities) and stores Stripe's account ID for it.\n3. Follow the Stripe-hosted onboarding link and finish on Stripe's pages.\n\nStripe performs the identity verification and the tax reporting (Form 1099-K), and the platform stays out of holding your most sensitive financial data.\n\n## Finishing on Stripe's side\n\nFill in the business's legal details, the representative's identity, and the bank account completely, in one sitting if you can. An incomplete onboarding leaves the account in a restricted state: it exists and the link is stored, but capabilities are not active, which means transfers do not flow. The portal's connections page is where you check state — if it says onboarding is incomplete, the money is not moving yet, whatever the sales screen says.\n\nCommon stall points: a legal name that does not match the tax record (doing-business-as versus legal entity confusion), an address Stripe cannot verify, or an identity document photo that does not pass. All are fixable, and all are on Stripe's side; the platform cannot override them. Budget a few days, not a few minutes, especially around holidays.\n\n## The multi-shop trap\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> If you own more than one franchise, each shop needs its own onboarding — one bank link per shop, no sharing. Onboarding shop A does nothing for shop B. Go shop by shop, checking the switcher each time, and confirm each shop shows a connected account before you move on. The failure mode is quiet: shop B takes orders, the money has nowhere to go, and you find out when the payout does not arrive.\n\n## What \"connected\" actually guarantees\n\nA completed onboarding with active card-payments and transfers capabilities means your shop can receive its share of transactions and can push payouts to your bank. It does not mean payouts are instant — they settle on the platform's payout schedule (a 7–14 day rolling delay keeps funds available for dispute reversals before they leave Stripe). It does not mean transfers cannot be frozen: the Company may suspend transfers as an enforcement measure under the franchise policies, including the Franchise Termination Policy (§2.4.41), and a freeze stops withdrawals while leaving the balance available for reversals and refunds.\n\nIf payouts stop and your Stripe onboarding is complete, the problem is not the bank link. Check for an enforcement hold, or a failed payout method on Stripe's side, before assuming the platform is at fault.\n\n## Changing banks later\n\nBank changes go through the same Stripe-hosted flow — update the details on Stripe's side rather than asking someone to edit a record. There is no bank account number stored on the platform's side; there is only Stripe's account ID. The sensitive data lives with the regulated party, as designed.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Bank connection and payout problems: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "reporting-your-gross-sales": "\n# Reporting Your Gross Sales\n\nThis page covers how to report Gross Sales: what counts, the reporting routine, the underreporting threshold, and how to amend a report. This page summarizes the reporting obligation in the Franchise Fees & Royalties Policy (§2.4.34), which governs. Royalties are calculated on Gross Sales, so it is the most expensive number in your business to get wrong — every error compounds into the royalty, the marketing fund contribution, and, if it is discovered on audit, interest and audit costs.\n\n## What counts\n\nGross Sales is defined by your agreement, not by approximation. The definition sets out gross revenue from the franchised business before deductions, with specific inclusions and exclusions spelled out. Confirm yours in the fee exhibit. The questions that actually cause disputes:\n\n- **Refunds.** Does a refunded sale reduce the period's Gross Sales, or was it counted when the sale happened? Your agreement says. Do not assume.\n- **Taxes collected.** Sales tax you collect is generally excluded — it is not your revenue. But \"generally\" is not \"always\"; check your agreement.\n- **Gift cards.** Sale of the card versus redemption of the card: one of these is the revenue event, and counting both is double-counting. Your agreement picks one.\n- **Discounts and comps.** Gross usually means before discounts, but some agreements net them. This is a real-money distinction at volume.\n\nThe agreement's definition controls — its inclusions, exclusions, and timing — and the reporting mechanics below operate around it.\n\n## The reporting routine\n\nYour agreement sets the cadence — weekly or monthly. The discipline is the same either way:\n\n1. **Close the period cleanly.** Make sure the period's orders, refunds, and adjustments are all posted before you report. A report filed while refunds are still arriving is a report you will have to amend.\n2. **Report one number, one way.** Gross Sales for the period, computed the same way every time. If your method changes (a new point-of-sale flow, new product lines), document the change and keep the old method's figures for the transition period.\n3. **Keep the backup.** The report is a number; the backup is the transaction detail behind it. Keep both. The franchisor may verify your reported figures against payment-processing records — that is the system working, not an accusation. If your number and the processor's number disagree, you want your detail in hand before that conversation. See Audits and Inspections (§3.8.9).\n\n## The underreporting trap\n\nThe Franchise Audit & Inspection Policy (§2.4.38) sets a 2% underreporting threshold that allocates audit costs to you. Read that number for what it is: a 2% error on real volume is an entirely plausible bookkeeping mistake, and it still costs you the audit. The threshold is not there to punish fraud — fraud is handled much more severely — it is there to make careless reporting expensive enough that nobody is careless. The cheapest audit is the one that never happens because your numbers reconcile.\n\nYour defenses are practical: reconcile reported Gross Sales to your payment processor statements every period before filing; investigate variances the same day, not at quarter-end; never let \"we will true it up later\" become a habit. Later never comes, and the true-up always favors the auditor.\n\n## Amending a report\n\nIf you find an error after filing, amend promptly and in writing. A voluntary amendment before anyone asks is a bookkeeping correction; the same error found on audit is underreporting. The difference between those two outcomes is entirely about who found it first. When you amend, state the period, the original figure, the corrected figure, and the cause. Keep it factual; nobody needs a narrative.\n\n## Related pages\n\n- Royalties and the Marketing Fund (§3.8.6)\n- Audits and Inspections (§3.8.9)\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Gross Sales reporting questions: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "royalties-and-the-marketing-fund": "\n# Royalties and the Marketing Fund\n\nThis page covers the royalty and the marketing fund contribution: what each pays for, how collection works, what happens when a payment fails, and how to dispute a fee. This page summarizes the Franchise Fees & Royalties Policy (§2.4.34) and the Franchise Marketing Fund Policy (§2.4.35), which govern. The two obligations are collected together and accounted separately: the royalty pays for the license and the system; the marketing fund contribution pays for collective marketing. They arrive on the same collection and they are not the same money — the franchisor does not offset one against the other, and neither should you in your books.\n\n## The royalty\n\nThe royalty is calculated on Gross Sales (see Reporting Your Gross Sales (§3.8.5)) at the royalty rate in your franchise agreement. The rate was entered at signup; if it was left blank, it was resolved before you signed — an executed agreement has a rate. The rate itself is stated in your agreement.\n\nWhat the rate applies to matters as much as the rate: Gross Sales as your agreement defines it, for the reporting period your agreement sets. A rate applied to the wrong base is the most common royalty dispute in franchising, and it is always avoidable — the base is Gross Sales, the rate is the agreement, the math is multiplication. When a royalty figure looks wrong, check the base first, the rate second, the arithmetic last, in that order, because that is the order of likelihood.\n\n## The marketing fund\n\nUnder the Franchise Marketing Fund Policy (§2.4.35), the fund is segregated — your contributions do not mingle with operating money — and the franchisor reports on it annually. You are not buying ads directly; you are buying into collective spend. In practice:\n\n- You contribute at the percentage of Gross Sales stated in your agreement, alongside the royalty.\n- You do not control how the fund is spent — that is the point of a fund — but you are entitled to the annual accounting of where it went.\n- Local marketing minimums, if your agreement has them, are separate from and additional to the fund contribution. One is collective; the other is yours. Do not confuse them.\n\nIf the annual fund report does not arrive, ask for it. The obligation to report is the franchisor's.\n\n## How collection works\n\nRoyalties and fund contributions are collected by ACH debit or by deduction against your connected payouts, on the schedule the agreement states. You maintain a valid payment method and sufficient collected funds. Under the Franchise Fees & Royalties Policy (§2.4.34):\n\n- **Failed collection:** notice at 3 business days, a second attempt at 7. Late charges accrue from the original due date per the fee schedule.\n- **Separate accounting:** royalty obligations and marketing fund obligations are never offset against each other. A dispute about one does not pause the other.\n- **The 30/60-day line:** non-payment beyond 30 days past due is a material breach. Beyond 60 days, it is grounds for termination proceedings under the Franchise Termination Policy (§2.4.41), subject to notice and cure. Sixty days of unpaid royalties is not a cash-flow strategy; it is the beginning of the end of the franchise.\n\n## Disputing a fee\n\nFee disputes go in writing within 30 days of the statement.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Pay the undisputed amounts on schedule during the dispute. Withholding everything while you argue about one line converts a billing disagreement into a breach. Dispute the line, pay the rest, and keep the paper trail.\n\nThe disputes worth having: a wrong Gross Sales base, a rate applied that does not match the agreement, a fee that is not in the fee schedule at all. The disputes that are not: \"I didn't know\" (the exhibit was there at signing), \"nobody told me\" (the statement told you). Read the exhibit once a year the way you would re-read your lease. See Your Franchise Agreement (§3.8.3).\n\n## The initial fee and the others\n\nThe initial franchise fee was due at signing — one-time, and non-refundable except as the agreement provides. Its amount is stated in your agreement. The rest of the fee schedule — transfer, renewal, training, technology, late charges — each activates on its own trigger. You do not need to memorize them; you need to know where the exhibit is, and to check it before any transaction that might trigger one: selling the business (transfer fee), renewing (renewal fee), adding staff who need training (training fees).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Royalty and marketing fund questions: support@unsaidmemories.com. Fee dispute notices: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "living-the-brand-standards": "\n# Living the Brand Standards\n\nThis page covers what the brand standards reach in daily operation, where compliance happens in your portal, how standards updates work, and what a standards notice means. The standards themselves are set by the Franchise Brand Standards Policy (§2.4.36), which governs; this page describes what they feel like on a Tuesday.\n\n## What \"the brand\" covers\n\nMore than the logo. The standards reach everywhere a customer encounters you:\n\n- **Marks and name.** How the brand name and logo appear — signage, uniforms, the website, social profiles, the van if you have one. There is a documented right way; improvising the logo in a design tool is the fastest route to a standards notice.\n- **Service presentation.** How services are named, described, and priced in the catalog. Your service menu should read as though it belongs to the same company as every other location — because it does.\n- **Customer experience.** Greeting, intake, handling, follow-up. The standards describe the floor of acceptable, not the ceiling: exceeding them is encouraged; improvising replacements for them is not.\n- **Facility.** Cleanliness, maintenance, layout cues. The standards here are specific because customer trust is physical — a stained ceiling tile undoes a great deal of marketing.\n\nNone of this is about stifling you. It is about the customer in another city getting the experience the brand promised. Your shop's reputation and every other shop's reputation are the same asset.\n\n## Where standards meet your portal\n\nMost standards compliance happens in screens you already use: services and pricing in the catalog, business information and hours in location settings, staff presentation in how you run the floor. The portal has no \"brand standards\" button — compliance is a property of doing the normal work correctly, not a separate task. That means drift is silent. Nobody alerts you that your service descriptions have slowly diverged from the brand voice over six months. You notice on audit day, or a customer notices first.\n\n## The update cycle\n\nStandards change: new services launch, presentation guidelines are refined, the brand evolves. Updates come through franchise communications and are binding from their effective date — \"I didn't see the email\" is not a defense, which is why the communications channel for standards updates should be an address someone actually reads. When an update lands:\n\n1. Read it the week it arrives, not the quarter it gets audited.\n2. Walk your shop against it — physically walk, eyes open — within two weeks.\n3. Fix what is off. Most fixes are cheap when they are fresh and expensive when they are findings.\n\n## Standards notices and cure\n\nWhen something is out of compliance, you receive a notice describing the deficiency and a cure period. Treat a notice like a smoke alarm: the point is not the noise, it is the fire. Cure within the period, document the cure (photos for physical items, screenshots for digital), and confirm receipt. Under the Franchise Brand Standards Policy (§2.4.36), repeated or uncured deficiencies move into the enforcement path: a single dusty corner is a notice; a pattern of ignored notices is a breach. The distance between those two is entirely about responsiveness.\n\nThe franchisees who never have standards problems are not the ones with the nicest shops. They are the ones who fix the small thing the week it is noticed instead of the quarter it is audited. Compliance is measured on audit; see Audits and Inspections (§3.8.9) and the Franchise Audit & Inspection Policy (§2.4.38).\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Brand standards questions: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "training-and-certification": "\n# Training and Certification\n\nThis page covers the training the franchise system requires: initial training, ongoing training through the learning system in your admin portal, training your own staff, and where the cost rules live. Training — initial and ongoing — is required by the Franchise Training Policy (§2.4.37), which governs, and the platform delivers it through the learning system built into the admin portal (/admin/lms-dashboard and its related sections): a curriculum with enrollment, progress tracking, assessments, and compliance records.\n\n## Initial training\n\nBefore or shortly after opening, you complete the initial curriculum: brand orientation, service standards, the systems you will use daily (booking, point of sale, CRM), and the policies that bind you. Your agreement may divide the cost of training, as stated in its fee schedule, but the obligation to complete it is not negotiable regardless of who pays. An owner who skips training does not just miss information; they start the relationship by signaling that the system's requirements are optional. That signal gets remembered.\n\nSchedule training as a critical-path item. Everything downstream — hiring, marketing, opening day — assumes a trained owner.\n\n## Ongoing training and the learning system\n\nThe learning system is not a one-time course; it is the channel for everything the system needs you to know after opening: new service launches, standards updates, system changes, and compliance refreshers. Enrollment, curriculum, progress, assessments, and compliance tracking are all in the portal. When new required training is issued, it shows up as an enrollment — treat it with the same urgency as a standards update (see Living the Brand Standards (§3.8.7)), because it usually accompanies one.\n\nAssessments are not decorative. If the system requires a passing score, a failed assessment is an incomplete requirement. Retake it, learn the material, and move on. The compliance record is what the franchisor sees on audit: \"completed,\" with a date, or not. See Audits and Inspections (§3.8.9).\n\n## Training your staff\n\nYour staff's training is your responsibility, not the franchisor's — with one distinction: brand-required certifications for specific roles (groomers handling certain services, for example) may have to come through the system's curriculum. Check the Franchise Training Policy (§2.4.37) for which roles require system certification and which you train yourself. Either way, keep records: who was trained, on what, and when. \"I trained them\" without a record is a sentence, not an answer, when something goes wrong.\n\nNew hires complete role-required training before they are turned loose on customers, not \"when things slow down.\" Things never slow down, and the customer who gets the untrained version of your shop does not know or care about your staffing timeline.\n\n## The cost question\n\nWho pays for what — travel to initial training, ongoing course fees, staff certification costs — is stated in your agreement's fee schedule; see Your Franchise Agreement (§3.8.3). Whatever it says, budget training as a real line item, not an afterthought. Operators who treat training as overhead to minimize end up paying for it anyway, as audit findings and standards cures.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Training and certification questions: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "audits-and-inspections": "\n# Audits and Inspections\n\nThis page covers the franchisor's audit and inspection rights: what gets audited, how to stay ready, how to conduct yourself during an audit, and what happens after the findings. This page summarizes the Franchise Audit & Inspection Policy (§2.4.38), which governs. The audit right is in the agreement you signed, and it is how the system protects every franchisee from the one who is not playing straight — an audit of your shop protects the operator in the next town, whose brand value depends on your compliance.\n\n## What gets audited\n\nTwo kinds of review, and they are different exercises:\n\n- **Financial audit.** Your reported Gross Sales against the underlying records — orders, payments, processor statements, refunds. The question is narrow: did you report what you earned? The 2% underreporting threshold in the Franchise Audit & Inspection Policy (§2.4.38) is the tripwire: cross it, and the audit's cost lands on you, on top of the shortfall plus interest. Below it, findings are still findings, but you are not paying for the audit itself.\n- **Operational inspection.** The shop itself, against the brand standards (see Living the Brand Standards (§3.8.7)): facility, presentation, service delivery, staff certification records. This is the walk-through with a checklist. It is pass/fail on specifics, not a general impression — which is good news, because specifics can be prepared for.\n\n## Stay ready all the time\n\nThe operators who dread audits are the ones who prepare for audits. The ones who do not are the ones who never stopped being ready:\n\n- **Reconcile monthly, not annually.** Reported Gross Sales tied to processor statements every period; see Reporting Your Gross Sales (§3.8.5). An auditor finding a variance you already investigated and documented is a non-event. An auditor finding one you never looked at is a finding.\n- **Keep the records.** Transaction detail, refund records, training completions, standards cures — retained and retrievable. \"The system has it\" is true until the auditor asks for the thing the system does not have. Your records are the backup to the platform's records, and the redundancy is the point.\n- **Fix notices immediately.** An uncured standards notice from six months ago, still open, is the first thing an inspector writes up. Cure periods are grace, not suggestions.\n\n## During the audit\n\nCooperate fully and promptly. Provide what is asked, when it is asked. Do not editorialize, do not slow-walk, and do not get defensive — none of those behaviors improves outcomes. If you disagree with a preliminary finding, say so factually, with your records in hand: \"Here is the transaction detail showing the refund was posted in the next period, which is why the figures differ\" resolves things that arguments do not.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Do not \"clean up\" records after receiving notice of an audit. Correcting a genuine error you found yourself is fine, and should be documented as a voluntary amendment (see Reporting Your Gross Sales (§3.8.5)). Reconstructing records to match what you wish you had reported is a different activity, with much worse consequences.\n\n## After the findings\n\nFindings come with required actions and timelines. Meet them. Pay what is owed — shortfall, interest, and audit costs if the threshold was crossed — on the stated schedule. Then fix the process that produced the finding, because a repeat finding is always treated more seriously than the first. An audit that finds the same underreporting two years running stops being about bookkeeping.\n\nThe audit right also runs the other direction in one respect: the marketing fund's annual accounting (see Royalties and the Marketing Fund (§3.8.6)). If it does not arrive, asking where the fund money went is your question to ask, and answering it is the franchisor's obligation.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Audit and inspection questions: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "admin-access-and-login": "\n# Admin Access and Login\n\nThis page covers how administrators sign in to the admin portal, how access is granted, what happens after sign-in, and how to resolve the common sign-in failures. Admin access is granted to provisioned personnel only; there is no self-service sign-up for the admin portal.\n\n## Before you begin\n\n- You must have an active staff record created by the owner or by an administrator who holds user-management permission. Access is granted person by person.\n- Your sign-in email must match the email on your staff record exactly. Matching is not case-sensitive, but a different address is treated as a different person.\n- Required permission to grant or change admin access: user-management permission. If you do not hold it, ask an administrator who does.\n\n## Signing in\n\nWhere the platform's sign-in door lives is deliberately left out of every page, this one included: a publicized login address invites attacks. The entry is handed to you privately when your access is provisioned, by the owner or the administrator who creates your staff record.\n\n1. Open the sign-in entry provisioned to you.\n2. Sign in with Google, or enter your admin email and password and select **SIGN IN**.\n3. Wait for the portal to confirm your session and resolve your role. Your authority comes from your staff record, not from the sign-in page; the page establishes who you are, and the record establishes what you may do.\n\nIf your session expires while you work, the portal returns you to the sign-in screen at the next session check. Work in an unsaved form may be lost. Sign in again to continue; your module permissions and tenant context are re-fetched on sign-in. If you are starting a long review session, sign in immediately before you begin.\n\n## Role routing after sign-in\n\nOnly platform administrators remain in the admin portal. Other roles are routed to the portal that matches their role:\n\n- Groomers are routed to the employee dashboard.\n- Customers are routed to the customer dashboard.\n- A person who holds an admin role inside a seller organization, or a reception membership, is routed to the seller dashboard. A seller-side admin role is not platform admin access, and it does not grant entry to the admin portal.\n\n## If you see \"not an administrator\"\n\nThis message means your identity was confirmed but no admin record exists for you, your staff record is inactive, or the email on the record does not match the email you signed in with. There is no self-service remedy.\n\n1. Confirm you signed in with the email on your staff record.\n2. If the email is correct, ask the owner or an administrator with user-management permission to check your staff record.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Never sign in with another administrator's credentials to work around an access failure. Every action in the audit log is attributed to the signed-in identity, so a shared sign-in makes your actions appear as another person's and destroys the reliability of the record.\n\n## Signing out\n\n1. Select sign-out in the portal header.\n2. Confirm you have returned to the sign-in screen. Sign-out ends the session; it does not merely hide it.\n\nOn a shared computer, always sign out when you leave. The portal may display the last signed-in identity to the next person who opens the browser.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n- **The portal shows a loading frame and never finishes.** The session check is failing, usually because of a network problem or a blocked or corrupted session cookie. Refresh the page. If the problem continues, treat it as an infrastructure problem, not a permissions problem, and escalate under the CONTACT callout below.\n- **You were returned to the sign-in page while working.** Your session expired. Sign in again.\n- **A colleague can see a section you cannot.** Module permissions differ between administrators by design. See Roles and Permissions (§3.9.4).\n- **Everyone can suddenly see every section.** Treat this as a symptom of a failing permissions check, not as a change in anyone's grants, and escalate it. See Roles and Permissions (§3.9.4).\n\n## Related pages\n\n- Roles and Permissions (§3.9.4)\n- User Management (§3.9.3)\n- Audit Logs (§3.9.15)\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Access and sign-in problems: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "refunds-and-returns": "\n# Refunds and Returns\n\nThis page covers the refunds surface at `/admin/refunds` and the returns surface at `/admin/returns`: how to issue a refund, how returns are recorded and decided, and the reconciliation routine that keeps refund records complete. This page summarizes the Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.1.19) and the Return and Refund Policy — Sellers (§2.3.6), which govern. A refund is not the same act as a chargeback; chargebacks are governed by the Chargeback Policy (§2.1.35).\n\n## Before you begin\n\n- Required permission: the refunds and disputes module grant, at write level to issue a refund.\n- You need the order open in the order detail view, so you can verify the payment before you refund it. See Orders Management (§3.9.9).\n\n## The refund record\n\nEach refund record is tied to its order and its payment, and states the amount, who issued it, when, and why. The reason is not optional. A refund recorded without a reason is a movement of money nobody can later explain, and it will be found at reconciliation.\n\n## Issuing a refund\n\n1. Open the order detail and confirm the payment was actually captured. Refunding an authorization that was never captured is a different operation — a void — with different effects. If the surface permits a refund against a payment that does not exist, stop and report it as a defect; do not use it as a workflow.\n2. Check the existing refund records for the order, and check for any refund still settling with the payment processor. Do this before every refund, without exception.\n3. Decide full or partial. For a partial refund, record the reason at line level: which items, and why those items. \"Partial refund\" alone is not a record.\n4. Apply the correct fee treatment. The treatment depends on who ordered the refund and whose fault it was, under the Seller Fee Schedule (§2.3.14) and the Fee Reversals and Adjustments Policy (§2.3.19), which govern. Where the Company ordered the refund, the Company's fee is refunded with it; where the refund results from the seller's fault, the fee treatment follows those documents. Record which treatment was applied on the refund record.\n5. On a marketplace order, confirm the corresponding reversal of the seller's share. The buyer refund and the reversal out of the seller's balance are two sides of one event. If they are performed as separate steps, perform them in the same session and verify that both completed. See Payouts Oversight (§3.9.11).\n6. Confirm the buyer was notified.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Issuing a refund moves money out of the Platform and cannot be undone from this surface. Verify the payment, the amount, the fee treatment, and that no refund for the same order is already recorded or settling before you issue it. A second refund issued \"to be safe\" while the first is still settling does not combine with the first — the buyer receives both.\n\n## Returns\n\nA return is not a refund. A return is the physical event that may justify a refund. The returns surface records the return request, the reason, the return shipment and tracking, the condition on receipt, and the disposition decision: restock, discard, or return to seller.\n\n- Return windows and who bears return shipping are set by the Returns and Refunds Policy (§2.1.19) for buyers and the Return and Refund Policy — Sellers (§2.3.6) for sellers, which govern. Do not set a different window at the desk. Where an exception is granted, record it as an exception with its reason; do not process it as if the window did not exist.\n- Record the condition on receipt with specifics — for example, that the packaging is opened and an item is chewed at one corner and its tags removed. The disposition decision affects who bears the cost, and \"damaged\" alone is not specific enough to settle a later disagreement about condition.\n\n## Reconciliation\n\nReconcile refund records against the payment processor's refund records regularly — daily in a busy operation, weekly at minimum. Every refund record must have a matching processor refund, and every processor refund must have a record explaining it.\n\n- **A record with no processor refund** means the refund was recorded but never carried out, and the buyer was promised money they did not receive. Correct it immediately; this is the most serious discrepancy.\n- **A processor refund with no record** means money moved without an explanation. Establish who issued it and why, create the record with the true reason and date, and establish how it was issued outside the surface.\n\n## Related pages\n\n- Orders Management (§3.9.9)\n- Payouts Oversight (§3.9.11)\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Refund discrepancies, including a recorded refund that was never paid: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "payouts-oversight": "\n# Payouts Oversight\n\nThis page covers oversight of seller payouts: the surfaces that show money in, money moving, and each seller's connected account and wallet position; the holds and reserves to watch; the reversal and account-freeze actions; and the reconciliation routine. This page summarizes the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1), the Payment Holds and Reserves Policy (§2.3.18), and the Final Settlement and Payout Policy (§2.3.17), which govern.\n\n## How seller money moves\n\nSeller payments are processed through connected accounts with the Company's payment processor, on destination charges. The buyer pays the Platform; the Company's fee is taken at the time of charge; the seller's share is routed to the seller's connected account. The Company's own bank account is not an intermediary for a third-party sale. Payouts from the seller's connected balance to the seller's bank are made on the rolling payout delay stated in the Final Settlement and Payout Policy (§2.3.17), which governs. Amounts recovered from a seller are taken by reversal of the original transfer. The processor performs the seller's identity verification and issues the seller's annual tax information return.\n\nUnder the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1), the Company collects payment as the seller's limited agent for collection only: the buyer's payment to the Company satisfies the buyer's payment obligation to the seller, and the Company's remedies — including recovery of amounts owed — are as that document states. Your oversight work is operational: you verify that the mechanics follow the documents. You do not vary them.\n\n## Before you begin\n\n- Required permission: the payments module grant — read level for oversight, write level for a reversal or an account freeze.\n- Before any reversal or freeze, identify the decision or governing provision that authorizes it, and have the original transfer reference available. A reversal can only be made against the transfer it reverses.\n\n## The oversight surfaces\n\n- **Payments** (`/admin/payments`) — the money-in view: charges, their processor references, fee amounts, and destinations.\n- **Deposits** (`/admin/deposits`) — the money-moving view: funds settling, in batches, with timing.\n- **Connected accounts** (`/admin/stripe-connections`) — which sellers have completed connected-account onboarding, their account status, and the state of their account capabilities. A seller who has not completed onboarding cannot receive transfers.\n- **Wallet records** — the Platform-side record per seller of balances, holds, and reserves: what the Platform shows as owed and what it shows as held back.\n\nThere is no single payout schedule view in the admin portal. Payout timing is set on the seller's connected account, not in the Platform's records, so verify a seller's payout delay with the processor. Until a dedicated payout record exists, reconcile across the wallet records, the processor's records, and the payments and deposits surfaces, following the routine below.\n\n## Holds and reserves to watch\n\nUnder the New Seller Payment Hold (§2.3.16) and the Payment Holds and Reserves Policy (§2.3.18), which govern:\n\n- **New sellers** are subject to an extended hold and a rolling reserve during the introductory period those documents state.\n- **Standard holds** run until delivery is confirmed, and then for the further period those documents state, so that returns and disputes can be resolved against the funds.\n- **Enforcement holds** are imposed as an enforcement step and are distinct from standard holds. Record them as enforcement holds, with the case they belong to.\n- **Rolling reserves** hold back a portion of a seller's funds against future chargebacks and refunds.\n\nHeld and reserved amounts must be distinguishable from the available balance in the wallet record. If you cannot tell, from the record alone, how much of a seller's balance is available, held, and reserved, escalate it: a seller asking where their money is deserves an answer in those three figures.\n\n## Reversals and freezes\n\n**Transfer reversal.** Where the Company decides a dispute against a seller, the amount is recovered from the seller's connected balance by reversing the original transfer, in whole or in part. Reverse the amount the decision states — no more.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> A transfer reversal takes money out of the seller's balance and cannot be undone from this surface. Confirm the decision, the amount, and the original transfer reference before you record it. A reversal with no matching decision is an unauthorized movement of the seller's money.\n\n**Capability freeze.** Disabling transfers on a seller's connected account stops payouts to the seller's external bank account without removing the balance. Funds already in the account remain available for reversals and refunds, which is the correct position during an investigation.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> A capability freeze stops a seller receiving their money. It is an enforcement action, not an administrative convenience: record the reason, the governing provision that authorizes it, and what the seller must do for transfers to be re-enabled, before or at the moment you apply it.\n\n## Reconciliation routine\n\nPerform this reconciliation weekly at minimum, and daily in periods of high volume:\n\n1. For each seller with movement, compare the wallet balance change with the connected-account balance change. Investigate any difference.\n2. Confirm every reversal with the processor has a matching decision on the Platform. A reversal without a decision is an unauthorized movement.\n3. Confirm every payout with the processor has a matching wallet debit. A payout without a record is a gap in the books.\n4. Review holds by age. A hold older than the period the governing document allows is overdue for release. Stale holds are the most common cause of \"where is my money\" reports, and they are the Platform's error.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n- **A seller reports a payout never arrived.** Check in order: (1) the connected account is in good standing — onboarding complete, no freeze; (2) whether the amount is held or reserved in the wallet records; (3) whether the processor actually sent the payout; (4) whether the seller's bank details on file are still valid. Most cases resolve at step (2): the money is held, and the seller was not told clearly.\n- **A payout went to the wrong bank account.** The seller's bank details were changed. Verify the change was legitimate before sending anything further: a change of bank details is a known account-takeover method, so treat the report as a security incident until it is shown to be genuine.\n- **A seller's wallet balance is negative.** Recoveries exceeded the balance available. Recovery is governed by the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1), and operationally the amount is set off against future payouts. Track every negative balance actively; one that is not being worked is a loss in waiting.\n\n## Related pages\n\n- Orders Management (§3.9.9)\n- Refunds and Returns (§3.9.10)\n- Fees and Financial Settings (§3.9.13)\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Payout and reversal problems: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Suspected account takeover or fraud: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "sellers-management": "\n# Sellers Management\n\nThis page covers the sellers surface at `/admin/sellers`: the seller list and detail view, the status transitions — approve, suspend, reject — seller type assignment, and brand authorizations. Every status change affects a seller's ability to earn and creates a record the seller may appeal, so this page states what each action does before it states how to perform it. This page summarizes the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1) and the Seller Onboarding Policy (§2.3.7), which govern.\n\n## Before you begin\n\n- Required permission: the sellers module grant, at write level for status changes and type changes.\n- Never act from a list row alone. Open the seller detail view, which shows verification documents, brand authorizations, listings, orders, wallet position, violations, and appeals, and act on the whole picture.\n\n## The sellers list and detail view\n\nThe list shows each seller's identity, status, seller type, verification state, and performance indicators, with filtering and pagination. A feed view shows the seller's operational timeline, and a violations view shows the seller's enforcement history.\n\n1. Open `/admin/sellers` and filter or search to the seller.\n2. Open the seller to reach the detail view.\n3. Read the verification, listings, wallet, violations, and appeals sections before deciding anything.\n\n## Status transitions\n\nStatus changes are recorded through the sellers surface with the seller and the action. The complete set of actions is:\n\n- **Approve** — status becomes approved. This is the onboarding gate: the seller is cleared to list.\n- **Suspend** — status becomes suspended. Listings are suppressed, payouts are held under the enforcement hold rules, and no new orders are taken.\n- **Reject** — status becomes rejected. This is the refusal of the application.\n\nThere is no probationary, warning, or pending-review status. Intermediate measures are taken through the enforcement steps — listing suppression and payout hold — not through seller status. If the action you want is not one of these three, you are reaching for the wrong tool; see Policy Enforcement (§3.9.6).\n\nEvery transition records an audit entry with the state before and the state after, the acting administrator, and the network address. If that audit entry is not written, treat the transition as unverified and check the record before relying on it.\n\n### Approve\n\nBefore approving, complete the verification checks in the next section. Approving a seller who has not completed verification is not a favor to the seller; it creates liability for the Company and for the seller.\n\n### Suspend\n\nSuspend only for cause — a violation record or an investigation finding. A suspension with no linked violation record is an action without a stated justification and is unlikely to withstand the seller's appeal.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Suspending a seller suppresses the seller's listings, holds the seller's payouts, and stops new orders. It acts directly on the seller's livelihood. Confirm the seller's identity, the active tenant, and the violation record or investigation finding that justifies the suspension before you record it, and state the reason on the record.\n\n### Reject\n\nRejection is final for that application. The seller may reapply only after curing the stated reason, so state the reason on the record. A rejection recorded without a reason generates a support contact and, in time, a dispute.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Rejecting an application ends it. Record the specific reason for the rejection before you record the decision, so the seller knows what must be cured before any reapplication.\n\n## The verification checks before approval\n\nComplete all of the following before you approve a seller:\n\n1. **Business verification** — the entity exists and its identity matches, under the Business Verification Policy (§2.3.8), which governs.\n2. **Identity verification under the INFORM Act** — identity verification for all sellers, bank and payment account verification, and annual re-verification for high-volume sellers, under the INFORM Act Compliance Policy (§2.3.11), which governs.\n3. **Tax identity** — the seller's tax identity information is collected and on file, under the Tax Identity and 1099 Reporting Policy (§2.3.10), which governs. The payment processor issues the annual information return; the Platform must still hold the identity.\n4. **Acceptance of the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1)** — including the limited payment collection agency terms. A seller who has not accepted the current Selling Terms is not onboarded, whatever the status field shows.\n\n## Seller type assignment\n\nSeller type is set through the same surface. The value is recorded in a standardized form. Type drives downstream behavior: the applicable fee schedule, program eligibility, and reporting segments. Set it deliberately at onboarding. Changing it later re-prices the seller's economics, so a type change is a commercial decision, not a clerical correction — record the reason for the change.\n\n## Brand authorizations\n\nAuthorization records sit alongside the seller record and show, for each gated brand, the authorization type, the evidence, the status, the validity period, and who reviewed it and when. A seller may list a gated brand on either of two bases: a purchase order within the period the Brand Gating Policy (Sellers) (§2.3.48) states, or a current manufacturer authorization. Review is completed within the period that policy states. There is no grace period on expiry: listings are suppressed when an authorization lapses.\n\nThe standing rule: **brand gating applies to seller listings only. It never applies to products the Company itself sells.** A brand owner's complaint about a Company listing is not a gating matter, and Company listings must not be suppressed through the seller enforcement tools.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n- **A seller says they are approved but cannot list.** Check three separate gates: the status is in fact approved in the record; there is no brand gating issue on the specific brand; and the seller's connected-account onboarding is complete, per Payouts Oversight (§3.9.11). The seller must pass all three.\n- **A suspended seller is still receiving orders.** The status was recorded, but listing suppression is a separate action. Verify the listings were actually suppressed.\n- **Two seller records appear to be one business.** This is governed by the Multiple Accounts Policy (§2.3.9), which governs when multiple accounts are permitted and when they amount to evasion. Link the related records in the notes; do not merge them, because merging destroys the separate audit histories.\n\n## Related pages\n\n- Policy Enforcement (§3.9.6)\n- Appeals Queue (§3.9.7)\n- Violations and Investigations (§3.9.8)\n- Payouts Oversight (§3.9.11)\n- Catalog Moderation (§3.9.5)\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Seller account questions: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "fees-and-financial-settings": "\n# Fees and Financial Settings\n\nThis page covers the fee schedule surface at `/admin/fees` and the financial settings surface at `/admin/financial-settings`: what the schedule contains, how a fee change is made, and what the financial settings control. This page summarizes the Seller Fee Schedule (§2.3.14) and the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1), which govern. A fee exists only if it is stated in the Seller Fee Schedule (§2.3.14). A fee that is not in the schedule is not chargeable, and an unset fee is charged at zero.\n\n## Before you begin\n\n- Required permission: the sellers module grant at write level for the fee schedule, and the administration grant for financial settings.\n- A fee change is a commercial decision with contractual consequences. Before you begin, have the decision that authorizes the change, the affected seller types, and the effective date.\n\n## The fee schedule\n\nThe schedule view shows the current fees and, because fees vary by seller type, the seller roster for context. The schedule has three families:\n\n- **Referral fees by category** — the percentage the Company takes on each sale, varying by product category, as stated in the Referral Fee Schedule (§2.3.15).\n- **Fulfillment fees** — fees for units fulfilled under Fulfillment by Portal Shop — Program Terms (§2.3.47), where applicable.\n- **Program fees** — subscription, placement, and service fees for programs the seller participates in.\n\nTwo rules follow from the exclusivity rule, and both bind at the desk:\n\n1. **You cannot charge a fee that is not in the schedule.** If there is no entry for it, the chargeable amount is zero. \"We have always charged for that\" is not a fee. Enter it in the schedule, with notice to sellers as required below, or stop charging it.\n2. **You cannot waive a fee that is in the schedule without a record.** A fee quietly not charged is revenue given away without authorization. A waiver — for a promotion, goodwill, or a partner arrangement — is recorded as a decision with its reason and its expiry.\n\n## Changing a fee\n\n1. Record the change in full: which fee, the old value, the new value, the effective date, and the seller types affected.\n2. Check the notice requirement. The Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1) governs notice and re-acceptance where a change affects fees. Sellers must be notified with the effective date before the change applies, in the manner that document requires.\n3. Enter the change through the fees surface with its effective date. The change must be prospective: it applies to transactions on or after the effective date, never to earlier transactions.\n4. Read the schedule back and confirm the change is recorded as intended.\n5. Check a recent transaction's fee calculation against the new schedule.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Changing a fee changes what the Company takes from every affected seller's sales from the effective date. A fee change entered without the seller notice the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1) requires is still recorded by the console, but it breaches those terms, and a fee applied retroactively to completed transactions breaches them further. The console does not send the seller notice and does not check the effective date against the notice period — both are your responsibility before you save the change.\n\n## Financial settings\n\n`/admin/financial-settings` holds the money rules that put the payment policies into effect: payout delay, hold periods, reserve percentages, and tax configuration, implementing the Payment Holds and Reserves Policy (§2.3.18) and the Final Settlement and Payout Policy (§2.3.17), which govern.\n\nTreat each setting as the implementation of a policy, not as a preference. The payout delay exists because the dispute and return period must run before funds are beyond recovery; shortening it for a seller removes that protection before the seller has earned it. A reserve percentage is not a suggestion. Before changing any financial setting, identify the provision that authorizes the new value. If you cannot identify one, do not make the change.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> A financial settings change takes effect across the Platform as soon as it is read. Lowering a reserve or shortening a hold releases the Company's protection on every affected seller at once, and funds paid out under the changed setting cannot be recalled by restoring the setting afterward.\n\n## Checking fees against the payment processor\n\nThe Company's fee is taken at the time of charge through the payment processor, so the schedule and the fee on each charge must agree. To check:\n\n1. Take a sample of recent charges.\n2. For each, compare the schedule rate multiplied by the transaction amount with the fee the processor recorded. A difference means either the schedule changed mid-period — check the effective dates — or the charge was calculated from an out-of-date schedule, which is a defect to report.\n3. For refunded transactions, confirm whether the fee was refunded or retained, and that the treatment matches the dispute or refund decision on record. See Refunds and Returns (§3.9.10).\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n- **Sellers are charged a fee nobody can find in the schedule.** Either the schedule view you are reading is out of date — refresh it and check effective dates — or a charge is being calculated outside the schedule. The second case is serious: find every place a fee is set and verify that each one reads the schedule.\n- **A fee changed, but older transactions show the old fee.** That is correct. Fees apply prospectively. Do not restate completed transactions at the new rate; a person who \"corrects\" historical transactions to the new fee has rewritten the financial record. Establish who did it from the audit log and restore the original figures.\n- **A seller's fee does not match their seller type.** Seller type assignment re-prices the seller; see Sellers Management (§3.9.12). Check when the type changed against the transactions in question, and whether the schedule has entries for that type. A seller type with no fee entries is charged zero in category fees — either an intentional onboarding arrangement or a missing schedule entry. Establish which before changing anything.\n\n## Related pages\n\n- Payouts Oversight (§3.9.11)\n- Sellers Management (§3.9.12)\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Fee schedule and financial settings questions: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "system-settings": "\n# System Settings\n\nThis page covers the settings surface at `/admin/settings` and its sections — locations, email, shipping, printing, backups, and users — and the discipline for changing any of them. Settings are read by the Platform while it runs, so a change takes effect across the Platform as soon as it is read, with no deployment, no partial rollout, and, for most settings, no confirmation prompt. A good value and a bad value propagate at the same speed.\n\n## Before you begin\n\n- Required permission: the administration module grant, at write level for changes.\n- Settings that implement a decided policy — pricing inputs, fee-related values, and anything else that touches money — are changed only on the authority of the document or decision that sets them. If you cannot name that authority, do not change the setting.\n\n## The discipline for every settings change\n\n1. Read and record the current value before you change it. Settings changes are recorded in the audit log, but having the previous value in your own notes is the fastest way back if the change misbehaves.\n2. Change one setting at a time. If two settings change together and the result is unexpected, you cannot tell which change caused it.\n3. Verify the effect. After a synchronization or pricing setting, watch the next cycle. After a display setting, load the affected page.\n4. Confirm the audit log entry matches what you intended. See Audit Logs (§3.9.15).\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Most settings changes take effect immediately, for every user, with no staging step. A wrong value in a pricing, discount, or shipping setting changes what buyers are charged and what the Company earns from the moment it is saved, and charges made under the wrong value cannot be corrected by restoring the setting afterward — each affected order must be dealt with on its own record.\n\nCurrent settings include display and operating values such as products per page, subscription discount rates, and synchronization timing and retry behavior. Treat each as Platform behavior, not as a local preference.\n\n## Locations\n\n`/admin/settings/locations` holds the physical sites: salons, warehouses, and pickup points. The location and tenant switcher in the admin shell reads these records; see Tenants, Locations, and Franchise Oversight (§3.9.17).\n\nLocation records carry the address, operating settings, and service configuration for the site. Appointments, staff schedules, and services resolve against the active location, so a location record with a wrong address is not a cosmetic error: it can misdirect bookings and produce a wrong tax jurisdiction. Treat location data as operating infrastructure. When a new site opens, the location record is part of the opening checklist, not an afterthought.\n\n## Email\n\n`/admin/settings/email` manages email templates and delivery configuration. The Platform sends transactional email — order confirmations, shipping notifications, appointment reminders, password resets, and enforcement notices.\n\n1. Before editing a live template, keep a copy of the current version.\n2. Edit, preview the result, and only then activate it.\n3. Test the variables in the template. A wrong variable renders as a blank or an error in a message the customer receives.\n\nEnforcement and legal notices are templates too — the suspension notice, the appeal decision, and similar messages. If an enforcement step assumes a notice is sent, confirm the template exists and is active. A status changed while its notice template is inactive is an enforcement action the affected party never hears about, and it generates disputes. See Policy Enforcement (§3.9.6).\n\n## Shipping\n\n`/admin/settings/shipping` holds carriers, methods, rate tables, and the values that feed the shipping calculation at checkout. Shipping is built into the displayed price, so these settings affect both what the buyer sees and the Company's margin.\n\nIf shipping looks wrong at checkout — too high, too low, or missing — check in this order:\n\n1. The item's weight data. Missing weights break the shipping calculation for that item.\n2. The rate tier configuration.\n3. Whether a shipping surcharge is currently active.\n\nDo not correct checkout shipping by editing individual prices. Correct the weight data or the tier configuration, so the correction applies consistently.\n\n## Printing\n\n`/admin/settings/printing` holds receipt and label printing configuration for the point-of-sale and fulfillment surfaces: printer assignments, receipt templates, and label formats. Send a test print after any change, and keep a spare printer configured, not merely available — a register that cannot print receipts during a busy period is an immediate operational failure.\n\n## Backups\n\n`/admin/settings/backups` holds backup configuration and status. Verify that backups are actually being produced. A backup configuration that has not produced a restorable backup in weeks is not a backup. Periodically verify a restore: a backup job reporting success and a backup that can be restored are two different claims, and only the second one protects the business.\n\n## Users\n\n`/admin/settings/users` is covered in full in User Management (§3.9.3). It sits under Settings because provisioning is Platform configuration rather than daily operations.\n\n## Related pages\n\n- User Management (§3.9.3)\n- Audit Logs (§3.9.15)\n- Tenants, Locations, and Franchise Oversight (§3.9.17)\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Settings problems and backup or restore concerns: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "audit-logs": "\n# Audit Logs\n\nThis page covers the audit log at `/admin/audit`: what it records, how to read it, how to investigate an event with it, and the limits of the current view. The audit log is the Platform's record of who did what. When something is questioned — a seller suspended without stated cause, a fee changed without notice, a refund nobody can explain — the log is where the answer is established, and it is the record an outside reviewer will ask for first.\n\n## Before you begin\n\n- Required permission: the administration module grant, at read level. Audit visibility is itself permissioned. If you cannot open the audit page, your administration grant is missing; see Roles and Permissions (§3.9.4).\n\n## Reading the log\n\nThe audit page returns the 50 most recent entries for the current tenant, newest first. Each entry shows its identifier, time, action, the acting role, the type and identifier of the record acted on, and supporting detail recorded with the event. Entries are displayed in the form: the action, the record type and a shortened identifier, and the recorded detail — or, where there is no target record, the action and the acting role.\n\nThe view has no paging. Fifty entries in an active operation may cover only hours. For an investigation reaching beyond the visible entries, the underlying records must be queried directly with a filter on the record in question.\n\n## What is recorded\n\nEntries are written by real events. There are no sample or test entries: an empty list means nothing recorded has happened yet in this tenant, and that is the correct answer, not a malfunction. Known sources of entries include:\n\n- **Settings changes** made in the settings surfaces; see System Settings (§3.9.14).\n- **Payment events** processed from the payment processor that move money.\n- **Permission changes** — grants assigned and revoked; see Roles and Permissions (§3.9.4).\n- **Seller status transitions** — recorded with the state before and the state after, the acting administrator, and the network address. The before-and-after pair is the important part: \"status changed to suspended\" is an event, while \"status changed from approved to suspended\" is evidence.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Never create an audit entry by performing a test action in production. Every entry records a real event, so a test suspension of a real seller is a real suspension. Verify logging behavior in a non-production tenant, or by reviewing the recording process, never by acting on a live record.\n\n## Investigating with the log\n\n1. Fix on the record in question — the seller, order, or setting — and follow every entry that names it. The log is chronological; the record is the thread that connects the entries.\n2. Read the before-and-after values, not only the action names. An entry with values shows what changed; an entry without them shows only that someone was there.\n3. Check the actor and the network address. The actor identifies the staff record responsible. An action from an unusual address is a question to ask, not an accusation — people travel — but it is always worth the question.\n4. Look for what is missing. A record that changed with no corresponding entry is the most important finding in any investigation. It means the change came through a path that does not record an entry, or from outside the admin portal entirely. Both possibilities must be established and corrected.\n\n## Limits to know\n\n- **Coverage is not complete.** Seller status transitions record entries. Establish whether any state-changing action you rely on records an entry with the same before-and-after discipline. An action that records no entry happened without proof, and the correction is to make the action record one — not to rely on administrators remembering to write things down.\n- **Recorded detail varies.** Some entries carry full context — old value, new value, reason. Others carry little or nothing, and tell you only that something happened. When you review the log, treat a detail-free entry as an incomplete record and press for the standard form: action, record, before, after, actor, network address.\n\n## Retention\n\nAudit entries are the record for enforcement, financial, and permission actions, and they are retained in accordance with the Company's regulatory retention requirements.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Never delete or purge audit entries to tidy the log. There is no housekeeping task for this record, and removing entries outside the retention requirements is destruction of the evidence on which enforcement and financial decisions stand. If storage grows, entries are archived under the retention requirements, not removed on judgment at the desk. Questions about retention periods go to legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Related pages\n\n- Roles and Permissions (§3.9.4)\n- Sellers Management (§3.9.12)\n- System Settings (§3.9.14)\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Audit log access and gaps in coverage: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Retention requirements and requests for records under legal process: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "reports-and-analytics": "\n# Reports and Analytics\n\nThis page covers the reports surface at `/admin/reports` and the analytics surface at `/admin/analytics`: what each provides, how to read a figure before acting on it, how reports are checked against the dashboard and the payment processor, and the rules for exporting and sharing figures. The dashboard answers whether anything needs attention today; reports answer whether the business is healthy; analytics helps establish why. See Admin Dashboard (§3.9.2).\n\n## Before you begin\n\n- Required permission: the reports module grant for reports, and the analytics module grant for analytics.\n- Before you rely on any figure, establish what it counts, over what period, and against what total. A figure without those three facts is not yet a basis for a decision.\n\n## Reports\n\nReports are the structured, repeatable views: sales by period, orders by status, refunds by reason, seller performance, and inventory positions. They read the same records the operating surfaces read — orders, refunds, sellers, inventory — presented in grouped form: by day, week, month, category, seller, or location.\n\nA report is only as reliable as its totals. A conversion rate without a visible base count is a number without a meaning, and a list of top sellers that does not say whether it ranks by revenue, units, or orders is three different lists under one title. When you read a report, confirm what it groups and what it counts before you act on it. When you request a new report, specify the grouping (per day, per order, per seller), the measure, and the total it is measured against. \"Sales report\" is not a specification.\n\n## Analytics\n\nAnalytics is the exploratory side: funnels, groupings over time, and event streams. Analyses are run by named actions: the console sends the action name with its parameters, such as date ranges and filters, and the corresponding analysis runs. The bookings funnel shown on the dashboard is one such analysis, built from booking funnel event records.\n\nBecause the available analyses are defined on the server side, the set of analyses grows with the Platform rather than with the page. If the analysis you need is not offered in the interface, it may still exist unexposed — and if it does not exist, the request to create it should be specific: \"bookings funnel grouped by location\" can be acted on; \"better analytics\" cannot.\n\n## Reading a funnel\n\nFunnels count events by stage name. That creates a specific failure to watch for: if the stage names recorded by the Platform change while the funnel's stage definitions do not, one stage silently becomes two — the old name stops growing, the new name starts from zero, and every conversion percentage after that point is wrong. When a funnel stage drops to nothing overnight, check whether the stage names changed before concluding that customer behavior changed. Behavior rarely changes overnight; names do.\n\n## Checking figures against each other\n\nThree comparisons should hold, and each disagreement has a usual explanation:\n\n- **Dashboard revenue for today against a sales report for today.** Both read the order records. Confirm both use the same definition of the day: a report grouped by one time zone and a dashboard grouped by another will disagree at the edges. Settle the time zone before escalating a difference.\n- **Platform fee revenue against the payment processor's fee records.** Small timing differences are normal, because processor notifications arrive with delay. A persistent gap is not; reconcile it under Fees and Financial Settings (§3.9.13).\n- **Refund reports against the processor's refunds.** Every Platform refund record requires a matching processor refund, and the reverse. Investigate any item that appears on only one side; see Refunds and Returns (§3.9.10).\n\n## Exporting and sharing figures\n\n1. Export the parameters with the numbers: the date range, the filters applied, and when the report was produced. A file of monthly sales without those facts cannot be interpreted later, and an uninterpretable export is a rumor in a spreadsheet.\n2. Do not make a decision from another person's screenshot or pasted figures. If a figure matters — a fee disagreement, an action based on seller performance, a figure reported upward — run the report yourself. A screenshot is out of date the moment it is taken and carries none of its parameters.\n\n## What analytics cannot establish\n\nAnalytics describes what happened, not why it happened. A funnel shows where buyers stopped; it does not show whether the cause was price, confidence, a broken control, or a carrier outage. A falling rebook rate does not say whether the cause is a policy change or a staffing change. Use analytics to decide where to look, then look: read the reviews, check the support queue, and ask the staff involved. See Support and Notifications (§3.9.18).\n\n## Related pages\n\n- Admin Dashboard (§3.9.2)\n- Refunds and Returns (§3.9.10)\n- Fees and Financial Settings (§3.9.13)\n- Support and Notifications (§3.9.18)\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Report discrepancies and requests for a new analysis: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "tenants-locations-and-franchise-oversight": "\n# Tenants, Locations, and Franchise Oversight\n\nThis page covers the tenant switcher in the admin header, how tenant separation works, location records, and what franchise oversight from the admin portal consists of. Every record in the Platform carries a tenant, and every admin view shows only the active tenant's records. Franchises are child tenants of the master tenant. This page summarizes the Franchise Agreement Policy (§2.4.30), which governs the franchisor's and franchisee's rights; where this page and a signed franchise agreement differ, the agreement controls.\n\n## Before you begin\n\n- Required permission: an admin account with access to each tenant you switch into. The switcher lists only the tenants your account may access.\n- Permissions are tenant-aware: the permission check prefers your employment record in the active tenant, so the same person may hold different powers in different tenants. See Roles and Permissions (§3.9.4).\n\n## Switching tenant\n\n1. Open the location and tenant control in the admin header. It lists the tenants your account may access, with each tenant's name, type, and whether it is the master tenant.\n2. Select the tenant you intend to work in.\n3. Wait for the page to reload in full. The reload is deliberate: tenant context is rebuilt completely — locations are re-resolved, permissions are re-read, and every view is re-run against the new tenant's records — so that no data from the previous tenant carries over.\n\nDo not work around the reload, and do not keep two tenants open in two tabs for casual comparison. Each tab is a separate context, and acting in the wrong one is easy.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Before any consequential action — a seller suspension, a fee change, a refund, an enforcement decision — read the tenant name in the header and confirm it is the tenant you intend. An action recorded against the wrong tenant's seller is not undone by an apology, and the audit log records the tenant with the action, which is how the error is usually discovered: by the party it harmed.\n\n## How tenant separation works\n\nEvery admin action resolves the active tenant and restricts its records to that tenant. In practice:\n\n- Sellers, orders, violations, appeals, and wallets are all tenant-scoped. There is no combined cross-tenant view in the admin portal. To compare two tenants, switch between them and compare your own notes.\n- The **master tenant** is the Platform operator's own tenant. It holds marketplace-level configuration, the fee schedule, and the policy documents. Franchise and seller tenants sit beneath it.\n- The tenant record itself carries the tenant's name, type, parent tenant, legal and tax identity, and the connected account used for its payment rails.\n\n## Locations\n\nEach tenant's location records hold its physical sites, with addresses, operating configuration, and service settings. The admin shell loads the active tenant's locations; the location picker, appointment surfaces, and tax and shipping decisions all resolve against these records.\n\nLocation accuracy is operational, not cosmetic. A wrong address misdirects bookings; a missing location breaks the location picker; a wrong tax configuration misstates tax at checkout. See System Settings (§3.9.14) for the locations section of Settings.\n\n## Franchise oversight\n\nFranchise records hold the agreement — its type, status, royalty rate, fee schedule, signed and expiry dates, and the agreement document — and the franchise locations, with business name, owner, status, address, and settings. The franchisor-side view is at `/admin/franchise`, distinct from the franchisee's own portal.\n\nOversight from the admin portal consists of the following:\n\n1. **Work inside the franchise tenant.** Switch to the franchise tenant to see its sellers, orders, staff, and settings. You administer within that tenant, not above it; the portal provides no combined view across all franchisees.\n2. **Take commercial terms from the agreement.** The agreement is the source of truth for royalty rate, fee schedule, territory, and term. The record in the portal carries those values, but the amounts are set by the agreement — they are not values for an administrator to set at the desk. If a franchisee disputes a royalty charge, the agreement document decides the question, not the portal record.\n3. **Apply brand standards and audits as policy matters.** Brand standards and audit rights are governed by the Franchise Brand Standards Policy (§2.4.36) and the Franchise Audit and Inspection Policy (§2.4.38), which govern. The admin surfaces show the data; those documents state what the data means and what may be done about it.\n\nRoyalty calculation — applying the rate to reported sales — follows the agreement's own definitions, including what counts toward the sales figure. Do not improvise that definition at the desk; if the agreement does not define a point, escalate it rather than deciding it.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Tenant separation protects a franchisee from the franchisor in the same way it protects tenants from each other. The franchisor's powers over a franchisee are the powers in the signed agreement — audit rights, brand standards, and termination under that agreement — exercised through those mechanisms. They are not a general right to enter the franchisee's tenant to change its prices, take its staff, or override its operations, and the admin portal must not be used as if they were.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n- **You changed something and nothing appears to have happened.** Check the active tenant. The change most likely landed in a different tenant from the one you are viewing.\n- **A franchisee can see another franchisee's data.** Treat this as a security incident, not a display error. Establish whether the affected views are correctly restricted to the tenant, and whether the person holds employment records in both tenants; see User Management (§3.9.3). Escalate immediately under the CONTACT callout below.\n- **The switcher shows tenants you should not be able to access.** Tenant visibility is controlled by the switchable-tenants list, not by the header display. Report it as an access-control defect.\n\n## Related pages\n\n- Roles and Permissions (§3.9.4)\n- Admin Access and Login (§3.9.1)\n- System Settings (§3.9.14)\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Tenant access problems and any suspected cross-tenant exposure: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Franchise agreement disputes and audit or termination matters: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "support-and-notifications": "\n# Support and Notifications\n\nThis page covers the support desk at `/admin/messages` and the notifications system. The support desk is a ticket queue you work; notifications are automated messages the Platform sends when events occur. The two fail in ways that look like each other, so this page also states how to tell a queue failure from a notification failure.\n\n## Before you begin\n\n- Required permission: the support module grant — read level to view the queue, write level to reply and change ticket status.\n- Before your first reply, establish which reply type is public and which is internal in the ticket view, and confirm it each time you reply. See the warning under Working a ticket.\n\n## The support desk\n\nThe messages surface is a ticket queue, not a chat window and not an inbox. Its functions are:\n\n- **List** — the queue itself, with filters. Work it oldest first within each priority. Working newest first is how a queue accumulates a permanent residue of difficult tickets everyone avoids.\n- **Detail** — the full ticket: who raised it, the subject, the history, and prior replies. Read the history before replying. The most common support failure is answering the question the ticket appears to ask, when the history shows that question was already answered.\n- **Reply** — a response recorded as either a public reply or an internal note.\n- **Update** — status, assignment, and priority changes.\n- **Remind** — a prompt on a stalled ticket. Use it for tickets waiting on someone else — a seller who has not responded, a buyer who has not supplied a photograph. A ticket waiting on you does not need a reminder; it needs you.\n\n## Working a ticket\n\n1. On arrival, assign every ticket a priority, an owner, and a category — for example billing, order status, enforcement, technical, or account access. Adopt a fixed set of categories and use it consistently; uncategorized tickets cannot be reported on, and unreported work is invisible work.\n2. Read the full history in the detail view.\n3. Select the reply type deliberately — public reply or internal note — and confirm the selection before sending.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Posting an internal note as a public reply is a confidentiality breach: internal notes may contain enforcement reasoning, fraud suspicions, or another customer's details. Confirm the reply type on every reply, not only your first.\n\n4. Reply, then set the status and owner to reflect what happens next.\n5. When the matter is resolved, close the ticket with a resolution note stating what was done. \"Resolved\" alone is not a note. Resolution notes are what the next person handling the same problem will learn from, and an open but resolved ticket distorts every measure the queue produces.\n\n## Tickets that are not ordinary support\n\n- **Enforcement matters.** A seller writing about a suspension is raising the beginning of, or a matter parallel to, the appeals process; see Appeals Queue (§3.9.7). Do not close an enforcement complaint with a support reply alone. Link the ticket to the violation or appeal record and make sure the formal process runs. Telling a suspended seller \"we are looking into it\" while no appeal is on record is a failure of process.\n- **Fraud signals.** Read the queue for patterns, not only for tickets. A sudden cluster of \"charged twice\" reports indicates a payments defect. A wave of \"my order never arrived\" from one supplier indicates a fulfillment failure. A report that bank details were changed without the seller's knowledge is a security incident; see Payouts Oversight (§3.9.11). The pattern is often more important than any single ticket in it.\n\n## Notifications\n\nNotifications are the Platform's automated outbound messages: order confirmations, shipping updates, appointment reminders, enforcement notices, and password resets. Unlike a support reply, a notification is triggered, not written. It fires when its event occurs and uses template content; see the email section of System Settings (§3.9.14).\n\nYour responsibilities for notifications are configuration and verification:\n\n1. **Know what triggers each notification.** Every automated message the Platform sends should map to a triggering event. A notification you cannot name a trigger for is an uncontrolled notification; establish its trigger or escalate it.\n2. **Verify the templates exist and are active.** A status change whose notice template is inactive or broken produces no message, and the action becomes a silent one. After any enforcement action, confirm the affected party was actually notified — check the notification record, not only the workflow. See Policy Enforcement (§3.9.6).\n3. **Do not substitute notifications for the support desk.** A buyer with a problem that does not fit a template needs a human reply. Templates answer the common cases; the queue handles the rest.\n\n## Telling the two failures apart\n\nWhen a user says they never received a message, establish which system was supposed to send it:\n\n1. Check the notification record for the message. If it was sent, the failure is in delivery — a spam folder or a wrong address on file.\n2. If no notification was sent, the failure is in the trigger or the template.\n3. If the user is waiting for a reply rather than an automated message, check the ticket. The reply may be sitting in the queue unposted, or posted as an internal note the user cannot see.\n\nFor a growing queue, establish the cause before asking for more staff. Either volume rose — a campaign, an outage, a policy change driving contacts — or throughput fell: too few staff, tickets stalled on one owner, or a broken triage step. Arrival rate, resolution rate, and the age distribution of open tickets distinguish the two.\n\n## Related pages\n\n- Policy Enforcement (§3.9.6)\n- Appeals Queue (§3.9.7)\n- System Settings (§3.9.14)\n- Orders Management (§3.9.9)\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Support queue and notification problems: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "admin-dashboard": "\n# Admin Dashboard\n\nThis page describes the admin dashboard at `/admin/dashboard`: the key performance tiles, the bookings funnel, today's appointments, and the supporting widgets. The dashboard is a read-only view of today. You cannot change an order, an appointment, or any other record from this page.\n\n## Before you begin\n\n- You must be signed in as a platform administrator. See Admin Access and Login (§3.9.1).\n- No additional permission is required to view the dashboard. Sections appear only where your module permissions allow them; see Roles and Permissions (§3.9.4).\n\n## The key performance tiles\n\nSix tiles appear across the top of the dashboard:\n\n1. **Today's Appointments** — the count of appointments starting today.\n2. **Today's Revenue** — amounts captured today, shown to two decimal places.\n3. **New Customers (30d)** — customers created in the trailing 30 days.\n4. **Low Inventory** — the count of items below their reorder threshold. This tile is a warning, shown in amber.\n5. **No-Show Rate (30d)** — the percentage of appointments marked no-show in the trailing 30 days, with the underlying counts shown beneath it. When no appointments fall in the window, the tile reads \"No data yet\" rather than 0%.\n6. **Rebook Rate (30d)** — the percentage of customers with completed or confirmed appointments who rebooked, with the underlying counts shown beneath it.\n\nRead each rate together with its counts. A rate calculated from a small number of appointments or customers does not support the same conclusions as one calculated from a large number.\n\n## The bookings funnel\n\nThe funnel shows booking stages over a 30-day window. Each stage shows its count and its conversion percentage relative to the first stage.\n\n- Stage names are displayed in title case. If a stage name looks wrong, check the underlying event names before concluding the funnel is broken.\n- An empty stage is shown as empty. A stage with no events usually means event recording has a gap at that stage; it is information, not a figure to estimate around.\n\n## Today's appointments\n\nThe appointments panel lists only appointments starting today. Each row shows the customer, pet, breed, service, groomer, status, and price.\n\nTo change an appointment status:\n\n1. Open the Appointments surface.\n2. Find the appointment and make the change there.\n\nStatus changes are not made from the dashboard, so that a display action can never be mistaken for a recorded change.\n\n## The widgets\n\nThree widgets appear below the main panels:\n\n- **Staff schedules** — who is working today and how many appointments each person has. Slot-level detail is not tracked by this widget, so empty slots mean the widget does not record slots, not that no availability exists.\n- **Alerts** — inventory alerts and document alerts, each with a message, detail, and count.\n- **Grooming records** — recent records from completed appointments.\n\n## When part of the dashboard is empty\n\nEach part of the dashboard loads independently. A failed load leaves that part empty or at its default values without affecting the rest of the page. A partially empty dashboard therefore usually indicates a failing data source, not a quiet business day. Check whether other administrators see the same gap before reporting business conclusions from the page, and escalate a persistent gap under the CONTACT callout below.\n\n## Using the dashboard\n\nUse the dashboard at the start of a shift to answer one question: what does today look like right now? Check that today's appointments are real, that revenue is moving, and whether low inventory or an alert needs attention. For trends and analysis, use Reports and Analytics (§3.9.16). For the records behind the revenue figures, use Orders Management (§3.9.9).\n\n## Related pages\n\n- Reports and Analytics (§3.9.16)\n- Orders Management (§3.9.9)\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Dashboard data problems: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "user-management": "\n# User Management\n\nThis page covers the two records that control who can use the admin portal — the login roster and the employment record — and how to provision a new administrator, remove access when a person leaves, and correct a mismatched login. Access decisions act on a person's ability to work and on the integrity of the audit record, so follow the order of steps given here.\n\n## The two records\n\n- **The login roster** ties a sign-in identity to the portal: the account, the email, and the portal role. The portal looks up this record when it resolves module permissions.\n- **The employment record** holds the person's name, email, role, active status, and tenant assignment. The permission check for each admin action requires an active employment record matching the signed-in email.\n\nA person may exist in one record without the other. Most access failures occur in that gap. Deactivating the employment record closes access to admin actions on the person's next request, even if the person can still sign in and the portal still displays sections to them.\n\n## Before you begin\n\n- Required permission: user-management permission, held by the owner or an administrator granted that scope.\n- You need the new administrator's correct work email. The permission check matches on the email address; a misspelled email is the most common provisioning error.\n\n## Provisioning a new administrator\n\n1. Create the employment record: name, email, role, and active tenant. Choose the role deliberately. The owner role bypasses every permission check, so grant it only where no lesser role is sufficient.\n2. Hand the person their sign-in entry privately, and tell them to sign in with that exact email, by Google sign-in or by email and password.\n3. Assign the person's module grants in both permission systems. See Roles and Permissions (§3.9.4).\n4. Confirm with the person that they can open the sections their work requires and complete an action in one of them. A person who can see sections but whose every action is refused usually has an email mismatch between the two records or a missing grant.\n\nThe Users surface at `/admin/settings/users` lists the roster and exposes role and permission assignment. It does not manage sign-in credentials, multi-factor settings, or provider account recovery; a person locked out of their sign-in provider must use that provider's recovery process.\n\n## Removing access when a person leaves\n\nDo these steps in this order:\n\n1. Deactivate the employment record. This is the step that closes access to admin actions, effective on the person's next request.\n2. Remove or restrict the person's module grants, so that a later reactivation does not silently restore the previous access.\n3. Remove the login roster record.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Do not remove the login roster record first. If the employment record remains active, an existing session can continue to perform admin actions until the session expires. Deactivation of the employment record is the step that ends access, and it must come first.\n\n## Correcting a mismatched login\n\nIf the right person has signed in with the wrong account — for example, a personal account instead of a work account — use the unlink function alongside the user records to detach the sign-in identity from the portal and employment records without deleting either record. Do not delete and recreate the records; deletion destroys the audit history tied to the original record.\n\n## Role notes\n\n- **Owner** — bypasses every permission check in both systems. An owner cannot be restricted by grants. Keep the number of owners as small as the operation allows, and review owner actions in the audit log with particular care; see Audit Logs (§3.9.15).\n- **Platform administrator** — must hold per-module grants for work beyond the base sections.\n- **Groomer, reception, and other employee roles** — these roles belong to the employee portal and are routed away from the admin portal. If an employee needs something done in the admin portal, an administrator does it; do not grant employee roles admin modules to work around a missing employee-portal feature.\n- **Seller-side admin roles** — these belong to the seller portal; see Admin Access and Login (§3.9.1).\n\n## Edge cases\n\n- **One email, records in two tenants.** The permission check prefers the employment record in the currently active tenant. A person with different roles in different tenants has different powers depending on the active tenant. Confirm the active tenant before investigating a permissions report.\n- **Duplicate records differing only by letter case.** Email matching is not case-sensitive, so records that differ only by case refer to one person while appearing twice in the list. Merge them to keep grant assignment clear.\n\n## Related pages\n\n- Admin Access and Login (§3.9.1)\n- Roles and Permissions (§3.9.4)\n- Audit Logs (§3.9.15)\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Access problems you cannot resolve from the records: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "roles-and-permissions": "\n# Roles and Permissions\n\nThis page explains the admin permission model. Two separate grant systems operate at once: one controls which sections a person can see, and the other controls which actions a person can perform. Most permission problems are a mismatch between the two, so this page states how each works, how they differ, and how to assign and remove grants correctly.\n\n## The two systems\n\n**Navigation grants — what a person can see.** These grants control the portal display: the section list, the header shortcuts, and the grouped areas of the portal. A section is shown when the person holds a grant for its module at any level other than none. Areas of the portal group related modules — for example, the dashboard belongs to the customer-relationship area; orders and order details belong to the orders area; sellers, listings, catalog review, and fees belong to the sellers area; trust and safety is its own area; and settings belong to the administration area. A grouped area appears if any module within it is granted.\n\n**Action grants — what a person can do.** These grants control the actions behind the display. Every admin action is checked before it runs: the caller's signed-in email is matched to an active employment record, the grant for the relevant module is read, and its level is compared against the level the action requires. Access levels rank in order: none, read, write, full. The owner role is allowed every action without a grant lookup.\n\nThe grantable modules are: accounting, administration, AI operations, analytics, appointments, content management, communications, customers, deposits, documents, gift cards and credits, inventory, invoices, learning management, marketing, orders, payments, policies, products and services, purchasing, refunds and disputes, reports, sellers, shipping, staff and groomer management, support, and trust and safety.\n\n## The gap between the two systems\n\nBecause the two systems are separate, both of these states are possible:\n\n- **A person can see a section but every action in it is refused.** The navigation grant is present; the action grant is missing, or the employment record is inactive. Correct the action grants.\n- **A person cannot see a section but could perform its actions if they reached them.** The action grant is present; the navigation grant is missing. Hiding a section is not a security boundary. The action check is the boundary, so never rely on a hidden section to keep a person out of its data.\n\nWhen you assign permissions, assign them in both systems, or record why you assigned in only one.\n\n## Before you begin\n\n- Required permission: user-management permission.\n- You need the person's employment record to be active and their sign-in email to match it; action checks fail without that match regardless of grants.\n\n## Assigning or changing grants\n\n1. Open the person's record in the Users surface and open the permission editor.\n2. Set the navigation grants for the sections the person's work requires.\n3. Set the action grants for the same modules, at the lowest level the work requires: read for viewing, write for ordinary changes, full only where the role demands it.\n4. Save. Grant changes take effect on the person's next action; there is no waiting period. If the person reports unchanged permissions, have them refresh the page — a stale page is not a stale grant.\n5. To remove access to a module, delete the grant rather than recording a level of none. Deletion is the clean denial in both systems.\n\n## If the permission display fails\n\nIf the navigation permission check cannot be completed — because of a network error or because the person has no portal record — the portal displays all sections rather than locking the administrator out. The action check does not do this: it refuses the action when it cannot verify permission. The consequence is limited to visibility. A person may see a section, such as payouts, during such a failure, but cannot perform actions in it without a valid grant.\n\n> [!NOTICE]\n> If every administrator suddenly sees every section, treat it as a sign that the permission check is failing, not as a change in anyone's grants, and escalate it under the CONTACT callout below.\n\n## The owner bypass\n\nThe owner role bypasses every check in both systems. Three consequences follow:\n\n- Keep the number of owners as small as the operation allows. Each additional owner is a person the permission system cannot restrict.\n- An owner cannot be limited by grants. Assigning an owner only certain modules has no effect. A person who needs restricted access must not hold the owner role.\n- Review owner actions in the audit log with particular care, because the log is the only control that applies. See Audit Logs (§3.9.15).\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n- **A new hire sees nothing.** The person may have no portal record yet, or may hold no grants. Check the roster first.\n- **A new hire sees everything.** The permission check is failing, or the person's sign-in email does not match the employment record. Check the email match before treating the event as a security incident.\n- **A person could perform an action yesterday and cannot today.** A grant was edited or the employment record was deactivated. Check the audit log before re-granting.\n- **The module you need is not offered in the editor.** The module exists but the editor does not list it yet. The grant can be recorded directly until the editor is updated; record the grant and report the editor gap.\n\n## Related pages\n\n- Admin Access and Login (§3.9.1)\n- User Management (§3.9.3)\n- Audit Logs (§3.9.15)\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Permission failures and suspected over-exposure of sections: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "catalog-moderation": "\n# Catalog Moderation\n\nThis page covers catalog moderation in the admin portal: the review queue at `/admin/catalog-review`, and the moderation checks performed on the products, pricing, listings, and taxonomy surfaces. Moderation keeps the catalog accurate — real products, correct prices, correct placement — because buyers purchase on the strength of what the catalog states. This page summarizes the listing rules in the seller policies (§2.3.25–§2.3.37), which govern.\n\n## Before you begin\n\n- Required permission: the products and services module grant, at write level for any change you make outside the review queue.\n- The review queue itself is a read surface. It does not approve, reject, edit, or suppress items. Catalog changes are made on the product and listing surfaces described below.\n\n## Using the review queue\n\n1. Open `/admin/catalog-review`.\n2. Search by product name, slug, or brand to narrow the list. The list is paginated at 50 items per page, and the header shows the total count.\n3. Read each row: product name, slug, brand, price, and status.\n4. Read any information banner shown above the list. The banner carries notes about the result set, such as a filter applied or a data caution, and it affects how the rows should be read.\n5. Record anything that needs action, and make the change on the surface that owns it — products, pricing, or listings — not from the queue.\n\n## Moderation checks\n\n**Products.** On the products surface, verify the fundamentals: one product family has one product record; variants are recorded as separate stock-keeping units; and the supplier linkage is correct. The same product supplied from two sources is one product with two supply sources, not two products. Where you find the same item recorded twice, treat it as a deduplication candidate — but deduplicate within the taxonomy, never by name or price alone, because one product may legitimately appear in more than one category.\n\n**Pricing.** The displayed price is stored on the price record together with the cost and shipping figures it was derived from, so moderation checks stored values, not formulas. Look for:\n\n- A stored price that does not agree with the cost and shipping figures on its own record.\n- Manually set price records. A manual price is not overwritten by pricing jobs; verify the manual setting was intentional before leaving it in place.\n- More than one current price for the same catalog item and price list. That is a data-integrity error, not a display variation.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Never record a comparison or strikethrough price that is not a real prior price or an approved promotion price. A strikethrough price without a real basis misstates the price history to the buyer.\n\n**Listings.** On the listings surface, check seller listings against the catalog. Brand gating applies: a seller may list a gated brand only with a verified authorization — a purchase order or a manufacturer authorization — under the Brand Gating Policy (Sellers) (§2.3.48), which governs. The gate applies to seller listings only; it does not apply to products the Company itself sells, which are shown as \"Sold by Portal Shop.\"\n\n**Categories, brands, and filters.** Taxonomy placement is a measure of a listing's reliability. A product placed in the wrong category is a moderation issue even when every other part of the record is correct, because buyers navigate and filter by category.\n\n## A regular moderation routine\n\n1. Open the review queue and scan the newest items.\n2. Check the price of anything that looks out of line against the figures on its price record.\n3. Check the category placement of newly imported items; supplier imports do not always arrive in the right category.\n4. For seller listings of gated brands, confirm a verified authorization is on record.\n5. Record what you changed and why. If the change is not in the audit log, it cannot be shown to have happened; see Audit Logs (§3.9.15).\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n- **The queue shows items that should not be sellable.** Status values in the catalog are not uniform in form. Verify the actual status values in the records before acting on a group of items; do not assume a status word means what it appears to mean.\n- **Prices look wrong across a whole supplier's range.** A pricing job may have run with a wrong input, such as a wrong cost figure or missing freight. Correct the input and re-run the price computation. Do not correct prices record by record at scale: hand-edited prices are marked as manual and are then skipped by later pricing jobs, which removes those records from automated correction permanently.\n- **The same product appears twice from one supplier.** Deduplicate within the taxonomy. Where prices differ, the lowest valid price stands; an item with a zero or missing price never displaces a priced item. Archive the duplicate record; do not delete it, so the change can be reversed.\n\n## Related pages\n\n- Sellers Management (§3.9.12)\n- Policy Enforcement (§3.9.6)\n- Audit Logs (§3.9.15)\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Catalog data-integrity problems: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "policy-enforcement": "\n# Policy Enforcement\n\nThis page covers the Trust and Safety hub at `/admin/trust-safety` and the admin policies surface at `/admin/policies`: how enforcement cases are organized, how an enforcement action moves from detection to resolution, and what the hub does not do. This page summarizes the Enforcement and Appeals Policy (§2.1.37) and the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1), which govern. Help content states how the console works; it does not create, extend, or soften any enforcement power.\n\n## Before you begin\n\n- Required permission: the trust and safety module grant. If you cannot reach the enforcement queues, check your grant first; see Roles and Permissions (§3.9.4).\n- Before taking any action described here, identify the governing document and the provision you are applying. An action that cannot be tied to a governing provision must not be taken.\n\n## The hub\n\nThe hub is an overview, not a work queue. It shows four counts, each linking to its queue:\n\n- **Open Violations** — violation records awaiting review.\n- **Pending Appeals** — appeals filed by sellers.\n- **Active Investigations** — investigations open or in progress.\n- **Resolved (30d)** — violations resolved in the last 30 days.\n\nBelow the counts is a recent-activity list showing each record's identifier, kind, label, status, and time. The counts are drawn from the current records when the page loads. If the counts fail to load, the cards display zeros. Zeros caused by a failed load are not evidence that no cases exist; verify the load before reporting that there are no open violations.\n\n## The three case records\n\nEnforcement work is held in three record types, each scoped to the active tenant:\n\n- **Violation records** — reports of a policy violation by a seller, carrying severity and type. Work them in the violations queue; see Violations and Investigations (§3.9.8).\n- **Appeal records** — a seller's challenge to an enforcement action. Decide them in the appeals queue; see Appeals Queue (§3.9.7).\n- **Investigation records** — longer-running inquiries, carrying status and priority. See Violations and Investigations (§3.9.8).\n\n## The policies surface\n\n`/admin/policies` is the admin-side view of the policy documents in this library, provided for the people who enforce them. The policy documents state the rules. If the admin surface and a policy document state a rule differently, the document governs what the rule is, and the difference is a defect to report — not a choice to make at the desk. Never enforce the surface's version over the document's version.\n\n## How an enforcement action proceeds\n\n1. **Detection.** A violation record is created: from a report by a buyer, a brand owner, or another reporter; from an automated policy evaluation; or entered manually by an administrator. The record states what was found, against whom, and under which rule.\n2. **Review.** Open the violations queue, filter by severity, type, and status, and review the record. The response depends on severity and type. Enforcement proceeds by graduated steps — notice, warning, listing suppression or restriction, payout hold, suspension, then termination — under the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1) and the Enforcement and Appeals Policy (§2.1.37), which govern. Serious violations, including counterfeiting, fraud, and product-safety violations, are not required to begin at the first step.\n3. **Action.** Change the record's status through the violations queue. Carry out the enforcement action itself — listing suppression, payout hold, or suspension — against the seller record and the related surfaces; see Sellers Management (§3.9.12).\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> An enforcement action acts on the seller's listings, funds, or ability to sell. Before you record it, confirm the active tenant, the seller's identity, the governing provision, and the step you are taking. A suspension or payout hold recorded against the wrong seller, or without a governing provision, is not cured by correcting it afterward — the seller's loss in the interval is real, and the record of the error remains.\n\n4. **Appeal.** The seller may appeal through the appeals queue. The appeal is decided on its own record. Do not edit the violation record to reflect the appeal outcome; the appeal record carries the decision.\n5. **Resolution.** When the case is complete, move the violation record to resolved. Resolved counts in the trailing 30 days are the measure of case throughput shown on the hub.\n\n## What the hub does not do\n\n- **It does not enforce automatically.** No process reads violation records and suspends sellers on its own. Every status change is a decision by an administrator through the queue. Do not tell a seller that an action was automatic.\n- **It does not notify the seller by itself.** A change of status does not send the seller a message unless a notification is in fact sent for that change. After an enforcement action, confirm the notification was sent; a seller who reports being suspended without notice may be describing a completed enforcement action whose notification never went out. See Support and Notifications (§3.9.18).\n\n## Related pages\n\n- Appeals Queue (§3.9.7)\n- Violations and Investigations (§3.9.8)\n- Sellers Management (§3.9.12)\n- Audit Logs (§3.9.15)\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Enforcement questions: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Legal process, including subpoenas and matters referred to counsel: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "appeals-queue": "\n# Appeals Queue\n\nThis page covers the appeals queue at `/admin/trust-safety/appeals`: how to find an appeal, what an appeal must contain, and how to record a decision. This page summarizes the Appeals Policy (§2.3.5), which governs. Where this page and that policy differ, the policy controls — including the filing window and the evidence required for each violation type.\n\n## Before you begin\n\n- Required permission: the trust and safety module grant, at the level required to record decisions.\n- Your task is to decide, on the record in front of you, whether the original decision stands. It is not to defend the original decision.\n\n## Finding an appeal\n\n1. Open `/admin/trust-safety/appeals`.\n2. Search by appeal identifier or seller name if the seller cannot give you the identifier.\n3. Filter by status — all, pending, or decided — to work the undecided cases.\n4. Open the appeal and read it in full: the enforcement action challenged, who filed it, when, the seller's statement, and the evidence attached.\n\n## What an appeal must contain\n\nThe Appeals Policy (§2.3.5) sets the evidence required by violation type. For appeals about authenticity and sourcing, supplier invoices are required, meeting the requirements in the Invoice Evidence for Appeals (§2.3.63), which governs: invoices must be itemized, dated, identify the supplier and the purchaser, cover quantities sufficient for the sales in question, and be unaltered. Brand authorization is also required where the brand is gated.\n\nAn appeal that asserts authenticity without the required invoices does not meet the standard. When you deny on that ground, state the missing evidence and state exactly what would cure the denial, so the seller knows what a complete filing requires.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> A forged invoice submitted in an appeal is not merely a failed appeal. It is a separate and more serious violation. Do not simply deny the appeal: escalate the matter to investigations. See Violations and Investigations (§3.9.8).\n\n## Recording a decision\n\n1. Read the seller's statement and every item of evidence.\n2. Decide the finding and the sanction as separate questions. Upholding the violation and reducing the sanction are different decisions; a first-time, minor violation by a seller with a clean history may warrant a lesser step than the same violation by a repeat offender, under the Appeals Policy (§2.3.5) and the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1), which govern.\n3. Record the decision through the decision control on the appeal.\n4. Confirm the row changes. The queue updates the row only after the server confirms the decision was recorded, and the decided time shown is the server's time. If the row does not change, the decision was not recorded — check the error and record it again. Do not assume it went through.\n5. Write the reason on the record: which evidence you credited, which you did not, and the rule that decided the case. A decision recorded without a reason invites a further challenge and creates legal exposure.\n6. Confirm the seller was notified of the decision. The queue does not send that notification by itself; see Support and Notifications (§3.9.18).\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> An appeal decision changes the seller's rights under an enforcement action — it may restore listings, release funds, or leave a suspension in force. Record the decision only when you have read the full record, and verify the row changed before telling the seller the outcome.\n\n## Deciding well\n\n- **Decide on the record, not the reputation.** The seller's history is relevant to the sanction, not to whether this violation occurred. A seller with prior violations who submits genuine invoices for this case prevails on this case.\n- **Watch timeliness.** Appeals are entitled to a decision within the period the Appeals Policy (§2.3.5) provides. The queue has no built-in timer, so track pending appeals yourself. An appeal left undecided past that period is itself a failure of process.\n- **Do not improvise the filing window or the next step after a denial.** Apply the Appeals Policy (§2.3.5) as written. If a seller asks what follows a denial, answer from that policy; if the policy does not provide a path, say so in writing on the record and escalate under the CONTACT callout below rather than inventing one.\n\n## What the queue does not do\n\n- It does not carry out the enforcement action; that action preceded the appeal.\n- It does not notify the seller of the decision by itself.\n- It does not change the violation record. The decision lives on the appeal record, and the violation record's history remains intact.\n\n## Related pages\n\n- Policy Enforcement (§3.9.6)\n- Violations and Investigations (§3.9.8)\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Appeal process questions: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Matters referred to counsel: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n",
  "violations-and-investigations": "\n# Violations and Investigations\n\nThis page covers the violations queue at `/admin/trust-safety/violations` and the investigations surface at `/admin/trust-safety/investigations`. Violations are the intake for enforcement; investigations hold the cases that do not fit a single queue record — coordinated fraud, counterfeit networks, and any matter where the seller appears to be acting in concert with others. This page summarizes the Selling Terms of Service (§2.3.1) and the Enforcement and Appeals Policy (§2.1.37), which govern.\n\n## Before you begin\n\n- Required permission: the trust and safety module grant.\n- Identify which record you are working in. The rules differ: enforcement actions are taken on violation records, and the seller's right to appeal attaches to the violation action, not to an investigation.\n\n## Working the violations queue\n\n1. Open `/admin/trust-safety/violations`.\n2. Set the severity filter first, and work by severity rather than by age. A recent counterfeit report takes priority over an older labeling complaint.\n3. Use the status filter (all, open, under review, resolved) and the type filter to narrow the list, and search by identifier or seller name when a seller contacts you.\n4. Read the type before the description. The type determines which policy applies, what evidence is required, and which enforcement steps are available. If the type does not match the description — for example, a record typed as counterfeit that describes slow shipping — correct the type before acting, because the action you take is justified by the type.\n5. Search the seller's name in the queue before deciding. One listing-accuracy violation is ordinarily a seller error; repeated violations by the same seller in a short period indicate a failure in the seller's process, and the response should address the pattern, not only the single record.\n6. Record the status change through the queue. Then refresh the queue and confirm the new status is still shown. A status change that does not survive a refresh was not recorded.\n\nEnforcement proceeds by graduated steps — notice, warning, listing suppression or restriction, payout hold, suspension, then termination — under the documents cited above, which govern.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Counterfeiting, fraud, and product-safety violations are not required to begin at the first step. Do not issue a warning for a counterfeit or safety violation because it feels proportionate: the Company's exposure for counterfeit and unsafe goods does not depend on the seller's intent or history. Take the step the governing document provides for the violation type, and record the provision you relied on.\n\n## Working investigations\n\nInvestigation records carry a status and a priority. Conduct them under these rules:\n\n- **Do not take enforcement action from an investigation.** An investigation is a container, not a violation record. When an investigation produces a specific, actionable finding, open a violation record and take the action on that record. The investigation stays open as the container, and the seller's right to appeal attaches to the violation action.\n- **Set priority as a resource decision.** High priority reserves time and test-purchase spending for cases with active buyer harm or legal exposure. If every investigation is high priority, the designation carries no meaning.\n- **Set a review date on every investigation, and close investigations that have run their course.** Closing with a note — for example, that the evidence was insufficient and the matter will be revisited if new reports arrive — is a complete and professional outcome. An investigation left open indefinitely is not.\n\n## Moving a case between queues\n\n- **Violations to investigations.** When a queue record appears to be part of something larger — the seller's supplier is a known counterfeit source, or several sellers share the same infringing catalog — open an investigation. The violation record remains as the intake record.\n- **Investigations to violations.** When an investigation produces specific findings, return them as violation records so the formal action-and-appeal process applies.\n- **Investigations to legal.** Counterfeit networks, fraud rings, and any matter that may lead to litigation or a referral to law enforcement leave the queues at this point. Keep the investigation record accurate and complete enough to hand to counsel: state facts, attribute sources, and do not record speculation as fact.\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Matters for referral to counsel, including counterfeit networks and suspected fraud rings: legal@unsaidmemories.com.\n> Enforcement queue questions: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n\n## Limitations to know\n\n- Cases are not assigned automatically; there is no rotation or workload balancing in the queue.\n- Neither violations nor investigations carry a built-in time limit display, so track review dates and pending age yourself.\n- There is no automated link analysis across records — for shared addresses, payment accounts, or other connections between sellers. Correlate records manually, and record the basis for any connection you rely on.\n\n## Related pages\n\n- Policy Enforcement (§3.9.6)\n- Appeals Queue (§3.9.7)\n- Sellers Management (§3.9.12)\n",
  "orders-management": "\n# Orders Management\n\nThis page covers the orders list at `/admin/orders` and the order detail view: how to find an order, how to read it, and which actions an administrator may take on it. A placed order is a financial record. This page summarizes the Order Formation and Acceptance Policy (§2.1.15) and the Payment Terms (§2.1.16), which govern.\n\n## Before you begin\n\n- Required permission: the orders module grant — read level to view, write level to cancel.\n- Have the buyer's name or email ready where possible. Buyers rarely know their order identifier; you can find the order faster from their details than they can find the identifier.\n\n## Finding and reading an order\n\n1. Open `/admin/orders`.\n2. Search or filter by buyer, status, or date range.\n3. Open the order to reach the detail view: line items, payment records, fulfillment state, shipment and tracking, and any refund records.\n\nOrder statuses follow the order life cycle: placed, confirmed, in fulfillment, shipped, delivered, cancelled, refunded. Downstream processes rely on these statuses, so read them exactly. An order shown as shipped with no tracking number is a data problem, not proof that a carrier lost the parcel; check the shipment records first.\n\nEach line item names the party fulfilling it — a supplier or a marketplace seller. When a buyer reports that part of an order arrived and part did not, the usual explanation is that different lines are fulfilled by different parties in different shipments. The detail view shows this; the buyer's order page may not show it as clearly.\n\n## Cancelling an order\n\nCancellation is governed on the seller side by the Order Cancellation Policy (§2.3.58) and on the buyer side by the buyer terms, which govern. To cancel:\n\n1. Open the order detail and confirm the order's current status and payment state.\n2. Establish the reason, and record it accurately. A buyer-requested cancellation and a fraud cancellation have different consequences: a fraud cancellation should also flag the buyer account; a buyer-requested cancellation should not.\n3. Record the cancellation. It releases any inventory held for the order, starts refund processing for captured payments, and notifies the buyer.\n4. Confirm the buyer notification was sent. See Support and Notifications (§3.9.18).\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Cancellation cannot be undone. Once cancelled, the order's inventory hold is released and refund processing for captured payments begins, and the buyer is notified. Confirm the order, the reason, and the payment state before you record the cancellation.\n\n## What you may not do to an order\n\n- **You may not edit a placed order.** Its lines, prices, and buyer cannot be changed. The price charged, the tax collected, and the payment captured are the record of what happened; editing them would falsify it. If an order is wrong, cancel it and create a new order at the buyer's request, or correct the outcome by refund and a new order where the error was the Company's.\n- **You do not issue refunds from the order page.** The detail view displays refunds; refunds are issued from the refunds surface. See Refunds and Returns (§3.9.10). The separation exists so that money is never moved from the same screen that displays history.\n\n## Fulfillment of supplier lines\n\nSupplier lines are fulfilled by a manual operation. When the order detail shows a supplier line with no fulfillment record, the correct reading is that the order has not yet been submitted to the supplier — not that submission is in progress. Do not tell a buyer an order \"is being processed\" when it is waiting for a person to submit it. State the actual position and arrange the submission.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n- **The order shows as paid, but the payment is not visible with the payment processor.** Check the payment records on the order detail first, including their processor references, then check the processor directly. The order status and the processor's state can differ during notification delays. The processor's record governs what money moved; the order record governs what was intended.\n- **Two charges appear for one order.** This is usually a retry: the buyer submitted payment twice, or a first attempt appeared to fail and was repeated. Check for two payment records against the order. If both were captured, refund one through the refunds surface; see Refunds and Returns (§3.9.10).\n- **The totals do not add up.** Line totals plus shipping plus tax, less discounts, must equal the order total. If they do not, check whether a price changed between the cart and checkout — prices are stored when recorded, so an old cart may carry an old price — or whether a discount was applied twice. The cost and shipping figures stored on each price record show how each line's price was derived.\n\n## Related pages\n\n- Refunds and Returns (§3.9.10)\n- Payouts Oversight (§3.9.11)\n\n> [!CONTACT]\n> Order and payment discrepancies: support@unsaidmemories.com.\n"
};
